/*
 * OCR Twenty First Century GCSE Biology B (J257) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Real OCR Twenty First Century GCSE Biology B (J257) questions
 * are context-led: a student's investigation, a real-world application, a table
 * of results or a described graph opens each question, practicals are examined
 * in context, and maths skills run throughout. Every item here follows that
 * pattern, written at both Foundation-overlap and Higher demand.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Part tariffs follow the real mix taken from the site's own past-paper
 * data for this specification.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Board style: one marking point per line with (1), allow / ignore
 * / do not accept notes, max n; 6-mark extended responses use the board's three-
 * level ladder with indicative content.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const BIOLOGY_OCR_B_GCSE_PRACTICE = {
  'B1.1': {
    name: 'What is the genome and what does it do?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about the genome.\n\nIn 2007 scientists published the first complete sequence of the DNA of a domestic cat.\n\nWhat is meant by the term genome?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `(all of) the genetic material / DNA of an organism ✓\nALLOW all the genes / DNA in a cell / in the nucleus of an organism\nIGNORE genetic information unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW a section of DNA / a gene` },
      { q: `DNA is a polymer found in the nucleus of cells.\n\nA DNA molecule is made of two strands that are twisted around each other.\n\nWhat name is given to the shape of a DNA molecule?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `double helix ✓\nIGNORE helix unqualified\nIGNORE spiral / twisted ladder` },
      { q: `A student looks at human cheek cells using a light microscope.\n\nThe student wants to find out where the chromosomes are in the cells.\n\nPut a ring around the part of the cell that contains the chromosomes.\n\ncell membrane      cytoplasm      mitochondrion      nucleus`, marks: 1, command: 'Put a ring', markScheme: `nucleus ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW if more than one option is ringed` },
      { q: `A teacher records the blood group of each student in a class.\n\nEvery student has one of four blood groups: A, B, AB or O.\n\nWhat type of variation is shown by human blood group?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `discontinuous (variation) ✓\nALLOW discrete / categoric (variation)\nIGNORE genetic (variation) unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW continuous` },
      { q: `A student extracts DNA from a kiwi fruit.\n\nThe student mashes the kiwi fruit and mixes it with washing-up liquid, salt and water.\n\nSuggest why the student adds washing-up liquid to the mashed kiwi fruit.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `to break down / break open the (cell / nuclear) membranes (so the DNA is released) ✓\nALLOW to burst the cells / to dissolve the (lipids in the) membranes\nIGNORE to break down the cell wall\nIGNORE to clean the DNA` },
      { q: `A mutation is a change in the base sequence of DNA.\n\nMutations happen naturally, but some factors increase the chance that they happen.\n\nGive one factor that increases the chance of a mutation.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any one from:\n• ionising radiation / named example e.g. X-rays / gamma rays ✓\n• ultraviolet / UV light / sunlight\n• (certain) chemicals / mutagens / carcinogens / named e.g. chemicals in tobacco smoke\nIGNORE radiation unqualified\nIGNORE viruses\nIGNORE age / diet` },
      { q: `This question is about DNA, genes and chromosomes.\n\nComplete the sentences.\n\nUse words from the list.\n\nalleles      chromosomes      cytoplasm      genes      nucleus      proteins\n\nIn a human body cell, DNA is found in long structures called ______ .\n\nSections of DNA called ______ tell the cell how to make proteins.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `chromosomes ✓\ngenes ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW alleles for the second gap` },
      { q: `The two strands of a DNA molecule are joined by pairs of bases.\n\nA scientist sequences a short section of one strand of DNA. The base sequence is:\n\nA T T G C A C G\n\nWrite the base sequence of the complementary strand.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `T A A C G T G C ✓✓\nALLOW 1 mark for at least four bases correct in the correct positions\nALLOW 1 mark for a fully complementary sequence written in the reverse order (C G T G C A A T)` },
      { q: `Labrador dogs can have black, brown or yellow fur.\n\nA vet says that the colour of a Labrador's fur is part of its phenotype, and the alleles it has for fur colour are part of its genotype.\n\nDescribe the difference between genotype and phenotype.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `genotype is the (combination of) alleles / genetic make-up an organism has ✓\nphenotype is the characteristics / features (that are expressed / observed) ✓\nALLOW phenotype is what the organism looks like / is a result of genotype and environment\nIGNORE genotype is genes unqualified` },
      { q: `A gardener took cuttings from one hydrangea plant, so all the new plants were genetically identical. She planted some cuttings in acidic soil and some in alkaline soil.\n\nThe plants in acidic soil grew blue flowers. The plants in alkaline soil grew pink flowers.\n\nWhat do these observations show about how the phenotype of an organism is produced? Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `phenotype is affected by the environment / genes and environment interact to produce phenotype ✓\nbecause the plants have the same genes / genotype / genome but different (flower colour) phenotypes in different soils / soil pH ✓\nIGNORE the soil changes the genes (CON)` },
      { q: `Identical twins have the same genome.\n\nTwo identical twin sisters were adopted by different families as babies. At age 30, one sister has a body mass of 62 kg and the other has a body mass of 78 kg.\n\nExplain why the twins have different body masses.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(they have the same genes so) the difference must be caused by the environment ✓\nany one named environmental factor e.g. diet / amount of food eaten / amount of exercise / lifestyle ✓\nALLOW phenotype depends on the interaction of genes and environment for 1 mark\nIGNORE they have different genes (CON)` },
      { q: `A class measured the height of 30 students. The heights ranged from 148 cm to 183 cm and could take any value between these.\n\nExplain why height is an example of continuous variation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(height) can have any value within a range / is not in separate groups / categories ✓\n(because it is) affected by many genes / and the environment ✓\nALLOW measured rather than counted for the first mark\nIGNORE it is measured in cm` },
      { q: `A teacher recorded the blood groups of 30 students. (data written for this practice question)\n\nBlood group A: 11 students\nBlood group B: 4 students\nBlood group AB: 2 students\nBlood group O: 13 students\n\nCalculate the percentage of students with blood group O.\n\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.\n\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(13 ÷ 30) × 100 ✓\n= 43.3 (%) ✓\nALLOW 43.33… or 43 for 1 mark (incorrect rounding) if working shown\nALLOW 2 marks for 43.3 with no working` },
      { q: `A scientist analyses a sample of double-stranded DNA from a bacterium. 21% of the bases in the sample are guanine (G).\n\nCalculate the percentage of bases in the sample that are adenine (A).\n\nPercentage of adenine = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `C = 21% (because G pairs with C) so A + T = 100 − 42 = 58% ✓\nA = 58 ÷ 2 = 29 (%) ✓\nALLOW 2 marks for 29 with no working\nALLOW 1 mark for 79 or 58 (incomplete)` },
      { q: `Lactase is an enzyme that digests the sugar lactose in milk. People who make lactase in their small intestine can digest milk.\n\nThe instructions for making lactase are carried by a gene called LCT.\n\nExplain how the LCT gene controls whether a person can digest milk.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the gene / DNA codes for (making) a protein / lactase ✓\nthe order of bases (in the gene) determines the order of amino acids (in the protein / lactase) ✓\nALLOW the lactase (made) digests lactose so the person can digest milk for the second mark if first mark not awarded\nIGNORE the gene makes lactase (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Phenylalanine hydroxylase is an enzyme that breaks down an amino acid in the body.\n\nIn some people a mutation in the gene for this enzyme changes one amino acid in the active site of the enzyme. The enzyme no longer works.\n\nExplain why changing one amino acid stops the enzyme working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the shape of the active site / enzyme changes ✓\nso the substrate no longer fits / binds (to the active site) ✓\nALLOW the protein folds differently for the first mark\nIGNORE the enzyme is denatured (unqualified)\nDO NOT ALLOW the enzyme is killed` },
      { q: `The human genome contains about 3 billion base pairs. Only about 1.5% of these base pairs are in DNA that codes for proteins.\n\nScientists have found that most genetic variants in a person's genome have no effect on their phenotype.\n\nUse the information to explain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `most (of the genome / variants) are in non-coding DNA ✓\nso the variant does not change a protein / the amino acid sequence of a protein ✓\nALLOW the variant may not change the amino acid coded for (even in coding DNA) for the second mark\nIGNORE the variant is recessive` },
      { q: `A student extracts DNA from strawberries. After filtering the mixture, the student slowly pours ice-cold ethanol down the side of the test tube. A layer of ethanol forms on top of the strawberry mixture. White strands appear where the two layers meet.\n\nExplain why the student adds ice-cold ethanol.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `DNA is insoluble in (cold) ethanol ✓\nso the DNA precipitates / comes out of solution / clumps together (so it can be seen) ✓\nALLOW the cold temperature slows enzymes that break down DNA for 1 mark\nIGNORE ethanol kills bacteria` },
      { q: `A student extracted DNA from a kiwi fruit and saw white, stringy strands in the test tube.\n\nThe student said: "I can see the double helix shape of the DNA."\n\nExplain why the student is wrong.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the strands are (many) DNA molecules clumped / tangled together ✓\na single DNA molecule / the double helix is too small / thin to be seen (with the naked eye / a light microscope) ✓\nALLOW the double helix structure was found using X-ray (crystallography / diffraction) evidence for the second mark\nIGNORE the DNA is broken / damaged` },
      { q: `The table shows the approximate size of the genomes of three organisms and the approximate number of genes each genome contains.\n\nOrganism | Genome size (million base pairs) | Number of genes\nnematode worm | 100 | 20 000\nrice | 430 | 40 000\nhuman | 3 100 | 20 000\n\nA student says: "Organisms with bigger genomes have more genes."\n\nDo the data support the student's statement? Explain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `no ✓\nhumans have a much bigger genome than the nematode worm but (about) the same number of genes / rice has a smaller genome than humans but more genes ✓\nALLOW correct comparative use of the figures\nDO NOT ALLOW yes (no marks)` },
      { q: `The human genome contains about 3.1 × 10⁹ base pairs. About 1.5% of these base pairs code for proteins.\n\nCalculate the number of base pairs in the human genome that code for proteins.\n\nGive your answer in standard form.\n\nNumber of base pairs = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3.1 × 10⁹ × 0.015 / (1.5 ÷ 100) ✓\n= 4.65 × 10⁷ ✓\nALLOW 4.7 × 10⁷\nALLOW 1 mark for 46 500 000 (not in standard form)` },
      { q: `Some human characteristics are caused only by genes, some only by the environment, and some by both.\n\nComplete the table by putting a tick (✓) in one box in each row.\n\nCharacteristic | Genes only | Environment only | Both genes and environment\nblood group | | |\na scar on the knee | | |\nbody mass | | |\nnatural eye colour | | |`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `blood group – genes only\na scar on the knee – environment only\nbody mass – both genes and environment\nnatural eye colour – genes only\nall four correct ✓✓\ntwo or three correct ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW a row with more than one tick` },
      { q: `Scientists studied 38 000 adults. They found that people with one variant of a gene called FTO had a higher mean body mass than people with other variants of the gene.\n\nA newspaper reported: "Scientists find the gene that causes obesity."\n\nExplain why the scientists' data do not show that the FTO variant causes obesity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the data only show a correlation / link (between the variant and body mass) ✓\nother factors / environment / diet / exercise also affect body mass ✓\nALLOW not everyone with the variant is obese / some people without the variant are obese for the second mark\nALLOW a correlation does not prove a cause for 1 mark\nIGNORE sample size too small` },
      { q: `A team of scientists studied how genes and the environment affect the risk of developing asthma. Before their results were published in a scientific journal, they were peer reviewed.\n\nExplain why the results were peer reviewed before they were published.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `other scientists / experts (in the same field) check the work / method / data / conclusions ✓\nto find errors / make sure the conclusions are valid / supported by the data (before others accept them) ✓\nIGNORE so other scientists can repeat the work (unqualified)\nIGNORE to make it fair` },
      { q: `A student measured the hand span of 40 students in her year group to the nearest 0.5 cm.\n\nThe student wants to display her results.\n\nState the type of graph the student should draw and explain your choice.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `histogram ✓\n(because) hand span is continuous (data) / can have any value (in a range) ✓\nALLOW frequency diagram with no gaps between bars / grouped frequency chart\nDO NOT ALLOW bar chart / line graph / pie chart for the first mark\nsecond mark can be awarded if graph type is wrong but reasoning about continuous data is correct` },
      { q: `A student plans to extract DNA from peas. The method uses ethanol.\n\nThe hazard card says that ethanol is highly flammable.\n\nDescribe the risk from using ethanol and one way the student could reduce this risk.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `(ethanol) could catch fire / ignite / cause burns ✓\nkeep away from (naked) flames / Bunsen burners / turn off Bunsen burners ✓\nALLOW use small volumes / keep lid on bottle / wear eye protection (if linked to splashing) for the second mark\nIGNORE wear gloves / be careful` },
      { q: `Sickle cell anaemia is a genetic disorder. It is caused by a change in a single base in the gene for haemoglobin.\n\nPeople with sickle cell anaemia have red blood cells that become stiff and curved, and can block small blood vessels.\n\nExplain how a change in a single base in the gene can change the shape of red blood cells.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the change in base sequence / mutation changes an amino acid (in haemoglobin) ✓\nso the haemoglobin (protein) has a different shape / folds differently ✓\n(abnormal) haemoglobin molecules stick together / form long fibres, distorting the cell ✓\nALLOW protein does not work properly / function changes for the second mark\nIGNORE the gene is damaged` },
      { q: `Siamese cats have pale fur on most of their body but dark fur on their ears, nose, paws and tail. These parts of the body are cooler than the rest.\n\nThe dark colour is made by an enzyme. The gene variant in Siamese cats codes for a version of the enzyme that only works at low temperatures.\n\nA Siamese cat had a patch of fur on its back shaved and kept covered with an ice pack. The new fur that grew there was dark.\n\nExplain these observations.\n\nUse ideas about genes and the environment.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `all the cat's cells have the same genes / gene variant ✓\nthe enzyme is only active / works at low temperature so pigment is only made in cooler areas ✓\nthe ice pack lowered the temperature of the skin so the enzyme worked / pigment was made (in the new fur) ✓\nALLOW the enzyme is denatured / inactive at the higher temperature of the body\nALLOW phenotype depends on interaction of genotype and environment for 1 mark if no other mark awarded` },
      { q: `A student took five cuttings from the same plant. The cuttings are genetically identical. The student grew the cuttings in the same conditions for four weeks, then measured their heights. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCutting | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5\nHeight (cm) | 14.2 | 15.1 | 13.8 | 14.6 | 9.3\n\nThe student said cutting 5 was an anomalous result.\n\nCalculate the mean height of the cuttings, leaving out the anomalous result.\n\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.\n\nMean height = ______ cm`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `14.2 + 15.1 + 13.8 + 14.6 = 57.7 ✓\n57.7 ÷ 4 = 14.425 ✓\n= 14.4 (cm) ✓\nALLOW 3 marks for 14.4 with no working\nALLOW 2 marks for 13.4 (mean including the anomalous result: 67.0 ÷ 5)` },
      { q: `DNA is a polymer made of many repeating units joined together.\n\nDescribe the structure of a DNA molecule.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\n• two strands (twisted into a) double helix ✓\n• (made of repeating units called) nucleotides ✓\n• each nucleotide is a sugar, a phosphate and a base ✓\n• (there are four bases) A, T, C and G ✓\n• complementary base pairing / A pairs with T and C pairs with G ✓\n• bases on the two strands are held together by (weak) hydrogen bonds ✓\nIGNORE amino acids (CON if stated as units of DNA)` },
      { q: `A student extracted DNA from a banana. The student mashed the banana, added a solution of washing-up liquid and salt, filtered the mixture and then poured ice-cold ethanol on top. Only a very small amount of DNA appeared.\n\nSuggest three changes to the method that could increase the amount of DNA extracted.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any three from:\n• mash / grind the banana for longer / more thoroughly (to break open more cells) ✓\n• use more banana / a larger mass of fruit ✓\n• leave the mixture with washing-up liquid for longer / warm it in a water bath (at about 60 °C) for 15 minutes ✓\n• make sure the ethanol is very cold / kept in the freezer ✓\n• pour the ethanol slowly down the side so it forms a separate layer ✓\n• wait longer for the DNA to precipitate ✓\n• use a fruit with more DNA per cell / a polyploid fruit e.g. strawberries ✓\nIGNORE repeat the experiment` },
      { q: `Mutations can happen in body cells or in gametes (sex cells).\n\nA man develops a mutation in the DNA of one of his skin cells after spending a long time in strong sunlight. A woman has a mutation in one of her egg cells.\n\nExplain why only the woman's mutation could be passed on to her children.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `skin / body cell mutations are not in gametes / are not passed on in sperm ✓\nthe egg (with the mutation) may be fertilised / form a zygote ✓\nso every cell of the child / offspring will contain the mutation ✓\nALLOW the skin cell mutation is only in that cell / cells produced from it by mitosis for the first mark\nIGNORE the man's mutation is recessive` },
      { q: `Scientists studied pairs of twins to find out whether genes affect the chance of developing type 2 diabetes. In each pair, at least one twin had type 2 diabetes. (data written for this practice question)\n\nType of twins | Percentage of pairs where both twins had type 2 diabetes\nidentical (same genome) | 76\nnon-identical (share about half their genetic variants) | 34\n\nExplain what the data suggest about the effects of genes and the environment on developing type 2 diabetes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `both twins more likely to have it when identical than non-identical / 76% compared with 34% ✓\n(so) genes have an effect / increase the risk ✓\nnot 100% in identical twins (so) environment / lifestyle also has an effect ✓\nALLOW named environmental factor e.g. diet / exercise for the third mark if linked to not 100%` },
      { q: `A scientist analyses a short double-stranded section of DNA. The section contains 2000 bases in total. 340 of the bases are adenine (A).\n\nCalculate the number of cytosine (C) bases in this section of DNA.\n\nNumber of cytosine bases = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `T = 340 (as A pairs with T) / A + T = 680 ✓\nC + G = 2000 − 680 = 1320 ✓\nC = 1320 ÷ 2 = 660 ✓\nALLOW 3 marks for 660 with no working\nALLOW 2 marks for 830 (2000 − 340 = 1660 ÷ 2, error in pairing)` },
      { q: `A farmer measured the length of 100 ears of wheat from one field. (data written for this practice question)\n\nLength (cm) | 6.0–6.9 | 7.0–7.9 | 8.0–8.9 | 9.0–9.9 | 10.0–10.9\nNumber of ears | 6 | 23 | 41 | 22 | 8\n\nDescribe the pattern shown by the data and explain what it shows about the variation in ear length.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `most ears are in the middle (8.0–8.9 cm) group with fewer at each extreme / normal distribution / bell-shaped ✓\n(this shows) continuous variation ✓\n(because ear length is) controlled by many genes / affected by the environment / named environmental factor e.g. water / soil minerals / light ✓\nALLOW correct use of data for the first mark e.g. 41 ears in 8.0–8.9 cm group` },
      { q: `Scientists studied three different genetic variants of a gene that codes for an enzyme. (data written for this practice question)\n\nVariant | Where the change is | Effect on enzyme | Enzyme activity (% of normal)\nP | non-coding DNA a long way from the gene | none | 100\nQ | coding DNA; one amino acid changed on the surface of the enzyme | small change in shape | 85\nR | coding DNA; one amino acid changed in the active site | large change in shape of active site | 0\n\nUse the data to explain why genetic variants can have different effects on phenotype.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `P: (in non-coding DNA so) no change to the protein / amino acid sequence, so no effect on phenotype ✓\nQ: amino acid change away from the active site only slightly changes shape so enzyme still mostly works / small effect on phenotype ✓\nR: change in active site means substrate cannot fit / bind so enzyme does not work, large effect on phenotype ✓\nALLOW effect depends on where the change is / whether it alters the protein shape for 1 mark if no other mark awarded` },
      { q: `A student carries out a practical to extract DNA from strawberries.\n\nComplete the table to explain the purpose of each step.\n\nStep | Purpose\nmash the strawberries in a sealed plastic bag | ______\nadd washing-up liquid | ______\nfilter the mixture through filter paper | ______\npour ice-cold ethanol slowly down the side of the tube | ______`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `mash: break open / break down the cell walls / cells (to release contents) ✓\nwashing-up liquid: break down / dissolve the (cell / nuclear) membranes (to release DNA) ✓\nfilter: remove cell debris / solid pieces of fruit / separate DNA solution from solids ✓\nethanol: DNA precipitates / comes out of solution / is insoluble in ethanol, so it can be seen / collected ✓\nIGNORE to make it easier (unqualified)` },
      { q: `This question is about the genome and how it affects characteristics.\n\nComplete the sentences.\n\nPut a ring around each correct answer.\n\nThe genome of an organism is all of its genes / genetic material / proteins.\n\nThe four bases in DNA are A, C, G and T / U / Z.\n\nThe characteristics of an organism are called its genotype / genome / phenotype.\n\nA change in the base sequence of DNA is called a mutation / nucleotide / variation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Put a ring', markScheme: `genetic material ✓\nT ✓\nphenotype ✓\nmutation ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed in a sentence` },
      { q: `Most adults in the world cannot digest lactose, the sugar in milk, because the gene for the enzyme lactase is switched off after early childhood.\n\nMany people of northern European descent can digest milk as adults. They have a genetic variant in a section of non-coding DNA near the lactase gene. The variant does not change the lactase enzyme.\n\nExplain how this variant in non-coding DNA allows these adults to digest milk.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `non-coding DNA (near a gene) controls whether the gene is switched on / expressed ✓\nthe variant affects (the ability of) RNA polymerase / the enzyme that copies the gene (to) bind ✓\nso the lactase gene stays switched on / continues to be transcribed / expressed in adults ✓\nso lactase is still made and lactose can be digested ✓\nIGNORE the variant changes the shape of lactase (CON)` },
      { q: `A student wants to extract DNA from strawberries.\n\nThe student has: strawberries, a sealable plastic bag, washing-up liquid, salt, water, a funnel and filter paper, test tubes, a water bath and ice-cold ethanol.\n\nDescribe a method the student could use to extract DNA from strawberries.\n\nIn your answer you should:\n• explain the purpose of the main steps\n• describe how the student could work safely.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed method with steps in a logical order, AND explains the purpose of most steps, AND includes a relevant safety precaution linked to a hazard.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A method with the main steps in a sensible order, AND explains the purpose of at least one step OR gives a safety precaution.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives some steps of a method OR a safety precaution.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• remove leaves / stalks and put strawberries in the bag; seal and mash / squash them\n• make an extraction solution of water, washing-up liquid and salt and add it to the bag; mix gently\n• leave in a water bath at about 60 °C for about 15 minutes\n• filter the mixture through filter paper into a test tube\n• slowly pour ice-cold ethanol down the side of the tube to form a layer on top\n• wait a few minutes; white strands (DNA) appear at the boundary; collect with a glass rod\nPurpose\n• mashing breaks open cell walls / cells\n• washing-up liquid breaks down cell and nuclear membranes so DNA is released\n• salt makes the DNA clump together\n• warming speeds up breakdown of membranes / denatures enzymes that would break down DNA\n• filtering removes cell debris\n• DNA is insoluble in cold ethanol so it precipitates and becomes visible\nSafety\n• ethanol is highly flammable: no naked flames / Bunsen burners off\n• wear eye protection in case of splashes (of ethanol / detergent)\n• take care with hot water bath / do not eat the fruit in the lab` }
    ]
  },
  'B1.2': {
    name: 'How is genetic information inherited?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about sex determination in humans.\nHuman body cells contain 23 pairs of chromosomes. One pair are the sex chromosomes.\n\nWhich sex chromosome is found in every human egg cell?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• X (chromosome) (1)\nALLOW one X\nDO NOT ALLOW XX / Y` },
      { q: `Polydactyly is an inherited condition in which a person has extra fingers or toes. It is caused by a dominant allele, D. The recessive allele is d.\n\nWrite down the genotype of a person who does not have polydactyly.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• dd (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW Dd / dD` },
      { q: `Gregor Mendel bred pea plants. Pea plants reproduce sexually. Pollen grains and egg cells are the plant's gametes.\n\nName the type of cell division that produces gametes.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• meiosis (1)\nIGNORE cell division / reduction division\nDO NOT ALLOW mitosis` },
      { q: `A human body cell contains 46 chromosomes.\n\nHow many chromosomes are in a human sperm cell?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• 23 (1)` },
      { q: `Priya and Tom have three children. All three children are girls.\n\nWhat is the probability that their next child will be a boy?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• 0.5 / 1/2 / 50% / 1 in 2 (1)\nALLOW 1 : 1 / evens\nIGNORE explanations about previous children` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is an inherited disorder caused by a recessive allele, f. The dominant allele is F.\nSam has the genotype Ff.\n\nWhich word describes Sam's genotype?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\nhaploid      heterozygous      homozygous      phenotype`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• heterozygous (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one word ringed` },
      { q: `This question is about the genome and inheritance.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nPut a ring around each correct answer.\nA section of DNA that codes for a protein is called a cell / gene / nucleus.\nDifferent versions of the same gene are called alleles / chromosomes / gametes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• gene (1)\n• alleles (1)\nALLOW variants for alleles if written in\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed in a sentence` },
      { q: `Biologists use special words to describe inheritance.\n\nDraw lines to connect each word to its correct meaning.\nWords: genotype; phenotype; homozygous; heterozygous\nMeanings: having two different alleles of a gene; the alleles an organism has for a gene; having two identical alleles of a gene; the characteristics an organism shows`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw lines', markScheme: `genotype → the alleles an organism has for a gene\nphenotype → the characteristics an organism shows\nhomozygous → having two identical alleles of a gene\nheterozygous → having two different alleles of a gene\n• all four correct (2)\n• two or three correct (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one line from a word` },
      { q: `This question is about sex determination in humans.\nA woman's body cells contain the sex chromosomes XX. A man's body cells contain XY.\n\nExplain why it is the sperm cell, and not the egg cell, that determines the sex of a baby.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• all egg cells contain an X chromosome (1)\n• sperm cells contain either an X or a Y chromosome, so the chromosome in the sperm decides whether the baby is XX (girl) or XY (boy) (1)\nALLOW half of sperm carry X and half carry Y` },
      { q: `A breeder keeps guinea pigs. The allele for black fur, B, is dominant to the allele for white fur, b.\nThe breeder crosses two guinea pigs that both have the genotype Bb. Over several litters they have 36 offspring.\n\nCalculate the number of the 36 offspring that are expected to have white fur.\nNumber with white fur = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• probability of bb / white = 1/4 / 25% (1)\n• 36 × 1/4 = 9 (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer of 9 with no working` },
      { q: `Gregor Mendel crossed pea plants that were heterozygous for seed shape. The allele for round seeds is dominant to the allele for wrinkled seeds.\nThe offspring produced 5474 round seeds and 1850 wrinkled seeds.\n\nCalculate the ratio of round seeds to wrinkled seeds.\nGive your answer in the form x : 1, to 2 decimal places.\nRatio = ______ : 1`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 5474 ÷ 1850 (1)\n• 2.96 (: 1) (1)\nALLOW 2.958… for 1 mark if not rounded to 2 decimal places` },
      { q: `Gregor Mendel crossed pea plants that were heterozygous for seed colour. A genetic diagram predicts a 3 : 1 ratio of yellow to green seeds.\nMendel counted 6022 yellow seeds and 2001 green seeds. This is a ratio of 3.01 : 1.\n\nSuggest why Mendel's results were not exactly 3 : 1.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• fertilisation is random / which gametes fuse is down to chance (1)\n• a genetic diagram only gives the probability of each outcome, so real numbers vary slightly from the prediction (1)\nALLOW pollen with either allele has an equal chance of fertilising an egg\nIGNORE human error / mistakes in counting unqualified` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is an inherited disorder.\nA family tree shows that Anna and Ben do not have cystic fibrosis. Their son Carl has cystic fibrosis.\n\nExplain how this family tree shows that cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• neither parent has the disorder but their child does (1)\n• so both parents must carry a hidden allele that is not expressed / both are heterozygous carriers, and Carl inherited the allele from each parent (1)\nALLOW Carl has two copies of the allele / is homozygous recessive` },
      { q: `Huntington's disease is an inherited disorder of the nervous system. It is caused by a dominant allele, H.\nA father has the genotype Hh. The mother has the genotype hh.\n\nCalculate the probability that their child will inherit Huntington's disease.\nGive your answer as a percentage.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• father's gametes H or h, mother's gametes all h, so offspring Hh : hh = 1 : 1 / 2 out of 4 are Hh (1)\n• 50% (1)\nAward 2 marks for 50% with no working` },
      { q: `Sperm cells and egg cells are haploid. Body cells are diploid.\n\nExplain why gametes must be haploid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• two gametes fuse at fertilisation (1)\n• so the fertilised egg / zygote has the full / diploid number of chromosomes (and not double) (1)\nALLOW 23 + 23 = 46 in humans for the second mark` },
      { q: `In the testes, one cell with 46 chromosomes divides by meiosis.\n\nDescribe the cells produced by this division.\nGive two features.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• four cells are produced (1)\n• each has 23 chromosomes / half the number of chromosomes / is haploid (1)\n• the cells are genetically different from each other / from the original cell (1)\nIGNORE 'smaller'` },
      { q: `Sickle cell anaemia is an inherited disorder of red blood cells. It is caused by a recessive allele.\nKofi is heterozygous for the sickle cell gene.\n\nExplain why Kofi does not have sickle cell anaemia.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Kofi has one dominant allele / one normal allele (1)\n• the dominant allele is expressed / two recessive alleles are needed for the disorder (to show in the phenotype) (1)\nALLOW Kofi is a carrier` },
      { q: `A website says: 'You can use a Punnett square to predict exactly how tall a child will grow.'\n\nExplain why this claim is wrong.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• height is controlled by many genes / is not a single-gene characteristic (1)\n• height is also affected by the environment, e.g. diet (1)\nALLOW most characteristics are the result of multiple genes` },
      { q: `In fruit flies, the allele for long wings, V, is dominant to the allele for short (vestigial) wings, v.\nA student crossed a long-winged fly with a short-winged fly. All 120 offspring had long wings.\n\nGive the genotype of the long-winged parent.\nExplain your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• VV (1)\n• if it were Vv, about half of the offspring would have short wings / some would be vv, but none were (1)` },
      { q: `Dogs have 78 chromosomes in each body cell.\n\nComplete the table to show the number of chromosomes in each type of dog cell.\nType of cell | Number of chromosomes\nSkin cell | 78\nSperm cell | ______\nEgg cell | ______\nFertilised egg cell | ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• sperm 39 and egg 39 (1)\n• fertilised egg 78 (1)` },
      { q: `Thalassaemia is an inherited blood disorder caused by a recessive allele.\nA couple are both carriers. Their first child has thalassaemia. They say: 'There is a 1 in 4 chance of a child having thalassaemia, so our next three children will not have it.'\n\nExplain why the couple are wrong.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• each fertilisation is a separate / independent event (1)\n• so every child has a 1 in 4 / 25% chance of having thalassaemia, whatever happened before (1)\nALLOW the ratio is a probability, not a fixed outcome` },
      { q: `A hospital recorded the number of babies born in one year (data written for this practice question).\nTotal number of babies born = 2846\nNumber of boys = 1459\n\nCalculate the percentage of babies that were boys.\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1459 ÷ 2846 × 100 (1)\n• 51.3 (%) (1)\nALLOW 51.26… for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele, f.\nTwo parents both have the genotype Ff.\n\nWhat is the probability that their child will be a carrier of cystic fibrosis?\nShow your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• offspring FF, Ff, Ff, ff / 2 out of 4 are Ff (1)\n• 1/2 / 0.5 / 50% (1)` },
      { q: `Rabbits reproduce sexually. Baby rabbits in the same litter look different from each other and from their parents.\n\nExplain how sexual reproduction leads to this variation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• meiosis produces gametes that are genetically different (from each other) (1)\n• which gametes fuse at fertilisation is random, so offspring get a new mix of alleles from both parents (1)\nALLOW half the alleles / genes from each parent` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele. Two parents are both carriers.\nThe probability of a child having cystic fibrosis is 1/4.\nThe probability of a child being a girl is 1/2.\n\nCalculate the probability that their next child will be a girl with cystic fibrosis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1/4 × 1/2 (1)\n• 1/8 / 0.125 / 12.5% (1)` },
      { q: `Some cats have extra toes. This is caused by a dominant allele, P. The recessive allele, p, gives the normal number of toes.\nA cat with extra toes was crossed with a cat with the normal number of toes. Half of the kittens had extra toes and half had normal toes.\n\nGive the genotypes of both parent cats.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• extra toes parent Pp (1)\n• normal parent pp (1)` },
      { q: `A breeder keeps guinea pigs. The allele for black fur, B, is dominant to the allele for white fur, b.\nThe breeder crosses a Bb guinea pig with a bb guinea pig.\n\nComplete the Punnett square and state the percentage of offspring expected to have white fur.\nGametes from Bb parent: B, b\nGametes from bb parent: b, b`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', diagram: true, markScheme: `• gametes correctly placed on the edges (B and b; b and b) (1)\n• offspring genotypes Bb, Bb, bb, bb (1)\n• 50% (1)\nALLOW ECF for the percentage from an incorrect Punnett square` },
      { q: `The sex of a human baby is decided by the sex chromosomes in the gametes.\n\nDraw a genetic diagram to show how sex is determined in humans.\nState the probability that a baby will be a boy.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• parents XX and XY, with gametes X, X and X, Y (1)\n• offspring XX, XX, XY, XY correctly shown (1)\n• probability of a boy = 1/2 / 0.5 / 50% (1)\nALLOW Punnett square or line diagram` },
      { q: `Polydactyly is caused by a dominant allele, D.\nA family tree shows that Ian and Jen both have polydactyly. Their daughter Kate does not have polydactyly.\n\nExplain how this family tree shows that polydactyly cannot be caused by a recessive allele.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• if polydactyly were recessive, Ian and Jen would both be homozygous recessive (1)\n• so all their children would have to have polydactyly (1)\n• but Kate does not, so the parents must be heterozygous / Dd and the allele for polydactyly must be dominant (1)` },
      { q: `Gregor Mendel crossed pure-breeding tall pea plants (TT) with pure-breeding dwarf pea plants (tt). All the offspring were tall.\nHe then crossed these tall offspring with each other. He grew 787 tall plants and 277 dwarf plants.\n\nCalculate the ratio of tall to dwarf plants in the form x : 1.\nUse your answer to explain whether Mendel's results support the prediction of a genetic diagram.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 787 ÷ 277 = 2.84 (: 1) (1)\nALLOW 2.8 : 1\n• a Tt × Tt genetic diagram predicts 3 : 1 (1)\n• 2.84 : 1 is close to 3 : 1, so the results support the prediction (1)` },
      { q: `In humans, gametes are produced by meiosis and fuse at fertilisation.\n\nExplain why meiosis keeps the number of chromosomes in body cells the same from one generation to the next.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• meiosis halves the number of chromosomes / makes haploid gametes (1)\n• (in humans) gametes have 23 chromosomes (1)\n• at fertilisation two gametes fuse so the zygote has 46 / the diploid number is restored (1)` },
      { q: `Thalassaemia is caused by a recessive allele, t.\nTwo parents are both carriers (Tt). They have a son, Leo, who does not have thalassaemia.\n\nCalculate the probability that Leo is a carrier of thalassaemia.\nExplain your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• possible offspring TT, Tt, Tt, tt (1)\n• Leo does not have thalassaemia, so he cannot be tt; only TT, Tt, Tt are possible (1)\n• 2/3 / 0.67 / 67% (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 1/2 for the third mark` },
      { q: `Huntington's disease is caused by a dominant allele, H.\nA man has Huntington's disease and is heterozygous. His partner does not have Huntington's disease.\n\nDraw a Punnett square to show the possible genotypes of their children.\nState the probability that a child will not have Huntington's disease.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• parental gametes H, h and h, h (1)\n• offspring Hh, Hh, hh, hh (1)\n• 1/2 / 0.5 / 50% (1)\nALLOW ECF for the probability from an incorrect Punnett square` },
      { q: `A gardener has a strawberry plant. Strawberry plants can make seeds by sexual reproduction. They also grow runners that become new plants (asexual reproduction).\nThe plants grown from seeds are all slightly different. The plants grown from runners are all identical to the parent plant.\n\nExplain why the plants grown from seeds show variation but the plants from runners do not.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• seeds come from gametes made by meiosis, which are genetically different (1)\n• fertilisation combines alleles from two gametes / two parents at random (1)\n• runners are made by mitosis so they have the same genes / alleles as the parent (1)` },
      { q: `In fruit flies, the allele for a normal body colour, E, is dominant to the allele for an ebony (dark) body, e.\nA student crossed an Ee fly with an ee fly. The results are shown (data written for this practice question).\nNormal body colour: 412 flies\nEbony body colour: 388 flies\n\nCalculate the percentage of offspring with a normal body colour.\nExplain whether the results match the expected outcome of this cross.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 412 ÷ 800 × 100 = 51.5 (%) (1)\n• Ee × ee is expected to give 50% normal / a 1 : 1 ratio (1)\n• 51.5% is close to 50%, so the results match (the difference is due to chance) (1)` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele.\nMaya and Josh do not have cystic fibrosis. Their baby has cystic fibrosis.\n\nExplain how two parents who do not have cystic fibrosis can have a child with the disorder.\nUse ideas about alleles in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• both parents are carriers / heterozygous / Ff (1)\n• each parent makes gametes, half carrying the recessive allele (1)\n• the baby inherited the recessive allele from both parents / is homozygous recessive / ff (1)` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele, f. The dominant allele is F.\nA family tree shows:\n• Rosa and David do not have cystic fibrosis.\n• Their children are Emma, who has cystic fibrosis, and Finn, who does not.\n• Emma has a child, Gina, with Hari. Hari does not have cystic fibrosis and is not a carrier. Gina does not have cystic fibrosis.\n\nGive the genotypes of Rosa, Emma, Hari and Gina.\nExplain how you worked out Rosa's and Gina's genotypes.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• Rosa Ff and Emma ff (1)\n• Hari FF and Gina Ff (1)\n• Rosa: Emma is ff so she inherited an f allele from each parent, but Rosa does not have cystic fibrosis so she must also have F (1)\n• Gina: she must inherit f from Emma and F from Hari (1)` },
      { q: `A breeder has a black guinea pig. The allele for black fur, B, is dominant to the allele for white fur, b. The breeder wants to know whether the black guinea pig is homozygous (BB) or heterozygous (Bb).\n\nDescribe how the breeder could find out the genotype of the black guinea pig.\nInclude the results expected for each genotype.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• breed the black guinea pig with a white / bb guinea pig (1)\n• look at the fur colour of a large number of offspring (1)\n• if BB, all offspring are black / Bb (1)\n• if Bb, about half the offspring are white / 1 : 1 ratio of black to white (1)\nALLOW any white offspring means the parent is Bb` },
      { q: `Lucy has cystic fibrosis, which is caused by a recessive allele. Her partner, Omar, does not have cystic fibrosis. Omar's sister has cystic fibrosis, but neither of his parents has the disorder.\n\nCalculate the probability that Lucy and Omar's first child will have cystic fibrosis.\nShow your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Omar's parents must both be carriers / Ff (1)\n• Omar is not ff, so the probability he is a carrier is 2/3 (1)\n• if Omar is Ff, a cross with Lucy (ff) gives a 1/2 chance of an ff child (1)\n• 2/3 × 1/2 = 1/3 / 0.33 / 33% (1)\nALLOW 1/2 for 2 marks if Omar is assumed to be Ff with correct working` },
      { q: `Thalassaemia is an inherited blood disorder caused by a recessive allele, t.\nAmir and Sara are both carriers of thalassaemia. They plan to have children and want to know how thalassaemia is inherited.\n\n* Explain how meiosis and fertilisation could lead to Amir and Sara having children with different phenotypes.\nInclude a genetic diagram and the probability of each phenotype in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of how meiosis produces haploid gametes carrying either allele AND how random fertilisation gives the different genotypes, with a correct genetic diagram and correct probabilities of both phenotypes. The reasoning is clear and logically structured. The information is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An explanation of meiosis or fertilisation AND a correct genetic diagram, OR a correct genetic diagram with correct probabilities and some explanation of how the gametes arise. There is a line of reasoning with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple statement about gametes or alleles OR an attempt at a genetic diagram. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMeiosis\n• Amir and Sara are both Tt / heterozygous\n• meiosis halves the chromosome number to make haploid gametes\n• the two alleles separate into different gametes\n• half of each parent's gametes carry T and half carry t\nFertilisation\n• which sperm fertilises which egg is random\n• the zygote is diploid and has two alleles for the gene, one from each parent\nGenetic diagram\n• gametes T, t × T, t\n• offspring TT, Tt, Tt, tt\n• TT and Tt children do not have thalassaemia; Tt children are carriers\n• tt children have thalassaemia\nProbabilities\n• 3/4 / 75% chance of a child without thalassaemia\n• 1/4 / 25% chance of a child with thalassaemia\n• 1/2 chance of a carrier child\n• each child has the same probability, as each fertilisation is independent` }
    ]
  },
  'B1.3': {
    name: 'How can and should gene technology be used?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about using bacteria to make medicines.\nPeople with type 1 diabetes inject insulin. Most of this insulin is made by bacteria that have had the human insulin gene added to their DNA.\n\nWhat name is given to an organism whose genome has been changed in this way?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• genetically modified (organism) ✓\nALLOW GM / GMO / genetically engineered / transgenic (organism)\nDO NOT ALLOW mutant / clone` },
      { q: `Scientists want to put a gene into a bacterial cell. They use a small ring of DNA called a plasmid to carry the gene into the cell.\n\nPut a ring around the correct word to complete the sentence.\nA structure used to carry a gene into a cell is called a chromosome / nucleus / ribosome / vector.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• vector ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one word ringed` },
      { q: `Scientists isolate the human insulin gene from human DNA. They cut the gene out of the DNA and also cut open a bacterial plasmid.\n\nName the type of enzyme used to cut the DNA.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• restriction (enzyme) ✓\nALLOW restriction endonuclease\nDO NOT ALLOW ligase / protease / carbohydrase` },
      { q: `This question is about cystic fibrosis.\nCystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele. Sam and Priya are planning to have children. They both have a genetic test, which shows that each of them has one copy of the cystic fibrosis allele. Neither of them has cystic fibrosis.\n\nWhat word describes a person who has one copy of a recessive allele but does not have the disorder?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• carrier ✓\nALLOW heterozygous\nIGNORE recessive` },
      { q: `A field trial of a genetically modified (GM) oilseed rape crop was carried out. The GM plants were grown in the middle of a field, surrounded by a wide strip of land with no oilseed rape growing on it.\n\nSuggest why the GM plants were grown surrounded by this strip of land.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• to reduce the chance of pollen / genes from the GM plants reaching other (non-GM / wild) plants ✓\nALLOW to stop cross-pollination / cross-breeding with other plants\nALLOW to stop the gene spreading to other plants\nIGNORE to keep it separate (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A survey asked people about the risk of eating food made from GM crops. Many people thought the risk was high, even though scientists have found no evidence that GM foods on sale cause harm to health.\n\nSuggest one reason why people may think the risk of eating GM food is higher than it really is.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any one from:\n• it is a new / unfamiliar technology ✓\n• people do not choose to take the risk / it is imposed on them / food may not be labelled ✓\n• the effects cannot be seen / are long-term / unknown ✓\n• (negative) media reports / stories in newspapers ✓\n• people do not trust the companies or scientists involved ✓\nIGNORE GM food is dangerous` },
      { q: `Genetic engineering can be used to give an organism a desirable characteristic.\n\nComplete the sentences about genetic engineering.\nPut a ring around each correct answer.\nGenetic engineering involves changing the genome / habitat / diet of an organism.\nThe gene that is needed is first copied / digested / isolated from the DNA of another organism.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• genome ✓\n• isolated ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed in a sentence` },
      { q: `Human growth hormone is a protein. Children who do not make enough of it can be treated with growth hormone made by genetically modified bacteria.\n\nPut statements A to E in the correct order to describe how the bacteria are produced and used.\nA Grow the selected bacteria in a large fermenter and collect the hormone.\nB Put the growth hormone gene into a plasmid.\nC Isolate the human growth hormone gene.\nD Select the bacteria that have taken up the plasmid.\nE Mix the plasmids with bacteria so that some bacteria take up a plasmid.\n\nWrite one letter in each box.`, marks: 2, command: 'Order', markScheme: `C B E D A ✓✓\nALLOW 1 mark for three letters in the correct position\nOR C before B before E (in any position) for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Vitamin A deficiency causes blindness in many children in countries where rice is the main food. Scientists have genetically modified rice so that the grains contain beta-carotene, which the human body converts into vitamin A. This rice is called Golden Rice.\n\nExplain how growing Golden Rice could improve the health of children in these countries.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• children already eat lots of rice / rice is their main food, so they would take in beta-carotene without changing their diet ✓\n• (body makes) more vitamin A so fewer children become blind / fewer suffer from vitamin A deficiency ✓\nALLOW cheaper / easier than giving vitamin A supplements for mark 1\nIGNORE healthier (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Bt cotton is a genetically modified crop. It contains a gene from the bacterium Bacillus thuringiensis. The gene codes for a protein that is toxic to the caterpillars that eat cotton plants.\n\nGive two benefits to a farmer of growing Bt cotton instead of non-GM cotton.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• fewer plants damaged / eaten by caterpillars ✓\n• higher yield / more cotton to sell ✓\n• less insecticide / pesticide needs to be bought or sprayed ✓\n• saves time / labour / fuel spraying ✓\n• less exposure of the farmer to harmful insecticides ✓\nIGNORE cheaper (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Some soya plants have been genetically modified to be resistant to a herbicide (weedkiller). Farmers can spray the whole field with the herbicide. The weeds die but the soya plants are not harmed.\n\nDescribe two possible risks to the environment of growing herbicide-resistant soya.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• the resistance gene could transfer (in pollen) to wild relatives / weeds ✓\n• producing herbicide-resistant weeds / superweeds ✓\n• fewer weeds so less food or habitat for insects / birds / reduced biodiversity ✓\n• more herbicide may be used, which can harm other organisms / pollute water ✓\nIGNORE harmful to health` },
      { q: `Before the 1980s, people with diabetes injected insulin that had been extracted from the pancreases of pigs and cattle. Pig insulin is very slightly different from human insulin. Now most people inject human insulin made by genetically modified bacteria.\n\nSuggest two advantages of using insulin made by GM bacteria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• it is identical to human insulin so less likely to cause an (allergic / immune) reaction ✓\n• large amounts can be made / supply does not depend on the number of animals slaughtered ✓\n• bacteria reproduce quickly so it can be made quickly ✓\n• acceptable to vegetarians / people whose religion forbids using pig products ✓\n• no animals need to be killed ✓\n• less risk of passing on animal diseases ✓\nIGNORE cheaper (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A farmer grew non-GM cotton for several years and then changed to Bt cotton. The table shows how often the farmer had to spray insecticide (data written for this practice question).\nType of cotton | Number of insecticide sprays per season\nNon-GM cotton | 12\nBt cotton | 3\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the number of insecticide sprays per season.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (12 − 3) ÷ 12 (× 100) ✓\n• 75 (%) ✓\nALLOW 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nDO NOT ALLOW 300 / 25` },
      { q: `When scientists put a gene into a plasmid, they also include a gene for resistance to an antibiotic. Only some of the bacteria mixed with the plasmids take up a plasmid.\n\nExplain how the antibiotic resistance gene allows the scientists to select the bacteria that have taken up the plasmid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• grow / culture the bacteria on agar (plates) containing the antibiotic ✓\n• only bacteria that have taken up the plasmid are resistant so survive / grow into colonies ✓\nALLOW bacteria without the plasmid are killed for mark 2` },
      { q: `Scientists use the same restriction enzyme to cut the human DNA containing a gene and to cut open a bacterial plasmid.\n\nExplain why the same restriction enzyme is used to cut both pieces of DNA.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• produces the same / matching / complementary sticky ends ✓\n• so the bases on the sticky ends can pair and the gene fits / joins into the plasmid ✓\nALLOW the enzyme cuts at the same base sequence for mark 1\nALLOW (DNA) ligase can then join them for mark 2` },
      { q: `Huntington's disease is caused by a dominant allele. A couple who are using IVF know that one of them carries the allele. Doctors remove one cell from each embryo and test it for the allele. Only embryos without the allele are placed in the mother's uterus.\n\nSuggest two reasons why some people object to testing embryos in this way.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• unused embryos (with the allele) are destroyed / embryos are potential human lives ✓\n• it is seen as choosing which people are born / ‘playing God’ ✓\n• could lead to choosing embryos for other (non-medical) characteristics / designer babies ✓\n• suggests that people with the disorder are less valued / discrimination against disabled people ✓\n• risk of harm to the embryo from removing a cell ✓\n• the test might give a false result ✓\nIGNORE it is unnatural (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Amniocentesis is a test that can be carried out during pregnancy to check a fetus for some genetic disorders. A small sample of fluid is taken from around the fetus using a needle. The test causes a miscarriage in about 1 in 200 pregnancies (data written for this practice question).\n\nIn one year, 3000 women had amniocentesis at a hospital.\nCalculate the number of these pregnancies that would be expected to end in a miscarriage caused by the test.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 3000 ÷ 200 ✓\n• 15 ✓\nALLOW 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nALLOW 0.5% × 3000 for mark 1` },
      { q: `Two couples are each told by a genetic counsellor that both partners are carriers of the cystic fibrosis allele. Each child they have will have a 1 in 4 chance of having cystic fibrosis.\nOne couple decides to have children naturally. The other couple decides to use IVF with embryo testing.\n\nSuggest why the two couples made different decisions when they were given the same information.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• they have different (ethical / religious) beliefs or values, e.g. about destroying embryos ✓\n• they think about / accept the risk differently ✓\n• one couple may have experience of living with someone with cystic fibrosis ✓\n• cost / availability / difficulty of IVF ✓\n• they may feel differently about caring for a child with cystic fibrosis ✓\nIGNORE they are different people` },
      { q: `Genetic tests can show whether a person has an allele that increases their risk of developing a disease later in life. In the UK, insurance companies have agreed not to ask most customers for the results of these tests.\n\nSuggest two reasons why this agreement was made.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• people with a ‘high-risk’ result could be refused insurance / charged more ✓\n• this would be unfair / discrimination as they cannot control their alleles ✓\n• people might avoid having a test that could benefit their health ✓\n• genetic information is private / personal ✓\n• having the allele does not mean they will definitely develop the disease ✓` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis is caused by a faulty allele. It causes thick, sticky mucus in the lungs. In one gene therapy trial, a working copy of the gene was sprayed into the lungs of patients so that it entered the cells lining the airways.\n\nExplain why this gene therapy would not stop a patient passing on the cystic fibrosis allele to their children.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• only the (body) cells in the airways / lungs are changed ✓\n• the sex cells / gametes (sperm or eggs) still carry the faulty allele ✓\nALLOW the reproductive organs / testes / ovaries are not changed for mark 2` },
      { q: `In some types of gene therapy, a harmless virus is used to carry a working copy of a gene into a patient's cells.\n\nSuggest one advantage and one risk of using a virus to carry the gene.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Advantage:\n• viruses naturally enter cells / insert their genetic material into cells ✓\nRisk, any one from:\n• the patient's immune system may attack the virus / cause an immune reaction ✓\n• the gene may be inserted in the wrong place in the genome / disrupt another gene / cause cancer ✓\n• the virus may become harmful / cause disease ✓` },
      { q: `Scientists have found that people respond differently to some medicines because of differences in their genome. For example, some people have a version of a gene that means their liver breaks down a particular drug very slowly.\n\nSuggest how testing a patient's genome before prescribing this drug could benefit the patient.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• the doctor can give a lower / different dose ✓\n• to avoid the drug building up / harmful side effects ✓\n• the doctor can choose a different drug that works better for them ✓\n• less time wasted trying drugs that do not work ✓\nALLOW personalised medicine / treatment matched to their genome for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A genetic screening test is used on a large number of people. Some people get a false negative result.\n\nExplain why a false negative result could be a problem for a couple who are planning to have children.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a false negative means the test says the person does not have / carry the allele when they do ✓\n• so the couple may not realise their children could inherit the disorder / they make decisions without knowing the real risk ✓` },
      { q: `Salmon have been genetically modified with a growth hormone gene from another species of salmon. The GM salmon grow to full size in about half the time of non-GM salmon. They are farmed in tanks.\n\nSuggest two concerns some people may have about farming GM salmon.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• GM salmon could escape and breed with wild salmon / the gene could spread into wild populations ✓\n• GM salmon could outcompete wild salmon for food ✓\n• possible (unknown) long-term effects on people who eat them ✓\n• animal welfare concerns ✓\n• ethical objections to changing animals' genomes / ‘playing God’ ✓\nIGNORE it is unnatural (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A newspaper headline says: ‘Scientists cannot prove GM food is safe’. Scientists say they have found no evidence that the GM foods on sale harm human health.\n\nExplain why scientists cannot say that any food is completely safe.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• every action / activity / food has some risk / nothing is risk-free ✓\n• there may be effects that have not yet been found / are long-term / too rare to have been detected ✓\nALLOW scientists can only show that the risk is very small` },
      { q: `In the UK, the law says that embryos created by IVF can only be tested for conditions on a list approved by a government body (the HFEA).\n\nSuggest why decisions about embryo testing are made by a government body rather than by individual doctors or parents.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• the decisions involve ethical questions that science alone cannot answer ✓\n• so the views of society / different groups can be taken into account ✓\n• to make sure the rules are the same for everyone / consistent ✓\n• to prevent testing for non-medical reasons / designer babies ✓\n• to protect people from misuse of the technology ✓` },
      { q: `Bacteria have been genetically modified so that they contain the human insulin gene. The bacteria are grown in large fermenters.\n\nExplain how this allows large amounts of human insulin to be produced.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the gene codes for / is the instructions for making the insulin protein ✓\n• bacteria use the gene to make (human) insulin / the bacteria can read the human gene ✓\n• bacteria reproduce quickly / divide rapidly, so there are many cells all making insulin ✓\n• each new bacterium gets a copy of the gene / plasmid ✓\nMax 3\nIGNORE bacteria make insulin naturally` },
      { q: `A new variety of Golden Rice contains 15 µg of beta-carotene in each gram of uncooked rice. A young child needs about 900 µg of beta-carotene each day. A typical child in one country eats 150 g of uncooked rice a day (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the mass of this rice a child would need to eat to get 900 µg of beta-carotene.\nUse your answer to state whether eating this rice could meet the child's daily need.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 900 ÷ 15 ✓\n• 60 (g) ✓\n• yes, because 60 g is less than the 150 g the child eats each day ✓\nALLOW ecf for the conclusion from an incorrect mass\nALLOW 2 marks for 60 g with no working` },
      { q: `Bt cotton produces a protein that kills bollworm caterpillars. In one region, Bt cotton was grown on most farms for ten years. After this time, farmers found that more and more bollworms were surviving on their Bt cotton.\n\nExplain why the number of surviving bollworms increased.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (variation in the population) some bollworms had (a mutation / allele giving) resistance to the Bt protein ✓\n• these bollworms survived (and others died) ✓\n• and reproduced, passing on the resistance allele to their offspring ✓\n• over many generations the proportion of resistant bollworms increased ✓\nMax 3\nDO NOT ALLOW bollworms became resistant because they were exposed to the protein / got used to it` },
      { q: `A study compared the yield of cotton in a region before and after farmers started to grow Bt cotton (data written for this practice question).\nPeriod | Type of cotton grown | Mean yield (tonnes per hectare)\n2000–2004 | Non-GM cotton | 2.2\n2010–2014 | Bt cotton | 2.9\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in mean yield. Give your answer to 2 significant figures.\nSuggest why these data alone do not show that growing Bt cotton caused the increase in yield.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (2.9 − 2.2) ÷ 2.2 × 100 ✓\n• 32 (%) ✓\n• other factors may have changed between the two periods, e.g. weather / rainfall / use of fertiliser / irrigation / different farmers or farming methods ✓\nALLOW 2 marks for 32 with no working; 31.8 (not 2 s.f.) scores 1 mark\nALLOW the data show a correlation, not a cause, for mark 3 if qualified` },
      { q: `Scientists want to put a gene into a plasmid so that bacteria can make a useful protein.\n\nDescribe how the gene is joined into the plasmid.\nUse ideas about enzymes and sticky ends in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• the gene and the plasmid are cut with the same restriction enzyme ✓\n• (this leaves) sticky ends that are complementary / have unpaired bases that match ✓\n• the gene and plasmid are mixed and DNA ligase joins / seals the DNA (backbone) ✓\nALLOW ‘matching sticky ends pair up’ for mark 2\nDO NOT ALLOW ligase cuts DNA` },
      { q: `In the UK, couples having IVF can have their embryos tested for some serious genetic disorders, such as cystic fibrosis. It is illegal to test embryos to choose characteristics such as eye colour.\n\nExplain why many people think testing embryos for cystic fibrosis is acceptable but choosing embryos for eye colour is not.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• cystic fibrosis is a serious disorder / causes suffering / shortens life ✓\n• so testing prevents harm / benefits the child (and family) ✓\n• eye colour does not affect health / is not a medical reason ✓\n• choosing eye colour could lead to designer babies / choosing children for appearance ✓\n• the benefit for eye colour does not outweigh the ethical concerns of destroying embryos ✓` },
      { q: `A genetic screening test is offered to 50 000 people. The condition it tests for affects 1 in 2500 people. The test gives a false positive result for 1% of people who do not have the condition (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the number of people who would get a false positive result.\nExplain why a positive result is always followed up with a second, different test.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• number without the condition = 50 000 − (50 000 ÷ 2500) = 49 980 ✓\n• false positives = 1% of 49 980 = 500 (499.8) ✓\n• a positive result may be false / most positive results are false positives, so a second test confirms it before decisions are made ✓\nALLOW 500 for 2 marks with no working\nALLOW 1 mark for 1% of 50 000 = 500 with no subtraction shown` },
      { q: `Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) is a genetic disorder. A faulty gene means that white blood cells do not work properly. In one gene therapy, stem cells are taken from the patient's bone marrow, given a working copy of the gene, and then put back into the patient.\n\nExplain why scientists put the working gene into bone marrow stem cells rather than into mature white blood cells.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• stem cells divide (continually) / are not specialised ✓\n• they produce new white blood cells (throughout life) ✓\n• which all contain the working gene ✓\n• mature white blood cells die / do not divide, so the effect would only be temporary ✓\nMax 3` },
      { q: `Huntington's disease is caused by a dominant allele. Symptoms usually start between the ages of 30 and 50. There is no cure. Many adults whose parent has Huntington's disease choose not to have a genetic test.\n\nExplain why some people choose not to have the test.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• they have a 1 in 2 / 50% chance of having the allele (so a positive result is likely) ✓\n• a positive result means they will develop the disease ✓\n• there is no cure / treatment, so knowing does not change the outcome ✓\n• knowing could cause anxiety / stress / depression ✓\n• it could affect insurance / employment / relationships ✓\n• they may prefer not to know ✓` },
      { q: `Gene therapy can be used to change body cells. It could also, in theory, be used to change sex cells or early embryos. In the UK it is illegal to use gene therapy on sex cells or embryos used for a pregnancy.\n\nExplain why gene therapy on sex cells or embryos raises more ethical concerns than gene therapy on body cells.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• changes to sex cells / embryos are passed on to future generations / offspring ✓\n• future generations cannot give consent ✓\n• any harmful (unintended) effects would also be inherited / long-term effects unknown ✓\n• could be used to choose non-medical characteristics / designer babies ✓\n• body cell therapy only affects the patient, who can consent ✓` },
      { q: `Genetic engineering has been used to produce many products.\n\nComplete the table.\nGM organism | Gene added | Benefit\nBacteria | Human insulin gene | ______\nGolden Rice | ______ | Helps prevent blindness caused by vitamin A deficiency\nBt maize | Gene for a protein toxic to insect larvae | ______\nSoya | ______ | Farmers can kill weeds without killing the crop`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• (large amounts of) human insulin for people with diabetes ✓\n• gene(s) for making beta-carotene ✓\nALLOW vitamin A gene / carotene gene\n• (crop protected from insects so) higher yield / less insecticide needed ✓\n• gene for herbicide / weedkiller resistance ✓` },
      { q: `Scientists want to produce a variety of wheat that is resistant to a fungal disease. A wild grass contains a gene that makes it resistant to the fungus.\n\nDescribe the main steps the scientists would use to produce the genetically modified wheat.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• isolate / cut out the resistance gene from the wild grass DNA (using a restriction enzyme) ✓\n• (replicate the gene and) put it into a vector, e.g. a plasmid / virus ✓\n• use the vector to insert the gene into wheat cells ✓\n• select the cells that have taken up the gene, e.g. using an antibiotic / herbicide resistance marker ✓\n• (grow the modified cells into plants and) test that the plants are resistant ✓\nMax 4` },
      { q: `A pregnant woman is offered a test to find out whether her fetus has a genetic disorder. The table compares two tests (data written for this practice question).\nTest | Stage of pregnancy when carried out (weeks) | Risk of causing miscarriage | Time to get results\nChorionic villus sampling (CVS) | 11–14 | 1 in 100 | 3 days\nAmniocentesis | 15–20 | 1 in 200 | 14 days\n\nCompare the two tests.\nUse the data to suggest why a woman might choose each test.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `• CVS can be done earlier in pregnancy (11–14 weeks compared with 15–20 weeks) ✓\n• CVS gives results more quickly (3 days compared with 14 days) ✓\n• amniocentesis has a lower risk of miscarriage (1 in 200 compared with 1 in 100 / half the risk) ✓\n• a woman might choose CVS to make an earlier decision / have more time to decide about the pregnancy OR might choose amniocentesis because it is less likely to cause a miscarriage ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for a correct comparison without data` },
      { q: `*A government is deciding whether to allow farmers to grow a genetically modified Bt maize. The maize contains a gene for a protein that kills stem borer caterpillars, which are a major pest.\nA report gives this information from field trials (data written for this practice question).\nCrop | Mean yield (tonnes per hectare) | Mean number of insecticide sprays per season | Cost of seed per hectare (£)\nNon-GM maize | 3.1 | 4 | 20\nBt maize | 4.0 | 1 | 55\nOther information in the report:\n• Bt maize seed is sold by one company. Farmers must buy new seed each year.\n• Pollen from maize can travel several kilometres in the wind.\n• Some people in the country say they will not buy food containing GM maize.\n\nEvaluate whether the government should allow farmers to grow Bt maize.\nUse the data and ideas about benefits, risks and ethics in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Uses the data to give benefits AND risks/costs AND considers ethical or decision-making issues, reaching a reasoned conclusion. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Uses some data to give benefits AND risks/costs OR gives benefits and risks with an ethical point but limited use of data. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives at least one benefit OR one risk, which may be from the data. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nBenefits (data)\n• yield is higher (4.0 vs 3.1 t/ha, about 29% more / 0.9 t/ha more)\n• fewer insecticide sprays (1 vs 4), so less cost / labour and less harm to other insects / the environment / farmers' health\n• more food for the population / more income for farmers\nRisks / costs\n• seed is more expensive (£55 vs £20 per hectare) and must be bought every year\n• farmers depend on one company / poorer farmers may not afford it\n• pollen could spread the gene to non-GM / wild maize, affecting other farmers or organic crops\n• stem borers could evolve resistance to the protein if grown widely\n• possible effects on non-target insects / food chains\n• unknown long-term effects\nEthics and decision-making\n• consumers' views: some will not buy GM food, so markets / exports may be lost; labelling allows choice\n• benefits and risks fall on different groups (company, farmers, consumers)\n• the right decision may be the one giving the most benefit to the most people\n• could allow it with regulation, e.g. separation distances, refuges of non-GM maize, monitoring\n• trial data may not apply to all farms / years (only field trial results, means only)\nConclusion: any reasoned conclusion supported by the evidence given` }
    ]
  },
  'B2.1': {
    name: 'What are the causes of disease?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about health and disease.\nSam has no infection and no illness. His doctor says that Sam is not in good health because he has been feeling very anxious and lonely for several months.\n\nSuggest why the doctor says Sam is not in good health even though he has no disease.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• health is a state of physical and mental (and social) well-being / not just the absence of disease ✓\nALLOW Sam's mental health / well-being is poor\nIGNORE he might catch a disease` },
      { q: `Athlete's foot causes itchy, cracked skin between the toes. It is spread by contact with infected skin or with damp surfaces such as changing-room floors and shared towels.\n\nWhat type of pathogen causes athlete's foot?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• fungus ✓\nALLOW fungi / fungal\nDO NOT ALLOW bacterium / virus` },
      { q: `Malaria is caused by a single-celled organism called Plasmodium. Plasmodium is carried from one person to another by female Anopheles mosquitoes when they feed on blood.\n\nWhat name is given to an organism, such as the mosquito, that carries a pathogen from one host to another?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• vector ✓\nIGNORE carrier / host` },
      { q: `Tobacco mosaic virus (TMV) infects tomato and tobacco plants. Infected leaves have a mosaic pattern of pale yellow patches between areas of normal green leaf.\n\nWhich green substance is missing from the cells in the pale yellow patches?`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `• chlorophyll ✓\nIGNORE chloroplasts` },
      { q: `Some viruses can enter a lysogenic cycle. In the lysogenic cycle the genetic material of the virus is inserted into the genome of the host cell and the virus does not immediately make new virus particles.\n\nExplain why the viral genetic material ends up in every new cell when the host cell divides.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the viral genetic material is part of the host cell's DNA / genome, so it is copied (replicated) with the host DNA before the cell divides ✓\nALLOW the virus genes are copied along with the cell's own genes` },
      { q: `Diseases can be described as communicable or non-communicable.\n\nWhich disease is non-communicable?\nTick (✓) one box.\n☐ Cholera\n☐ Coronary heart disease\n☐ Measles\n☐ Tuberculosis`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• Coronary heart disease ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one box ticked` },
      { q: `This question is about pathogens.\nDifferent types of pathogen cause disease in humans and in plants.\n\nDraw lines to connect each disease to the type of pathogen that causes it.\nDiseases: Measles; Salmonella food poisoning; Malaria; Ash dieback\nType of pathogen: Bacterium; Fungus; Protist; Virus`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', markScheme: `Measles – Virus\nSalmonella food poisoning – Bacterium\nMalaria – Protist\nAsh dieback – Fungus\n• all four lines correct ✓✓\n• two or three lines correct ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one line from a disease (that disease scores no credit)` },
      { q: `Diseases are often divided into two types.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nUse words from the list.\ncommunicable   genes   non-communicable   pathogen   vector\nA disease that can be passed from one person to another is a ______ disease. It is caused by a ______ such as a bacterium or virus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• communicable ✓\n• pathogen ✓\nALLOW infectious for communicable` },
      { q: `In 2022 there were serious floods in parts of Pakistan. In the weeks after the floods, the number of cases of cholera in the flooded areas increased.\nCholera is caused by a bacterium that is found in the faeces of infected people.\n\nExplain why flooding caused an increase in cases of cholera.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• flood water mixes with / is contaminated by sewage / faeces (containing cholera bacteria) ✓\n• people drink the contaminated water / eat food washed in it, so the bacteria enter their bodies / gut ✓\nALLOW clean drinking water supplies / toilets are damaged\nIGNORE bacteria grow in water (unqualified)` },
      { q: `HIV is a virus that infects white blood cells. Around the world, millions of people are living with HIV.\n\nGive two ways HIV can be passed from one person to another.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• unprotected sexual intercourse / sex without a condom ✓\n• sharing needles (by drug users) / contaminated needles ✓\n• from mother to baby (during pregnancy / birth / breastfeeding) ✓\n• transfusion of infected blood ✓\nALLOW contact between body fluids (e.g. blood, semen) for one mark\nDO NOT ALLOW kissing / coughing / sneezing / touching` },
      { q: `Salmonella is a type of bacterium that can be found in raw chicken and raw eggs. People who eat food containing Salmonella can develop food poisoning, with stomach cramps, vomiting and diarrhoea.\n\nExplain how the Salmonella bacteria cause these symptoms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• bacteria reproduce / multiply (rapidly) in the gut / intestine ✓\n• bacteria release / produce toxins (that damage gut cells / tissues) ✓\nALLOW poisons for toxins\nIGNORE bacteria eat the cells` },
      { q: `Influenza is caused by a virus that infects cells lining the airways. People with influenza often have a sore throat and a cough.\n\nExplain how the influenza virus damages the cells lining the airways.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• virus enters the cells and uses the cell to make copies of itself / replicate ✓\n• (new viruses are released and) the cell bursts / is destroyed ✓\nALLOW virus takes over the cell's machinery\nIGNORE virus releases toxins` },
      { q: `A food scientist investigated how quickly Salmonella bacteria reproduce in a chicken sandwich left in a warm room.\nOne Salmonella cell divides into two cells every 30 minutes at this temperature (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate how many bacteria there will be after 3 hours if the sandwich starts with one bacterium.\nAnswer = ______ bacteria`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• number of divisions = 3 × 60 ÷ 30 = 6 ✓\n• 2⁶ = 64 (bacteria) ✓\nALLOW 64 with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW 1 → 2 → 4 → 8 → 16 → 32 → 64 shown as a sequence` },
      { q: `Crown gall disease is caused by the bacterium Agrobacterium tumefaciens. The bacterium enters a plant through a wound and inserts some of its genes into the plant's cells. This causes the cells to divide in an uncontrolled way, forming a large swelling called a gall on the stem.\n\nSuggest why plants with large galls on their stems often grow less well.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• the gall damages / blocks the xylem / phloem / transport tissue in the stem ✓\n• so less water / mineral ions reach the leaves OR less sugar / sucrose reaches other parts of the plant ✓\nALLOW less photosynthesis because less water reaches leaves\nALLOW the gall uses up the plant's resources / energy (for 1 mark)` },
      { q: `Ash dieback is a disease of ash trees caused by a fungus. It was first confirmed in the UK in 2012 in young ash trees that had been imported from a nursery in Europe. Within a few years it had been found in woodlands across the UK.\n\nSuggest two ways the fungus could have spread across the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• spores carried by the wind ✓\n• sale / transport / planting of infected young trees / saplings ✓\n• on people's footwear / clothing / vehicles / tools ✓\n• carried by animals ✓\n• spores spread from fallen infected leaves (in leaf litter) ✓\nIGNORE water / insects (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Gonorrhoea is a sexually transmitted infection caused by a bacterium. Many people with gonorrhoea have no symptoms. Others have pain when urinating or a discharge.\n\nSuggest two reasons why gonorrhoea spreads easily within a population.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• many infected people have no symptoms so do not know they are infected / do not get treated ✓\n• so continue to have (unprotected) sex / pass it on to partners ✓\n• spread by unprotected sex / not using a condom ✓\n• (some strains are) resistant to antibiotics so infection lasts longer ✓\n• people with many sexual partners ✓` },
      { q: `Tuberculosis (TB) is caused by a bacterium that infects the lungs. The World Health Organization reports that people living with HIV are many times more likely to develop TB than people without HIV.\n\nExplain why people with HIV are more likely to develop TB.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• HIV infects / destroys (some) white blood cells ✓\n• so the immune system is weakened / cannot fight / destroy the TB bacteria ✓\nALLOW fewer antibodies produced\nIGNORE HIV causes TB` },
      { q: `Human papillomavirus (HPV) is passed from person to person during sex. Almost all cases of cervical cancer are linked to HPV infection.\n\nExplain why cervical cancer is described as a non-communicable disease even though it is linked to a communicable disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the cancer itself cannot be passed / spread from one person to another ✓\n• HPV infects cells and causes changes / mutations that make cells divide uncontrollably / form a tumour ✓\nALLOW the virus is communicable but the cancer is not\nIGNORE cancer is caused by genes` },
      { q: `The table shows the number of new cases of TB recorded in one country (data written for this practice question).\nYear | Number of new TB cases\n2015 | 4800\n2020 | 3600\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in new TB cases between 2015 and 2020.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 4800 − 3600 = 1200 ✓\n• 1200 ÷ 4800 × 100 = 25 (%) ✓\nALLOW 25 with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW ECF for an incorrect subtraction` },
      { q: `Malaria is common in tropical areas. Mosquitoes lay their eggs in still water such as ponds, swamps and puddles.\n\nExplain why malaria is more common in areas with lots of still water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• more mosquitoes can breed / lay eggs / there are more mosquitoes ✓\n• so more mosquitoes (vectors) can carry Plasmodium / the protist between people / bite people ✓\nALLOW more people bitten by infected mosquitoes\nDO NOT ALLOW malaria spreads in the water` },
      { q: `Measles is caused by a virus. The virus is spread in tiny droplets when an infected person coughs or sneezes. An infected person can pass on the virus for several days before a rash appears.\n\nExplain why measles can spread quickly through a class of children who have not been vaccinated.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• children are in close contact / share the same air, so they breathe in droplets containing the virus ✓\n• infected children can pass it on before they know they have it / before symptoms appear (so are not kept at home) ✓\nALLOW measles is airborne\nIGNORE children do not wash their hands` },
      { q: `Some viruses reproduce using the lytic cycle.\nThe statements describe the stages of the lytic cycle, but they are in the wrong order.\nA New virus particles are assembled inside the host cell.\nB The virus attaches to the host cell and injects its genetic material.\nC The host cell bursts, releasing the new virus particles.\nD The viral genes are used to make copies of the viral genetic material and new viral proteins.\n\nPut the statements in the correct order.\nWrite one letter in each box.`, marks: 2, command: 'Put', markScheme: `B → D → A → C\n• all four correct ✓✓\n• two letters in the correct position ✓` },
      { q: `Viruses that infect bacteria can reproduce by the lytic cycle or by the lysogenic cycle.\n\nDescribe two differences between the lytic cycle and the lysogenic cycle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• in the lysogenic cycle the viral genetic material is inserted into the host's genome / DNA; in the lytic cycle it is not ✓\n• in the lytic cycle new viruses are made straight away; in the lysogenic cycle the virus is inactive / dormant (for a time) ✓\n• in the lytic cycle the host cell bursts / is destroyed; in the lysogenic cycle the host cell survives (and divides) ✓\n• in the lysogenic cycle the viral DNA is copied every time the host cell divides ✓\nALLOW lysogenic can switch to lytic later\nEach point must be a comparison` },
      { q: `Priya has had a long-term illness that makes it difficult for her to leave her home. Her doctor is worried that she may be developing depression.\n\nSuggest two reasons why a long-term physical illness can lead to a mental health problem such as depression.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• cannot work / do normal activities / hobbies ✓\n• loneliness / less contact with friends / social isolation ✓\n• stress / worry about the illness / the future ✓\n• (constant) pain / tiredness ✓\n• money worries from not working ✓` },
      { q: `Powdery mildew is a disease of barley caused by a fungus. White patches of fungus grow on the leaves and release large numbers of spores. In the UK, barley is often grown in very large fields containing only barley plants.\n\nSuggest why powdery mildew can spread quickly through a large field of barley.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• spores are carried by the wind / air ✓\n• plants are close together / all plants are the same species so spores easily land on another host plant ✓\nALLOW plants may all have the same (lack of) resistance\nIGNORE spread by insects` },
      { q: `A newspaper headline says: "You can catch lung cancer from someone who has it".\n\nExplain why the headline is wrong.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• lung cancer is a non-communicable disease ✓\n• it is not caused by a pathogen / cannot be passed from person to person ✓\nALLOW it is caused by factors such as smoking / genes / mutations (for second mark)` },
      { q: `A sample of milk was contaminated with 200 bacteria. The milk was left in a warm kitchen, where each bacterium divided every 20 minutes (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the number of bacteria in the milk after 5 hours.\nGive your answer in standard form to 2 significant figures.\nNumber of bacteria = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• number of divisions = 5 × 60 ÷ 20 = 15 ✓\n• 200 × 2¹⁵ = 200 × 32 768 = 6 553 600 ✓\n• 6.6 × 10⁶ ✓\nALLOW 6 553 600 for 2 marks\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect number of divisions` },
      { q: `Tobacco mosaic virus (TMV) infects tomato plants. Farmers find that tomato plants infected with TMV are smaller and produce fewer tomatoes than healthy plants. The leaves of infected plants have pale yellow patches.\n\nExplain why tomato plants infected with TMV grow less.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the virus damages / destroys chloroplasts / chlorophyll in leaf cells ✓\n• so less light is absorbed / less photosynthesis takes place ✓\n• so less glucose / sugar is made for respiration / growth / making new biomass (e.g. cellulose, starch, proteins) ✓\nIGNORE less food (unqualified)` },
      { q: `The table shows the number of cases of cholera in two towns over one year (data written for this practice question). Both towns have similar populations.\nTown | Source of drinking water | Cases of cholera per year\nTown P | Untreated water taken from a river; sewage also goes into the river | 312\nTown Q | Treated water supplied through pipes; sewage pipes separate | 4\n\nExplain the difference in the number of cholera cases in the two towns.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• cholera is caused by a bacterium that is spread in water contaminated with faeces / sewage ✓\n• in town P sewage containing the bacteria gets into the river / drinking water so people swallow the bacteria ✓\n• in town Q the water is treated / bacteria are killed / sewage does not mix with drinking water ✓\nALLOW use of figures e.g. town P has 78 times as many cases\nIGNORE town P is dirtier` },
      { q: `Malaria kills hundreds of thousands of people each year. It is caused by a protist called Plasmodium, which is spread by mosquitoes.\n\nDescribe how malaria is passed from an infected person to a healthy person.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• a (female) mosquito feeds on / bites an infected person and takes in the protist / Plasmodium with the blood ✓\n• the protist develops / reproduces inside the mosquito ✓\n• the mosquito bites a healthy person and the protist enters their blood (in the mosquito's saliva) ✓\nALLOW the mosquito is a vector\nDO NOT ALLOW the mosquito injects a virus / bacterium` },
      { q: `Some viruses can stay inside a person's body for many years without causing any symptoms, and then suddenly cause disease, for example when the person is stressed or ill. Scientists explain this using the lysogenic cycle.\n\nDescribe how the lysogenic cycle allows a virus to stay in the body without causing symptoms, and then cause symptoms later.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• viral genetic material is inserted into the host cell's DNA / genome ✓\n• it is copied when the cell divides / stays inactive / dormant so no new viruses are made and cells are not destroyed ✓\n• later (e.g. when triggered) the virus enters the lytic cycle, makes new viruses and the cells burst, causing symptoms ✓\nALLOW the virus genes become active again\nIGNORE the virus hides from the immune system (unqualified)` },
      { q: `HIV is a virus. Without treatment, a person infected with HIV will usually develop AIDS. People with AIDS often die from infections that a healthy person would recover from.\n\nExplain how HIV infection can lead to a person dying from another infection.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• HIV infects / reproduces inside white blood cells (lymphocytes) ✓\n• the white blood cells are destroyed (over time) so the immune system is weakened ✓\n• so the body cannot fight / destroy other pathogens, e.g. TB bacteria / pneumonia ✓\nALLOW fewer antibodies made\nIGNORE HIV kills the person directly` },
      { q: `Scientists followed two groups of adults in one region for a year and recorded how many developed TB (data written for this practice question).\nGroup | Number of people | Number who developed TB\nPeople with HIV | 2500 | 50\nPeople without HIV | 100 000 | 100\n\nCalculate how many times more likely a person with HIV was to develop TB than a person without HIV.\nUse the data to support your answer.\nAnswer = ______ times`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• with HIV: 50 ÷ 2500 = 0.02 / 2% ✓\n• without HIV: 100 ÷ 100 000 = 0.001 / 0.1% ✓\n• 0.02 ÷ 0.001 = 20 (times) ✓\nALLOW 20 with no working for 3 marks\nALLOW ECF from incorrect percentages` },
      { q: `At a family barbecue, several people ate chicken that was still pink in the middle. About 12 to 36 hours later, some of them developed vomiting, diarrhoea and a fever. Tests showed Salmonella bacteria in their faeces.\n\nExplain why the symptoms appeared several hours after the chicken was eaten.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the chicken was not cooked enough to kill the Salmonella bacteria ✓\n• it takes time for the bacteria to reproduce / multiply to large numbers in the gut ✓\n• the bacteria release toxins (that damage gut cells) which cause the symptoms ✓\nALLOW symptoms only appear when there are enough bacteria / enough toxin\nIGNORE incubation period (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Scientists surveyed ash trees in one woodland each year to see how many were infected with ash dieback (data written for this practice question).\nYear | Ash trees infected (%)\n2014 | 5\n2016 | 20\n2018 | 45\n2020 | 70\n\nDescribe the trend in the data and suggest why ash dieback spreads more quickly once some trees in a woodland are infected.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• percentage of infected trees increases (each year) ✓\n• the increase is faster after 2016 / increase of 15 percentage points from 2014 to 2016 then 25 every two years after ✓\nALLOW quotes data e.g. from 5% to 70%\n• infected trees produce (more) fungal spores which are carried by the wind to nearby trees ✓\nALLOW more infected leaves fall and release spores` },
      { q: `Avian influenza (bird flu) is caused by a virus. It is spread through contact with infected birds, their droppings and contaminated feathers. In some years, avian influenza spreads between poultry farms in the UK.\n\nSuggest three ways the virus could be carried from one farm to another.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• wild birds (e.g. ducks / geese) flying between farms ✓\n• on workers' boots / clothing / hands ✓\n• on vehicles / equipment / tyres ✓\n• moving infected birds / eggs between farms ✓\n• in droppings / feathers / bedding carried by wind or water ✓\n• by rodents / other animals ✓` },
      { q: `This question is about communicable diseases in humans.\n\nComplete the table.\nDisease | Type of pathogen | How the disease is spread\nMeasles | Virus | ______\nTuberculosis (TB) | ______ | In droplets in the air when infected people cough\nAthlete's foot | ______ | Contact with infected skin or damp surfaces\nMalaria | Protist | ______`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• Measles – in droplets in the air (from coughs / sneezes) ✓\n• TB – bacterium ✓\n• Athlete's foot – fungus ✓\n• Malaria – by mosquitoes / a vector / insect bite ✓\nALLOW airborne for measles\nDO NOT ALLOW water / contact for malaria` },
      { q: `Both bacteria and viruses can cause disease in humans. Cholera is caused by a bacterium. Influenza is caused by a virus.\n\nCompare how bacteria and viruses cause the symptoms of disease.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• both reproduce / multiply rapidly inside the body ✓\n• bacteria (usually) reproduce outside / between body cells; viruses reproduce inside host cells ✓\n• bacteria produce / release toxins that damage cells / tissues ✓\n• viruses take over the cell to make new viruses ✓\n• viruses cause cells to burst / be destroyed when new viruses are released ✓\n• bacteria can damage cells / tissues directly (e.g. TB in lung tissue) ✓\nMAX 3 if no comparison made` },
      { q: `Scientists followed a large group of women for 20 years. They recorded whether each woman had a long-lasting HPV infection and whether she developed cervical cancer (data written for this practice question).\nGroup | Number of women | Number who developed cervical cancer\nWith long-lasting HPV infection | 8000 | 96\nWithout HPV infection | 40 000 | 6\n\n(i) Calculate the percentage of women in each group who developed cervical cancer.\n(ii) A student says, "Every woman with HPV will develop cervical cancer." Use the data to explain why the student is wrong.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(i)\n• with HPV: 96 ÷ 8000 × 100 = 1.2 (%) ✓\n• without HPV: 6 ÷ 40 000 × 100 = 0.015 (%) ✓\n(ii)\n• only 1.2% / 96 out of 8000 women with HPV developed cancer, so most did not ✓\n• HPV increases the risk / chance (by about 80 times) but other factors are needed / not all infected cells mutate ✓\nALLOW women with HPV were 80 times more likely to develop cervical cancer\nALLOW some women without HPV still developed cervical cancer` },
      { q: `HIV and tuberculosis (TB) are two communicable diseases that are common in some parts of the world. The World Health Organization reports that TB is a leading cause of death among people living with HIV.\nHIV is a virus. TB is caused by a bacterium.\n\nDescribe how HIV and TB are each spread, and explain why the two diseases together are more dangerous than either one alone.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nDescribes how both HIV and TB are spread AND gives a detailed explanation of how HIV makes TB infection more likely and more serious.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nDescribes how at least one disease is spread AND gives a simple explanation of why the diseases together are more dangerous.\nOR describes how both diseases are spread in detail with limited explanation.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nGives a simple description of how one of the diseases is spread OR a simple statement about the link between them.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nSpread\n• HIV spreads through body fluids: unprotected sex, sharing needles, infected blood, mother to baby during birth / breastfeeding\n• TB spreads in droplets in the air when an infected person coughs / sneezes / talks\n• TB spreads more easily in crowded / poorly ventilated housing\nInteraction\n• HIV infects and destroys white blood cells / lymphocytes\n• immune system is weakened, so fewer antibodies are made / pathogens are not destroyed\n• person with HIV is more likely to be infected by TB bacteria\n• TB bacteria that were inactive in the lungs can become active\n• TB bacteria multiply and damage lung tissue more quickly / more severely\n• person with both may develop AIDS and die from TB\n• a person with active TB who also has HIV can pass TB on to more people` }
    ]
  },
  'B2.2': {
    name: 'How do organisms protect themselves against pathogens?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about how the human body stops pathogens getting in.\nTears are produced all the time and wash over the surface of the eye. Tears contain an enzyme that breaks down the cell walls of bacteria.\n\nName the enzyme in tears that destroys bacteria.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Lysozyme ✓\nIGNORE: carbohydrase / protease\nDO NOT ALLOW: amylase` },
      { q: `Food and drink often contain bacteria. Many of these bacteria are killed when they reach the stomach.\n\nName the chemical made by the stomach that kills most of these bacteria.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Hydrochloric acid ✓\nALLOW: HCl\nIGNORE: stomach acid / acid (on its own)` },
      { q: `This question is about the immune system.\nWhite blood cells are found in the blood. There are different types of white blood cell, and each type has a different job.\n\nWhich type of white blood cell produces antibodies?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Lymphocyte ✓\nALLOW: B cell / B lymphocyte\nDO NOT ALLOW: phagocyte` },
      { q: `Holly leaves are shiny because they are covered in a waterproof layer. This layer helps to stop fungal spores from getting into the leaf.\n\nWhat is the name of this waterproof layer on the surface of a leaf?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `(Waxy) cuticle ✓\nIGNORE: wax / epidermis` },
      { q: `Patients with large burns have lost a large area of skin. In hospital they are often treated in a sterile room, and nurses wear gloves and gowns.\n\nSuggest why burns patients are treated in a sterile room.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Idea that the skin (barrier) is missing so pathogens could easily enter the body / to reduce the number of pathogens that reach the burn ✓\nIGNORE: to keep it clean (on its own)` },
      { q: `Measles virus particles have proteins on their surface. When a measles virus enters the body, lymphocytes recognise these proteins.\n\nWhat name is given to the proteins on the surface of a pathogen that are recognised by the immune system?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Antigens ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW: antibodies` },
      { q: `Cigarette smoke contains chemicals that damage the cilia lining the airways. People who smoke get more chest infections than people who do not smoke.\n\nExplain why people who smoke get more chest infections.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Cilia (damaged so) do not move / sweep mucus (up and out of the airways) ✓\nMucus containing trapped pathogens stays in / reaches the lungs (so pathogens can infect lung cells) ✓\nALLOW: pathogens are not removed from the airways for 1 mark if no other mark scored` },
      { q: `The airways have defences that stop pathogens reaching the lungs.\n\nComplete the sentences about these defences.\nUse words from the list: antibodies, cilia, mucus, platelets, stomach, throat.\n\nCells lining the airways make sticky ______ that traps pathogens. Tiny hairs called ______ beat and move it up to the ______ , where it is swallowed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `mucus ✓ cilia and throat (both correct) ✓\nALLOW: 1 mark for any two words correct` },
      { q: `This question is about non-specific defences in the human body.\n\nDraw lines to connect each defence to the way it protects the body.\nDefences: Skin; Stomach acid; Tears; Mucus\nWays it protects: Contains an enzyme that kills bacteria; Forms a tough outer layer that pathogens cannot pass through; Traps pathogens in the airways; Has a low pH that kills bacteria in food`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `Skin – tough outer layer pathogens cannot pass through; Stomach acid – low pH that kills bacteria in food; Tears – contains an enzyme that kills bacteria; Mucus – traps pathogens in the airways\nAll 4 correct ✓✓\n2 or 3 correct ✓\nMore than one line from a defence – that line is wrong` },
      { q: `Amir cuts his finger on a piece of glass. After a few minutes the bleeding stops and a clot forms over the cut.\n\nExplain how the clot helps to protect Amir from infection.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Platelets (cause the blood to) clot / form a scab over the cut ✓\nThe clot / scab seals the wound / acts as a barrier so pathogens cannot enter (the blood / body) ✓\nIGNORE: stops bleeding` },
      { q: `The surface of human skin is covered with millions of harmless bacteria. These are sometimes called the skin's normal microbes. They do not cause disease.\n\nExplain how these harmless bacteria can help to protect the body from pathogens.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `They compete with pathogens (for space / nutrients) ✓\nSo pathogens cannot grow / reproduce / colonise the skin ✓\nALLOW: they produce chemicals that kill / stop the growth of pathogens for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Clostridium difficile is a bacterium that can cause severe diarrhoea. It is usually kept under control by the large numbers of harmless bacteria living in the large intestine.\nPatients who have taken a long course of antibiotics are much more likely to become ill with C. difficile.\n\nSuggest why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Antibiotics kill (many of) the harmless bacteria in the gut ✓\nSo there is less competition and C. difficile can multiply / grow rapidly ✓\nALLOW: C. difficile is not killed by the antibiotic / is resistant` },
      { q: `Some people take a medicine that reduces the amount of acid made by the stomach.\nScientists recorded the number of people who caught a food-borne bacterial infection in one year (data written for this practice question):\nPeople taking the medicine: 2.1 per 1000 people\nPeople not taking the medicine: 0.7 per 1000 people\n\nCalculate how many times more likely people taking the medicine were to catch a food-borne infection.\nAnswer = ______ times`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2.1 ÷ 0.7 ✓\n= 3 (times) ✓\nALLOW: 3 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A hospital recorded the number of burns patients who developed a skin infection (data written for this practice question).\nOf 180 patients with burns covering more than 20% of their body, 54 developed an infection.\n\nCalculate the percentage of these patients who developed an infection.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(54 ÷ 180) × 100 ✓\n= 30 (%) ✓\nALLOW: 0.3 for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A healthy adult has about 7000 white blood cells in each mm³ of blood. An adult has about 5 000 000 mm³ of blood in their body (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the total number of white blood cells in this adult's blood.\nGive your answer in standard form.\nAnswer = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `7000 × 5 000 000 = 35 000 000 000 ✓\n= 3.5 × 10¹⁰ ✓\nALLOW: 35 000 000 000 / 35 × 10⁹ for 1 mark (not in correct standard form)` },
      { q: `A student looks at a stained blood smear with a light microscope. She draws a phagocyte. In her drawing the phagocyte is 36 mm wide. The real width of the phagocyte is 12 µm.\n1 mm = 1000 µm.\n\nCalculate the magnification of her drawing.\nMagnification = × ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Conversion: 36 mm = 36 000 µm OR 12 µm = 0.012 mm ✓\n36 000 ÷ 12 = (×) 3000 ✓\nALLOW: 3000 with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW: 3 for 1 mark (failure to convert units)` },
      { q: `Jo had measles as a child, so she is now immune to measles. As an adult she catches mumps, which is caused by a different virus.\n\nExplain why the antibodies Jo made against measles do not protect her against mumps.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Antibodies are specific / each antibody only fits one antigen ✓\nThe mumps virus has different antigens (so measles antibodies cannot bind to it) ✓\nALLOW: shape of antibody is complementary to measles antigen only` },
      { q: `A doctor takes a blood test from a patient with a high temperature. One type of phagocyte is counted.\nNormal range: 2.0–7.5 × 10⁹ cells per litre of blood\nPatient's result: 15.0 × 10⁹ cells per litre of blood\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nHow many times greater is the patient's result than the top of the normal range? Suggest a reason for the patient's result.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `15.0 ÷ 7.5 = 2 (times) ✓\nPatient has an infection / the body is making more phagocytes to destroy pathogens ✓` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the concentration of antibodies in a person's blood after they were infected with a virus (data written for this practice question).\nThe highest antibody concentration after the first infection was 20 arbitrary units.\nThe highest antibody concentration after a second infection by the same virus was 400 arbitrary units.\n\nCalculate how many times greater the highest antibody concentration was after the second infection.\nAnswer = ______ times`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `400 ÷ 20 ✓\n= 20 (times) ✓` },
      { q: `This question is about plant defences.\nThe cells of a plant leaf are each surrounded by a cell wall made of cellulose.\n\nExplain how the cell wall helps protect the plant against pathogens.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Cell wall is tough / strong / rigid ✓\nActs as a (physical) barrier so pathogens cannot enter the cell ✓\nALLOW: makes it difficult for fungal hyphae / bacteria to get into the cell` },
      { q: `Mint and garlic plants produce chemicals that kill some bacteria and fungi. These chemicals are stored in their leaves.\n\nExplain why making these chemicals helps the plants to survive.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The chemicals are antimicrobial / kill pathogens (that land on or enter the leaves) ✓\nSo the plant does not become diseased / can keep photosynthesising / growing ✓\nIGNORE: references to deterring animals` },
      { q: `A student investigated whether the waxy cuticle protects bean leaves from a fungus. She gently wiped the wax off five leaves and left five leaves untouched. She sprayed all ten leaves with the same volume of fungal spore suspension. After 7 days she counted the number of fungal spots on each leaf.\n\nIdentify the independent variable and the dependent variable in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Independent: whether the wax / cuticle was removed or not ✓\nDependent: number of fungal spots (on each leaf) ✓\nIGNORE: volume of spores / type of fungus` },
      { q: `HIV is a virus that infects and destroys one type of lymphocyte. If HIV is not treated, the number of these lymphocytes in the blood falls. The person is then said to have AIDS.\n\nSuggest why a person with AIDS often becomes ill with infections that a healthy person would fight off.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Fewer lymphocytes so fewer / no antibodies are made ✓\nSo pathogens are not destroyed / can multiply in the body ✓\nALLOW: immune system is weakened / does not work properly for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Max caught chickenpox when he was 4 years old. When he was 10, his sister caught chickenpox. Max was exposed to the same virus but did not become ill.\n\nExplain why Max did not become ill the second time.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Memory cells (from the first infection) were still in his blood ✓\nSo antibodies were made quickly / in large amounts and destroyed the virus before he felt ill ✓\nALLOW: he was immune for 1 mark only if no other mark scored` },
      { q: `Some viruses, such as the influenza (flu) virus, change the shape of their surface proteins very often. A person can catch flu many times during their life.\n\nExplain why a person can catch flu again even though they have had flu before.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The (new) virus has different antigens ✓\nMemory cells / antibodies (from earlier infection) do not recognise / bind to the new antigens (so no fast secondary response) ✓` },
      { q: `When bacteria enter a cut, phagocytes move to the area from the blood.\n\nDescribe how a phagocyte destroys a bacterium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Phagocyte engulfs / surrounds / ingests the bacterium ✓\nDigests it using enzymes ✓\nALLOW: description of the membrane flowing round the bacterium` },
      { q: `This question is about how the immune system destroys a pathogen the first time it enters the body.\n\nPut statements A to D in the correct order.\nA The lymphocyte divides to make many identical lymphocytes.\nB A lymphocyte with a complementary antibody recognises the antigen on the pathogen.\nC Phagocytes engulf the clumped pathogens.\nD The lymphocytes release antibodies that bind to the antigens and clump the pathogens together.\nWrite one letter in each box.`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `B → A → D → C\nAll 4 in correct order ✓✓✓\n2 or 3 in the correct position ✓✓\n1 in the correct position ✓` },
      { q: `This question is about the immune response.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nPut a ring around the correct options.\nWhen a pathogen enters the body for the first time, antibodies are made slowly / quickly by lymphocytes / phagocytes.\nEach type of antibody fits only one type of antigen / enzyme.\nAfter the infection, memory / red blood cells stay in the blood.`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `slowly AND lymphocytes ✓\nantigen ✓\nmemory ✓` },
      { q: `The stomach wall makes hydrochloric acid. The pH inside the stomach is about 2. People who take medicines that reduce stomach acid production are more likely to get food poisoning.\n\nExplain how stomach acid kills bacteria, and why people who take these medicines are more likely to get food poisoning.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Low pH / acid denatures (the enzymes / proteins of) the bacteria ✓\n(so the bacteria die / cannot reproduce) – ALLOW: changes the shape of the active site of bacterial enzymes ✓\nWith the medicine the pH is higher / less acidic, so more bacteria survive and reach the intestine ✓` },
      { q: `Skin is the body's largest organ. It is made of layers of cells, and the outer layer is made of dead cells.\nA patient has a large burn on their arm.\n\nExplain why the patient is at greater risk of infection than a person whose skin is undamaged.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Skin is a physical barrier (to pathogens) ✓\nThe barrier is broken / missing where the skin is burned ✓\nSo pathogens can enter the body / blood / tissues more easily (and multiply) ✓\nALLOW: harmless skin microbes that compete with pathogens are lost` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the concentration of antibodies in a person's blood (data written for this practice question).\nThe person was infected with a virus on day 0. Antibody concentration stayed close to 0 until day 5, rose to a peak of 20 arbitrary units on day 15, and fell to 2 arbitrary units by day 30.\nOn day 60 the person was infected by the same virus again. Antibody concentration rose from day 60 to a peak of 400 arbitrary units on day 67, and was still 150 arbitrary units on day 90.\n\nDescribe three differences between the response to the first infection and the response to the second infection.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from:\nSecond response starts more quickly / no delay (first response had a delay of about 5 days) ✓\nSecond response reaches its peak sooner (7 days compared with 15 days) ✓\nSecond response produces more antibody (400 compared with 20 au / 20 times as much) ✓\nAntibody concentration stays high for longer after the second infection ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW: references to memory cells (this is explanation, not description)` },
      { q: `When a person is infected by a pathogen for a second time, antibodies are made much faster and in much greater amounts than the first time. The person usually does not develop any symptoms.\n\nExplain why the secondary immune response is faster and larger than the primary response.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Memory cells were made during the first infection ✓\nMemory cells recognise the (same) antigen quickly / straight away ✓\nThey divide quickly to produce many lymphocytes that make large amounts of antibody ✓\nALLOW: pathogens are destroyed before they can multiply enough to cause symptoms` },
      { q: `A student investigated whether the waxy cuticle protects bean leaves from a fungus. She wiped the wax off five leaves and left five leaves untouched, then sprayed all leaves with the same fungal spore suspension (data written for this practice question).\nNumber of fungal spots after 7 days:\nWax left on: 2, 3, 1, 2, 2\nWax removed: 11, 14, 9, 12, 14\n\nCalculate the mean number of spots on the leaves with the wax removed. Use the results to explain the role of the waxy cuticle.\nMean = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(11 + 14 + 9 + 12 + 14) ÷ 5 = 12 ✓\nLeaves with wax removed had more spots (12 compared with a mean of 2 / 6 times as many) ✓\nThe cuticle is a (physical) barrier that stops fungal spores / hyphae entering the leaf ✓` },
      { q: `Sam is a doctor. He wants to show his students how antibody levels change when a person is infected with the same pathogen twice.\n\nSketch a graph of antibody concentration against time to show the primary and secondary responses. Mark on your graph the time of each infection.`, marks: 3, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `Axes labelled antibody concentration (y) and time (x), with first and second infection marked ✓\nAfter first infection: delay, then small, slow rise followed by a fall ✓\nAfter second infection: rapid rise with little delay, to a much higher peak, staying higher for longer ✓` },
      { q: `Some people have a condition called dry eye, where the eyes make very few tears. People with dry eye get more eye infections caused by bacteria.\n\nExplain why people with dry eye get more bacterial eye infections.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Tears contain lysozyme / an enzyme ✓\nwhich breaks down / kills bacteria (cell walls) ✓\nFewer tears so fewer bacteria are killed / washed away (and they can grow on the eye) ✓\nALLOW: tears wash pathogens off the surface of the eye for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Fungal pathogens of wheat land on the leaf surface as spores. The spores grow hyphae that try to enter the leaf. Most of the leaf surface is covered by a waxy cuticle, but there are small pores called stomata.\n\nExplain how the structure of the leaf surface protects the wheat plant, and suggest why many fungi enter the leaf through stomata.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Waxy cuticle is a (physical) barrier / waterproof so spores cannot germinate / hyphae cannot pass through ✓\nCell walls (of epidermis) are a second barrier ✓\nStomata are openings / gaps with no cuticle so hyphae can grow in without breaking a barrier ✓` },
      { q: `This question is about the body's non-specific defences.\nThe body has physical, chemical and microbial barriers to pathogens.\n\nComplete the table.\nColumns: Defence | Type of barrier (physical, chemical or microbial) | How it protects the body\nSkin | Physical | ______\nStomach acid | ______ | Kills bacteria in food and drink\nHarmless bacteria in the gut | ______ | Compete with pathogens so they cannot grow\nMucus and cilia | Physical | ______`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `Skin: (tough) barrier that pathogens cannot pass through / covers the body ✓\nStomach acid: chemical ✓\nHarmless bacteria: microbial ✓\nMucus and cilia: (mucus) traps pathogens and (cilia) sweep them out of the airways / to the throat ✓` },
      { q: `Two people, P and Q, were exposed to the same strain of a bacterium on day 0. Person P had been infected with this strain three years earlier. Person Q had never been infected before (data written for this practice question).\nPerson P: antibodies detected on day 2; peak of 360 arbitrary units on day 6; no symptoms.\nPerson Q: antibodies detected on day 6; peak of 30 arbitrary units on day 14; ill from day 3 to day 12.\n\nCompare the immune responses of P and Q. Use the data in your answer and explain the differences.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `P's antibodies are made sooner (day 2 compared with day 6) / P reaches peak 8 days sooner ✓\nP's peak antibody concentration is 12 times higher (360 ÷ 30 = 12) ✓\nP has memory cells (from the earlier infection) that recognise the antigen ✓\nSo P destroys the bacteria before they multiply enough to cause symptoms; Q has a primary response and becomes ill ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW: P has antibodies left over from three years ago` },
      { q: `A student investigated whether the waxy cuticle protects bean leaves from a fungus. She wiped the wax off five leaves with her finger and left five leaves untouched. She sprayed the leaves with fungal spore suspension and counted the fungal spots after 7 days.\nHer teacher said the method could be improved to make the results more valid.\n\nSuggest two improvements to the method. Explain why each improvement would make the results more valid.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two improvement + explanation pairs:\nUse the same volume / concentration of spore suspension on every leaf ✓ so only wax removal affects the number of spots ✓\nUse leaves of the same size / age / from the same plant ✓ so leaf differences do not affect the result / fair test ✓\nRemove wax in a way that does not damage the leaf (e.g. with a solvent and also wipe the control leaves) ✓ so damage / wounds are not the cause of extra spots ✓\nKeep all leaves at the same temperature / humidity / light ✓ because these affect fungal growth ✓\nUse more leaves / repeat ✓ to reduce the effect of anomalous results / see if results are repeatable ✓\nMax 2 marks for improvements without explanations` },
      { q: `* A cold virus is carried in tiny droplets in the air. Ellie breathes in droplets containing the virus. Most of the virus particles are stopped before they reach the cells deep in her lungs, but some infect cells lining her nose. Ellie has a cold for a week. The next year she breathes in the same cold virus again but she does not become ill.\n\nExplain how Ellie's body defends itself against the virus the first time, and why she does not become ill the second time.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Explains how non-specific defences stop most virus particles AND explains the primary immune response with antibodies AND explains why memory cells give a fast secondary response so she does not become ill.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes non-specific defences AND describes antibody production, OR explains the primary and secondary responses in detail.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Identifies at least one defence (e.g. mucus or antibodies) OR states that she is immune the second time.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nNon-specific defences\n• mucus in the nose and airways traps virus particles\n• cilia sweep mucus up to the throat where it is swallowed\n• stomach acid destroys swallowed pathogens\nFirst infection\n• virus has antigens on its surface\n• lymphocytes with complementary antibodies recognise the antigens\n• lymphocytes divide / make many copies\n• antibodies are produced, bind to the antigens and clump viruses together\n• phagocytes engulf and digest viruses\n• this takes days, so the virus multiplies and she has symptoms for a week\n• memory cells are produced\nSecond infection\n• memory cells recognise the same antigen\n• antibodies are made faster and in larger amounts (secondary response)\n• virus is destroyed before it can multiply enough to cause symptoms` }
    ]
  },
  'B2.3': {
    name: 'How can we prevent the spread of infections?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about cholera.\nCholera is a disease caused by a bacterium. People catch cholera by drinking water that has been contaminated with faeces from an infected person.\nAfter a flood, there is a risk of a cholera outbreak in a town.\n\nGive one way the spread of cholera can be reduced in the town.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any one from:\n• boil / chlorinate / treat drinking water OR provide bottled / clean water (1)\n• treat sewage / keep sewage separate from drinking water / repair sewers (1)\n• wash hands (with soap) after using the toilet / before preparing food (1)\n• isolate infected people (1)\n• vaccinate people against cholera (1)\nIGNORE 'keep clean' / 'be hygienic' unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW use antibiotics (treats, does not prevent spread)` },
      { q: `Malaria is a disease caused by a protist called Plasmodium.\nPlasmodium is carried from one person to another by female Anopheles mosquitoes. The mosquito is not harmed by Plasmodium.\n\nWhat name is given to an organism, such as the mosquito, that carries a pathogen from one host to another?`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• vector (1)\nIGNORE carrier / host\nDO NOT ALLOW pathogen / parasite` },
      { q: `Ash dieback is a disease of ash trees caused by a fungus. The disease was first confirmed in the UK in 2012. Most scientists think it arrived on young ash trees imported from nurseries in Europe.\nIn 2012 the UK government banned the import of ash trees from other countries.\n\nSuggest why the government banned imports of ash trees.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• to stop more (trees infected with) the fungus / pathogen / spores being brought into the UK (1)\nALLOW to stop the disease spreading to new areas of the UK from imported trees\nIGNORE to protect ash trees unqualified` },
      { q: `The MMR vaccine is given to young children in the UK. It protects them against measles, mumps and rubella.\n\nComplete the sentence.\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\nA vaccine contains: antibiotics / dead or inactive forms of a pathogen / memory cells / white blood cells.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• dead or inactive forms of a pathogen (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `About 95% of a population needs to be immune to measles to give herd immunity.\nThe table shows the percentage of children in a region who received the MMR vaccine by the age of 2 (data written for this practice question).\nYear | Children vaccinated by age 2 (%)\n2010 | 96\n2011 | 95\n2012 | 95\n2013 | 94\n2014 | 93\n\nIn which year did the percentage of children vaccinated first fall below the level needed for herd immunity?`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• 2013 (1)` },
      { q: `Influenza (flu) is caused by a virus. In the UK, people at high risk are offered a new flu vaccine every autumn.\n\nSuggest why a new flu vaccine is needed every year.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• the (flu) virus mutates / changes its antigens, so memory cells from last year's vaccine no longer recognise it (1)\nALLOW there are new strains / types of flu virus each year\nIGNORE the vaccine wears off unqualified` },
      { q: `Bird flu is a disease of chickens and other birds. It is caused by a virus. The virus spreads between birds in droppings, on feathers, and on boots, vehicles and equipment.\nA farmer keeps chickens. Bird flu has been found on a farm 5 km away.\n\nOther than vaccinating the chickens, describe two ways the farmer could prevent the virus spreading onto the farm.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• keep the chickens indoors / housed to stop contact with wild birds (1)\n• disinfect boots / clothing / vehicles / equipment entering the farm (1)\n• use foot dips (of disinfectant) at entrances (1)\n• restrict visitors / vehicles coming onto the farm (1)\n• do not bring in new birds / do not move birds between farms (1)\n• cover feed and water so wild birds cannot reach it (1)\nIGNORE kill the chickens\nIGNORE 'be hygienic' unqualified` },
      { q: `HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system. It is passed on in body fluids such as blood and semen, and from a mother to her baby.\n\nDescribe two ways the spread of HIV can be reduced.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• use condoms during sex (1)\n• do not share needles / use clean needles (for injecting drugs) (1)\n• screen / test donated blood (1)\n• people who are HIV positive take antiretroviral drugs, so there is less virus in their body fluids (1)\n• HIV-positive mothers take antiretroviral drugs / do not breastfeed (1)\n• testing so people know their HIV status and can avoid passing it on (1)\nALLOW people at high risk take PrEP / preventive antiretroviral drugs\nIGNORE vaccinate (no HIV vaccine)\nDO NOT ALLOW antibiotics` },
      { q: `A grower has a greenhouse of tomato plants. Some of the plants have mottled yellow patches on their leaves. Tests show that the plants are infected with tobacco mosaic virus (TMV).\nTMV is spread when sap from an infected plant gets onto a healthy plant, for example on hands, tools or clothing.\n\nSuggest two ways the grower could stop TMV spreading to the other plants.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• remove and destroy / burn the infected plants (1)\n• wash hands / wear (new) gloves after touching infected plants (1)\n• disinfect / sterilise tools (between plants) (1)\n• change / wash clothing after working with infected plants (1)\n• keep infected plants away from / isolate them from healthy plants (1)\n• grow TMV-resistant varieties in future (1)\nIGNORE spray with pesticide / fungicide\nDO NOT ALLOW antibiotics` },
      { q: `Malaria is spread by mosquitoes, which bite people at night.\nA charity gives families in a village bed nets that have been soaked in insecticide.\n\nExplain how these bed nets reduce the spread of malaria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the net stops mosquitoes biting people (while they sleep) (1)\n• the insecticide kills mosquitoes (that land on the net) (1)\n• so (Plasmodium / the pathogen) is not passed to or from people / fewer vectors to carry it (1)\nMax 2\nALLOW net acts as a barrier between mosquito and person` },
      { q: `A school has 1250 students. 1150 of the students have had two doses of the MMR vaccine.\n\nCalculate the percentage of students who have had two doses of the MMR vaccine.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (1150 ÷ 1250) × 100 (1)\n• 92 (%) (2)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\nALLOW 0.92 × 100 for the first mark` },
      { q: `Before a vaccination programme started, a country had 4800 cases of a disease in one year.\nTen years after the programme started, there were 360 cases in one year (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the number of cases.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (4800 − 360) = 4440 OR 4440 ÷ 4800 (1)\n• 92.5 (%) (2)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\nALLOW 92 or 93 (%)\nALLOW 1 mark for 7.5 (%) (percentage remaining)` },
      { q: `Babies cannot have the MMR vaccine until they are about 1 year old.\nIn a town, 96% of children and adults are immune to measles.\n\nExplain how the high level of immunity in the town protects babies who are too young to be vaccinated.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (very) few people can catch measles / become infected, so the virus cannot spread / is not passed on (1)\n• so the baby is unlikely to come into contact with an infected person / the measles virus (1)\nALLOW 'herd immunity' for 1 mark if not explained\nIGNORE the baby is immune` },
      { q: `Scientists can estimate the percentage of a population that must be immune to stop a disease spreading using this equation:\nherd immunity threshold = 1 − (1 ÷ R₀)\nR₀ is the average number of people that one infected person passes the disease on to, in a population where no one is immune.\nFor measles, R₀ is about 15.\n\nCalculate the herd immunity threshold for measles as a percentage.\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.\nHerd immunity threshold = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1 − (1 ÷ 15) = 0.933… OR 14/15 (1)\n• 93.3 (%) (2)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 93.33… or 93 (%) not given to 1 decimal place` },
      { q: `Ebola is a disease caused by a virus. It is spread by direct contact with the blood, vomit or other body fluids of an infected person.\nDuring Ebola outbreaks, patients are treated in isolation units. Health workers in the units wear full protective suits, gloves and face shields.\n\nExplain why patients with Ebola are kept in isolation units.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• to stop other people coming into contact with their body fluids / the virus (1)\n• so the virus / disease is not passed on / cannot spread to other people / the community (1)\nIGNORE to treat them / so they get better` },
      { q: `In some countries, people are advised to boil their drinking water during a cholera outbreak.\n\nExplain why boiling drinking water reduces the spread of cholera.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (high temperature) kills the (cholera) bacteria / pathogens in the water (1)\n• so people do not drink / take in the pathogen and do not become infected (1)\nALLOW denatures enzymes in the bacteria\nDO NOT ALLOW kills the virus\nIGNORE removes the bacteria` },
      { q: `A doctor gives a parent information about measles and the MMR vaccine (data written for this practice question).\n• About 1 in every 1000 children who catch measles develops a serious brain infection.\n• About 1 in every 1 000 000 doses of the MMR vaccine causes a serious allergic reaction.\n\nUse the information to explain why the doctor recommends the MMR vaccine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the risk of serious harm from measles is greater / higher than the risk from the vaccine (1)\n• (risk from measles) is 1000 times greater OR 1 in 1000 compared with 1 in 1 000 000 (1)\nALLOW the benefit of vaccination outweighs the risk` },
      { q: `The risk of serious harm from the MMR vaccine is much lower than the risk of serious harm from catching measles. However, some parents still choose not to have their children vaccinated.\n\nSuggest two reasons why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• they think / perceive the risk of the vaccine to be higher than it is (1)\n• they have read false information / misinformation / scare stories about the vaccine (1)\n• measles is now rare so they think their child will not catch it / they have never seen it (1)\n• the risk from the vaccine is one they choose to take, so it feels worse than a natural risk (1)\n• religious or personal beliefs (1)\n• they rely on other people being vaccinated / herd immunity (1)\n• fear of needles / injections for the child (1)\nIGNORE cost` },
      { q: `Ash dieback fungus produces spores on fallen ash leaves. The spores are carried by the wind, sometimes for tens of kilometres.\nThe owner of a wood cuts down and burns all the infected ash trees in the wood.\n\nExplain why this may not stop ash dieback returning to the wood.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• spores can be blown in (by the wind) from infected trees in other woods / areas (1)\n• the fungus / spores may still be present on fallen leaves / leaf litter (in the wood) (1)\n• some infected trees may not yet show symptoms so are not removed (1)\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Clubroot is a disease of cabbages and other brassica plants. It is caused by a pathogen that lives in the soil and can survive there for several years. The pathogen does not infect other types of crop.\nA farmer grows cabbages in a different field each year, and grows crops such as wheat and potatoes in between. This is called crop rotation.\n\nExplain how crop rotation reduces clubroot in the cabbages.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• when other crops are grown, there are no host plants for the pathogen (in that field) (1)\n• so the amount of pathogen in the soil falls / the pathogen dies out before cabbages are planted there again (1)\nALLOW the pathogen cannot reproduce / multiply without brassicas` },
      { q: `Public health officials compare outbreaks in towns of different sizes by working out the number of cases per 100 000 people.\nA town has a population of 64 000. There were 48 cases of whooping cough in the town in one year (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the number of cases per 100 000 people.\nCases per 100 000 people = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (48 ÷ 64 000) × 100 000 (1)\n• 75 (2)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\nALLOW 0.00075 × 100 000 for the first mark` },
      { q: `A new disease is spreading in a country. Scientists think people can carry the pathogen for up to 14 days before they show any symptoms (data written for this practice question).\nAnyone who has been in close contact with an infected person is asked to quarantine (stay at home, away from other people) for 14 days.\n\nExplain why the quarantine period is 14 days.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a person could be infected / carrying the pathogen for up to 14 days without symptoms (1)\n• so they could pass it on without knowing / keeping them away from others for 14 days stops them infecting others (1)\nALLOW if they have no symptoms after 14 days, they are (very) unlikely to be infected` },
      { q: `In the UK, the NHS offers a free flu vaccine every year to people aged 65 and over and to people with long-term health conditions. Most healthy adults under 65 are not offered it free.\n\nSuggest two reasons why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• older people / people with health conditions are more likely to become seriously ill / die from flu (1)\n• (their immune system is weaker so) they benefit most from vaccination (1)\n• the cost of vaccinating everyone would be (too) high / limited money for the NHS (1)\n• limited supply of vaccine (made each year) (1)\n• healthy adults usually recover from flu (without treatment) (1)\n• reduces hospital admissions in winter (1)\nIGNORE healthy people do not get flu` },
      { q: `Chlamydia is a sexually transmitted infection caused by a bacterium. Most people with chlamydia have no symptoms. Young people in England are offered a free chlamydia test.\n\nExplain why testing young people for chlamydia reduces the spread of the infection.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• people without symptoms would not know they are infected (without a test) (1)\n• once diagnosed they can be treated / use condoms / tell partners, so they do not pass it on (1)\nALLOW treated with antibiotics so the bacteria are killed` },
      { q: `Doctors recorded the number of new cases of norovirus each week on a cruise ship (data written for this practice question).\nWeek 1: 4 cases; week 2: 15 cases; week 3: 52 cases; week 4: 31 cases; week 5: 9 cases; week 6: 2 cases.\nAt the start of week 4, the crew began cleaning all surfaces with bleach twice a day and kept passengers with symptoms in their cabins.\n\nDescribe the pattern in the number of cases.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• cases increased (rapidly) from week 1 to week 3 / to a peak of 52 in week 3 (1)\n• cases then decreased (each week) from week 4 to week 6 / to 2 in week 6 (1)\nALLOW the fall begins after the cleaning and isolation started\nIGNORE explanations` },
      { q: `Smallpox was a disease caused by a virus. A worldwide vaccination programme led to smallpox being declared eradicated in 1980. Influenza (flu) is also caused by a virus, but it is very unlikely ever to be eradicated by vaccination.\nThe smallpox virus only infected humans, and its antigens changed very little over time.\n\nSuggest two reasons why flu cannot be eradicated by vaccination in the same way.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• the flu virus mutates / changes its antigens (often), so a vaccine does not give lasting protection (1)\n• the flu virus also infects animals (such as birds and pigs), so it can survive in animals / be passed back to humans / animals cannot all be vaccinated (1)\nALLOW there are many different strains of flu\nALLOW people with flu can be infectious before symptoms show` },
      { q: `Jo had the MMR vaccine when she was a child. Years later, she is in a classroom with a child who has measles. Jo breathes in the measles virus but does not become ill.\n\nExplain why Jo does not become ill.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the vaccine contained (dead / inactive) measles virus / antigens, which caused her white blood cells / lymphocytes to make antibodies (1)\n• memory cells (specific to measles) were made and stay in the blood (1)\n• when she meets the (live) virus, memory cells cause antibodies to be made more quickly / in larger amounts (1)\n• so the virus is destroyed before it can cause symptoms / before she becomes ill (1)\nMax 3\nIGNORE immune unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW the vaccine contains antibodies` },
      { q: `In one year, 640 000 children in a country reached the age of 2 (data written for this practice question). 87.5% of them had received the MMR vaccine.\nThe government wants 95% of children to be vaccinated by the age of 2.\n\nCalculate how many more children would need to have been vaccinated to reach the target.\nNumber of children = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• number vaccinated = 0.875 × 640 000 = 560 000 (1)\n• number needed = 0.95 × 640 000 = 608 000 (1)\n• 48 000 (3)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks\nALLOW 2 marks for (95 − 87.5) = 7.5% seen with an arithmetic error in the final step\nALLOW 1 mark for 80 000 (number unvaccinated)` },
      { q: `Malaria is spread by Anopheles mosquitoes. Mosquitoes lay their eggs in still water, such as ponds, puddles and water tanks. The larvae live in the water until they become adults.\n\nDescribe three ways the spread of malaria can be reduced by controlling the mosquito.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• drain / remove standing water / cover water tanks, so there is nowhere to breed (1)\n• spray insecticide (inside homes / on walls) to kill adult mosquitoes (1)\n• use (insecticide-treated) bed nets (1)\n• put oil / chemicals on water surfaces to kill larvae (1)\n• add fish that eat mosquito larvae to ponds (1)\n• use insect repellent / cover skin / screens on windows (1)\n• release sterile / genetically modified mosquitoes (1)\nIGNORE kill mosquitoes unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW vaccinate the mosquitoes` },
      { q: `The table shows the percentage of children vaccinated against measles and the number of measles cases in a country each year (data written for this practice question).\nYear | Children vaccinated (%) | Measles cases\n2014 | 94 | 120\n2015 | 93 | 210\n2016 | 92 | 380\n2017 | 91 | 640\n2018 | 90 | 900\n2019 | 89 | 1350\n\nDescribe and explain the relationship between the percentage of children vaccinated and the number of measles cases.\nUse data from the table in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• as the percentage vaccinated decreases, the number of cases increases (1)\n• use of data, e.g. vaccination fell by 5 percentage points (94% to 89%) but cases rose more than ten times / from 120 to 1350 (1)\n• (vaccination rate is below the threshold for herd immunity, so) more people are susceptible / not immune, so the virus can spread more easily (1)\nALLOW negative correlation for the first mark\nIGNORE references to the rate of increase without data for the second mark` },
      { q: `There have been no measles cases at a primary school for many years.\nA parent says: "There is no point in my child having the MMR vaccine. Nobody gets measles any more."\n\nExplain why the parent is wrong.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• measles is rare because most people have been vaccinated / because of herd immunity (1)\n• the measles virus still exists / can be brought in, e.g. by travellers from other countries (1)\n• if fewer people are vaccinated, vaccination rates fall below the herd immunity level (1)\n• so measles could spread / outbreaks could happen again, and the unvaccinated child could catch it (1)\nMax 3` },
      { q: `A local council manages a large wood. About 30% of its ash trees have ash dieback. Ash dieback spreads by spores carried by the wind.\nThe council is considering cutting down and burning every ash tree in the wood, including healthy trees.\nA scientist points out that a small number of ash trees seem to be naturally resistant to the disease.\n\nSuggest why it would be better not to cut down all of the ash trees.\nUse ideas about resistance in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• resistant trees could survive the disease / not be killed (1)\n• they have (alleles / genes for) resistance that can be passed on to offspring / seeds (1)\n• so future ash trees / the population could become resistant OR they could be used to breed resistant trees (1)\n• cutting down all trees would remove these trees / reduce biodiversity / remove habitats for other organisms (1)\n• spores could still blow in from elsewhere, so cutting all trees may not stop the disease (1)\nMax 3` },
      { q: `The herd immunity threshold can be estimated using this equation:\nherd immunity threshold = 1 − (1 ÷ R₀)\nR₀ is the average number of people that one infected person passes the disease on to, in a population where no one is immune.\nFor one disease, the herd immunity threshold is 80% (0.80).\n\n(i) Calculate R₀ for this disease.\n(ii) Measles has an R₀ of about 15. Explain why a higher percentage of the population must be vaccinated against measles than against this disease.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(i)\n• 1 ÷ R₀ = 1 − 0.80 = 0.20 (1)\n• R₀ = 5 (2)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\n(ii)\n• measles is passed on to more people by each infected person, so more people must be immune to stop it spreading (1)\nALLOW measles is more infectious / spreads more easily` },
      { q: `A cholera outbreak happened in a camp for people who had lost their homes. The table shows the number of new cases each week (data written for this practice question).\nWeek | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8\nNew cases | 12 | 30 | 64 | 110 | 118 | 80 | 41 | 15\nAt the end of week 4, two things happened at the same time:\n• patients with cholera were moved to an isolation tent\n• all drinking water in the camp was treated with chlorine.\nA newspaper reported: "Isolating patients stopped the cholera outbreak."\n\nEvaluate this claim.\nUse the data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• the data supports the idea that the measures worked: cases fell after week 5 from 118 to 15 by week 8 (1)\n• but two measures started at the same time, so you cannot tell which one caused the fall (1)\n• cholera is spread in (contaminated) water, so treating the water is likely to have had a large / larger effect (1)\n• cases still rose in week 5, because people already infected / incubation period OR the outbreak was not stopped completely (15 cases in week 8) (1)\nMax 3` },
      { q: `A head teacher collected data from ten schools. Schools where pupils washed their hands more often had fewer days of absence caused by stomach bugs.\nThe head teacher concluded: "Hand washing causes fewer pupils to be absent with stomach bugs."\n\nExplain why the data does not prove that hand washing causes the lower absence.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the data only shows a correlation / link between hand washing and absence (1)\n• other factors could cause the difference, e.g. how well the school is cleaned / school size / age of pupils / how food is prepared / outbreaks in the local area (1)\n• (the factors were not controlled, so) a correlation does not show that one factor causes the other (1)\n• only ten schools / small sample, so the pattern may be due to chance (1)\n• there needs to be a plausible mechanism or a controlled study / trial to show cause (1)\nMax 3` },
      { q: `A country is deciding whether to vaccinate 200 000 children against a disease (data written for this practice question).\n• The vaccine costs £18 per child.\n• Without vaccination, 4000 of the children are expected to catch the disease.\n• With vaccination, only 200 of the children are expected to catch the disease.\n• Treating one child who catches the disease costs £1500.\n\nCalculate how much money the country would save by vaccinating the children.\nMoney saved = £ ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• cost without vaccination = 4000 × 1500 = £6 000 000 (1)\n• cost with vaccination = (200 000 × 18) + (200 × 1500) = 3 600 000 + 300 000 = £3 900 000 (1)\n• saving = £2 100 000 (3)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks\nALLOW £2.1 million\nALLOW 2 marks for £2 400 000 (cost of treating the 200 children left out)` },
      { q: `This question is about controlling the spread of different diseases.\n\nComplete the table to describe how each method reduces the spread of the disease.\nDisease | Method of control | How the method reduces the spread of the disease\nCholera | Treating sewage before it reaches rivers | ______\nMalaria | Sleeping under bed nets | ______\nGonorrhoea | Using condoms | ______\nTobacco mosaic virus | Burning infected plants | ______`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• cholera: (pathogen / bacteria) does not get into drinking water / bacteria in faeces are killed before they reach water (1)\n• malaria: stops mosquitoes / vectors biting people, so Plasmodium / the protist is not passed on (1)\n• gonorrhoea: stops the exchange of body fluids / semen / acts as a barrier, so the bacteria are not passed on during sex (1)\n• TMV: removes / kills the source of the virus, so it cannot be passed to healthy plants (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW virus for cholera, malaria or gonorrhoea` },
      { q: `Bovine TB is a disease of cattle caused by a bacterium. In parts of England, badgers also carry the bacterium and can pass it to cattle.\nThe government has used several methods to try to reduce bovine TB in cattle:\n• testing cattle regularly and killing cattle that test positive\n• restricting the movement of cattle from farms with TB\n• culling (killing) badgers in some areas\n• vaccinating badgers in some areas.\n\nEvaluate the use of culling badgers compared with vaccinating badgers to reduce bovine TB in cattle.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Any four from:\nCulling\n• removes badgers that could pass the bacteria to cattle (1)\n• but badgers from other areas may move in / survivors move further, which could spread TB to new farms (1)\n• ethical concerns about killing animals / effect on badger population / protected species (1)\nVaccination\n• vaccinated badgers are less likely to become infected / pass on TB, and badgers are not killed (1)\n• but every badger must be trapped and vaccinated, which is difficult / expensive / slow / needs repeating (1)\n• vaccination does not help badgers that are already infected (1)\nOverall\n• a conclusion that weighs up evidence, e.g. neither alone is enough; cattle testing and movement controls are also needed (1)\nMax 4; for full marks both culling and vaccination must be addressed` },
      { q: `Scientists modelled the early stages of an outbreak of a new disease (data written for this practice question).\nThere are 50 cases on day 0.\n• If no control measures are used, the number of cases doubles every 4 days.\n• If people wear masks, wash their hands and isolate when ill, the number of cases doubles every 10 days.\n\nCalculate how many fewer cases there would be on day 20 if the control measures are used.\nGive this as a percentage of the number of cases without control measures.\nNumber of fewer cases = ______\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• without control measures: 20 ÷ 4 = 5 doublings, 50 × 2⁵ = 1600 cases (1)\n• with control measures: 20 ÷ 10 = 2 doublings, 50 × 2² = 200 cases (1)\n• number of fewer cases = 1600 − 200 = 1400 (1)\n• percentage = (1400 ÷ 1600) × 100 = 87.5 (%) (1)\nALLOW error carried forward from incorrect numbers of cases\nALLOW 88 (%)` },
      { q: `A measles outbreak has started in a city of 2 million people (data written for this practice question).\n• 86% of children in the city have had two doses of the MMR vaccine.\n• Most of the cases are in children who have not been vaccinated.\n• About 1 in 1000 children who catch measles develops a serious brain infection.\n• The MMR vaccine causes a serious allergic reaction in about 1 in 1 000 000 doses.\nThe city's health officials are deciding how to stop the outbreak. They are considering:\n• a vaccination campaign in schools\n• asking people with measles, and unvaccinated children who have been in contact with them, to stay at home\n• making the MMR vaccine compulsory for all children starting school.\n\nEvaluate the methods the health officials are considering for controlling the measles outbreak.\nUse the information and your own knowledge.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed evaluation of at least two methods, with benefits AND drawbacks, linked to how measles spreads and herd immunity, AND uses the data (e.g. risk comparison or 86% vs about 95%). Reaches a reasoned conclusion.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes benefits or drawbacks of at least two methods, OR evaluates one method in detail, with some explanation of how it reduces spread.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives a simple statement about one or more methods, e.g. vaccination stops people catching measles.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nHow measles spreads / herd immunity\n• measles is a virus spread in droplets in the air; it is very infectious\n• about 95% immunity is needed for herd immunity; 86% is well below this, so the virus can spread among susceptible people\nVaccination campaign in schools\n• vaccine makes memory cells so children are protected; raises immunity towards the herd immunity level\n• protects babies and people who cannot be vaccinated\n• risk of serious harm from measles (1 in 1000) is far greater than from the vaccine (1 in 1 000 000)\n• takes time for immunity to develop / two doses needed, so it may not stop the current outbreak quickly\n• some parents may still refuse; cost of the campaign\nIsolation / staying at home\n• reduces contact between infected and susceptible people, so fewer people are infected\n• acts quickly on the current outbreak\n• people may be infectious before symptoms show, so some spread still happens\n• children miss school and parents miss work; hard to enforce; does not give long-term protection\nCompulsory vaccination\n• would raise coverage above the herd immunity level and prevent future outbreaks\n• ethical issue: removes parents' choice / personal freedom\n• may cause resentment or loss of trust; some children cannot have the vaccine for medical reasons\nConclusion\n• e.g. a combination is best: isolation to slow the current outbreak, plus a school vaccination campaign for long-term protection, with compulsion only if voluntary uptake stays low` }
    ]
  },
  'B2.4': {
    name: 'How can we identify the cause of an infection?',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student is transferring bacteria from a culture bottle onto a sterile agar plate.\nBefore using the wire inoculating loop, the student holds it in a Bunsen burner flame until it glows red hot.\n\nWhy does the student heat the loop until it glows red hot?`, marks: 1, command: 'Why', markScheme: `• to kill (any) microorganisms / bacteria on the loop ✓\nALLOW to sterilise the loop\nALLOW so the culture is not contaminated\nIGNORE to clean the loop` },
      { q: `A gardener notices that the leaves of her tomato plants have a pattern of pale yellow and green patches.\nShe does not have access to a laboratory.\n\nGive one source of information the gardener could use to identify the disease from these symptoms.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• gardening manual / book / (reliable) gardening website / plant disease identification app ✓\nALLOW ask an expert gardener / garden centre\nIGNORE ask a friend` },
      { q: `A rapid test strip uses monoclonal antibodies to detect a virus in a nasal swab.\nThe strip has a test line (T) and a control line (C). A coloured line should always appear at C when the test has been carried out correctly.\nA patient's strip shows a coloured line at T but no line at C.\n\nWhat should the patient conclude from this result?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• the test has not worked / the result is invalid (so the test must be repeated) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW the patient has the virus` },
      { q: `In a school laboratory, bacteria growing on agar plates are incubated at 25 °C.\n\nWhy are the plates not incubated at 37 °C?`, marks: 1, command: 'Why', markScheme: `• 37 °C is (close to) human body temperature so pathogens / harmful bacteria (that infect humans) are more likely to grow ✓\nALLOW to reduce the risk of growing pathogens\nIGNORE 37 °C is too hot / would kill the bacteria` },
      { q: `A hospital scientist looks at a stained sample from a patient's blood using a light microscope.\nThe eyepiece lens has a magnification of ×10 and the objective lens has a magnification of ×40.\n\nCalculate the total magnification.\n\nTotal magnification = ×______`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (×) 400 ✓` },
      { q: `Scientists want to grow samples of different pathogens on nutrient agar plates.\nThe pathogens are: a bacterium, a fungus and a virus.\n\nWhich of these pathogens will not grow on nutrient agar?\nTick (✓) one box.\nBacterium ☐\nFungus ☐\nVirus ☐`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• Virus ✓\nIf more than one box is ticked award 0 marks` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of an antibiotic on bacteria.\nThe student places a paper disc soaked in the antibiotic onto an agar plate covered in bacteria and incubates it.\nAfter two days there is a circular clear zone around the disc. The diameter of the clear zone is 14 mm.\n\nCalculate the area of the clear zone.\nUse the formula: area = πr²\nUse π = 3.14\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nArea = ______ mm²`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• r = 14 ÷ 2 = 7 (mm) ✓\n• area = 3.14 × 7² = 154 (mm²) ✓\nALLOW 153.86 or 154 for 2 marks\nALLOW 615 (using r = 14, not halved) for 1 mark only` },
      { q: `A student tests three antiseptics on bacteria. Paper discs are soaked in each antiseptic and placed on an agar plate covered in bacteria.\nThe student also places a fourth disc on the plate that has been soaked in sterile water.\n\nExplain why the student includes the disc soaked in sterile water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• it is a control ✓\n• to show that any clear zone is caused by the antiseptic and not by the (paper) disc / water ✓\nALLOW to show the bacteria grow normally without antiseptic\nALLOW for comparison with the antiseptic discs for 1 mark if 'control' is not given` },
      { q: `After setting up a bacterial culture on an agar plate, a student fixes the lid to the base of the Petri dish with two short strips of sticky tape. The dish is not sealed all the way round.\n\nExplain why the lid is taped but not sealed all the way round.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• taping stops the lid being opened / stops microorganisms from the air getting in / stops bacteria escaping ✓\n• not sealing allows oxygen to enter so anaerobic bacteria / harmful bacteria / pathogens do not grow ✓\nALLOW so the culture remains aerobic` },
      { q: `A student describes the method they used to transfer bacteria onto an agar plate:\n'I took the lid off the Petri dish and put it on the bench. I took the lid off the bottle of bacteria and put my loop into the culture. Then I spread the bacteria over the agar.'\n\nSuggest two ways the student could improve their method so that the technique is aseptic.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• sterilise the loop in a (Bunsen) flame before (and after) use ✓\n• only lift the lid of the Petri dish slightly / do not put the lid on the bench ✓\n• pass the neck of the culture bottle through a flame (after removing the lid) ✓\n• work near a (lit) Bunsen burner / so air currents carry microorganisms away ✓\n• disinfect the bench before starting ✓\n• replace the lids as quickly as possible ✓\nIGNORE wear gloves / goggles` },
      { q: `A wheat farmer is worried that her crop may be infected with a fungal pathogen. The plants look healthy.\nShe sends leaf samples to a laboratory. The laboratory tests the samples for the DNA of the fungus.\n\nExplain why a DNA test can identify the disease before any symptoms appear.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the pathogen / its DNA is present in the plant (in small amounts) before it causes visible damage / symptoms ✓\n• the test detects a DNA sequence / base sequence that is unique to the pathogen ✓\nALLOW DNA can be copied / amplified so only a small amount is needed` },
      { q: `Monoclonal antibodies are used in test kits to identify the pathogen causing a disease.\nA test kit for the bacterium that causes chlamydia uses one type of monoclonal antibody.\n\nExplain why this antibody will only bind to the chlamydia bacterium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the antibody has a (specific) shape that is complementary to the antigen ✓\n• only the chlamydia bacterium has this antigen (on its surface) ✓\nALLOW antibodies are specific to one antigen for 1 mark\nDO NOT ALLOW antibody is complementary to the bacterium / the antibody kills the bacterium` },
      { q: `This question is about how a rapid test strip detects a pathogen.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nPut a ring around the correct options.\n\nThe test strip contains antibodies / antigens that are all identical. They bind to one specific antibody / antigen on the surface of the pathogen.\nThese antibodies are attached to a coloured dye / an enzyme, so a line appears when the pathogen is present.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• antibodies\n• antigen\n• coloured dye\nAll three correct = 2 marks ✓✓\nTwo correct = 1 mark ✓` },
      { q: `A student does a rapid test for a bacterial infection. The test strip has a test line (T) and a control line (C).\nThe student's strip shows a coloured line at C only.\n\nState what this result shows and explain why the line at C is needed.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `• negative result / the bacterium (antigen) is not present (or not detected) ✓\n• the line at C shows that the test is working / the sample has moved along the strip ✓\nALLOW the C line shows the antibodies on the strip are working` },
      { q: `A single bacterium is placed into a nutrient broth. The bacterium divides by binary fission every 20 minutes.\n\nCalculate the number of bacteria present after 2 hours.\nAssume no bacteria die.\n\nNumber of bacteria = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• number of divisions = 120 ÷ 20 = 6 ✓\n• 2⁶ = 64 ✓\nALLOW 64 with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW 12 (6 × 2) for 0 marks` },
      { q: `A sample of milk contains 500 bacteria. The milk is left in a warm room. The bacteria divide every 30 minutes.\n\nCalculate the number of bacteria in the milk after 4 hours.\nAssume no bacteria die.\nGive your answer in standard form.\n\nNumber of bacteria = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• number of divisions = 240 ÷ 30 = 8 (so 500 × 2⁸) ✓\n• 1.28 × 10⁵ ✓\nALLOW 128 000 (not in standard form) for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A doctor thinks that a patient may have an infection caused by the influenza virus.\nThe hospital laboratory has a light microscope but not an electron microscope.\n\nExplain why the light microscope cannot be used to identify the virus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• viruses are (much) smaller than bacteria / cells ✓\n• a light microscope does not have a high enough resolution (to see viruses) ✓\nALLOW light microscope cannot distinguish between two points close together\nIGNORE magnification is not high enough unless linked to resolution` },
      { q: `A scientist prepares a microscope slide of a sample of sputum taken from a patient with a chest infection.\nBefore looking at the slide, the scientist adds a stain.\n\nExplain why the scientist adds a stain to the sample.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• bacteria / cells are (almost) transparent / colourless ✓\n• the stain colours the bacteria / increases contrast so they can be seen (and their shape identified) ✓\nALLOW different bacteria take up the stain differently so they can be distinguished` },
      { q: `Tobacco mosaic virus (TMV) is a pathogen of tomato and tobacco plants.\nA farmer walks through a field of tomato plants looking for plants infected with TMV.\n\nDescribe two symptoms the farmer would look for.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• mosaic / mottled pattern of discolouration on the leaves ✓\n• yellow / pale patches on leaves ✓\n• stunted growth / small plants ✓\n• curled / distorted / malformed leaves ✓\n• fewer / smaller / blotchy fruit ✓\nALLOW discoloured leaves for 1 mark only` },
      { q: `A tree surgeon finds an ash tree with dying leaves and dark marks on its bark. She uses a field test kit that contains monoclonal antibodies against the fungus that causes ash dieback. The kit gives a result in 10 minutes.\n\nGive one advantage and one disadvantage of using the field test kit rather than sending a sample to a laboratory.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• advantage: (much) faster result / can be done on site / no need to transport samples / cheaper / no specialist equipment needed ✓\n• disadvantage: only tests for one (specific) pathogen / may be less accurate / less sensitive / more likely to give false results (than laboratory tests) ✓` },
      { q: `A patient goes to the doctor with a sore throat. The doctor takes a throat swab and sends it to a laboratory to find out what type of pathogen is causing the infection, before deciding on treatment.\n\nExplain why the doctor needs to know the type of pathogen before prescribing antibiotics.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• antibiotics only work against bacteria / do not work against viruses ✓\n• to avoid unnecessary use of antibiotics so reducing the development of (antibiotic) resistance ✓\nALLOW so that the correct / most effective antibiotic can be chosen` },
      { q: `Two antibiotics, P and Q, were tested on the same species of bacterium.\n(data written for this practice question)\nAntibiotic P produced a clear zone of area 78.5 mm².\nAntibiotic Q produced a clear zone of area 201.1 mm².\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the area of the clear zone from antibiotic P to antibiotic Q.\nGive your answer to the nearest whole number.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (201.1 − 78.5) ÷ 78.5 × 100 ✓\n• 156 (%) ✓\nALLOW 122.6 ÷ 78.5 × 100 for first mark\nALLOW 256 for 1 mark (Q as a percentage of P)` },
      { q: `A student measured the diameter of the clear zone around an antiseptic disc on four repeat plates.\n(data written for this practice question)\nPlate 1: 12 mm\nPlate 2: 13 mm\nPlate 3: 21 mm\nPlate 4: 11 mm\n\nCalculate the mean diameter of the clear zone. Do not include any anomalous result.\n\nMean diameter = ______ mm`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• identifies plate 3 / 21 mm as anomalous (and excludes it) ✓\n• (12 + 13 + 11) ÷ 3 = 12 (mm) ✓\nALLOW 14.25 for 1 mark (anomaly included)` },
      { q: `After incubating an agar plate, a student looks at the bacterial colonies that have grown. The teacher tells the student not to open the Petri dish.\n\nExplain why the Petri dish should not be opened after incubation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• pathogens / harmful bacteria may have grown (in the culture) ✓\n• opening the dish could release them / the student could be infected (by breathing them in or touching them) ✓\nALLOW microorganisms may have mutated / large numbers of bacteria are present` },
      { q: `A student measures the clear zone around an antibiotic disc. The zone is not a perfect circle.\nThe student measures the diameter of the zone in two directions at right angles to each other.\n\nSuggest why the student measures the diameter in two directions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• the diameter is different in different directions / the zone is not circular ✓\n• so a mean diameter can be calculated / to give a more accurate value for the area ✓` },
      { q: `A patient has an infected wound. A swab from the wound is sent to a hospital laboratory.\nThe scientists spread the sample onto an agar plate and incubate it for 24 hours before doing any tests.\n\nExplain why the scientists culture the bacteria before testing them.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the swab contains only a small number of bacteria ✓\n• culturing produces (many) more bacteria / colonies so they can be identified / tested with different antibiotics ✓\nALLOW to grow a pure culture of a single type of bacterium` },
      { q: `A student investigates how well a mouthwash kills bacteria. A paper disc soaked in mouthwash is placed on an agar plate covered in bacteria.\nAfter incubation, the clear zone around the disc has a diameter of 2.2 cm.\n\nCalculate the area of the clear zone in mm².\nUse the formula: area = πr²\nUse π = 3.14\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nArea = ______ mm²`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• conversion: 2.2 cm = 22 mm ✓\n• r = 22 ÷ 2 = 11 (mm) ✓\n• area = 3.14 × 11² = 380 (mm²) ✓\nALLOW 379.94 or 380 for 3 marks\nALLOW 3.80 (cm², not converted) for 2 marks\nALLOW 1520 (r = 22) for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A hospital scientist tests four antibiotics, A, B, C and D, on bacteria taken from a patient. Each antibiotic is on a paper disc on the same agar plate.\n(data written for this practice question)\nDiameter of clear zone after 48 hours:\nAntibiotic A: 10 mm\nAntibiotic B: 18 mm\nAntibiotic C: 6 mm\nAntibiotic D: no clear zone\n\nIdentify the antibiotic the doctor should prescribe and calculate the area of its clear zone.\nUse π = 3.14\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nAntibiotic ______\nArea = ______ mm²`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• antibiotic B ✓\n• r = 9 (mm) ✓\n• area = 3.14 × 9² = 254 (mm²) ✓\nALLOW 254.34 or 254\nALLOW ECF for area using wrong antibiotic` },
      { q: `Monoclonal antibodies are used in diagnostic tests for many diseases.\nScientists start making a monoclonal antibody by injecting a mouse with an antigen from a pathogen.\n\nDescribe how the scientists then produce large amounts of the monoclonal antibody.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (B) lymphocytes / white blood cells that make the (specific) antibody are taken from the mouse ✓\n• the lymphocytes are fused with tumour / cancer / myeloma cells (to form hybridoma cells) ✓\n• the hybridoma cells divide rapidly / are cloned / are grown in culture and release (identical) antibodies (which are collected and purified) ✓\nALLOW a single hybridoma cell is selected and cloned` },
      { q: `A home test kit for a sexually transmitted infection uses a test strip. A urine sample is added to one end of the strip and moves along it.\nThe strip contains mobile monoclonal antibodies attached to blue dye. Further along the strip, at the test line, are monoclonal antibodies that are fixed in place.\n\nExplain how the test strip shows a positive result when the pathogen is present in the sample.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (antigens on) the pathogen bind to the mobile antibodies (with dye) ✓\n• the pathogen–antibody(–dye) complex moves along the strip and binds to the fixed antibodies (at the test line) ✓\n• dye builds up / concentrates at the test line so a blue line appears ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW antibodies bind to the pathogen DNA` },
      { q: `A patient has a bacterial infection of the blood.\n(data written for this practice question)\nA laboratory can identify the bacterium by culturing it on agar plates; this takes 2 to 3 days.\nA monoclonal antibody test identifies the same bacterium in 1 hour.\n\nExplain why identifying the bacterium by culturing takes much longer, and why the faster test benefits the patient.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• culturing: bacteria need time to divide / reproduce to form visible colonies / large enough numbers ✓\n• monoclonal antibodies bind directly to antigens already in the sample (so no growth needed) ✓\n• benefit: correct treatment / antibiotic can start sooner so the patient is less likely to become seriously ill / die / spread the infection ✓` },
      { q: `A student tests three antiseptics, A, B and C, on the same species of bacterium.\n(data written for this practice question)\nArea of clear zone:\nAntiseptic A: 28.3 mm²\nAntiseptic B: 63.6 mm²\nAntiseptic C: 113.1 mm²\nThe student used one plate and did not repeat the experiment.\nThe student concludes: 'Antiseptic C is the best antiseptic to use in hospitals.'\n\nEvaluate the student's conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• supports: C produced the largest clear zone / killed the most bacteria / C area is four times A ✓\n• against: only one species of bacterium was tested / hospitals have many different bacteria ✓\n• against: no repeats so results may not be repeatable / reliable / anomaly not identified ✓\nALLOW other valid limitations, e.g. concentration of antiseptics may differ; C may be harmful to human tissue / too expensive\nMax 2 if no supporting point` },
      { q: `Plant diseases can be identified in the field by looking at symptoms, or in a laboratory using tests such as DNA testing.\nA farmer sees potato plants with brown patches on their leaves.\n\nExplain why identifying the disease in a laboratory is more reliable than identifying it from symptoms in the field.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• different pathogens can cause similar / the same symptoms ✓\n• symptoms may be caused by other factors, e.g. mineral deficiency / weather / pests ✓\n• laboratory tests detect the specific pathogen / its DNA / its antigens (so the identification is certain) ✓\nALLOW laboratory tests can detect the pathogen before symptoms appear` },
      { q: `A scientist grows bacteria in a nutrient broth.\n(data written for this practice question)\nAt the start there are 200 bacteria. After 2.5 hours there are 6400 bacteria.\n\nCalculate the time taken for the bacteria to divide once.\nAssume all the bacteria divide at the same rate and none die.\n\nTime = ______ minutes`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 6400 ÷ 200 = 32 ✓\n• 32 = 2⁵ so 5 divisions ✓\n• 150 ÷ 5 = 30 (minutes) ✓\nALLOW 0.5 hours for 2 marks if units not converted to minutes` },
      { q: `A sample of blood is taken from a patient with an unknown infection. Scientists extract DNA from the sample.\nThey compare the DNA with a database of the DNA sequences of known pathogens.\n\nExplain how this DNA test identifies the pathogen.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• each species of pathogen has a unique / different DNA (base) sequence ✓\n• the (base) sequence of DNA in the sample is compared with sequences of known pathogens ✓\n• a match identifies the pathogen / (the DNA does not match human DNA so it must come from the pathogen) ✓\nALLOW DNA probes / primers that only bind to the pathogen's DNA are used` },
      { q: `A student is using agar plates to compare how well different antibiotics kill bacteria. The teacher tells the student to use aseptic technique throughout.\n\nExplain why aseptic technique is important for the safety of the student and for the validity of the results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• safety: prevents the bacteria escaping into the environment / infecting the student ✓\n• safety: prevents contamination by microorganisms from the air / skin which could be pathogens ✓\n• validity: contaminating microorganisms could also grow on the plate so clear zones / growth would not be caused only by the antibiotic on the bacteria being tested ✓` },
      { q: `A student wants to grow an even layer ('lawn') of bacteria across the surface of a sterile agar plate. The student is given a bottle of bacterial culture, a sterile pipette, a glass spreader, a beaker of ethanol and a Bunsen burner.\n\nDescribe how the student should prepare the plate using aseptic technique.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• disinfect the bench and work near a lit Bunsen burner / in the updraught of a Bunsen ✓\n• remove the bottle lid and flame the neck of the bottle (briefly) ✓\n• use a sterile pipette to transfer a few drops of culture, lifting the lid of the Petri dish only slightly ✓\n• dip the spreader in ethanol and pass it through the flame / sterilise the spreader and allow to cool, then spread the bacteria evenly over the agar ✓\n• tape the lid on (not fully sealed) and label / invert the plate ✓\nAny four ✓ for 4 marks` },
      { q: `A scientist tests two antibiotics, X and Y, on bacteria. She does three repeats for each antibiotic.\n(data written for this practice question)\nDiameter of clear zone (mm):\nAntibiotic X: 15, 16, 17 (mean 16)\nAntibiotic Y: 20, 22, 21\nThe area of the clear zone for antibiotic X, using its mean diameter, is 201 mm².\n\nCalculate how many times larger the area of the clear zone is for antibiotic Y than for antibiotic X, using the mean diameter for Y.\nUse π = 3.14\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\n\nAnswer = ______ times larger`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• mean diameter Y = (20 + 22 + 21) ÷ 3 = 21 (mm) ✓\n• r = 10.5 (mm) ✓\n• area = 3.14 × 10.5² = 346 (mm²) ✓\n• 346 ÷ 201 = 1.7 (times) ✓\nALLOW 346.185 ÷ 201 = 1.72 → 1.7\nALLOW ECF at each stage` },
      { q: `A hospital needs to choose a test to find out whether patients have tuberculosis (TB), a bacterial infection.\n(data written for this practice question)\nCulture on agar: time for result 3 weeks; cost £20; correctly detects 99% of patients with TB; also shows which antibiotics kill the bacteria.\nDNA test: time for result 2 hours; cost £60; correctly detects 95% of patients with TB; shows whether the bacteria carry a gene for resistance to one antibiotic.\nMonoclonal antibody test strip: time for result 30 minutes; cost £5; correctly detects 60% of patients with TB; does not give information on antibiotics.\n\nEvaluate which test the hospital should use. Use the information given.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• culture is most accurate / detects 99% but takes 3 weeks so treatment is delayed / patient may infect others ✓\n• DNA test is fast (2 hours) and detects 95% but is the most expensive ✓\n• test strip is cheapest and fastest but misses 40% of patients with TB / gives many false negatives ✓\n• valid conclusion with reason, e.g. DNA test is best because it is fast and accurate and helps choose an antibiotic; OR use DNA test first then confirm antibiotic choice with culture ✓\nMax 3 if no conclusion` },
      { q: `A student wants to find out which of three antibiotics, A, B and C, is most effective against a species of bacterium that is safe to use in schools.\nThe student is given: a bottle of the bacterial culture, sterile agar plates, paper discs soaked in each antibiotic, sterile forceps, a glass spreader, ethanol, a Bunsen burner, an incubator and a ruler.\n\nDescribe a method the student could use to carry out this investigation and explain how the results should be processed to decide which antibiotic is most effective.\nIn your answer you should describe:\n• how to work safely and aseptically\n• the variables that should be controlled\n• the measurements and calculations needed.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Please refer to the marking instructions for guidance on marking this question.\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Gives a detailed aseptic method AND describes control variables AND explains how to measure the clear zones and calculate their areas (πr²) to compare the antibiotics.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Gives a method with some aseptic steps AND EITHER some control variables OR how the clear zones are measured and compared.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives a simple method, e.g. put the discs on agar with bacteria and see which has the biggest clear area.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod and aseptic technique\n• disinfect the bench; work near a lit Bunsen burner\n• flame the neck of the culture bottle; lift the Petri dish lid only slightly\n• sterilise the spreader in ethanol / flame and spread bacteria evenly over the agar\n• use sterile / flamed forceps to place one disc of each antibiotic on the plate, spaced apart\n• include a control disc soaked in sterile water\n• tape the lid on with two strips (not sealed); incubate at 25 °C (not 37 °C) for 48 hours\n• do not open plates after incubation; dispose of / autoclave plates safely\nControl variables\n• same species and volume of bacteria; same concentration of each antibiotic; same size discs; same agar; same temperature and time of incubation\n• repeat with several plates\nMeasurements and calculations\n• measure the diameter of each clear zone (in two directions at right angles) with a ruler, through the base of the dish\n• calculate mean diameter; halve to find radius; area = πr²\n• calculate a mean area from repeats, ignoring anomalies\n• the antibiotic with the largest clear zone area is the most effective` }
    ]
  },
  'B2.5': {
    name: 'How can lifestyle, genes, and the environment affect health?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about cardiovascular disease (CVD).\n\nA doctor takes a blood sample from a patient. The doctor tests the blood for a fatty substance that can build up in the walls of arteries and increase the risk of CVD.\n\nName this fatty substance.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `cholesterol ✓\nALLOW LDL / low-density lipoprotein\nIGNORE fat / lipid unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW glucose` },
      { q: `Tobacco smoke contains many different chemicals. Some of these chemicals damage the lungs and some damage the heart and blood vessels.\n\nName the chemical in tobacco smoke that makes smoking addictive.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `nicotine ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW tar / carbon monoxide` },
      { q: `Drinking large amounts of alcohol over many years is a risk factor for several non-communicable diseases. One of these diseases is cirrhosis, where healthy tissue in an organ is replaced by scar tissue.\n\nWhich organ is damaged in cirrhosis?`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `liver ✓\nIGNORE kidney / stomach` },
      { q: `A leaflet in a doctor's surgery says:\n\n"1 in every 20 people who smoke 20 cigarettes a day for 30 years will develop lung cancer." (data written for this practice question)\n\nWhat is this risk as a percentage?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `5 (%) ✓\nALLOW 5 in 100\nDO NOT ALLOW 20 (%)` },
      { q: `Scientists collected data from 2400 adults about the mean number of hours each person spent sitting down each day and their resting blood pressure.\n\nThe scatter graph of their results shows the points rising from bottom left to top right, with most points close to a straight line.\n\nWhat type of correlation is shown by the scatter graph?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `(strong) positive (correlation) ✓\nIGNORE direct / proportional\nDO NOT ALLOW negative` },
      { q: `Scientists studied whether people who drink sugary drinks every day are more likely to develop type 2 diabetes. They compared a group of daily sugary drink users with a group who rarely drink them.\n\nThe scientists made sure that both groups had the same range of ages.\n\nSuggest why the scientists matched the two groups for age.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `age is another factor that affects the risk of (developing) type 2 diabetes / is a confounding factor ✓\nALLOW so that any difference is due to sugary drinks (and not age)\nALLOW to make it a fair comparison if linked to age affecting risk\nIGNORE fair test unqualified` },
      { q: `Doctors use body mass index (BMI) to help decide whether a person's mass puts them at risk of some non-communicable diseases.\n\nBMI = mass (kg) ÷ (height (m))²\n\nTom has a mass of 85 kg and a height of 1.75 m.\n\nCalculate Tom's BMI.\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.\n\nBMI = ______ kg/m²`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `85 ÷ 1.75² / 85 ÷ 3.0625 ✓\n= 27.8 (kg/m²) ✓\nALLOW 27.76 / 27.755 for 1 mark only (not to 1 d.p.)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `The table shows how BMI is used to describe a person's body mass.\n\nBMI (kg/m²) | Description\nbelow 18.5 | underweight\n18.5 to 24.9 | healthy weight\n25.0 to 29.9 | overweight\n30.0 to 39.9 | obese\n40.0 and above | severely obese\n\nPriya has a BMI of 31.2 kg/m².\n\nUse the table to describe Priya's body mass and name one non-communicable disease she has an increased risk of developing.`, marks: 2, command: 'Use', markScheme: `obese ✓\nany one from:\n• cardiovascular disease / coronary heart disease / heart attack / stroke\n• type 2 diabetes\n• (named) cancer e.g. bowel / breast cancer\n• high blood pressure ✓\nIGNORE diabetes unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW type 1 diabetes / any communicable disease` },
      { q: `Waist:hip ratio is another measure used to assess the risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nwaist:hip ratio = waist measurement ÷ hip measurement\n\nHealth guidelines say that a man has an increased risk of cardiovascular disease if his waist:hip ratio is above 0.90.\n\nAmir has a waist measurement of 94 cm and a hip measurement of 100 cm.\n\nCalculate Amir's waist:hip ratio and state whether he has an increased risk of cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `94 ÷ 100 = 0.94 ✓\n(yes) increased risk because 0.94 is above 0.90 ✓\nALLOW ecf for the conclusion from an incorrect ratio\nIGNORE units` },
      { q: `Two men have the same BMI of 29 kg/m². Man A is a professional rugby player. Man B does very little exercise and carries most of his fat around his abdomen.\n\nExplain why waist:hip ratio may give a better indication of the risk of cardiovascular disease than BMI for these two men.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `BMI does not distinguish between fat and muscle (so Man A's BMI is high because of muscle) ✓\nwaist:hip ratio shows how much fat is stored around the abdomen / waist, which is more closely linked to CVD risk ✓\nALLOW Man B will have a higher waist:hip ratio (and higher risk) for the second marking point\nIGNORE BMI is inaccurate unqualified` },
      { q: `A study found that people who own a car are more likely to develop cardiovascular disease than people who do not own a car.\n\nA newspaper headline said: "Owning a car causes heart disease".\n\nExplain why the study does not show that owning a car causes heart disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the study only shows a correlation / link (not cause) ✓\nanother factor could cause both, e.g. car owners may walk / exercise less / may be older / may have a different diet ✓\nALLOW owning a car has no mechanism to cause heart disease for the second marking point\nIGNORE study was not fair` },
      { q: `Two studies investigated whether eating red meat is linked to bowel cancer.\n\nStudy 1 used 60 people from one town.\nStudy 2 used 48 000 people from 10 different countries.\n\nBoth studies found a link between eating large amounts of red meat and bowel cancer.\n\nExplain why scientists have more confidence in the conclusion from Study 2.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Study 2 has a (much) larger sample size ✓\nso the result is less likely to be due to chance / the sample is more representative of the whole population ✓\nALLOW people from different countries so the results apply more widely / to people with different lifestyles or genes\nIGNORE more reliable / accurate unqualified` },
      { q: `Scientists followed a group of 2000 smokers and 3000 non-smokers for 10 years. The table shows how many people in each group developed cardiovascular disease (CVD) during this time. (data written for this practice question)\n\nGroup | Number of people | Number who developed CVD\nsmokers | 2000 | 46\nnon-smokers | 3000 | 39\n\nCalculate the percentage of smokers who developed CVD.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(46 ÷ 2000) × 100 ✓\n= 2.3 (%) ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `In a 10-year study, 2.3% of smokers and 1.3% of non-smokers developed cardiovascular disease. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate how many times greater the risk of developing cardiovascular disease was for smokers than for non-smokers.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2.3 ÷ 1.3 ✓\n= 1.8 (times greater) ✓\nALLOW 1.77 / 1.769 for 1 mark only (not to 2 s.f.)` },
      { q: `In a survey, people were asked to rank the risks they worried about most. Many people said they worried about being harmed in a plane crash, but few people worried about developing cardiovascular disease, even though cardiovascular disease kills far more people each year.\n\nSuggest two reasons why people may underestimate their risk of developing cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\n• CVD develops slowly / over many years so the harm does not seem immediate ✓\n• lifestyle risks (e.g. smoking, diet) are taken voluntarily / feel under the person's control ✓\n• plane crashes get more media coverage / are dramatic so seem more likely ✓\n• people enjoy the activity (e.g. eating fatty food) so ignore the risk ✓\n• people may not know their own risk factors e.g. blood pressure / cholesterol level ✓\n• many deaths from CVD are not reported individually / are seen as common or normal ✓` },
      { q: `Tobacco smoke contains carbon monoxide.\n\nCarbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin in red blood cells.\n\nExplain why carbon monoxide increases the risk of a heart attack.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(carbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin so) the blood carries less oxygen ✓\nso the heart has to work harder / beat faster (to supply enough oxygen to the body) ✓\nALLOW heart muscle receives less oxygen for the second marking point\nDO NOT ALLOW carbon monoxide carries oxygen` },
      { q: `Tar in tobacco smoke contains carcinogens.\n\nExplain how carcinogens in tar can lead to a tumour forming in the lungs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(carcinogens) cause mutations / changes to DNA / genes (in lung cells) ✓\ncells divide uncontrollably / cell division is not controlled ✓\nALLOW mitosis is uncontrolled\nIGNORE cancer cells grow unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW cells divide by meiosis` },
      { q: `Doctors advise adults to do at least 150 minutes of moderate exercise each week to reduce their risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nGive two ways in which regular exercise reduces the risk of cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\n• reduces blood pressure ✓\n• reduces (blood) cholesterol level / increases HDL ✓\n• helps to maintain a healthy body mass / reduces obesity ✓\n• strengthens the heart muscle ✓\n• reduces the risk of type 2 diabetes ✓\nIGNORE keeps you fit / healthy unqualified` },
      { q: `The table shows the percentage of adults who smoke and the death rate from cardiovascular disease (CVD) in four countries. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCountry | Adults who smoke (%) | CVD deaths per 100 000 people per year\nA | 12 | 150\nB | 20 | 210\nC | 28 | 260\nD | 35 | 330\n\nDescribe the relationship shown by the data.\nUse data from the table in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `as the percentage of adults who smoke increases, the CVD death rate increases / positive correlation ✓\nuse of data e.g. 12% smokers gives 150 deaths but 35% gives 330 deaths ✓\nIGNORE smoking causes CVD\nDO NOT ALLOW proportional / directly proportional` },
      { q: `Leo is 52 years old. His father and his grandfather both had heart attacks before the age of 55.\n\nLeo's doctor says that Leo has a higher than average risk of developing cardiovascular disease.\n\nExplain why the doctor says this.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(CVD in the family suggests) Leo may have inherited genes / alleles / genetic variants that increase the risk of CVD ✓\ne.g. variants that cause high blood cholesterol / high blood pressure ✓\nALLOW Leo may share the same lifestyle / diet as his family for the second marking point\nIGNORE Leo will definitely get CVD` },
      { q: `In one country, the number of deaths from cardiovascular disease fell from 180 000 in 1990 to 135 000 in 2020. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in deaths from cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(180 000 – 135 000) ÷ 180 000 × 100 / 45 000 ÷ 180 000 × 100 ✓\n= 25 (%) ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `UK health guidelines advise adults not to drink more than 14 units of alcohol each week.\n\nDescribe two effects on the body of regularly drinking much more alcohol than this over many years.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\n• liver damage / cirrhosis / liver disease ✓\n• increased risk of (named) cancer e.g. liver / mouth / breast cancer ✓\n• high blood pressure / increased risk of CVD / stroke ✓\n• brain damage ✓\n• weight gain / obesity ✓\nIGNORE drunk / addiction / effects on behaviour` },
      { q: `Many processed foods contain a lot of salt. Eating a diet high in salt increases a person's blood pressure.\n\nExplain why high blood pressure increases the risk of cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(high blood pressure) damages the lining / walls of arteries ✓\nso fatty deposits / plaques build up more easily / arteries become narrower / blood clots form ✓\nALLOW makes the heart work harder\nALLOW blood vessels may burst (causing a stroke)` },
      { q: `The World Health Organization reports that the number of deaths from non-communicable diseases, such as cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes, is increasing in many low- and middle-income countries.\n\nSuggest two reasons for this increase.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\n• diets are changing to include more processed food / fat / sugar / salt ✓\n• more people live in cities / have jobs with less physical activity ✓\n• more people smoke / tobacco is marketed more ✓\n• people are living longer / fewer die of communicable diseases (so more live to develop NCDs) ✓\n• rising obesity ✓\n• limited access to health care / screening / treatment ✓` },
      { q: `Scientists plan a study to find out whether eating a large amount of processed meat is linked to heart disease. They will follow 5000 adults for 20 years.\n\nAt the start of the study, the scientists record the age, sex and smoking habits of every person.\n\nExplain why the scientists record this information.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `age / sex / smoking are other factors that affect the risk of heart disease ✓\nso the scientists can take these into account / match the groups / see whether the link is due to processed meat (and not these factors) ✓\nALLOW to control confounding factors for the second marking point\nIGNORE to make it a fair test unqualified` },
      { q: `A local council wants to know how many adults in its town are obese. A survey estimated that 28% of adults in the town are obese.\n\nThere are 52 000 adults in the town. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the number of adults in the town who are estimated to be obese.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `52 000 × 0.28 / 52 000 × 28 ÷ 100 ✓\n= 14 560 ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Leo is having a health check-up. The doctor tells Leo that he eats too much saturated fat.\n\nExplain how a diet high in saturated fat can lead to a heart attack.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\n• raises (blood) cholesterol level ✓\n• fatty deposits / plaques build up in the walls of the coronary arteries ✓\n• the arteries become narrower / a blood clot forms / blood flow is blocked ✓\n• less oxygen / glucose reaches the heart muscle (so less respiration) ✓\n• heart muscle cells die / are damaged ✓\nALLOW coronary heart disease / CHD\nDO NOT ALLOW blood is blocked in veins` },
      { q: `Doctors use body mass index (BMI) to assess a person's body mass.\n\nBMI = mass (kg) ÷ (height (m))²\n\nA doctor wants Sara to reach a BMI of 24.0 kg/m². Sara is 1.60 m tall.\n\nCalculate the mass Sara needs to be to have a BMI of 24.0 kg/m².\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.\n\nMass = ______ kg`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass = BMI × height² ✓\n= 24.0 × 1.60² / 24.0 × 2.56 ✓\n= 61.4 (kg) ✓\nALLOW 61.44 for 2 marks (not to 1 d.p.)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `A student asked five adults how many units of alcohol they drank last week. (data written for this practice question)\n\nAdult | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5\nUnits of alcohol | 12 | 18 | 6 | 22 | 14\n\nUK guidelines advise adults to drink no more than 14 units each week.\n\nCalculate the mean number of units drunk by the five adults.\nThe student says: "These adults drink more than the guideline." Comment on this statement.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(12 + 18 + 6 + 22 + 14) ÷ 5 / 72 ÷ 5 ✓\n= 14.4 (units) ✓\nany one from:\n• the mean is (slightly) above 14 units but two / some adults drank less than (or equal to) the guideline ✓\n• sample of five / only one week is too small to draw a conclusion ✓\n• people may not report their drinking accurately ✓\nALLOW ecf from incorrect mean for the comment` },
      { q: `The table shows the risk of dying from lung cancer for people who smoke different numbers of cigarettes each day, compared with non-smokers. A risk of 1 means the same risk as a non-smoker. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCigarettes smoked per day | Relative risk of dying from lung cancer\n0 (non-smoker) | 1\n1 to 14 | 8\n15 to 24 | 13\n25 or more | 25\n\nExplain how these data, together with other evidence, support the claim that smoking causes lung cancer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `risk increases as the number of cigarettes smoked increases / dose–response relationship ✓\ne.g. smoking 25 or more a day gives 25 times the risk of a non-smoker ✓\nthere is a mechanism / tar contains carcinogens that cause mutations (which explains the link) ✓\nALLOW many large studies / repeated studies have found the same correlation for the third marking point\nIGNORE proves` },
      { q: `A scientist plotted data from 20 countries. The graph showed that countries where people eat more saturated fat have higher death rates from coronary heart disease.\n\nThe scientist concluded: "Eating saturated fat causes coronary heart disease."\n\nEvaluate the scientist's conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `supports: there is a (positive) correlation between saturated fat intake and death rate from CHD ✓\nlimitation (any two from):\n• correlation does not show cause / another factor could explain the link, e.g. smoking / exercise / wealth / age of population ✓\n• data are for whole countries, not individuals ✓\n• only 20 countries / small sample ✓\n• there may be other differences, e.g. in genes / health care between countries ✓\nALLOW plausible mechanism (fat raises cholesterol) supports the conclusion as an alternative first marking point\nmax 3` },
      { q: `A student read about a study into whether exercise reduces blood pressure.\n\nThe study used 40 male office workers aged 25 to 30 from one company. Half of them jogged for 30 minutes each day for 4 weeks and half did no extra exercise. Their blood pressure was measured once at the end of the 4 weeks.\n\nSuggest three ways the study could be improved so that scientists could have more confidence in its conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any three from:\n• use a larger sample ✓\n• include women / people of different ages / people with different jobs or from different places ✓\n• measure blood pressure at the start (as well as the end) ✓\n• repeat blood pressure measurements and calculate a mean ✓\n• carry out the study for longer than 4 weeks ✓\n• control other factors, e.g. diet / smoking / alcohol ✓\n• randomly allocate people to the two groups ✓` },
      { q: `The table shows the percentage of adults with type 2 diabetes in two areas of the same city. (data written for this practice question)\n\nArea | Adults with type 2 diabetes (%) | Adults who are obese (%) | Fast-food outlets per 10 000 people\nNorthfield | 9.5 | 34 | 14\nSouthgate | 4.2 | 18 | 5\n\nSuggest reasons for the difference in the percentage of adults with type 2 diabetes in the two areas.\nUse data from the table in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Northfield has a higher percentage of obese adults (34% compared with 18%) ✓\nobesity is a risk factor for type 2 diabetes ✓\nNorthfield has more fast-food outlets (14 vs 5 per 10 000) so people may eat more high-fat / high-sugar food ✓\nALLOW other factors, e.g. differences in age / exercise / genes / income between the areas\nIGNORE fast food causes diabetes unqualified` },
      { q: `Scientists studied pairs of twins to find out whether genes affect the risk of coronary heart disease (CHD).\n\nIdentical twins have the same genes. Non-identical twins share about half of their genes.\n\nThe scientists found that when one twin had CHD, the other twin also had CHD in:\n• 44% of identical twin pairs\n• 14% of non-identical twin pairs.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nExplain what these data show about the effects of genes and the environment on the risk of CHD.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `identical twins are more likely to both have CHD (44% compared with 14%) ✓\nso genes affect the risk of CHD / some people have a genetic predisposition ✓\nbut identical twins do not always both have CHD (56% of pairs do not) so environment / lifestyle also affects the risk ✓\nALLOW CHD is caused by an interaction of genes and lifestyle for the third marking point` },
      { q: `Doctors can treat cardiovascular disease in different ways.\n\nPatient X is given statins to take every day. Statins lower the level of cholesterol in the blood.\nPatient Y has a stent inserted into a narrowed coronary artery. A stent is a small mesh tube that holds the artery open.\n\nCompare the benefits and risks of these two treatments.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `any three from:\n• statins do not need surgery / are less invasive, but must be taken for life / every day ✓\n• statins can have side effects, e.g. muscle pain / liver problems ✓\n• a stent works immediately / quickly increases blood flow to the heart muscle ✓\n• a stent needs a surgical procedure, with risks such as infection / bleeding / blood clots ✓\n• statins slow the build-up of plaques but do not open a narrowed artery ✓\nAt least one point must compare the two treatments for 3 marks` },
      { q: `A newspaper article says: "Drinking alcohol every day doubles your risk of mouth cancer."\n\nThe article explains that the risk of developing mouth cancer is 0.3% for people who do not drink and 0.6% for people who drink alcohol every day. (data written for this practice question)\n\nExplain why many people still choose to drink alcohol every day after reading this article.\nUse ideas about risk in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\n• the actual / absolute risk is still small (0.6% / 6 in 1000) ✓\n• "doubles" makes the risk sound bigger than it is ✓\n• people see benefits (e.g. enjoyment / social) that they think outweigh the risk ✓\n• the risk is taken voluntarily / people feel in control of it ✓\n• the harm happens a long time in the future ✓\n• people think it will not happen to them ✓` },
      { q: `Scientists studied 5250 adults for 10 years. They grouped the adults by how much exercise they did each week and recorded how many developed cardiovascular disease (CVD). (data written for this practice question)\n\nAmount of exercise | Number of adults | Number who developed CVD | Percentage who developed CVD (%)\nlow | 1250 | 75 | ______\nmoderate | 2400 | 96 | 4.0\nhigh | 1600 | 40 | ______\n\nComplete the table by calculating the two missing percentages.\nThen describe what the data show and give one reason why the data do not prove that exercise prevents CVD.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `low: 75 ÷ 1250 × 100 = 6.0 (%) ✓\nhigh: 40 ÷ 1600 × 100 = 2.5 (%) ✓\nthe more exercise people do, the lower the percentage who develop CVD / negative correlation ✓\nany one from:\n• correlation does not prove cause ✓\n• people who exercise more may also have healthier diets / smoke less / be younger ✓\n• other factors e.g. genes were not controlled ✓\nALLOW ecf from incorrect percentages for the description` },
      { q: `About 1 in 6 deaths from cardiovascular disease in the UK is linked to smoking. Tobacco smoke contains nicotine, carbon monoxide and other chemicals that damage the lining of blood vessels.\n\nExplain how smoking increases the risk of cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any four from:\n• nicotine increases heart rate / blood pressure ✓\n• carbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin so the blood carries less oxygen ✓\n• so the heart has to work harder ✓\n• chemicals damage the lining of arteries ✓\n• so fatty deposits / plaques build up / arteries narrow ✓\n• blood clots are more likely to form / blood becomes stickier ✓\n• a blocked coronary artery reduces oxygen to heart muscle, causing a heart attack ✓\nIGNORE tar causes CVD unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW carbon monoxide damages the lungs as a cause of CVD` },
      { q: `A doctor measured two men, Jake and Owen. (data written for this practice question)\n\nMan | Mass (kg) | Height (m) | Waist (cm) | Hip (cm)\nJake | 98 | 1.82 | 86 | 104\nOwen | 70 | 1.65 | 92 | 98\n\nBMI = mass (kg) ÷ (height (m))²\nwaist:hip ratio = waist ÷ hip\n\nA BMI of 25.0 to 29.9 is overweight. A man has an increased risk of cardiovascular disease if his waist:hip ratio is above 0.90.\n\nCalculate the BMI and waist:hip ratio for each man.\nUse your answers to suggest which man has the higher risk of cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `BMI: Jake 98 ÷ 1.82² = 29.6 and Owen 70 ÷ 1.65² = 25.7 ✓\nwaist:hip: Jake 86 ÷ 104 = 0.83 and Owen 92 ÷ 98 = 0.94 ✓\nOwen (has the higher risk) ✓\nbecause his waist:hip ratio is above 0.90 / he has more fat around his abdomen, even though his BMI is lower; Jake's high BMI may be due to muscle ✓\nALLOW BMI 29.59 / 25.71 and ratios 0.827 / 0.939\nALLOW ecf from calculated values for the conclusion` },
      { q: `Leo is 52 years old. He is having a health check-up. The table shows some of his results.\n\nMeasurement | Leo's result | Healthy range\nBMI (kg/m²) | 31.4 | 18.5 to 24.9\nblood pressure (mmHg) | 150/95 | below 140/90\nblood cholesterol (mmol/dm³) | 6.8 | below 5.0\ncigarettes smoked per day | 15 | 0\nexercise per week (minutes) | 20 | at least 150\n\nLeo's father had a heart attack at the age of 50.\n\nEvaluate Leo's risk of developing cardiovascular disease (CVD) and explain what Leo could do to reduce his risk.\nUse information from the table in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nUses data to evaluate several risk factors, including the genetic factor and how they increase the risk of CVD, AND explains a range of actions Leo could take with reasons.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nIdentifies some risk factors from the table with some explanation of how they increase the risk of CVD, AND suggests some actions Leo could take.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nIdentifies at least one risk factor OR one action Leo could take.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nRisk factors (from data)\n• BMI 31.4 is above the healthy range / obese\n• blood pressure 150/95 is above 140/90 – damages artery walls\n• cholesterol 6.8 is above 5.0 – fatty plaques build up in coronary arteries\n• smokes 15 a day – nicotine raises heart rate / blood pressure; carbon monoxide reduces oxygen carried; chemicals damage arteries\n• only 20 minutes of exercise compared with at least 150 recommended\n• father's early heart attack suggests Leo may have inherited genetic variants that increase risk\n• several risk factors together increase risk more than one alone\n• risk factors increase the chance but do not mean Leo will definitely develop CVD\nActions\n• stop smoking (e.g. nicotine replacement / support)\n• eat less saturated fat / salt and more fruit and vegetables to lower cholesterol / blood pressure\n• exercise more, building up to at least 150 minutes per week\n• lose mass to reduce BMI\n• take medication, e.g. statins / blood pressure medicines, if advised by a doctor\n• Leo cannot change his genes, so changing lifestyle factors is more important` }
    ]
  },
  'B2.6': {
    name: 'How can we treat disease?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about treating infections.\nAmira has a sore throat and a high temperature. Her doctor tells her the infection is caused by a virus and does not prescribe antibiotics.\n\nWhy would antibiotics not cure Amira's infection?`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `antibiotics only kill / stop the growth of bacteria (and have no effect on viruses) ✓\nALLOW viruses do not have the structures (e.g. cell wall) that antibiotics act on\nIGNORE antibiotics kill germs` },
      { q: `Different medicines are used to treat diseases caused by different types of pathogen.\n\nComplete the sentences about these medicines.\nPut a ring around the correct options.\nAntibiotics are used to treat infections caused by bacteria / fungi / viruses.\nAntiviral drugs work by killing the virus / slowing down the reproduction of the virus / boosting the number of white blood cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `bacteria ✓\nslowing down the reproduction of the virus ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed in a sentence` },
      { q: `HIV is a virus that infects white blood cells. People with HIV take antiretroviral drugs, which are a type of antiviral, every day for the rest of their lives.\n\nSuggest why it is difficult to develop antiviral drugs that destroy viruses without harming the patient.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `viruses reproduce inside the patient's (host) cells ✓\nso a drug that destroys the virus is likely to damage / kill the body cells as well ✓\nALLOW viruses use the host cell's own structures / enzymes to make new viruses` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of an antibiotic on bacteria. She spread bacteria evenly on agar jelly in a Petri dish, placed a paper disc soaked in the antibiotic in the centre and incubated the dish.\nAfter two days there was a circular clear zone around the disc where no bacteria had grown. The diameter of the clear zone was 18 mm.\n\nCalculate the area of the clear zone.\nUse the equation: area = πr²\nUse π = 3.14 and give your answer to 3 significant figures.\nArea = ______ mm²`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `r = 9 (mm) ✓\narea = 3.14 × 9² = 254 (mm²) ✓\nALLOW 254.34 / 254.5 for 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 1017 (diameter used instead of radius)` },
      { q: `A student compared three antibiotics, A, B and C, using paper discs on agar plates covered with bacteria. She measured the diameter of the clear zone around each disc on three plates.\n(data written for this practice question)\nAntibiotic | Plate 1 diameter (mm) | Plate 2 diameter (mm) | Plate 3 diameter (mm)\nA | 20 | 22 | 21\nB | 8 | 9 | 10\nC | 12 | 14 | 13\n\nCalculate the mean area of the clear zone for antibiotic C.\nUse the equation: area = πr²\nUse π = 3.14 and give your answer to the nearest whole number.\nMean area = ______ mm²`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean diameter = (12 + 14 + 13) ÷ 3 = 13 (mm) ✓\nr = 6.5 (mm) ✓\narea = 3.14 × 6.5² = 133 (mm²) ✓\nALLOW 132.7 / 132.67\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect mean diameter for the last 2 marks\nALLOW 2 marks for 531 (diameter used as radius)` },
      { q: `A student tested how well an antibiotic killed bacteria. On the same agar plate she placed one paper disc soaked in the antibiotic and one paper disc soaked in sterile water.\n\nExplain why the student included the disc soaked in sterile water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `it is a control ✓\nto show that any clear zone is caused by the antibiotic (and not by the paper disc / water) ✓\nALLOW so that it can be compared with the antibiotic disc` },
      { q: `Students in a school laboratory grew bacteria on agar plates to test the effect of antibiotics. Their teacher reminded them to use aseptic techniques.\n\nDescribe two aseptic techniques the students should use.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\npass the inoculating loop through a (blue Bunsen) flame before and after use ✓\nopen the lid of the Petri dish only slightly / for as short a time as possible ✓\nwork near a lit Bunsen burner ✓\ntape the lid on (with two strips, not all the way round) ✓\nuse sterile agar / sterile Petri dishes / disinfect the bench ✓\nIGNORE wear gloves / goggles` },
      { q: `In a school laboratory, agar plates of bacteria used to test antibiotics are incubated at 25 °C.\n\nGive one reason why the plates are not incubated at 37 °C.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to reduce the chance of growing pathogens / bacteria that could harm humans ✓\nALLOW 37 °C is human body temperature, so harmful bacteria would grow well\nIGNORE bacteria would grow too fast` },
      { q: `MRSA is a strain of the bacterium Staphylococcus aureus. It is resistant to the antibiotic methicillin and to several other antibiotics. MRSA infections are most common in hospitals, where antibiotics are used often.\n\nExplain how a population of bacteria such as MRSA becomes resistant to an antibiotic.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(random) mutation in the DNA / genes of a bacterium ✓\ngives the bacterium resistance to the antibiotic ✓\nwhen the antibiotic is used, non-resistant bacteria are killed but the resistant bacterium survives ✓\nthe resistant bacterium reproduces / divides and passes on the resistance gene ✓\nso over time / generations most of the population is resistant ✓\nAny four ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW the antibiotic causes the mutation\nDO NOT ALLOW the bacteria become immune` },
      { q: `Scientists monitored the percentage of E. coli samples from hospital patients that were resistant to antibiotic X.\n(data written for this practice question)\nYear | Samples resistant to antibiotic X (%)\n2015 | 8\n2018 | 13\n2021 | 19\n2024 | 26\n\nDescribe the trend in the data and suggest a reason for it.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `trend: the percentage of resistant samples increases (each time / over time) ✓\nuses data, e.g. increases from 8% to 26% / by 18 percentage points between 2015 and 2024 ✓\nreason: antibiotic X is used more (or overused), so resistant bacteria are selected / survive and reproduce ✓\nALLOW patients not completing courses / antibiotics prescribed for viral infections as the reason\nIGNORE bacteria become immune` },
      { q: `A laboratory tested 250 samples of bacteria from patients with chest infections. 35 of the samples were resistant to the antibiotic amoxicillin.\n\nCalculate the percentage of samples that were resistant to amoxicillin.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `35 ÷ 250 (× 100) ✓\n= 14 (%) ✓\nALLOW 0.14 for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Health organisations are worried that antibiotics will stop working against many infections in the future. They have asked doctors to change the way they prescribe antibiotics.\n\nDescribe two ways doctors can reduce the development of antibiotic-resistant bacteria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\ndo not prescribe antibiotics for viral infections / for minor infections that will clear up on their own ✓\ntest to find which bacterium is causing the infection before prescribing ✓\nprescribe an antibiotic that is specific to that bacterium ✓\ntell patients to finish the full course ✓\nuse antibiotics that are kept for emergencies only when others fail ✓\nIGNORE stop using antibiotics` },
      { q: `Josh was prescribed a seven-day course of antibiotics for a bacterial infection. After four days he felt better and wanted to stop taking the tablets.\n\nExplain why Josh should finish the course of antibiotics.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `some bacteria may still be alive (even though symptoms have gone) ✓\nthese surviving bacteria are the most resistant, so they could multiply / cause the infection to return / spread resistance ✓\nALLOW to make sure all of the bacteria are killed for the first mark` },
      { q: `A resistant bacterium gets into a wound. In the conditions in the wound, the bacterium divides once every 20 minutes.\n\nCalculate how many resistant bacteria there will be after 4 hours, starting from one bacterium.\nUse your answer to explain why antibiotic resistance can spread through a population of bacteria very quickly.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `4 hours = 12 divisions ✓\n2¹² = 4096 (bacteria) ✓\nbacteria reproduce very quickly, so a resistance gene is passed to a large number of offspring in a short time ✓\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect number of divisions` },
      { q: `On some farms, healthy animals such as pigs and chickens are given antibiotics in their food to stop them becoming ill. This is now banned in the UK.\n\nExplain why scientists are concerned about giving antibiotics to healthy farm animals.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `it increases the use of antibiotics, so resistant bacteria are more likely to be selected / survive ✓\nresistant bacteria can spread to humans, so antibiotics will not work on human infections ✓\nALLOW resistance genes can be passed to bacteria that cause human disease` },
      { q: `HIV is a virus that damages the immune system. There is no cure for HIV, but there are medicines that keep the amount of virus in the blood very low.\n\nName the type of medicine used to treat HIV.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `antiviral ✓\nALLOW antiretroviral\nDO NOT ALLOW antibiotic / vaccine` },
      { q: `Scientists are developing a new medicine to treat a skin infection.\nThe statements A to D describe some stages in the testing of the medicine.\nA The medicine is tested on a small number of healthy volunteers.\nB The medicine is tested on human cells and tissues grown in the laboratory.\nC The medicine is tested on a large number of patients with the infection.\nD The medicine is tested on animals.\n\nPut statements A to D in the correct order.\nWrite one letter in each box.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `B D A C ✓✓\nALLOW 1 mark for B before D and A before C, or for two letters in the correct place` },
      { q: `When a new medicine is developed it is first tested on human cells and tissues in the laboratory. This is called preclinical testing.\n\nGive one reason why the medicine is tested on cells in the laboratory before it is tested on animals or people.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to check whether the medicine is toxic / harmful to cells ✓\nALLOW to check that it works / has the expected effect\nALLOW so that harmful medicines are rejected before animals or people are used` },
      { q: `In the first stage of a clinical trial, a new medicine is given to a small group of healthy volunteers at a very low dose. The dose is increased slowly.\n\nExplain why the trial is carried out in this way.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `to check that the medicine is safe / to find any side effects ✓\nlow dose (and small group) so that if there are side effects, few people are harmed / effects are less severe ✓\nALLOW to find the safe / maximum dose\nALLOW healthy people are used because ill patients may be harmed more by side effects` },
      { q: `Doctors tested a new medicine for migraine headaches. One group of patients was given the new medicine. Another group was given a placebo.\n\nDescribe what a placebo is and explain why one was used in this trial.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `a placebo is a dummy pill / treatment that looks the same but contains no medicine ✓\nto check whether any improvement is caused by the medicine (and not by the patient believing they are being treated) ✓\nALLOW so the results for the medicine can be compared with a control group` },
      { q: `A trial of a new medicine for asthma was double-blind.\n\nExplain why the trial was carried out as a double-blind trial.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `neither the patients nor the doctors know who is receiving the medicine (and who is receiving the placebo) ✓\nso the results are not affected by bias / by what the patients or doctors expect to happen ✓\nALLOW doctors cannot treat the two groups differently / record results unfairly` },
      { q: `Scientists are planning a clinical trial of a new medicine for a type of cancer that is often fatal. They decide not to give any patients a placebo. Instead, one group of patients will be given the new medicine and the other group will be given the best existing medicine.\n\nExplain why the scientists designed the trial in this way.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `giving a placebo would mean some patients receive no treatment ✓\nthis would be unethical because their disease could get worse / they could die ✓\ncomparing with the existing medicine shows whether the new medicine is better than the treatment already used ✓\nALLOW patients in both groups still receive a treatment that is known to work` },
      { q: `A company tested a new medicine for high blood pressure on 6000 patients in 20 different hospitals, rather than on 60 patients in one hospital.\n\nExplain why using a large number of patients makes the results more useful.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `reduces the effect of individual differences / variation between patients / random chance ✓\nso there is more confidence that the results are repeatable / that differences are caused by the medicine ✓\nALLOW rare side effects are more likely to be found\nALLOW results are more representative of the population` },
      { q: `A clinical trial tested an antiviral drug for influenza. The table shows how many days each patient in the drug group had symptoms.\n(data written for this practice question)\nPatient | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5\nDays with symptoms | 4 | 5 | 3 | 6 | 4\nThe mean number of days with symptoms for patients given a placebo was 6.0 days.\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the mean number of days with symptoms for patients given the antiviral drug compared with the placebo.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean for drug group = 22 ÷ 5 = 4.4 (days) ✓\n(6.0 − 4.4) ÷ 6.0 × 100 ✓\n= 27 (%) ✓\nALLOW 26.7 for 2 marks (not 2 s.f.)\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect mean` },
      { q: `Two students compared antibiotics P and Q using paper discs on agar plates of bacteria. They measured the diameter of the clear zone on three plates for each antibiotic.\n(data written for this practice question)\nAntibiotic | Plate 1 (mm) | Plate 2 (mm) | Plate 3 (mm) | Mean (mm)\nP | 15 | 17 | 22 | 18\nQ | 16 | 17 | 18 | 17\nOne student says: 'Antibiotic P is more effective than antibiotic Q.'\n\nEvaluate this claim.\nUse the data in the table.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `supports: the mean diameter for P is larger than for Q (18 mm compared with 17 mm) ✓\nagainst: the ranges overlap (P 15–22 mm, Q 16–18 mm) / the difference in means is small ✓\nP's results are more spread out / 22 mm may be an outlier, so the claim is not well supported / more repeats are needed ✓\nALLOW without the 22 mm value the mean for P would be 16 mm, lower than Q` },
      { q: `A company that makes medicines issued a press release about its new cholesterol-lowering drug.\n'In our trial, 40 patients took our drug for 8 weeks. Their blood cholesterol fell by a mean of 30%. In a group given a placebo, blood cholesterol fell by a mean of 10%. Our drug will save thousands of lives.'\nThe trial was paid for by the company and the results have not yet been published in a scientific journal.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nEvaluate the company's claim that the drug will save thousands of lives.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Any four from:\nfor: the drug group had a larger fall in cholesterol than the placebo group (30% compared with 10%) ✓\nfor: a placebo group / control was used ✓\nagainst: only 40 patients / small sample, so the results may be due to chance ✓\nagainst: only 8 weeks, so long-term effects / side effects are not known ✓\nagainst: the trial measured cholesterol, not deaths / heart attacks, so it does not show lives are saved ✓\nagainst: funded by the company, so there may be bias ✓\nagainst: not peer reviewed / not repeated by other scientists ✓\nTo gain 4 marks, at least one point for and one point against` },
      { q: `A new medicine was tested in a large clinical trial. The medicine was effective at treating the disease, but it was not approved for use.\n\nSuggest one reason why the medicine was not approved.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `it had (serious / too many) side effects ✓\nALLOW it was not more effective than existing medicines / it was too expensive to make / risks outweighed benefits` },
      { q: `It can take more than 10 years for a new medicine to go from discovery to being prescribed by doctors. Many possible medicines are rejected along the way.\n\nExplain why developing a new medicine takes so long.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `it has to pass through several stages of testing (preclinical, then clinical trials) ✓\neach stage must show the medicine is safe and effective before the next can start / long trials are needed to find side effects ✓\nALLOW large numbers of patients need to be recruited / data must be checked (peer reviewed) and approved` },
      { q: `Before a new medicine is tested on humans, it is usually tested on animals such as mice.\n\nGive one reason why animals are used and one reason why some people object to this.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `reason used: to test the medicine in a whole living organism (with many organ systems) / to check it is safe before giving it to humans ✓\nobjection: the animals may suffer / be harmed / be killed / animals have rights ✓\nALLOW objection: animals are different from humans, so results may not apply to humans` },
      { q: `A doctor wanted to test whether a new cream heals eczema. She let her patients choose whether to use the new cream or their usual cream. After two weeks she looked at the patients' skin and decided which patients had improved. 12 patients took part.\n\nDescribe three ways the doctor could improve the design of her trial.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `allocate patients to groups at random (instead of letting them choose) ✓\nmake the trial double-blind / blind, so neither the doctor nor the patients know which cream is used ✓\nuse a larger number of patients ✓\nALLOW use a placebo cream / control group\nALLOW measure improvement objectively, e.g. measure area of affected skin\nALLOW match the groups for age / severity of eczema\nAny three ✓` },
      { q: `Cardiovascular disease can be caused when fatty deposits build up inside the coronary arteries. Many patients are prescribed statins.\n\nName the substance in the blood that statins reduce.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `cholesterol ✓\nALLOW LDL / low-density lipoprotein\nIGNORE fat` },
      { q: `Sam has a narrowed coronary artery. A surgeon treats it by inserting a stent.\n\nDescribe how a stent helps Sam.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `the stent holds the artery open / widens the artery ✓\nso more blood (oxygen / glucose) can flow to the heart muscle ✓\nALLOW reduces the risk of a heart attack / reduces chest pain for the second mark` },
      { q: `Before starting a course of statins, Leah's blood cholesterol was 6.4 mmol/dm³. After six months of taking statins it was 4.8 mmol/dm³.\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in Leah's blood cholesterol.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(6.4 − 4.8) ÷ 6.4 × 100 ✓\n= 25 (%) ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 33 (%) (4.8 used as the denominator)` },
      { q: `In a trial, 2400 people at high risk of cardiovascular disease were split at random into two groups. One group took a statin and the other took a placebo for five years.\n(data written for this practice question)\nGroup | Number of people | Number who had a heart attack\nStatin | 1200 | 54\nPlacebo | 1200 | 90\n\nCalculate the percentage of people in each group who had a heart attack.\nUse your answers to calculate how many fewer heart attacks there would be for every 1000 people taking the statin.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `statin: 54 ÷ 1200 × 100 = 4.5 (%) ✓\nplacebo: 90 ÷ 1200 × 100 = 7.5 (%) ✓\n7.5 − 4.5 = 3.0 (%), so 30 fewer (per 1000 people) ✓\nALLOW ECF from incorrect percentages` },
      { q: `Rob is 68 years old. His doctor suggests that he has a stent fitted in a narrowed coronary artery.\n\nGive two risks of having a stent fitted.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\ninfection (from the operation) ✓\na blood clot forming (at the stent) ✓\ndamage to / bleeding from the artery during the procedure ✓\nthe artery may narrow again ✓\nALLOW reaction to the anaesthetic\nIGNORE it might not work` },
      { q: `When a patient's heart is badly damaged by cardiovascular disease, they may be given a heart transplant from a donor. After the transplant the patient must take immunosuppressant drugs for the rest of their life.\n\nExplain why the patient must take immunosuppressant drugs and why this puts them at risk.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the donor heart has (different) antigens, so it is recognised as foreign ✓\nthe drugs stop the immune system / white blood cells attacking the heart / stop rejection ✓\nrisk: the patient's immune system is weakened, so they are more likely to get infections ✓\nALLOW antibodies would be made against the new heart` },
      { q: `Priya has high blood pressure and high cholesterol. Her doctor advises her to eat less saturated fat, take more exercise and stop smoking before prescribing any medicines.\n\nExplain why doctors often recommend lifestyle changes before medicines or surgery.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `lifestyle changes have no side effects / no risks from surgery ✓\nthey are cheap / cost the health service little ✓\nthey reduce the risk factors (e.g. lower cholesterol / blood pressure) so fatty deposits build up more slowly ✓\nALLOW they can also reduce the risk of other diseases (e.g. type 2 diabetes, lung cancer)\nAny three ✓` },
      { q: `Two patients have coronary heart disease. Patient A is treated with a stent. Patient B is treated with statins.\n\nCompare the advantages and disadvantages of the two treatments.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `stent works immediately, whereas statins take months / years to have an effect ✓\nstent needs surgery (risk of infection / clots), whereas statins do not need surgery ✓\nstatins must be taken for life (and patients may forget), whereas a stent is a single procedure ✓\nstatins can have side effects, e.g. muscle pain / liver damage ✓\nstent treats one narrowed artery, whereas statins reduce cholesterol in all blood vessels / slow further deposits ✓\nAny four ✓\nTo gain 4 marks, both treatments must be referred to` },
      { q: `Graham is 61 years old. He gets chest pain when he walks uphill. Tests show that one of his coronary arteries is 80% blocked and his blood cholesterol is 7.2 mmol/dm³. He is overweight and smokes.\nHis doctor gives him information about three possible treatments.\n(data written for this practice question)\nTreatment | Time to have an effect | Main risks | Approximate cost to the health service\nStatins | 3 to 6 months | muscle pain, liver damage in a small number of people | £20 per year, for life\nStent | immediate | infection, blood clot, artery narrowing again | £5000 once\nHeart bypass surgery | immediate, after several weeks recovery | infection, stroke, risks of major surgery and anaesthetic | £12 000 once\n\nEvaluate the treatment options for Graham.\nUse the information and your own knowledge.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Evaluates at least two treatments, using data from the table and Graham's information, and makes a reasoned recommendation that considers both benefits and risks. The line of reasoning is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes advantages and disadvantages of at least two treatments, with some use of the data, OR evaluates one treatment in detail with a conclusion. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives at least one advantage or disadvantage of one treatment. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\n• statins: cheapest per year; lower cholesterol so slow further fatty deposits in all arteries; no surgery; but take 3–6 months, so do not relieve the 80% blockage quickly; must be taken for life; side effects\n• stent: works immediately to widen the blocked artery and restore blood flow to heart muscle; relieves chest pain; but surgery risks (infection, clot); artery can narrow again; does not treat the cause / high cholesterol\n• bypass: treats the blockage immediately; but most expensive (£12 000); highest risk (stroke, anaesthetic); long recovery; may only be needed if several arteries are blocked\n• lifestyle: Graham should also stop smoking, lose weight and change his diet, as these risk factors would make any treatment less effective\n• a reasoned conclusion, e.g. a stent to treat the blockage now, together with statins and lifestyle changes to stop the disease getting worse` },
      { q: `In the UK, around 200 heart transplants are carried out each year, but many more patients are waiting for a new heart.\n\nSuggest why the number of heart transplants is limited and describe one alternative treatment for a patient waiting for a transplant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `there are not enough donor hearts / donors (that match the patient's tissue type) ✓\nalternative: an artificial heart / a mechanical device that pumps blood (until a donor heart is found) ✓\nALLOW ventricular assist device / pacemaker / medicines to support the heart` }
    ]
  },
  'B3.1': {
    name: 'What happens during photosynthesis?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about photosynthesis.\nPlants make their own food by photosynthesis. Photosynthesis uses carbon dioxide and water.\n\nName the gas that plants release into the air during photosynthesis.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• oxygen / O₂ (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW O / carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `Palisade cells in a leaf carry out most of the photosynthesis in a plant.\n\nWhich sub-cellular structure in a palisade cell is where photosynthesis takes place?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\nchloroplast      mitochondrion      nucleus      ribosome`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• chloroplast (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `Leaves look green because of a pigment found inside chloroplasts.\n\nWhat is the name of the green pigment that absorbs light for photosynthesis?`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• chlorophyll (1)\nIGNORE chloroplast\nALLOW phonetic spelling, e.g. chlorofil` },
      { q: `Photosynthesis is described as an endothermic reaction.\n\nWhat does endothermic mean?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• (a reaction that) takes in energy (from the surroundings) (1)\nALLOW absorbs / needs energy (e.g. light energy)\nDO NOT ALLOW releases / gives out energy` },
      { q: `A student investigates how light intensity affects the rate of photosynthesis in pondweed. She counts the bubbles of gas given off by the pondweed in one minute.\nEach time she moves the lamp to a new distance, she waits five minutes before she starts counting bubbles.\n\nSuggest why she waits five minutes before counting.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• to allow the (rate of) photosynthesis to adjust / settle to the new light intensity (1)\nALLOW so the pondweed can acclimatise / so the rate becomes steady\nIGNORE 'to make it fair'` },
      { q: `Leaf discs cut from a spinach leaf sink in water when the air is removed from them. When the discs photosynthesise, gas collects inside the discs and they float.\nA student puts the discs into a dilute solution of sodium hydrogencarbonate instead of pure water.\n\nWhy does she use sodium hydrogencarbonate solution?`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• it provides / is a source of carbon dioxide (for photosynthesis) (1)\nALLOW so carbon dioxide is not a limiting factor\nIGNORE 'to make them float'` },
      { q: `This question is about photosynthesis.\nPlants take in substances from their surroundings and use light energy to make glucose.\n\nComplete the word equation for photosynthesis.\n______ + water → glucose + ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• carbon dioxide (1)\n• oxygen (1)\nALLOW CO₂ and O₂\nIGNORE light / chlorophyll written over the arrow` },
      { q: `The balanced symbol equation for photosynthesis is shown with some parts missing.\n6CO₂ + ___H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + ______\n\nComplete the balanced symbol equation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• 6 (H₂O) (1)\n• 6O₂ (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 6O / O₁₂ / 3O₄ for second mark` },
      { q: `A student puts some pondweed in a test tube of water next to a lamp. She counts the bubbles of oxygen given off.\nShe counts 48 bubbles in 4 minutes (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the rate of photosynthesis in bubbles per minute.\nRate = ______ bubbles per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 48 ÷ 4 (1)\n• 12 (bubbles per minute) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A student investigated the rate of photosynthesis in pondweed with the lamp 20 cm away. She counted the bubbles produced in one minute, four times (data written for this practice question).\nBubbles per minute: 21, 24, 35, 23\n\nCalculate the mean number of bubbles per minute. Do not include the anomalous result.\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.\nMean = ______ bubbles per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (21 + 24 + 23) ÷ 3 / 68 ÷ 3 (1)\n• 22.7 (1)\nALLOW 1 mark for 25.8 (mean including 35)\nALLOW 1 mark for 22.66… / 22.67 not rounded to 1 d.p.` },
      { q: `In a leaf disc investigation, a student measures the time taken for the leaf discs to float. She calculates the rate of photosynthesis using:\nrate = 1 ÷ time taken to float\nAt one light intensity the discs took 12.5 minutes to float (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the rate of photosynthesis.\nGive the unit.\nRate = ______ unit ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.08 (1)\n• min⁻¹ / per minute / 1/min (1)\nALLOW 0.080` },
      { q: `Light intensity is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from a light source.\nWith a lamp 10 cm away from some pondweed, the light intensity is 640 arbitrary units (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the light intensity at the pondweed when the lamp is moved to 20 cm away.\nLight intensity = ______ arbitrary units`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• distance doubled so intensity is ÷ 2² / ÷ 4 / × 1/4 (1)\n• 160 (arbitrary units) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nDO NOT ALLOW 320 (intensity halved) for either mark` },
      { q: `A student used a filament lamp to investigate the effect of light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis in pondweed. Her teacher told her to put a glass tank of water between the lamp and the pondweed.\n\nExplain why the tank of water was used.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the lamp gives out heat / the tank absorbs heat from the lamp (1)\n• (to keep) temperature constant / stop temperature changing the rate of photosynthesis (1)\nALLOW temperature is a control variable / temperature would also affect the rate` },
      { q: `A student measures the rate of photosynthesis in pondweed by counting bubbles of oxygen.\nHer teacher says that counting bubbles is not a very accurate way to measure the volume of oxygen produced.\n\nSuggest one reason why, and suggest a better way to measure the oxygen produced.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• bubbles are different sizes / bubbles are hard to count when they are produced quickly (1)\n• collect the gas in a gas syringe / measuring cylinder / capillary tube and measure the volume (1)\nIGNORE 'use a camera' unless linked to counting` },
      { q: `A scientist measured the rate of photosynthesis of a plant at different light intensities. Carbon dioxide concentration and temperature were kept constant.\nThe graph of rate of photosynthesis (y-axis) against light intensity (x-axis) shows a straight line rising steeply from the origin, then the line curves and becomes horizontal (levels off) at high light intensities.\n\nDescribe the pattern shown by the graph.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• as light intensity increases the rate of photosynthesis increases (1)\n• (then) the rate levels off / stays constant / reaches a maximum at high light intensity (1)\nALLOW 'directly proportional at first' for first mark\nIGNORE explanations` },
      { q: `The rate of photosynthesis in a plant stops increasing at high light intensity. Light is no longer the limiting factor.\n\nGive two factors that could be limiting the rate of photosynthesis at high light intensity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• carbon dioxide concentration (1)\n• temperature (1)\n• amount of chlorophyll / number of chloroplasts (1)\nALLOW CO₂ / heat for temperature\nIGNORE water / light / oxygen` },
      { q: `A geranium plant has variegated leaves. Each leaf has green areas and white areas. The white areas have no chlorophyll.\nA student left the plant in bright light for 6 hours. She then tested a leaf for starch with iodine solution. The green areas turned blue-black. The white areas stayed orange-brown.\n\nExplain why the white areas did not turn blue-black.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• no chlorophyll so no light absorbed / no photosynthesis (in the white areas) (1)\n• so no glucose made to be converted into starch (1)\nALLOW no starch made / stored (in the white areas) for second mark` },
      { q: `Before testing leaves for starch, a student put a plant in a dark cupboard for 48 hours.\n\nExplain why she put the plant in the dark before the investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• to destarch the leaves / to remove (the starch already) in the leaves (1)\n• (the plant cannot photosynthesise in the dark so) starch is used up (in respiration) / any starch found later must have been made during the investigation (1)` },
      { q: `Tomato growers use greenhouses to grow tomatoes in winter. Some growers burn paraffin in heaters inside their greenhouses.\nBurning paraffin produces carbon dioxide, water and heat.\n\nExplain how using a paraffin heater could increase the yield of tomatoes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• increases temperature / carbon dioxide concentration so these are not limiting / so the rate of photosynthesis increases (1)\n• (so) more glucose made for growth / more biomass (1)\nALLOW both factors named with 'rate of photosynthesis increases' for first mark` },
      { q: `The reactions of photosynthesis are controlled by enzymes in the chloroplasts.\nA student found that the rate of photosynthesis of pondweed decreased sharply between 40 °C and 50 °C.\n\nExplain why the rate decreased.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• enzymes are denatured / active site changes shape (1)\n• (so) substrate no longer fits the active site / fewer enzyme–substrate complexes form (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW enzymes are killed\nIGNORE 'enzymes stop working' alone` },
      { q: `Photosynthesis involves many enzyme-controlled reactions. Each enzyme catalyses only one reaction.\n\nUse the lock and key model to explain why each enzyme catalyses only one reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the active site has a specific shape (1)\n• only one substrate is complementary / fits into the active site (1)\nALLOW enzyme is the lock and substrate is the key\nDO NOT ALLOW active site on the substrate` },
      { q: `This question is about leaves and photosynthesis.\n\nDraw one line from each structure to its role in photosynthesis.\nStructures: chlorophyll; chloroplast; stoma; xylem\nRoles: absorbs light energy; carries water to the leaf; lets carbon dioxide into the leaf; sub-cellular structure where photosynthesis happens`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw lines', markScheme: `chlorophyll → absorbs light energy\nchloroplast → sub-cellular structure where photosynthesis happens\nstoma → lets carbon dioxide into the leaf\nxylem → carries water to the leaf\n• all four correct (2)\n• two or three correct (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one line from a structure` },
      { q: `A grower increased the carbon dioxide concentration in her greenhouse. The yield of cucumbers increased from 32 kg per m² to 40 kg per m² (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in yield.\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (40 − 32) ÷ 32 × 100 / 8 ÷ 32 × 100 (1)\n• 25 (%) (1)\nALLOW 1 mark for 20 (%) (8 ÷ 40 × 100)` },
      { q: `Photosynthesis takes place in two main stages in the chloroplast.\nIn the second stage, carbon dioxide combines with hydrogen to make glucose.\n\nDescribe what happens in the first stage of photosynthesis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• light energy (absorbed by chlorophyll) is used to split water (1)\n• into hydrogen (ions) and oxygen / oxygen is released as a waste product (1)\nIGNORE 'light reaction' alone` },
      { q: `Glucose made in photosynthesis is a store of chemical energy for the plant. Photosynthesis is an endothermic reaction.\n\nExplain why photosynthesis is endothermic.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• energy is taken in / transferred from the surroundings as light (1)\n• (the energy) is stored in glucose / products have more energy than the reactants (carbon dioxide and water) (1)\nALLOW energy needed to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water` },
      { q: `A student measured the oxygen given off by some pondweed using a gas syringe.\nThe pondweed produced 3.6 cm³ of oxygen in 5 minutes (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen production.\nGive the unit.\nRate = ______ unit ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.72 (1)\n• cm³ per minute / cm³/min / cm³ min⁻¹ (1)\nALLOW 0.012 cm³/s for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student collected the oxygen produced by some pondweed in a capillary tube.\nThe pondweed produced 0.45 cm³ of oxygen in 90 seconds (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen production in cm³ per hour.\nRate = ______ cm³ per hour`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.45 ÷ 90 = 0.005 (cm³ per second) (1)\n• × 3600 / conversion of seconds to hours (1)\n• 18 (cm³ per hour) (1)\nALLOW 0.3 cm³ per minute × 60 for the first two marks\nAward 3 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `Amir moves a lamp from 15 cm to 30 cm away from some pondweed. At 15 cm the light intensity is 900 lux (data written for this practice question).\nAmir says: "Doubling the distance from the lamp halves the light intensity."\n\nUse the inverse square law to show that Amir is wrong. Calculate the light intensity at 30 cm.\nLight intensity at 30 cm = ______ lux`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• light intensity ∝ 1/distance² / doubling distance reduces intensity by 2² (1)\n• so intensity falls to a quarter (not a half) (1)\n• 900 ÷ 4 = 225 (lux) (1)\nALLOW 1 mark for 450 lux with no inverse square reasoning` },
      { q: `A student measured the rate of photosynthesis of pondweed at different temperatures.\nThe graph shows that the rate rises slowly from 10 °C to a peak at 35 °C, then falls steeply to almost zero at 50 °C.\n\nExplain the shape of the graph.\nUse ideas about enzymes in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (up to 35 °C) molecules have more (kinetic) energy / more collisions between enzymes and substrates / more enzyme–substrate complexes (1)\n• 35 °C is the optimum temperature (for the enzymes) (1)\n• above 35 °C enzymes denature / active site changes shape so substrate no longer fits (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW enzymes are killed` },
      { q: `Jo investigates how carbon dioxide concentration affects the rate of photosynthesis. She puts pondweed into test tubes containing different concentrations of sodium hydrogencarbonate solution. She counts the bubbles given off in one minute.\n\nFor this investigation, describe:\n• the independent variable\n• the dependent variable\n• one variable she should control.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• independent: concentration of sodium hydrogencarbonate / carbon dioxide (1)\n• dependent: number of bubbles (per minute) / rate of photosynthesis / volume of oxygen (1)\n• control: any one from light intensity / distance of lamp / temperature / length or mass of pondweed / type of pondweed / volume of solution (1)` },
      { q: `A grower measured the rate of photosynthesis of lettuce plants at different light intensities in a greenhouse kept at 20 °C. The carbon dioxide concentration was 0.04%.\nAs light intensity increased, the rate increased until it reached a light intensity of 400 arbitrary units. Above 400 arbitrary units the rate stayed the same.\n\nExplain why the rate stayed the same above 400 arbitrary units.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• light intensity is no longer the limiting factor (1)\n• another factor is limiting / in shortest supply (1)\n• carbon dioxide concentration / temperature is (now) the limiting factor (1)\nALLOW 'something other than light is limiting' for second mark` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of carbon dioxide concentration on photosynthesis using spinach leaf discs. Her results are in the table (data written for this practice question).\nSodium hydrogencarbonate concentration (%) | Mean time for discs to float (min)\n0.0 | did not float in 30 min\n0.2 | 18.0\n0.4 | 11.0\n0.6 | 8.0\n0.8 | 8.0\n\nCalculate the rate of photosynthesis at 0.4% using rate = 1 ÷ time.\nThen explain why the time to float did not change between 0.6% and 0.8%.\nRate at 0.4% = ______ min⁻¹`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.091 (min⁻¹) (1)\nALLOW 0.09 / 0.0909\n• carbon dioxide is no longer the limiting factor (above 0.6%) (1)\n• another factor (e.g. light intensity / temperature) is limiting (1)` },
      { q: `A grower measured the yield of tomatoes in three greenhouses at different temperatures (data written for this practice question).\nTemperature (°C) | Yield (kg per m²)\n20 | 30\n25 | 38\n30 | 39\nHeating the greenhouse from 25 °C to 30 °C costs a lot more money.\n\nEvaluate whether the grower should heat the greenhouse to 25 °C or 30 °C.\nUse the data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• increasing from 20 °C to 25 °C increases yield by 8 kg per m² (1)\n• increasing from 25 °C to 30 °C only increases yield by 1 kg per m² / about 3% (1)\n• conclusion: 25 °C is best because extra heating cost would be more than the value of the extra yield / temperature is no longer the main limiting factor above 25 °C (1)\nALLOW a justified conclusion for 30 °C only if linked to value of crop exceeding heating cost` },
      { q: `Hydrogencarbonate indicator is red at normal carbon dioxide levels. It turns purple when carbon dioxide concentration decreases and yellow when carbon dioxide concentration increases.\nA student put a leaf above hydrogencarbonate indicator in a sealed tube. She set up two tubes. Tube A was left in bright light. Tube B was covered in foil. After 4 hours the indicator in tube A was purple and the indicator in tube B was yellow.\n\nExplain the results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• tube A: the leaf photosynthesised (faster than it respired) (1)\n• so carbon dioxide was taken in / concentration decreased (1)\n• tube B: no photosynthesis in the dark, only respiration so carbon dioxide released / concentration increased (1)` },
      { q: `Palisade cells are found near the upper surface of a leaf. Each palisade cell contains many chloroplasts.\n\nExplain how this helps the leaf to make glucose.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• chloroplasts contain chlorophyll (1)\n• (chlorophyll) absorbs light (energy) (1)\n• more chloroplasts / near upper surface means more light absorbed so more / faster photosynthesis (1)` },
      { q: `A scientist measured the rate of photosynthesis of a plant at different light intensities, at two temperatures: 15 °C and 25 °C. Carbon dioxide concentration was kept the same.\nAt low light intensity the two lines on the graph are the same. At high light intensity both lines level off, but the line for 25 °C levels off at a higher rate than the line for 15 °C.\n\nExplain these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• at low light intensity light is the limiting factor (so temperature has no effect) (1)\n• at high light intensity temperature is the limiting factor (1)\n• (at 25 °C) enzymes work faster / more enzyme–substrate collisions so the rate is higher (1)` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis in pondweed. She used the formula:\nrelative light intensity = 10 000 ÷ distance²\nwhere distance is in cm. Her results are in the table (data written for this practice question).\nDistance (cm) | Relative light intensity | Bubbles per minute\n10 | 100 | 52\n20 | 25 | 15\n25 | ______ | 10\n40 | ______ | 4\n\nCalculate the two missing relative light intensities.\nThen describe the relationship between relative light intensity and the rate of photosynthesis.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 25 cm: 10 000 ÷ 625 = 16 (1)\n• 40 cm: 10 000 ÷ 1600 = 6.25 (1)\nALLOW 6.3 / 6\n• as relative light intensity increases the rate of photosynthesis increases (1)\n• (approximately) directly proportional / doubling light intensity roughly doubles the rate (1)\nALLOW use of data, e.g. light intensity × 4 from 25 to 100 gives about × 3.5 rate, for fourth mark` },
      { q: `A student measured the rate of photosynthesis of pondweed at different temperatures (data written for this practice question).\nTemperature (°C) | Bubbles per minute\n10 | 6\n20 | 13\n30 | 24\n40 | 18\n50 | 2\n\nPlot the results on a graph and draw a suitable line.\nPut temperature on the x-axis and bubbles per minute on the y-axis.`, marks: 4, command: 'Plot', diagram: true, markScheme: `• axes labelled with units: temperature (°C) and bubbles per minute (1)\n• suitable linear scales using more than half of the grid (1)\n• all five points plotted correctly (±½ small square) (1)\nALLOW 4 points correct for this mark only if one error\n• smooth curve through / close to the points, rising to a peak (around 30–35 °C) then falling (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW dot-to-dot ruled lines for the final mark` },
      { q: `Ben investigates how light intensity affects the rate of photosynthesis. He puts pondweed in a beaker of tap water on the window sill in a classroom. He puts a lamp at 10 cm, 20 cm and 30 cm. At each distance he counts the bubbles for 10 seconds, once.\n\nSuggest four improvements to Ben's method. Explain why each improvement would make the results more valid or repeatable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• do it in a dark room / away from the window so the lamp is the only light source / light from the window does not vary (1)\n• use a heat filter / tank of water / LED and measure temperature so temperature is controlled (1)\n• add sodium hydrogencarbonate so carbon dioxide is not limiting (1)\n• count for longer, e.g. 1 minute, to reduce error (1)\n• repeat each distance (at least three times) and calculate a mean (1)\n• use more distances / a wider range to see the pattern (1)\n• leave pondweed to acclimatise for a few minutes at each distance (1)\n• use a gas syringe to measure volume of gas, as bubble sizes vary (1)\nEach mark must have an improvement with a reason` },
      { q: `Pondweed releases bubbles of oxygen when it photosynthesises. You are given pondweed, a lamp, a metre rule, a boiling tube, sodium hydrogencarbonate solution, a stop clock and a thermometer.\n\n* Describe a method to investigate how light intensity affects the rate of photosynthesis in pondweed.\nExplain how you would use your results to reach a conclusion.\nIn your answer you should describe:\n• how you would change the light intensity and measure the rate\n• things you would control to make the results valid.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed method that would give valid results, including how light intensity is changed, how the rate is measured, at least two controlled variables with how they are controlled, AND a clear explanation of how the results are processed (e.g. mean rates, inverse square or graph) to reach a conclusion. The reasoning is clear and logically structured. The information is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A method describing how light intensity is changed and how the rate is measured, with at least one controlled variable, OR a simpler method with some processing of results. There is a line of reasoning with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple method, e.g. moving a lamp and counting bubbles, with little detail. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• put pondweed (cut end up) in a boiling tube of sodium hydrogencarbonate solution\n• place lamp at a set distance, measured with metre rule, e.g. 10 cm\n• leave for a few minutes to acclimatise\n• count bubbles in a set time, e.g. 1 minute, using stop clock\n• repeat at different distances, e.g. 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 cm\n• repeat each distance three times and calculate a mean\nControl\n• same piece / length of pondweed\n• temperature kept constant, checked with thermometer, e.g. water bath / heat filter / LED lamp\n• same concentration of sodium hydrogencarbonate so carbon dioxide is not limiting\n• darkened room so lamp is the only light source\nUsing results\n• calculate rate (bubbles per minute)\n• light intensity ∝ 1/distance² / calculate relative light intensity\n• plot graph of rate against light intensity (or distance)\n• conclusion: rate increases as light intensity increases, until another factor is limiting\n• identify anomalies and check repeatability` }
    ]
  },
  'B3.2': {
    name: 'How do producers get the substances they need?',
    questions: [
      { q: `Potato plants make sucrose in their leaves. The sucrose is stored as starch in the potato tubers under the ground.\n\nName the plant tissue that transports sucrose from the leaves to the tubers.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• phloem (1)\nALLOW sieve tubes\nDO NOT ALLOW xylem` },
      { q: `This question is about how plants take in water.\nWater from the soil enters the root hair cells of a plant through a partially permeable membrane.\n\nName the process by which water enters root hair cells.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• osmosis (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW diffusion / active transport` },
      { q: `A leaf is photosynthesising in bright sunlight.\n\nWhich gas diffuses into the leaf through the stomata?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\ncarbon dioxide      nitrogen      oxygen      water vapour`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• carbon dioxide (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `A student uses a light microscope to look at the lower surface of a leaf. She sees many small pores called stomata. Each stoma has a pair of cells around it.\n\nWhat is the name of the cells that surround each stoma?`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• guard cells (1)\nIGNORE epidermal cells` },
      { q: `A student uses a potometer to investigate how light intensity affects the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. She moves a lamp to different distances from the shoot and records how far an air bubble moves along a capillary tube in 5 minutes.\n\nWhat is the dependent variable in this investigation?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• distance moved by the air bubble (in 5 minutes) / rate of water uptake (1)\nIGNORE water uptake unqualified\nDO NOT ALLOW light intensity / distance of the lamp` },
      { q: `Xylem vessels carry water from the roots to the leaves of a tree. The walls of xylem vessels are thickened with a substance called lignin.\n\nSuggest why it is important that xylem walls contain lignin.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• strengthens the vessel so it does not collapse (under tension) / supports the plant (1)\nALLOW makes it rigid / waterproof\nIGNORE protects the xylem` },
      { q: `This question is about osmosis in plant cells.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nUse words from the list.\nconcentrated      dilute      fully      partially      protein      water\nOsmosis is the movement of ______ molecules across a ______ permeable membrane.\nDuring osmosis, water moves from a more ______ solution to a more ______ solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• water AND partially (1)\n• dilute AND concentrated (in that order) (1)` },
      { q: `Plants take in or give out substances through their roots and leaves.\n\nDraw lines to connect each substance to how it moves into or out of the plant.\nSubstances: carbon dioxide; nitrate ions; oxygen; water\nHow it moves: active transport into root hair cells; diffusion into the leaf through stomata; diffusion out of the leaf through stomata; osmosis into root hair cells`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw lines', markScheme: `carbon dioxide → diffusion into the leaf through stomata\nnitrate ions → active transport into root hair cells\noxygen → diffusion out of the leaf through stomata\nwater → osmosis into root hair cells\n• all four correct (2)\n• two or three correct (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one line from a substance` },
      { q: `A student investigates osmosis using cylinders of potato. She weighs a potato cylinder, leaves it in distilled water for 30 minutes, then blots it dry and weighs it again.\nStarting mass = 2.50 g\nFinal mass = 2.85 g\n\nCalculate the percentage change in mass of the potato cylinder.\nPercentage change in mass = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (2.85 − 2.50) ÷ 2.50 × 100 (1)\n• (+)14 (%) (1)\nALLOW 14.0\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A student leaves a potato cylinder in a concentrated sucrose solution for 30 minutes.\nStarting mass = 3.20 g\nFinal mass = 2.72 g\n\nCalculate the percentage change in mass of the potato cylinder.\nShow whether the change is an increase or a decrease.\nPercentage change in mass = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (2.72 − 3.20) ÷ 3.20 × 100 / 0.48 ÷ 3.20 × 100 (1)\n• −15 (%) (1)\nALLOW 15% decrease / 15% loss for 2 marks\n15 with no sign or indication of decrease = 1 mark` },
      { q: `A class investigates osmosis. Each group cuts five potato cylinders using a cork borer. The cylinders are placed in sucrose solutions of different concentrations. The cylinders are weighed before and after 30 minutes.\nThe teacher asks the class to record percentage change in mass, not change in mass.\n\nExplain why the class records percentage change in mass.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the cylinders have different starting masses (1)\n• percentage change allows a fair comparison between cylinders / groups (1)\nALLOW the change in mass depends on the starting mass` },
      { q: `In an osmosis investigation, potato cylinders are removed from sucrose solutions and weighed.\nThe method says: "Gently blot each cylinder with a paper towel before weighing it."\n\nExplain why the cylinders are blotted before they are weighed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• to remove (excess) solution from the surface of the cylinder (1)\n• so the mass recorded is only the mass of the potato / so the results are accurate / so the mass is not too high (1)\nIGNORE to make it fair` },
      { q: `A student places potato cylinders in sucrose solutions of different concentrations for 1 hour. Her results are shown in the table (data written for this practice question).\nSucrose concentration (mol/dm³) | Percentage change in mass (%)\n0.0 | +18.0\n0.2 | +8.5\n0.4 | −2.0\n0.6 | −11.0\n0.8 | −17.5\nThe concentration of the solution inside the potato cells is the same as the sucrose concentration that gives no change in mass.\n\nEstimate the concentration of the solution inside the potato cells.\nUse the data in the table.\nConcentration = ______ mol/dm³`, marks: 2, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `• between 0.2 and 0.4 mol/dm³ (1)\n• 0.36 mol/dm³ (1)\nALLOW any value from 0.34 to 0.38 for 2 marks\nALLOW value read from a correctly drawn graph line crossing 0%` },
      { q: `A student repeats one part of an osmosis investigation four times. She records the percentage change in mass of potato cylinders in distilled water (data written for this practice question).\nRepeat 1 = 12.1%\nRepeat 2 = 11.8%\nRepeat 3 = 4.3%\nRepeat 4 = 12.4%\n\nCalculate the mean percentage change in mass.\nDo not include any anomalous results.\nMean = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 4.3 (%) is anomalous and left out / (12.1 + 11.8 + 12.4) ÷ 3 (1)\n• 12.1 (%) (1)\n(12.1 + 11.8 + 4.3 + 12.4) ÷ 4 = 10.15 scores 0 marks` },
      { q: `Root hair cells grow out from the surface of plant roots into the soil. Each root hair cell has a long, thin extension.\n\nExplain how the shape of a root hair cell helps the plant take in water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (long thin extension gives a) large surface area (1)\n• so more water can be absorbed by osmosis / water absorbed faster (1)\nALLOW in contact with more soil water for second marking point` },
      { q: `Plants take in substances to help them stay alive.\nRoot hair cells contain many mitochondria.\n\nExplain why root hair cells contain many mitochondria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• mitochondria carry out (aerobic) respiration to make ATP / release energy (1)\n• energy / ATP is needed for active transport of mineral ions (into the root hair cell) (1)\nALLOW named mineral ion e.g. nitrate\nDO NOT ALLOW energy is made / produced` },
      { q: `A gardener grows tomato plants in soil that contains very few nitrate ions. The plants grow slowly and are much smaller than plants grown in soil with added fertiliser.\n\nExplain why the plants grow slowly without nitrate ions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• nitrate is needed to make amino acids / proteins (1)\n• proteins are needed to make new cells / for growth (1)\nALLOW nitrogen for nitrate\nIGNORE nitrate is needed for photosynthesis` },
      { q: `A student uses a potometer to measure the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. An air bubble in the capillary tube moves 42 mm in 5 minutes.\n\nCalculate the rate of water uptake in mm per minute.\nRate = ______ mm per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 42 ÷ 5 (1)\n• 8.4 (mm per minute) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A student paints clear nail varnish on the lower surface of a leaf. When it is dry, she peels it off and looks at it under a light microscope.\nShe counts 18 stomata in a field of view. The area of the field of view is 0.15 mm².\n\nCalculate the number of stomata per mm² of leaf.\nNumber of stomata = ______ per mm²`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 18 ÷ 0.15 (1)\n• 120 (per mm²) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A potometer measures how fast a leafy shoot takes up water. It is often used to estimate the rate of transpiration.\n\nExplain why the rate of water uptake measured by a potometer is not exactly the same as the rate of transpiration.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• some of the water taken up is used by the plant (1)\n• e.g. in photosynthesis / to keep cells turgid / stored in cells (1)\nALLOW not all water taken up is lost by evaporation from the leaves for first marking point` },
      { q: `When setting up a potometer, the instructions say: "Cut the stem of the shoot under water and fit it into the potometer while it is still under water."\n\nExplain why the stem is cut under water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• prevents air entering the xylem / stops air bubbles forming in the xylem (1)\n• air would block the flow of water (up the xylem) so the results would be inaccurate / lower (1)` },
      { q: `Stomata are usually open during the day and closed at night. Each stoma is surrounded by two guard cells.\n\nDescribe how the guard cells open a stoma.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• water moves into the guard cells by osmosis / the guard cells take in water (1)\n• the guard cells become turgid / swell and bend / curve apart, opening the pore (1)\nALLOW guard cells become curved / change shape` },
      { q: `A pot plant is watered and covered with a plastic bag to stop water evaporating from the soil. The whole pot is weighed. It is weighed again 48 hours later (data written for this practice question).\nMass at the start = 486.0 g\nMass after 48 hours = 480.0 g\n\nCalculate the mean rate of water loss from the plant in grams per hour.\nRate = ______ g per hour`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (486.0 − 480.0) ÷ 48 / 6.0 ÷ 48 (1)\n• 0.125 (g per hour) (1)\nALLOW 0.13\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A shop sells bags of lettuce. When lettuce leaves go floppy, they can be made crisp again by putting them in cold water.\nThe cells in the leaves take in water and swell, but they do not burst.\n\nExplain why the lettuce cells do not burst.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• plant cells have a (cellulose) cell wall (1)\n• the cell wall is strong / rigid and stops the cell expanding further (once it is turgid) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW cell membrane stops it bursting` },
      { q: `Aphids are small insects that feed on plants. An aphid pushes its mouthparts into the stem of a plant and feeds on a liquid that is rich in sugar.\n\nName the tissue the aphid feeds from and describe the process that moves the sugar through this tissue.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• phloem (1)\n• translocation: (dissolved) sugars / sucrose are moved from the leaves to other parts of the plant (e.g. roots / growing tips / storage organs) (1)\nALLOW sugar moves in both directions / up and down the plant\nDO NOT ALLOW xylem` },
      { q: `A student counts the stomata on the upper and lower surfaces of a beech leaf (data written for this practice question).\nUpper surface = 40 stomata per mm²\nLower surface = 280 stomata per mm²\n\nCalculate the ratio of stomata on the upper surface to stomata on the lower surface.\nGive your answer in its simplest form.\nRatio = ______ : ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 40 : 280 / 280 ÷ 40 = 7 (1)\n• 1 : 7 (1)\nALLOW 0.14 : 1 for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A student places a potato cylinder in a 0.8 mol/dm³ sucrose solution. After 1 hour the cylinder has lost mass and feels soft and floppy.\n\nExplain why the potato cylinder lost mass.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the sucrose solution is more concentrated than the solution inside the potato cells / the potato cells contain a more dilute solution (1)\n• water moves out of the cells by osmosis (1)\n• through the partially permeable (cell) membrane (1)\nALLOW higher water concentration inside the cells than outside\nDO NOT ALLOW sucrose / sugar moves into or out of the cells` },
      { q: `A student investigates how the concentration of salt solution affects the mass of carrot cylinders. She places one carrot cylinder in each of five salt solutions of different concentrations and weighs each cylinder before and after 45 minutes.\n\nFor this investigation, describe:\n• the independent variable\n• the dependent variable\n• a control variable that should be kept constant.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• independent: concentration of the salt solution (1)\n• dependent: (percentage) change in mass of the carrot cylinder (1)\n• control: any one from: time in solution / volume of solution / size / length / diameter of cylinder / temperature / same carrot (1)\nIGNORE amount of solution` },
      { q: `During the day, the concentration of carbon dioxide in the air spaces inside a leaf is lower than in the air outside the leaf.\n\nExplain why carbon dioxide moves into the leaf during the day.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• carbon dioxide is used up in photosynthesis (by leaf cells) (1)\n• so there is a concentration gradient / higher concentration outside than inside (1)\n• carbon dioxide diffuses into the leaf through the stomata (1)\nALLOW moves down its concentration gradient for second marking point` },
      { q: `After heavy rain, a farmer's field becomes waterlogged. The air spaces in the soil fill with water, so the soil contains very little oxygen.\nThe crop plants in the field take up fewer nitrate ions than usual.\n\nExplain why the plants take up fewer nitrate ions.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• less oxygen for (aerobic) respiration in root (hair) cells (1)\n• so less ATP / energy is released (1)\n• nitrate ions are taken up by active transport, which needs energy / ATP (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW energy is made / produced` },
      { q: `A student uses a potometer to measure water uptake by a leafy shoot. The air bubble moves 42 mm along the capillary tube in 5 minutes.\nThe cross-sectional area of the inside of the capillary tube is 0.80 mm².\nVolume = cross-sectional area × distance moved\n\nCalculate the rate of water uptake in mm³ per minute.\nRate = ______ mm³ per minute`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• volume = 0.80 × 42 = 33.6 (mm³) (1)\n• rate = 33.6 ÷ 5 (1)\n• 6.72 (mm³ per minute) (1)\nALLOW 6.7\nAward 3 marks for correct answer with no working\n8.4 = 1 mark (rate in mm per minute only)` },
      { q: `A student uses a potometer to measure the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot in still air and with a fan blowing air over the leaves (data written for this practice question).\nCondition | Rate of water uptake (mm per minute)\nStill air | 12\nFan on | 27\n\nExplain why the rate of water uptake increased when the fan was switched on.\nUse ideas about transpiration in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• moving air removes water vapour from around the leaf / stomata (1)\n• this keeps a steep concentration gradient (of water vapour) between the inside and outside of the leaf (1)\n• so water vapour diffuses out (through the stomata) faster, so more water is pulled up the xylem / taken up (1)\nALLOW rate more than doubles / increases by 15 mm per minute / 125% as part of answer\nIGNORE wind blows the water out` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the rate of transpiration of a sunflower plant at three temperatures. All other conditions were kept the same (data written for this practice question).\nTemperature (°C) | Rate of water loss (g per hour)\n10 | 4.0\n20 | 7.5\n30 | 12.0\n\nDescribe and explain the effect of temperature on the rate of transpiration.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• as temperature increases the rate of water loss increases (1)\n• (at higher temperature) water molecules have more (kinetic) energy / move faster (1)\n• so water evaporates (from cells inside the leaf) and diffuses out of the stomata faster (1)\nALLOW rate is three times higher at 30 °C than at 10 °C for first marking point` },
      { q: `A student investigates water loss from four leaves of the same type and size. She covers some leaf surfaces with petroleum jelly, which is waterproof. She hangs the leaves up and measures the mass lost after 24 hours (data written for this practice question).\nLeaf | Surface covered | Mass lost (g)\nA | none | 2.4\nB | upper surface only | 2.0\nC | lower surface only | 0.5\nD | both surfaces | 0.1\n\nExplain the results for leaves B and C.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• most stomata are on the lower surface of the leaf (1)\n• (in leaf C) petroleum jelly blocks the stomata so water vapour cannot diffuse out / little transpiration (1)\n• (in leaf B) the stomata on the lower surface are still open, so water loss is only a little less than leaf A (1)\nALLOW few stomata on upper surface for third marking point` },
      { q: `Water travels from the roots to the leaves of a tall tree in xylem vessels.\nXylem vessels are made from dead cells joined end to end. The cells have no end walls and their walls are thickened with lignin.\n\nExplain how the structure of xylem vessels is adapted to their function.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• dead cells / no cytoplasm / hollow, so nothing blocks the flow of water (1)\n• no end walls so they form a continuous tube (from root to leaf) (1)\n• lignin makes the walls strong so the vessels do not collapse / supports the plant (1)\nALLOW water and mineral ions for water` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the concentration of nitrate ions in the soil water around some plant roots and inside the root hair cells (data written for this practice question).\nConcentration of nitrate ions in soil water = 0.5 mmol/dm³\nConcentration of nitrate ions in root hair cells = 10.0 mmol/dm³\n\nUse the data to explain why the plant must use active transport, and not diffusion, to take in nitrate ions.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the concentration inside the root hair cells is higher than in the soil / 20 times higher (1)\n• so nitrate ions must move against the concentration gradient / from low to high concentration (1)\n• active transport uses energy from respiration / ATP; diffusion only moves particles down a concentration gradient (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW energy is made / produced` },
      { q: `A sunflower plant takes in water from the soil. The water travels up the plant and most of it is lost from the leaves.\n\nDescribe how water moves from the soil, through the plant and out into the air.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• water enters root hair cells by osmosis (1)\n• water moves up the stem in the xylem (1)\n• water evaporates from the surfaces of cells inside the leaf / into the air spaces (1)\n• water vapour diffuses out of the leaf through the stomata (1)\nALLOW transpiration stream / water is pulled up by water loss from the leaves for second marking point` },
      { q: `Plants have two transport tissues, xylem and phloem.\n\nComplete the table to compare xylem and phloem.\nColumns: Feature | Xylem | Phloem\nSubstance transported | ______ | ______\nDirection of movement | ______ | ______\nAre the cells living or dead? | ______ | ______\nName of the process | transpiration | ______`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• xylem: water and mineral ions; phloem: (dissolved) sugars / sucrose (1)\n• xylem: upwards / roots to leaves; phloem: up and down / both directions / from leaves to other parts (1)\n• xylem: dead; phloem: living (1)\n• translocation (1)\nEach row needs both cells correct for the mark` },
      { q: `A student wants to investigate how light intensity affects the rate of water uptake by a leafy shoot. She has a potometer, a leafy shoot, a lamp, a metre rule and a stopwatch.\n\nDescribe how she could carry out this investigation so that her results are valid.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• place the lamp at different (measured) distances from the shoot (1)\n• measure the distance the air bubble moves in a set time (e.g. 5 minutes) (1)\n• allow the shoot to adjust to each light intensity before timing (1)\n• reset the bubble (using the reservoir / tap) between readings (1)\n• keep temperature constant e.g. use an LED / heat shield / water screen (1)\n• keep air movement / humidity / same shoot constant (1)\n• repeat at each distance and calculate a mean (1)` },
      { q: `Osmosis is the movement of water through a partially permeable membrane.\nA student wants to find out how the concentration of sucrose solution affects the mass of potato cylinders. She is given a potato, a cork borer, a knife, a ruler, a balance, paper towels, boiling tubes and sucrose solutions of five different concentrations.\n\n* Describe a method the student could use, and explain how she should process her results to find the concentration of the solution inside the potato cells.\nIn your answer you should describe:\n• how she should carry out the investigation\n• things she should do or control to make her results as accurate as possible\n• how she should use her results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed method that would produce valid results, including control of variables, AND a clear explanation of how percentage change in mass is calculated and used, including plotting a graph to find the concentration that gives no change in mass. The reasoning is clear and logically structured. The information is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A workable method with some control of variables AND some processing of results, e.g. calculating percentage change in mass OR plotting a graph. There is a line of reasoning with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Some basic steps of a method OR a simple statement about weighing the cylinders. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• cut cylinders with the cork borer and trim to the same length with the knife and ruler\n• blot and weigh each cylinder on the balance and record the starting mass\n• place one cylinder in each sucrose concentration (including distilled water as 0 mol/dm³)\n• leave for the same time, e.g. 30 minutes / 1 hour\n• remove, blot dry with paper towel and reweigh\nAccuracy and control\n• same volume of solution in each tube\n• same length / diameter of cylinder, from the same potato\n• same temperature / time\n• remove peel / blot each cylinder in the same way\n• repeat each concentration (three times) and calculate a mean, ignoring anomalies\nUsing the results\n• percentage change in mass = (final mass − starting mass) ÷ starting mass × 100\n• percentage change allows comparison because starting masses differ\n• plot percentage change in mass (y-axis) against sucrose concentration (x-axis) and draw a line of best fit\n• where the line crosses 0% change is the concentration of the solution in the potato cells\n• at this concentration there is no net movement of water by osmosis\n• mass gain below this concentration shows water moved in; mass loss above it shows water moved out` }
    ]
  },
  'B3.3': {
    name: 'How are organisms in an ecosystem interdependent?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about a pond ecosystem.\nA pond contains many different species, including frogs, water snails, pondweed and dragonfly larvae.\n\nWhich word describes all the frogs living in the pond?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\ncommunity      ecosystem      habitat      population`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• population (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one word ringed` },
      { q: `A food chain from a grassland is shown in words:\ngrass → grasshopper → shrew → owl\n\nWhich trophic level is the shrew at?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• (trophic level) 3 / third (1)\nALLOW secondary consumer` },
      { q: `Bluebells are plants that grow on the floor of woodlands in the UK. Bluebells grow leaves and flower in early spring, before the leaves on the trees above them have fully opened.\n\nSuggest which resource the bluebells are avoiding competition with the trees for.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• light (1)\nALLOW sunlight\nIGNORE energy / space` },
      { q: `Leaves that fall from trees in autumn are broken down by decomposers in the soil.\n\nName one type of microorganism that is a decomposer.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• bacteria / fungi (1)\nALLOW named fungus e.g. mould / mushroom\nIGNORE earthworms / woodlice (not microorganisms)\nDO NOT ALLOW virus` },
      { q: `Plants need nitrogen to make proteins. Plants cannot use nitrogen gas from the air.\n\nIn what form do plants absorb most nitrogen from the soil?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• nitrate (ions) (1)\nALLOW NO₃⁻ / ammonium ions\nDO NOT ALLOW nitrogen / nitrogen gas / nitrite` },
      { q: `A compost bin contains dead plant material. Microorganisms in the compost bin release carbon dioxide into the air.\n\nName the process in the microorganisms that releases the carbon dioxide.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• (aerobic) respiration (1)\nIGNORE decomposition / decay\nDO NOT ALLOW photosynthesis / combustion` },
      { q: `This question is about how biologists describe the living world.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nPut a ring around each correct answer.\nAll the populations of different species living in a woodland make up a community / ecosystem / organism.\nThe living organisms in a woodland together with the non-living parts, such as soil and water, make up a community / ecosystem / population.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• community (1)\n• ecosystem (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed in a sentence` },
      { q: `Carbon is cycled between living organisms and the air.\n\nDraw lines to connect each process to what it does in the carbon cycle.\nProcesses: photosynthesis; respiration; combustion; feeding\nWhat the process does: releases carbon dioxide when fossil fuels are burned; moves carbon compounds from one organism to another; removes carbon dioxide from the air; releases carbon dioxide from living cells`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw lines', markScheme: `photosynthesis → removes carbon dioxide from the air\nrespiration → releases carbon dioxide from living cells\ncombustion → releases carbon dioxide when fossil fuels are burned\nfeeding → moves carbon compounds from one organism to another\n• all four correct (2)\n• two or three correct (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one line from a process` },
      { q: `This question is about the water cycle.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nPut a ring around each correct answer.\nWater vapour is lost from the leaves of plants by respiration / transpiration / translocation.\nWater vapour in the air cools and condenses / evaporates / precipitates to form clouds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• transpiration (1)\n• condenses (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed in a sentence` },
      { q: `A rocky shore food web is described in words:\nseaweed is eaten by limpets and periwinkles; limpets and periwinkles are eaten by crabs; crabs are eaten by gulls; dog whelks eat limpets.\n\nLimpets and periwinkles are in competition with each other.\nExplain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• they both eat seaweed / have the same food source (1)\n• the seaweed is a limited resource / there is not enough for all of them (1)\nALLOW they may also compete for space on the rocks for 1 mark\nIGNORE they are both eaten by crabs` },
      { q: `A pond food web is described in words:\npondweed and algae are eaten by water snails and mayfly nymphs; mayfly nymphs are eaten by dragonfly larvae and small fish; water snails are eaten by small fish; small fish are eaten by herons.\n\nA chemical spill kills all the mayfly nymphs in the pond.\nDescribe two ways other animals in the pond could be affected.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any 2 from:\n• dragonfly larvae have less food so their numbers decrease (1)\n• small fish have less food / eat more water snails, so the snail population decreases (1)\n• water snails have less competition for pondweed / algae, so their numbers increase (1)\n• herons may have less food if small fish decrease (1)\nALLOW dragonfly larvae may die / move to another pond\nDO NOT ALLOW effects on plants only` },
      { q: `Students measured the biomass at two trophic levels in a field (data written for this practice question).\nBiomass of grass = 2400 g per m²\nBiomass of grasshoppers = 180 g per m²\n\nCalculate the efficiency of biomass transfer from the grass to the grasshoppers.\nUse the equation: efficiency = (biomass transferred to next level ÷ biomass at first level) × 100\nEfficiency = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (180 ÷ 2400) × 100 (1)\n• 7.5 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nALLOW 0.075 for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the biomass of organisms in a lake (data written for this practice question).\nPhytoplankton: 850 kg\nZooplankton (which feed on phytoplankton): 68 kg\n\nCalculate the percentage of the phytoplankton biomass that is transferred to the zooplankton.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (68 ÷ 850) × 100 (1)\n• 8 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A student drew a pyramid of biomass for this food chain:\noak tree → caterpillar → blue tit → sparrowhawk\nThe bar for the oak tree is the widest and the bar for the sparrowhawk is the narrowest.\n\nExplain why the bar for the sparrowhawk is the narrowest.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• biomass is lost at each trophic level / transfer (1)\n• so there is less biomass at each level up the chain / only a small amount reaches the top level (1)\nALLOW energy for biomass\nIGNORE sparrowhawks are small / there are fewer sparrowhawks` },
      { q: `Food webs are models that ecologists use to show feeding relationships in an ecosystem.\n\nGive two reasons why a food web is only a model of the feeding relationships in a real ecosystem.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any 2 from:\n• it does not show the number of each organism / population sizes (1)\n• it does not show the biomass / amount of food transferred (1)\n• it may not show all the species / decomposers / microorganisms (1)\n• diets change with season / age, which is not shown (1)\n• it is a simplified version of reality (1)` },
      { q: `Apple trees produce flowers in spring. Honeybees visit the flowers to collect nectar. Apples only form from flowers that have been pollinated.\nIn one orchard the number of honeybees fell because of disease.\n\nExplain why the number of apples produced in the orchard is likely to fall.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• fewer flowers are pollinated (1)\n• so fewer flowers develop into fruit / apples (1)\nALLOW bees transfer pollen between flowers\nALLOW apple trees depend on bees / interdependence for 1 mark if no other mark given` },
      { q: `A gardener makes compost from grass cuttings and dead leaves. Every week she turns the compost over with a fork. This mixes air into the compost.\n\nExplain why turning the compost makes the material decompose faster.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• more oxygen is available to the decomposers / microorganisms (1)\n• so they can carry out more aerobic respiration / grow and reproduce faster (1)\nALLOW bacteria / fungi for decomposers\nIGNORE it lets heat out` },
      { q: `Fresh meat goes off because it is decomposed by bacteria. Meat kept in a fridge at 4 °C lasts several days longer than meat kept in a room at 22 °C.\n\nExplain why meat in the fridge decomposes more slowly.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• low temperature slows the enzymes (of the bacteria) (1)\n• so the bacteria grow / reproduce / respire more slowly (1)\nIGNORE kills bacteria\nDO NOT ALLOW enzymes are denatured` },
      { q: `A farmer grows wheat. The wheat plants take up nitrate ions from the soil through their roots.\n\nExplain why wheat plants need nitrate ions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• to make amino acids / proteins (1)\n• for growth / to make new cells / enzymes (1)\nALLOW to make DNA / chlorophyll for second mark\nIGNORE for energy / for photosynthesis unqualified` },
      { q: `Clover plants have swellings on their roots called root nodules. The nodules contain nitrogen-fixing bacteria.\nSome farmers grow clover in a field one year and then plough it into the soil before planting a crop the next year.\n\nExplain why this increases the yield of the crop.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the bacteria convert nitrogen from the air into nitrogen compounds / ammonium / nitrates (1)\n• when the clover is decomposed, nitrates are added to the soil that the crop can use to make proteins / grow (1)\nALLOW less fertiliser is needed` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the concentration of carbon dioxide in the air inside a forest during a sunny summer day and during the night.\nThe carbon dioxide concentration was lower during the day than during the night.\n\nExplain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• during the day the trees photosynthesise / take in carbon dioxide (1)\n• the rate of photosynthesis is greater than the rate of respiration in the day, but at night only respiration happens (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW plants only respire at night` },
      { q: `Producers make glucose by photosynthesis. They use glucose to make other large molecules in their biomass.\n\nComplete the table to show the small molecules each large molecule is made from.\nColumns: Large molecule | Small molecules it is made from\nCarbohydrate (e.g. starch) | ______\nProtein | ______\nLipid | ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• carbohydrate: sugars / glucose (1)\n• protein: amino acids AND lipid: fatty acids and glycerol (1)\nAll three correct = 2 marks; one or two correct = 1 mark\nDO NOT ALLOW fatty acids only for lipid` },
      { q: `A rabbit eats grass. The grass contains large molecules such as starch and proteins.\n\nExplain why the rabbit must digest the grass before it can use the molecules to build its own biomass.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• large molecules are too big / insoluble to be absorbed (into the blood / cells) (1)\n• digestion breaks them into small soluble molecules (e.g. sugars, amino acids) that can be absorbed and rebuilt into the rabbit's own molecules (1)\nALLOW enzymes break down large molecules` },
      { q: `A student wanted to find out if a sample of ground-up seeds contained protein.\nThe student added biuret solution to the sample.\n\nDescribe the colour change if protein is present.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (starts) blue (1)\n• (changes to) purple / lilac / violet (1)\nIGNORE pink\nDO NOT ALLOW blue-black` },
      { q: `Ecologists counted aphids and ladybirds on a rose bush. Ladybirds eat aphids (data written for this practice question).\nColumns: Month | Number of aphids | Number of ladybirds\nApril | 120 | 2\nMay | 850 | 6\nJune | 1400 | 19\nJuly | 600 | 31\nAugust | 150 | 12\n\nUse the data to describe how the number of ladybirds depends on the number of aphids.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• ladybird numbers rise after aphid numbers rise / when there is more food (1)\n• ladybird numbers fall (in August) after aphid numbers fall / there is a time lag of about a month (1)\nALLOW ladybirds peak in July, one month after the aphid peak in June\nALLOW use of figures from the table` },
      { q: `A student put 50 g of dead leaves into a mesh bag and buried it in soil. After 8 weeks the student dug up the bag, dried the leaves and found their mass was 32 g.\n\nCalculate the percentage of the mass of leaves that had been decomposed.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (50 − 32) = 18 g AND (18 ÷ 50) × 100 (1)\n• 36 (%) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working\nALLOW 64% for 1 mark (percentage remaining)` },
      { q: `Sea otters live in kelp forests off the coast of North America. Sea otters eat sea urchins. Sea urchins eat kelp, a large seaweed. Many species of fish shelter and breed among the kelp.\nIn the 1800s, sea otters were hunted for their fur and almost disappeared from some areas.\n\nExplain why hunting sea otters caused the number of fish in these areas to decrease.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• fewer sea otters means fewer sea urchins are eaten / sea urchin numbers increase (1)\n• more kelp is eaten (by sea urchins) / kelp decreases (1)\n• fish lose shelter / breeding sites (so fewer fish survive and reproduce) (1)\nALLOW kelp forest is destroyed\nALLOW the populations are interdependent for no extra credit` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the biomass at each trophic level in a lake food chain (data written for this practice question).\nColumns: Organism | Trophic level | Biomass (g per m²)\nPhytoplankton | 1 | 12 000\nZooplankton | 2 | 1080\nSmall fish | 3 | 97\n\nCalculate the efficiency of biomass transfer from the zooplankton to the small fish.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nEfficiency = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (97 ÷ 1080) × 100 (1)\n• 8.98… (1)\n• 9.0 (%) (1)\nAward 3 marks for correct answer of 9.0 with no working\nALLOW 9 for 2 marks (not to 2 s.f.)\nALLOW ECF for correct rounding of an incorrect calculated value` },
      { q: `A young cow eats 100 kg of grass. Over the same time, the biomass of the cow increases by only 4 kg.\n\nExplain why only a small amount of the biomass of the grass becomes biomass in the cow.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any 3 from:\n• some parts of the grass are not eaten / roots are left behind (1)\n• some grass is not digested / is egested as faeces (1)\n• some is excreted as waste / urea in urine (1)\n• (glucose is) used in respiration so carbon is lost as carbon dioxide (1)\n• energy from respiration is used for movement / keeping warm (1)\nIGNORE energy is lost unqualified` },
      { q: `A student investigated whether a fungus releases enzymes onto its food.\nThe student grew the fungus on agar jelly containing starch. After 3 days, the student poured iodine solution over the agar.\nThe agar turned blue-black everywhere except in a clear ring around the fungus.\n\nExplain these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• blue-black shows starch is present (1)\n• the fungus releases an enzyme / amylase / carbohydrase onto / out of its cells (into the agar) (1)\n• the enzyme digests / breaks down starch (into sugars) around the fungus, so no starch is left there / no blue-black colour (1)\nALLOW extracellular digestion\nDO NOT ALLOW the fungus absorbs starch` },
      { q: `Scientists investigated the effect of temperature on the decomposition of dead leaves. They kept samples of leaves at different temperatures for 4 weeks (data written for this practice question).\nColumns: Temperature (°C) | Mass of leaves decomposed (%)\n10 | 12\n20 | 26\n30 | 41\n40 | 46\n60 | 5\n\nDescribe and explain the pattern in the results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• decomposition increases as temperature increases up to 40 °C, then falls sharply at 60 °C (1)\n• higher temperature makes enzymes (of the microorganisms) work faster / microorganisms grow and reproduce faster (1)\n• at 60 °C enzymes are denatured / microorganisms die (1)\nALLOW use of data in the description` },
      { q: `A rabbit eats grass. When the rabbit dies, its body lies on the ground.\n\nExplain how carbon atoms in the rabbit's body could become part of grass again.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• decomposers / bacteria / fungi break down the rabbit's body (1)\n• decomposers respire, releasing carbon dioxide into the air (1)\n• grass takes in carbon dioxide for photosynthesis / to make glucose (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW grass absorbs carbon compounds through its roots` },
      { q: `Leaves contain proteins. When leaves fall, the nitrogen in these proteins can be used again by plants.\n\nExplain how the nitrogen in a dead leaf becomes available to plants.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• decomposers / bacteria / fungi break down the proteins in the leaf (1)\n• releasing ammonium compounds / ammonia into the soil (1)\n• nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium into nitrates, which are absorbed by plant roots (1)\nALLOW 2 marks for decomposers convert proteins to nitrates (which plants absorb)` },
      { q: `Large areas of the Amazon rainforest have been cut down. Scientists have found that rainfall in some cleared areas has decreased.\n\nExplain how cutting down trees can reduce rainfall in that area.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• fewer trees means less transpiration (1)\n• so less water vapour is released into the air (1)\n• so less condensation / fewer clouds form, so less precipitation (1)\nALLOW less evaporation from leaves for transpiration` },
      { q: `Red squirrels are native to the UK. Grey squirrels were introduced from North America in the 1870s. Grey squirrels are larger than red squirrels and can digest acorns more efficiently.\nA survey of one woodland found these numbers (data written for this practice question).\nColumns: Year | Red squirrels | Grey squirrels\n1990 | 240 | 15\n2000 | 110 | 160\n2010 | 20 | 310\n\nExplain why the number of red squirrels fell.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• red and grey squirrels compete for the same food / acorns / nuts / habitat / nest sites (1)\n• grey squirrels are better competitors / get more food because they are larger / digest acorns more efficiently (1)\n• so fewer red squirrels survive and reproduce (as grey squirrels increase) (1)\nALLOW grey squirrels carry squirrelpox virus which kills red squirrels for one mark\nALLOW use of data e.g. red fell from 240 to 20 as grey rose from 15 to 310` },
      { q: `A field can produce 10 000 kg of wheat a year. The wheat can be eaten by people, or fed to cattle that are then eaten by people. About 10% of the biomass of the wheat becomes biomass in the cattle.\n\nExplain why more people can be fed from the field if they eat the wheat rather than the cattle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• eating wheat means one fewer trophic level / transfer (1)\n• biomass is lost at each transfer (e.g. respiration, faeces) (1)\n• only about 1000 kg of biomass would be in the cattle compared with 10 000 kg of wheat (1)\nALLOW a calculation of 10 000 × 0.1 = 1000 kg for the third mark` },
      { q: `Ecologists measured the biomass at three trophic levels in a grassland (data written for this practice question).\ngrass: 4500 g per m²\ngrasshoppers: 410 g per m²\nspiders: 52 g per m²\n\nCalculate the efficiency of biomass transfer from the grass to the grasshoppers and from the grasshoppers to the spiders.\nCompare the two values and suggest a reason for the difference.\nGive your answers to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (410 ÷ 4500) × 100 = 9.11 (%) (1)\n• (52 ÷ 410) × 100 = 12.7 (%) (1)\n• transfer from grasshoppers to spiders is more efficient (1)\n• suggestion: animal tissue / meat is easier to digest than grass / grass contains cellulose that is hard to digest, so less is lost in faeces (1)\nALLOW 9.1 and 13 for the calculation marks if not given to 3 s.f., but max 1 of the first 2 marks\nALLOW ECF for comparison from incorrect values` },
      { q: `A student wants to investigate how temperature affects the rate of decomposition of grass cuttings.\nThe student has bags made of fine mesh, grass cuttings, a balance and incubators that can be set to different temperatures.\n\nDescribe how the student could carry out this investigation.\nIn your answer, include the independent variable, the dependent variable and two variables to control.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• independent variable: temperature, e.g. bags placed in incubators at 10, 20, 30, 40 °C (1)\n• dependent variable: (percentage) loss in (dry) mass of grass after a fixed time (1)\n• any 2 control variables, e.g. same starting mass of grass; same type / age of grass; same time; same water content / moisture; same amount of soil / microorganisms added; same mesh size (2)\nALLOW 1 mark for only one control variable\nALLOW repeat at each temperature and calculate a mean in place of one control variable` },
      { q: `In the 1950s, a disease called myxomatosis killed most of the rabbits in Britain. Ecologists recorded the effects on one area of chalk grassland (data written for this practice question).\nColumns: Year | Rabbits per hectare | Foxes per km² | Height of grass (cm)\n1952 | 30 | 4.0 | 3\n1955 | 2 | 2.5 | 15\n1960 | 8 | 3.0 | 11\n\nRabbits eat grass. Foxes eat rabbits.\nExplain the changes in the numbers of foxes and the height of the grass between 1952 and 1960.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• grass height increased (from 3 cm to 15 cm) between 1952 and 1955 because fewer rabbits were eating / grazing it (1)\n• fox numbers fell (from 4.0 to 2.5 per km²) because they had less food / fewer rabbits to eat (1)\n• between 1955 and 1960 rabbit numbers increased (as survivors reproduced / became resistant), so grass height decreased again (to 11 cm) (1)\n• fox numbers increased again (to 3.0 per km²) as more rabbits / food became available (1)\nALLOW foxes may switch to other prey\nALLOW use of data from the table` },
      { q: `A woodland in the UK contains oak trees, many species of insect, birds, and a large population of soil microorganisms. Each year large amounts of dead leaves fall to the woodland floor.\nSome areas of the woodland are being cleared. The trees are cut down and burned, and the land is used for farming.\n\n* Explain how carbon is cycled in the woodland, and how clearing and burning the trees changes the carbon cycle.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of how carbon moves between the air, producers, consumers and decomposers in the woodland AND a clear explanation of how cutting down and burning the trees increases carbon dioxide in the air and reduces its removal. The reasoning is clear and logically structured. The information is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): An explanation of some processes of the carbon cycle in the woodland AND some description of the effect of clearing or burning trees, OR a detailed explanation of the woodland carbon cycle only. There is a line of reasoning with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple statement about photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition or burning. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nCarbon cycle in the woodland\n• oak trees take in carbon dioxide for photosynthesis\n• carbon is used to make glucose / starch / cellulose / proteins / biomass in the trees\n• carbon passes to insects and birds by feeding / along food chains\n• all organisms respire, releasing carbon dioxide into the air\n• dead leaves, dead organisms and waste are broken down by decomposers / microorganisms\n• decomposers release enzymes and respire, returning carbon dioxide to the air\nClearing and burning the trees\n• burning / combustion releases the carbon stored in wood as carbon dioxide quickly\n• fewer trees, so less photosynthesis / less carbon dioxide removed from the air\n• carbon dioxide concentration in the atmosphere increases\n• fewer dead leaves, so fewer decomposers / less food for woodland food webs\n• crops store less carbon than trees / carbon is removed when crops are harvested\n• more carbon dioxide adds to the greenhouse effect / climate change` }
    ]
  },
  'B3.4': {
    name: 'How are populations affected by conditions in an ecosystem?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about a pond ecosystem.\nA pond contains water snails, frogs, pondweed and many other organisms.\n\nWhat is the name given to all of the water snails of one species living in the pond?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Population ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW: community / habitat / ecosystem` },
      { q: `A group of students is studying a woodland. They want to compare the conditions under the trees with the conditions in a clearing.\n\nName the piece of equipment they should use to measure light intensity.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Light meter ✓\nALLOW: light sensor / lux meter / light-dependent resistor connected to a data logger\nIGNORE: camera` },
      { q: `Kai places a 1 m² quadrat on a lawn. The quadrat is divided into 100 equal squares.\nMoss covers more than half of 37 of the squares. Kai counts only these squares.\n\nCalculate the percentage cover of moss in the quadrat.\n\nPercentage cover = ______ %`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `37 (%) ✓` },
      { q: `Scientists estimated the numbers of rabbits and foxes on an area of farmland (data written for this practice question).\nEstimated number of rabbits = 2400\nEstimated number of foxes = 60\n\nCalculate the ratio of rabbits to foxes.\nGive your answer in its simplest form.\n\nRatio = ______ : 1`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `40 (: 1) ✓\nALLOW: 40 : 1` },
      { q: `Mia wants to estimate the number of daisies on a school field. She plans to place ten quadrats on the field.\nHer teacher tells her to use random numbers to decide where to put each quadrat.\n\nSuggest why Mia should use random numbers to place the quadrats.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `To avoid bias / so she does not choose areas with more (or fewer) daisies / so the sample is representative of the whole field ✓\nIGNORE: to make it fair (on its own) / to make it accurate (on its own)` },
      { q: `This question is about factors that affect a population of limpets on a rocky shore.\nThe students listed some of the factors on the shore:\n• temperature of the rock pools\n• number of crabs that eat limpets\n• salt concentration of the water\n• amount of time exposed to the air at low tide\n\nWhich factor in the list is a biotic factor?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Number of crabs (that eat limpets) ✓` },
      { q: `A student wanted to estimate the number of woodlice living under a hedge.\nShe caught 48 woodlice, marked each one with a small dot of non-toxic paint and released them.\nTwo days later she caught 60 woodlice. 12 of these woodlice were marked.\n\nCalculate the estimated number of woodlice living under the hedge.\nUse the equation: estimated population = (number caught first time × number caught second time) ÷ number marked in second sample\n\nEstimated population = ______ woodlice`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(48 × 60) ÷ 12 ✓\n= 240 ✓\nALLOW: 240 with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW: 2880 seen for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Students counted the number of limpets in five quadrats on a rocky shore (data written for this practice question).\nNumbers of limpets: 14, 11, 16, 3, 13\nOne of the quadrats was placed mostly on sand instead of rock.\n\nCalculate the mean number of limpets per quadrat. Do not include the anomalous result.\n\nMean = ______ limpets per quadrat`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(14 + 11 + 16 + 13) ÷ 4 / 54 ÷ 4 ✓ (anomalous result 3 excluded)\n= 13.5 ✓\nALLOW: 14 (rounded) for 2 marks if working shown\nALLOW: 11.4 for 1 mark (mean including 3)` },
      { q: `Snowshoe hares are eaten by lynx in the forests of Canada. The hare population changes in a cycle that lasts about ten years.\nIn one area, scientists estimated that the hare population fell from 85 000 to 12 000 over three years (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the hare population.\nGive your answer to 1 decimal place.\n\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(85 000 − 12 000) ÷ 85 000 (× 100) / 73 000 ÷ 85 000 ✓\n= 85.9 (%) ✓\nALLOW: 85.88… for 1 mark (not to 1 decimal place)\nDO NOT ALLOW: 14.1 (%)` },
      { q: `Bladder wrack is a seaweed that grows on rocky shores. At low tide the seaweed at the top of the shore is out of the water for much longer than the seaweed lower down the shore.\nVery little seaweed grows at the top of the shore.\n\nExplain why very little seaweed grows at the top of the shore.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any two from:\nIt is exposed to the air for longer so loses more water / dries out ✓\nIt is exposed to larger changes in temperature / higher temperatures in the sun ✓\nIt cannot take in (dissolved) carbon dioxide or mineral ions from seawater for as long, so less photosynthesis / growth ✓\nIGNORE: it gets less water (on its own)` },
      { q: `Bluebells grow on the floor of oak woodlands in the UK. The bluebells grow and flower in spring, before the oak trees have grown a full covering of leaves.\n\nName two resources that the bluebells and the oak trees compete for.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Any two from:\nLight ✓\nWater ✓\nMineral ions / nutrients (from the soil) ✓\nSpace ✓\nIGNORE: food / carbon dioxide / oxygen` },
      { q: `Grey squirrels were brought to Britain from North America in the 1800s. Since then the population of native red squirrels has fallen in most areas where grey squirrels live.\nGrey squirrels are larger than red squirrels and can digest acorns more easily.\n\nExplain why the population of red squirrels has fallen.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Grey squirrels compete with red squirrels for the same food / resources ✓\nGrey squirrels are more successful so red squirrels get less food and fewer survive / reproduce ✓\nALLOW: grey squirrels carry a disease (squirrelpox) that kills red squirrels, for 1 mark\nIGNORE: grey squirrels kill red squirrels` },
      { q: `Scientists recorded the numbers of a predator and its prey over 20 years. A graph of their results shows that both populations go up and down in cycles.\nEach peak in the predator population happens about one year after a peak in the prey population.\n\nExplain why the peak in the predator population happens after the peak in the prey population.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `When there is more prey, there is more food for the predators ✓\nSo more predators survive and reproduce, but it takes time for their young to be born / grow (so the predator numbers rise later) ✓\nIGNORE: predators eat prey (on its own)` },
      { q: `Lynx feed mainly on snowshoe hares. A scientist noticed that a few years after the hare population fell, the lynx population also fell.\n\nExplain why the lynx population fell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `There were fewer hares so less food for the lynx ✓\nSo fewer lynx survived / more lynx starved / fewer lynx reproduced ✓\nALLOW: increased competition between lynx for food` },
      { q: `A group of students investigated how the plants change from the edge of a pond out into a grassy field.\nThey placed a tape measure in a straight line from the water's edge into the field and placed a quadrat every 2 m along the tape.\n\nExplain why they placed quadrats along a line instead of placing them randomly.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `They are investigating how the distribution of plants changes (with distance from the pond) / along a gradient ✓\nPlacing quadrats along a line samples every part of the gradient / shows the change in conditions in one direction ✓\nIGNORE: it is quicker / easier` },
      { q: `Scientists use the capture–recapture method to estimate the number of field voles in a meadow.\n\nGive two assumptions the scientists must make for the estimate to be valid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\nMarked voles mix randomly back into the population (before the second sample) ✓\nThe marks are not lost / do not wash off ✓\nMarking does not affect the chance of a vole surviving / being eaten / being caught again ✓\nNo voles are born, die, move in or move out between the two samples ✓\nThe second sample is caught in the same way / same area as the first ✓` },
      { q: `Ella is estimating the population of garden snails using the capture–recapture method. She plans to mark each snail with a spot of brightly coloured nail varnish on the top of its shell.\nHer teacher suggests that she uses a small spot of non-toxic paint on the underside of the shell instead.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the teacher's method of marking is better.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Non-toxic paint will not harm / kill the snails ✓\nA mark on the underside will not be seen by predators (such as thrushes), so marked snails are not more likely to be eaten ✓\nALLOW: so marking does not affect the snails' survival, for the second mark` },
      { q: `Students estimated the percentage cover of grass in five quadrats in a field (data written for this practice question).\nPercentage cover of grass: 45%, 60%, 30%, 55%, 40%\n\nCalculate the mean percentage cover of grass.\n\nMean percentage cover = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(45 + 60 + 30 + 55 + 40) ÷ 5 / 230 ÷ 5 ✓\n= 46 (%) ✓` },
      { q: `Sam investigated how the number of dandelions changes with distance from a hedge.\nHe placed a quadrat at 1 m intervals along a transect from the hedge and counted the dandelions in each quadrat.\n\nFor this investigation, identify:\n• the independent variable\n• the dependent variable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Independent variable: distance from the hedge ✓\nDependent variable: number of dandelions (in each quadrat) ✓\nIGNORE: light intensity` },
      { q: `White-clawed crayfish are the only crayfish native to UK rivers. Signal crayfish were brought to the UK from North America in the 1970s.\nSignal crayfish carry a disease called crayfish plague. They are not harmed by it, but it kills white-clawed crayfish.\n\nExplain why the population of white-clawed crayfish falls when signal crayfish arrive in a river.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Signal crayfish pass the pathogen / crayfish plague to the white-clawed crayfish, which have no resistance and die ✓\nSignal crayfish compete with white-clawed crayfish for food / space / shelter ✓\nALLOW: signal crayfish eat white-clawed crayfish\nALLOW: new pathogen is a biotic factor that increases deaths, for 1 mark` },
      { q: `A factory releases warm water into a river. Downstream of the factory, the concentration of dissolved oxygen in the water is lower.\nScientists found far fewer mayfly nymphs downstream of the factory than upstream.\n\nExplain why there are fewer mayfly nymphs downstream of the factory.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Mayfly nymphs need (high levels of dissolved) oxygen for (aerobic) respiration ✓\nWith less oxygen fewer nymphs can survive / they move away / they die ✓\nIGNORE: the water is too hot (on its own)` },
      { q: `Rhys wants to estimate the number of clover plants in a field. He has time to place only three quadrats.\n\nExplain why his estimate is unlikely to be accurate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Clover is not spread evenly across the field / numbers vary between quadrats ✓\nA small sample is less likely to be representative / one unusual quadrat has a large effect on the mean ✓\nALLOW: he should use more quadrats (and calculate a mean) for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Students set pitfall traps in a woodland to catch ground beetles. A pitfall trap is a container sunk into the soil so that its rim is level with the surface.\nThey left the traps overnight. In the morning, some of the beetles had been eaten by other animals in the traps.\n\nSuggest two ways the students could improve the method so that fewer beetles are harmed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\nCheck / empty the traps more often ✓\nPut a cover / lid / stone (raised) over the trap to keep out rain and larger predators ✓\nPut leaves / soil in the trap to give shelter ✓\nMake small drainage holes so the beetles do not drown ✓\nRelease beetles back where they were caught ✓\nIGNORE: use more traps` },
      { q: `Scientists measured soil moisture and counted the number of plant species in quadrats along a transect from a river bank (data written for this practice question).\nDistance from river (m): 0, 5, 10, 15, 20\nSoil moisture (%): 62, 48, 35, 24, 18\nNumber of plant species: 4, 7, 11, 13, 14\n\nDescribe the relationship between soil moisture and the number of plant species.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `As soil moisture decreases, the number of plant species increases / negative correlation ✓\nUse of data, e.g. 62% moisture has 4 species but 18% moisture has 14 species ✓\nALLOW: the increase gets smaller as moisture falls (e.g. only one more species from 24% to 18%)` },
      { q: `Heather plants grow on moorland. There are very few heather plants on a busy footpath that crosses the moor, but many heather plants a few metres away from the path.\n\nSuggest two reasons why very few heather plants grow on the footpath.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\nTrampling damages / breaks the stems and leaves (so less photosynthesis) ✓\nTrampling compacts the soil ✓\nSo less air / oxygen in the soil for roots / water drains less well / roots cannot grow easily ✓\nSeedlings are crushed before they can grow ✓` },
      { q: `Students measured the light intensity at each quadrat along a transect from inside a wood into an open field. They took the measurements over a whole day: the first quadrat at 9 am and the last quadrat at 3 pm.\n\nExplain why their light intensity results may not be valid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Light intensity changes during the day / with weather / cloud cover ✓\nSo differences between quadrats may be caused by time of day and not by position along the transect ✓\nALLOW: measurements should be taken at the same time / as quickly as possible, for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Students estimated the number of buttercups in a field that measures 30 m by 40 m (data written for this practice question).\nThey placed ten quadrats randomly. Each quadrat measured 0.5 m × 0.5 m.\nNumber of buttercups in each quadrat: 4, 7, 2, 0, 5, 6, 3, 8, 1, 4\n\nCalculate the estimated number of buttercups in the whole field.\n\nEstimated number = ______ buttercups`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Mean = 40 ÷ 10 = 4 (buttercups per quadrat) ✓\nArea of quadrat = 0.25 m² so 4 ÷ 0.25 = 16 per m² / area of field = 1200 m² ✓\n16 × 1200 = 19 200 ✓\nALLOW: 4 × (1200 ÷ 0.25) = 4 × 4800 = 19 200\nALLOW: 4800 (no. of quadrats in field) seen for 1 mark` },
      { q: `Water fleas (Daphnia) live in ponds. A scientist took ten 100 cm³ samples of water from different places in a pond. The mean number of water fleas in each sample was 6.2.\nThe volume of water in the pond is 25 m³. 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³.\n\nCalculate the estimated number of water fleas in the pond.\nGive your answer in standard form.\n\nEstimated number = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Volume of pond = 25 000 000 cm³ / 2.5 × 10⁷ cm³ ✓\n(25 000 000 ÷ 100) × 6.2 = 1 550 000 ✓\n= 1.55 × 10⁶ ✓\nALLOW: 6.2 ÷ 100 = 0.062 per cm³ for the first mark\nALLOW: 1 550 000 for 2 marks (not in standard form)` },
      { q: `Scientists used capture–recapture to study a population of great crested newts in a pond. They estimate that there are 500 newts in the pond.\nThey caught and marked 80 newts in the first sample. In the second sample they caught 75 newts.\n\nCalculate how many of the newts in the second sample they would expect to be marked.\nUse the equation: estimated population = (number caught first time × number caught second time) ÷ number marked in second sample\n\nNumber of marked newts = ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Correct rearrangement: number marked = (number caught first time × number caught second time) ÷ estimated population ✓\n(80 × 75) ÷ 500 ✓\n= 12 ✓\nALLOW: 6000 ÷ 500 for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student used capture–recapture to estimate the population of beetles in a garden. She marked the beetles with water-based paint. It rained heavily between the first and second samples, and some of the paint washed off.\n\nExplain how this would affect her estimate of the population size.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Fewer marked beetles would be recognised / counted in the second sample ✓\nSo the number marked in the second sample (the divisor) is smaller ✓\nSo the estimate of the population would be too high / an overestimate ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW: estimate is too low` },
      { q: `Scientists recorded the numbers of a species of owl and the voles they eat over twelve years (data written for this practice question).\nThe vole population peaked in years 2, 6 and 10. The owl population peaked in years 3, 7 and 11.\nAt each peak there were about 4000 voles but only about 40 owls.\n\nDescribe and explain the pattern shown by these data.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Both populations rise and fall in a regular cycle (every 4 years) / the owl peaks are one year after the vole peaks ✓\nMore voles means more food so more owls survive and breed, then more owls eat more voles so the vole population falls ✓\nFewer voles means less food so owl numbers fall, which allows the vole population to rise again ✓\nALLOW: fewer owls than voles because energy / biomass is lost at each trophic level` },
      { q: `Sea otters live in kelp forests off the coast of North America. Sea otters eat sea urchins, and sea urchins eat kelp (a large seaweed).\nIn the past, sea otters were hunted for their fur and almost disappeared from some areas.\n\nExplain how hunting the sea otters affected the kelp in these areas.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Fewer sea otters so fewer sea urchins were eaten / sea urchin population increased ✓\nMore sea urchins ate more kelp ✓\nSo the amount of kelp / kelp forest decreased ✓\nALLOW: other species living in the kelp forest also decrease (loss of habitat) as alternative to third mark only if first two marks given` },
      { q: `Students used a transect up a rocky shore. They counted two species of barnacle in quadrats at different heights above the low tide mark (data written for this practice question).\nHeight above low tide mark (m): 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5\nNumber of species A: 0, 2, 18, 46, 30\nNumber of species B: 52, 40, 21, 3, 0\n\nCompare the distributions of species A and species B along the transect.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Species A is found higher up the shore / species B is found lower down the shore ✓\nSpecies A is most numerous at 2.0 m and species B is most numerous at 0.5 m ✓\nBoth species are found together between 1.0 m and 2.0 m / they overlap in the middle of the shore ✓\nALLOW: as height increases species B decreases but species A increases then decreases` },
      { q: `Woodlice live under logs and stones in a garden. A student wants to estimate the size of the woodlouse population using pitfall traps and the capture–recapture method.\n\nDescribe the method she should use.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Collect a sample of woodlice (using pitfall traps), count them and mark them (in a harmless way) ✓\nRelease them where they were caught and leave time for them to mix back into the population ✓\nCollect a second sample (in the same way and place), count the total and count how many are marked ✓\nALLOW: use of the equation (first sample × second sample ÷ number marked) for the third mark if second sample idea is given` },
      { q: `Wood anemone is a small plant that grows on the floor of woodlands. Scientists found that the percentage cover of wood anemone is lowest in the centre of a dense wood and highest near the edge.\n\nExplain why the percentage cover of wood anemone is lowest in the centre of the wood.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The tree canopy is denser in the centre so the light intensity is lower ✓\nSo the rate of photosynthesis is lower ✓\nSo the plants make less glucose / biomass so fewer plants grow / survive ✓\nALLOW: more competition for light (from the trees) for first mark` },
      { q: `A student carried out a transect from a hedge into a field. She found that as distance from the hedge increased, both the light intensity and the number of daisies increased.\nShe concluded: "Daisies grow best where the light intensity is highest."\n\nEvaluate her conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `The data shows a positive correlation between light intensity and number of daisies (which supports the conclusion) ✓\nA correlation does not show that light is the cause / other factors also change with distance from the hedge ✓\nExample of another factor, e.g. soil moisture / trampling / competition with hedge plants / soil pH ✓\nALLOW: only one transect so results may not be repeatable / should repeat the transect` },
      { q: `Students investigated the plants growing under a large tree in a park. They placed quadrats at 2 m intervals along a transect from the tree trunk out into open grassland. They measured the light intensity and estimated the percentage cover of grass and ivy in each quadrat (data written for this practice question).\nDistance from trunk (m): 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10\nLight intensity (lux): 800, 1500, 3200, 6500, 9800, 10 200\nPercentage cover of grass (%): 5, 15, 35, 70, 90, 95\nPercentage cover of ivy (%): 80, 60, 25, 5, 0, 0\n\nDescribe the distribution of grass and ivy along the transect and explain how light intensity could cause this distribution.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Grass percentage cover increases with distance from the trunk (from 5% to 95%) ✓\nIvy percentage cover decreases with distance from the trunk / ivy is not found beyond 8 m ✓\nGrass needs a high light intensity for photosynthesis / grows faster in the open, so it is more common where light intensity is high ✓\nIvy can survive in low light / shade, but in the open it is outcompeted by grass (for light, water or space) ✓\nALLOW: correct use of data for light intensity, e.g. light increases from 800 to 10 200 lux` },
      { q: `Students estimated the number of grasshoppers in a meadow using capture–recapture.\nOn Monday they caught 64 grasshoppers with sweep nets, marked them and released them.\nOn Tuesday they caught 50 grasshoppers. 8 of these were marked.\n\nCalculate the estimated number of grasshoppers in the meadow and suggest two reasons why this estimate may not be accurate.\nUse the equation: estimated population = (number caught first time × number caught second time) ÷ number marked in second sample`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(64 × 50) ÷ 8 ✓\n= 400 (grasshoppers) ✓\nAny two reasons from (max 2):\nOne day may not be long enough for marked grasshoppers to mix back into the population ✓\nGrasshoppers may have flown / moved into or out of the meadow ✓\nMarked grasshoppers may be more easily seen by predators / marks may rub off ✓\nThe second sample is small / only one recapture was done ✓\nSome grasshoppers may have died or hatched ✓` },
      { q: `Himalayan balsam is a plant that was brought to the UK in the 1800s. It now grows along many river banks.\nHimalayan balsam grows up to 2 m tall and grows faster than most native plants. Each plant can produce up to 800 seeds. It dies back in winter, leaving bare soil on the river bank.\n\nExplain how the spread of Himalayan balsam can affect the populations of native plants and of the insects that feed on them.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Himalayan balsam is taller so it shades native plants / competes with them for light ✓\nNative plants photosynthesise less so fewer survive / grow / reproduce (so their populations fall) ✓\nIt also competes for space / water / mineral ions (and produces many seeds so spreads quickly) ✓\nInsects that feed on native plants have less food so their populations fall ✓\nALLOW: bare soil in winter leads to erosion of the bank / loss of habitat` },
      { q: `* A group of students wants to find out how the distribution of a plant species, plantain, changes from a shaded area under a row of trees out into an open sports field. They think that light intensity affects the distribution.\n\nDescribe a method the students could use to collect data to test their idea.\nIn your answer you should describe:\n• how they would collect the data\n• how they would make sure their results are valid and repeatable.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Gives a detailed and logically ordered method using a transect, with measurements of both plantain abundance and light intensity, AND clear steps to make the results valid and repeatable.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes a transect or quadrat method that measures plantain abundance and light intensity, with at least one step to improve validity or repeatability.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives a simple method for counting plantain OR measuring light intensity.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nCollecting data\n• lay a tape measure in a straight line from the base of the trees into the open field\n• place a quadrat at regular intervals, e.g. every 1 m or 2 m, along the tape\n• count the number of plantain plants in each quadrat or estimate percentage cover (using a grid quadrat)\n• measure light intensity at each quadrat using a light meter held at the same height\n• record results in a table of distance, light intensity and number / percentage cover\nValidity and repeatability\n• take all light readings at the same time of day / as quickly as possible / in the same weather\n• use the same size quadrat each time\n• use a rule for plants partly inside the quadrat, e.g. count only if more than half is inside\n• repeat with two or more transects at different places along the row of trees and calculate a mean for each distance\n• identify plantain carefully, e.g. using an identification key\n• measure other factors that could change, e.g. soil moisture or trampling, to check they are not causing the pattern\n• plot a graph of plantain against light intensity to look for a correlation` }
    ]
  },
  'B4.1': {
    name: 'What happens during cellular respiration?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about cellular respiration.\nAll living cells respire. The energy transferred by respiration is used to make a molecule that cells use for processes such as muscle contraction and active transport.\n\nName this molecule.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `ATP ✓\nALLOW: adenosine triphosphate\nDO NOT ALLOW: ADP / glucose / energy` },
      { q: `Priya runs a 200 m race as fast as she can. Her leg muscle cells cannot get enough oxygen, so some of their respiration is anaerobic.\n\nName the product of anaerobic respiration in Priya's muscle cells.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Lactic acid ✓\nALLOW: lactate\nDO NOT ALLOW: ethanol / carbon dioxide` },
      { q: `A student sets up a respirometer to measure the rate of respiration of maggots. The maggots are on a wire gauze platform in a boiling tube. Below the gauze is a layer of soda lime. A capillary tube containing a drop of coloured liquid is attached to the bung.\n\nWhat is the purpose of the soda lime?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `To absorb carbon dioxide (given out by the maggots) ✓\nALLOW: removes CO₂\nDO NOT ALLOW: absorbs oxygen` },
      { q: `A baker mixes flour, water, sugar and yeast to make bread dough. The dough is left in a warm place for one hour and it gets bigger.\n\nWhich gas, made by the yeast, causes the dough to rise?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Carbon dioxide ✓\nALLOW: CO₂\nDO NOT ALLOW: oxygen / ethanol` },
      { q: `Liver cells carry out many reactions that need a lot of energy. A scientist looked at liver cells with an electron microscope and saw that each cell contained over 1000 of one type of organelle.\n\nName the organelle where most of the reactions of aerobic respiration take place.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Mitochondria ✓\nALLOW: mitochondrion\nIGNORE: spelling if recognisable\nDO NOT ALLOW: ribosome / nucleus / chloroplast` },
      { q: `A student investigated the rate of respiration of yeast at 30 °C. She put a tube of yeast in glucose solution into a water bath at 30 °C. She waited 10 minutes before she started to collect the gas.\n\nSuggest why she waited 10 minutes before starting to collect the gas.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `So the yeast mixture reached (the temperature of the water bath /) 30 °C ✓\nALLOW: to allow the yeast to acclimatise / equilibrate / start respiring at a steady rate\nIGNORE: to make it a fair test (on its own)` },
      { q: `This question is about aerobic respiration.\nAerobic respiration happens in the cells of animals, plants and many microorganisms.\n\nComplete the word equation for aerobic respiration.\nglucose + ______ → ______ + water`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `oxygen ✓\ncarbon dioxide ✓\nALLOW: O₂ and CO₂\nIGNORE: energy / ATP added to the products` },
      { q: `A student investigated respiration in yeast. He mixed yeast with glucose solution in a test tube and connected the tube to a gas syringe (data written for this practice question).\nAfter 4 minutes, 36 cm³ of carbon dioxide had collected in the gas syringe.\n\nCalculate the mean rate of carbon dioxide production.\nRate = ______ cm³ per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `36 ÷ 4 ✓\n= 9 (cm³ per minute) ✓\nALLOW: 9 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student used a respirometer to measure the rate of respiration of woodlice. As the woodlice used oxygen, a drop of coloured liquid moved along a capillary tube towards them (data written for this practice question).\nThe drop moved 18 mm in 6 minutes.\n\nCalculate the rate at which the drop moved.\nRate = ______ mm per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `18 ÷ 6 ✓\n= 3 (mm per minute) ✓\nALLOW: 3.0` },
      { q: `Anaerobic respiration happens in many different organisms. The products are not the same in all of them.\n\nDraw lines to connect each organism or cell to the products of its anaerobic respiration.\nOrganism or cell: Human muscle cell; Yeast; Bacteria used to make yoghurt; Root cell of a plant in waterlogged soil\nProducts: Lactic acid; Ethanol and carbon dioxide`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `Human muscle cell – lactic acid; Yeast – ethanol and carbon dioxide; Bacteria used to make yoghurt – lactic acid; Plant root cell – ethanol and carbon dioxide\nAll 4 correct ✓✓\n2 or 3 correct ✓\nMore than one line from an organism or cell – that line is wrong` },
      { q: `Heart muscle cells contract about 70 times every minute, all day and all night. ATP cannot be stored in large amounts in cells.\n\nExplain why respiration must take place all the time in heart muscle cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Heart muscle cells need a constant supply of energy / ATP for contraction ✓\nATP is used up quickly / cannot be stored, so must be made continuously (by respiration) ✓\nALLOW: if respiration stopped the cells would run out of ATP / die` },
      { q: `A student soaked some pea seeds in water so they started to germinate. She put the germinating peas into a vacuum flask and pushed a thermometer into the peas. She put boiled peas into a second vacuum flask.\nAfter 3 days the temperature of the germinating peas had risen. The temperature of the boiled peas had not changed.\n\nExplain why the temperature of the germinating peas rose.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The germinating peas are alive / respiring (the boiled peas are dead) ✓\nRespiration is exothermic / transfers energy to the surroundings as heat ✓\nIGNORE: respiration produces energy\nDO NOT ALLOW: respiration makes heat energy from nothing` },
      { q: `Germinating peas were kept in a vacuum flask with a thermometer (data written for this practice question).\nTemperature at the start: 21.0 °C\nTemperature after 3 days: 27.5 °C\n\nCalculate the mean rate of temperature rise.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nRate = ______ °C per day`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(27.5 − 21.0) ÷ 3 OR 6.5 ÷ 3 ✓\n= 2.2 (°C per day) ✓\nALLOW: 2.17 / 2.167 for 1 mark (not 2 significant figures)` },
      { q: `Lena investigated anaerobic respiration in yeast. She boiled some glucose solution and let it cool. She then added yeast and poured a layer of oil on top of the mixture.\n\nExplain why she poured a layer of oil on top of the mixture.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `To stop oxygen (from the air) entering / dissolving in the mixture ✓\nSo the yeast respires anaerobically / so respiration is anaerobic only ✓\nALLOW: boiling removes the dissolved oxygen and the oil stops it coming back` },
      { q: `A student set up two tubes, each containing glucose solution. He added live yeast to tube A and the same mass of boiled yeast to tube B. Both tubes were kept at 30 °C.\nGas bubbles were made in tube A. No bubbles were made in tube B.\n\nSuggest why no bubbles were made in tube B.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Boiling killed the yeast / the yeast was dead ✓\nThe enzymes (for respiration) were denatured so respiration / production of carbon dioxide could not happen ✓\nALLOW: the active sites of the enzymes changed shape` },
      { q: `A student measured the volume of carbon dioxide made by yeast in 5 minutes. She repeated the experiment four times (data written for this practice question).\nVolume of carbon dioxide (cm³): 22, 25, 41, 19\n\nIdentify the anomalous result and calculate the mean volume of carbon dioxide, ignoring the anomalous result.\nMean = ______ cm³`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Anomalous result is 41 (cm³) ✓\nMean = (22 + 25 + 19) ÷ 3 = 22 (cm³) ✓\nALLOW: ECF for a mean of the three values that were not identified as anomalous` },
      { q: `Root hair cells take in nitrate ions by active transport. Muscle cells contract to move the body.\nBoth of these processes use ATP made by respiration.\n\nGive two other processes in living cells that use ATP.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\nSynthesis / making of large molecules (e.g. proteins / starch / DNA / lipids) ✓\nCell division / growth ✓\nTransmission of nerve impulses ✓\nKeeping body temperature constant (in mammals and birds) ✓\nIGNORE: active transport / muscle contraction (given in the stem)\nIGNORE: respiration / breathing` },
      { q: `This question is about aerobic respiration.\nThe balanced symbol equation for aerobic respiration is incomplete.\n\nComplete the balanced symbol equation for aerobic respiration.\nC₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → ______ + ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `6CO₂ ✓\n6H₂O ✓\nALLOW: in either order\nALLOW: 1 mark for CO₂ + H₂O (correct formulae, not balanced)\nDO NOT ALLOW: Co2 / CO² / h2o` },
      { q: `Scientists estimate how many ATP molecules are made when one glucose molecule is respired (data written for this practice question).\nAerobic respiration: 32 molecules of ATP per glucose molecule\nAnaerobic respiration: 2 molecules of ATP per glucose molecule\n\nCalculate how many times more ATP is made per glucose molecule by aerobic respiration than by anaerobic respiration.\nAnswer = ______ times`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `32 ÷ 2 ✓\n= 16 (times) ✓` },
      { q: `When a muscle cell respires anaerobically it transfers much less energy from each glucose molecule than when it respires aerobically.\n\nExplain why anaerobic respiration transfers less energy than aerobic respiration.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Glucose is only partly broken down / is not completely broken down (to carbon dioxide and water) ✓\nLactic acid still contains (a lot of chemical) energy / stored energy ✓\nALLOW: ethanol still contains energy (for yeast / plants)` },
      { q: `After heavy rain a field of wheat is flooded. The soil stays waterlogged for two weeks. The leaves of the wheat plants turn yellow because the plants cannot take in enough mineral ions.\n\nExplain why the roots cannot take in enough mineral ions when the soil is waterlogged.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Waterlogged soil has little oxygen so root cells respire anaerobically / cannot respire aerobically ✓\nLess ATP / energy is made so less active transport (of mineral ions) ✓\nALLOW: ethanol (made by anaerobic respiration) is toxic and damages / kills root cells for second mark` },
      { q: `Sam investigated the effect of temperature on the rate of respiration of yeast (data written for this practice question).\nRate of carbon dioxide production at 35 °C: 8.0 cm³ per minute\nRate of carbon dioxide production at 55 °C: 0.4 cm³ per minute\n\nExplain why the rate was very low at 55 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Enzymes (that control respiration) are denatured (at high temperature) ✓\nThe active site changes shape so the substrate no longer fits / fewer enzyme–substrate complexes form ✓\nALLOW: yeast cells are killed\nDO NOT ALLOW: enzymes are killed` },
      { q: `A baker measured the volume of some bread dough before and after leaving it to rise in a warm place for 60 minutes (data written for this practice question).\nVolume at the start: 150 cm³\nVolume after 60 minutes: 330 cm³\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the volume of the dough.\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(330 − 150) ÷ 150 × 100 OR 180 ÷ 150 × 100 ✓\n= 120 (%) ✓\nALLOW: 220% for 1 mark (volume after as a percentage of volume at start)` },
      { q: `A respirometer contains germinating seeds and soda lime in a sealed boiling tube. A capillary tube with a drop of coloured liquid is attached. During the experiment the drop moves towards the seeds.\n\nExplain why the drop moves towards the seeds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `The seeds use / take in oxygen (for aerobic respiration) and the carbon dioxide they give out is absorbed by the soda lime ✓\nSo the volume of gas / pressure in the tube decreases (and the drop is pulled towards the seeds) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW: the seeds give out less carbon dioxide than they use` },
      { q: `A wine maker adds yeast to grape juice in a sealed tank. The yeast ferments the sugar in the juice. After a few weeks bubbles stop being produced, even though the tank is kept at the same temperature.\n\nSuggest two reasons why fermentation stops.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\nThe sugar / glucose (substrate) is used up ✓\nThe concentration of ethanol becomes high enough to kill the yeast / ethanol is toxic to yeast ✓\nThe yeast cells die (for another named reason, e.g. lack of nutrients) ✓\nIGNORE: oxygen runs out` },
      { q: `A scientist used a respirometer to measure oxygen uptake by 2.0 g of maggots (data written for this practice question).\nThe maggots took in 0.24 cm³ of oxygen in 10 minutes.\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen uptake per gram of maggots.\nRate = ______ cm³ per gram per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.24 ÷ 10 ÷ 2.0 OR 0.024 ÷ 2.0 OR 0.12 ÷ 10 ✓\n= 0.012 (cm³ per gram per minute) ✓\nALLOW: 1.2 × 10⁻²` },
      { q: `This question is about aerobic and anaerobic respiration.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nPut a ring around the correct options.\nAerobic respiration needs carbon dioxide / oxygen and transfers more / less energy than anaerobic respiration.\nMost of the reactions of aerobic respiration take place in the mitochondria / nucleus.\nAnaerobic respiration in yeast produces ethanol / lactic acid and carbon dioxide.`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `oxygen AND more ✓\nmitochondria ✓\nethanol ✓` },
      { q: `A student used a respirometer to measure the volume of oxygen taken in by woodlice (data written for this practice question).\nThe capillary tube has a radius of 0.5 mm. The drop of coloured liquid moved 24 mm in 10 minutes.\nVolume of a cylinder = πr²l, where r is the radius and l is the length.\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen uptake in mm³ per minute.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nRate = ______ mm³ per minute`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Volume = π × 0.5² × 24 ✓\n= 18.8 (mm³) / 18.85 (mm³) ✓\nRate = 18.85 ÷ 10 = 1.9 (mm³ per minute) ✓\nALLOW: 6π for the volume\nALLOW: ECF from an incorrect volume for the third mark\nALLOW: 1.88 / 1.885 for 2 marks (not 2 significant figures)` },
      { q: `Sam investigated the effect of temperature on the rate of respiration in yeast. He measured the volume of carbon dioxide collected in a gas syringe in 5 minutes (data written for this practice question).\nAt 20 °C: 15 cm³\nAt 35 °C: 40 cm³\n\nCalculate the rate of carbon dioxide production at 35 °C. Explain why the rate is higher at 35 °C than at 20 °C.\nRate = ______ cm³ per minute`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `40 ÷ 5 = 8 (cm³ per minute) ✓\nRespiration is controlled by enzymes / respiration is a series of enzyme-controlled reactions ✓\nAt higher temperature molecules have more kinetic energy so there are more (successful) collisions between enzymes and substrate / more enzyme–substrate complexes form per second ✓\nALLOW: 35 °C is closer to the optimum temperature of the enzymes for the third mark` },
      { q: `Jamal wants to find out how temperature affects the rate of respiration of maggots. He puts the same number of maggots into a respirometer in a water bath. He measures how far a drop of coloured liquid moves along a capillary tube in 5 minutes at 10 °C, 15 °C, 20 °C and 25 °C.\n\nFor this investigation, identify:\n• the independent variable\n• the dependent variable\n• one variable that should be kept the same.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Independent variable: temperature (of the water bath) ✓\nDependent variable: distance moved by the drop (in 5 minutes) ✓\nControl variable: any one from: mass / number / size / age of maggots; mass of soda lime; time allowed; diameter of capillary tube ✓\nIGNORE: amount of maggots\nDO NOT ALLOW: number of maggots if given as the independent variable` },
      { q: `Ben is a 400 m runner. Near the end of a race his leg muscles start to hurt and feel tired. After the race he keeps breathing deeply and quickly for several minutes.\n\nExplain why Ben's muscles feel tired near the end of the race and why he keeps breathing deeply after the race.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Oxygen cannot be supplied to the muscles fast enough so they respire anaerobically ✓\nLactic acid builds up (in the muscles) causing fatigue / pain ✓\nExtra oxygen is needed after the race to break down the lactic acid ✓\nALLOW: to repay the oxygen debt for the third mark\nIGNORE: to get his breath back` },
      { q: `Yeast can respire aerobically when oxygen is present, and anaerobically when oxygen is absent.\n\nCompare aerobic respiration with anaerobic respiration in yeast.\nGive three differences.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Any three from:\nAerobic needs oxygen but anaerobic does not ✓\nAerobic produces water but anaerobic does not / anaerobic produces ethanol but aerobic does not ✓\nAerobic transfers more energy / makes more ATP per glucose molecule ✓\nAerobic takes place (mainly) in mitochondria, anaerobic in the cytoplasm ✓\nGlucose is completely broken down in aerobic respiration but only partly broken down in anaerobic ✓\nIGNORE: both produce carbon dioxide (this is a similarity)\nEach point must be comparative` },
      { q: `A student measured the total volume of carbon dioxide collected from yeast in glucose solution at 25 °C (data written for this practice question).\nTime (min): 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12\nTotal volume of CO₂ (cm³): 0, 6, 13, 19, 25, 28, 29\n\nCalculate the mean rate of carbon dioxide production in the first 8 minutes. Suggest one reason why the rate decreased after 8 minutes.\nRate = ______ cm³ per minute`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `25 ÷ 8 ✓\n= 3.1 (cm³ per minute) ✓\nALLOW: 3.125 / 3.13\nAny one reason from: glucose / substrate is being used up; ethanol builds up and is toxic to / inhibits the yeast; yeast cells start to die ✓\nIGNORE: yeast gets tired / temperature changes` },
      { q: `A sperm cell has a tail that moves it towards an egg cell. The middle section of a sperm cell, just behind the head, is packed with mitochondria.\n\nExplain why the sperm cell has many mitochondria.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Mitochondria are where (aerobic) respiration takes place ✓\nTo make / release a lot of ATP / energy ✓\nATP / energy is needed for the tail to move / for the sperm to swim (to the egg) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW: mitochondria make energy` },
      { q: `Asha counted the number of gas bubbles given off from a tube of yeast in sugar solution in 1 minute. She used this to compare the rate of respiration with different sugars. Her teacher said her method would not give accurate results.\n\nSuggest one problem with counting bubbles and suggest how Asha could improve her method. Explain why your improvement is better.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Problem: bubbles are not all the same size / bubbles are hard to count when they are produced quickly / some gas may dissolve or escape uncounted ✓\nImprovement: collect the gas in a gas syringe / an upturned measuring cylinder (and measure the volume) ✓\nExplanation: measures the (total) volume of gas so it is more accurate / does not depend on bubble size ✓` },
      { q: `A student put germinating peas into flask A and boiled peas into flask B. Before setting up flask B, the boiled peas were rinsed in disinfectant. Both flasks were vacuum flasks with a thermometer (data written for this practice question).\nFlask A: 20.5 °C at the start, 26.0 °C after 3 days\nFlask B: 20.5 °C at the start, 20.5 °C after 3 days\n\nUse the data to explain the results, and suggest why the boiled peas were rinsed in disinfectant.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Temperature in A rose by 5.5 °C but did not change in B ✓\nGerminating peas respire (and respiration is exothermic); boiled peas are dead so do not respire ✓\nDisinfectant kills microorganisms (on the boiled peas), which would respire / decompose the peas and release heat ✓` },
      { q: `This question is about respiration in different organisms.\n\nComplete the table.\nColumns: Type of respiration | Organism | Is oxygen needed? | Products\nAerobic | Human | Yes | ______\nAnaerobic | Human | ______ | Lactic acid\nAnaerobic | Yeast | No | ______\nAnaerobic | ______ | No | Ethanol and carbon dioxide`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `Carbon dioxide and water ✓\nNo ✓\nEthanol and carbon dioxide ✓\nPlant / named plant / plant root cell ✓\nALLOW: (named) fungus for the fourth mark` },
      { q: `Fermentation by microorganisms is used to make many foods and drinks. Bread is made using yeast. Beer is made using yeast and a sugary liquid made from barley.\n\nExplain how fermentation by yeast is used in making bread and in making beer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Yeast respires anaerobically / ferments sugar / glucose ✓\nproducing ethanol and carbon dioxide ✓\nBread: carbon dioxide (bubbles) makes the dough rise ✓\nBeer: ethanol / alcohol is the product wanted / carbon dioxide makes the beer fizzy ✓\nALLOW: in bread the ethanol evaporates during baking\nIGNORE: references to oxygen being needed` },
      { q: `A student used two respirometers kept side by side at 15 °C (data written for this practice question).\nTube A contained woodlice and soda lime. Tube B contained glass beads of the same volume as the woodlice, and soda lime.\nIn 5 minutes the drop in tube A moved 12 mm towards the woodlice. The drop in tube B moved 1 mm in the same direction.\n\nExplain why tube B was set up, and calculate the rate of movement of the drop that was caused by the respiration of the woodlice. Suggest why the drop in tube B moved.\nRate = ______ mm per minute`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Tube B is a control to show that any movement is due to the woodlice / to show the effect of factors other than respiration ✓\n(12 − 1) ÷ 5 OR 11 ÷ 5 ✓\n= 2.2 (mm per minute) ✓\nDrop in B moved because of a change in temperature / atmospheric pressure (causing the gas in the tube to contract) ✓\nALLOW: 2.4 mm per minute for 1 mark (control not subtracted)` },
      { q: `* A student wants to investigate how temperature affects the rate of respiration in yeast.\nShe has yeast, glucose solution, test tubes, a bung with a delivery tube, a gas syringe, water baths and a stop clock.\n\nDescribe a method the student could use to collect valid results for this investigation.\nIn your answer you should describe:\n• how she would change the temperature and measure the rate of respiration\n• the variables she would control to make sure the results are valid.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Describes a clear method that would work, including how the temperature is changed (at least 4 temperatures) AND how the volume of gas is measured over a set time and used to find a rate AND identifies at least two control variables and repeats.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes a method that changes the temperature and measures the gas produced, AND identifies at least one control variable OR repeats.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives some relevant steps, e.g. putting yeast in glucose solution in a water bath OR measuring the gas produced.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nMethod\n• mix a measured mass of yeast with a measured volume of glucose solution in a test tube\n• place the tube in a water bath at a set temperature, e.g. 20 °C\n• leave for a few minutes to reach the temperature of the water bath\n• connect the tube to the gas syringe with the bung and delivery tube\n• start the stop clock and measure the volume of carbon dioxide collected after a set time, e.g. 5 minutes\n• calculate rate = volume ÷ time\n• repeat at a range of temperatures, e.g. 20, 30, 40, 50, 60 °C\n• check the temperature with a thermometer during the experiment\nControl variables and validity\n• same mass of yeast\n• same volume and concentration of glucose solution\n• same time for collecting gas\n• same type / batch of yeast\n• check the apparatus is airtight\n• repeat each temperature (at least three times) and calculate a mean\n• identify and ignore anomalous results\nSafety\n• take care with hot water baths` }
    ]
  },
  'B4.2': {
    name: 'How do we know about mitochondria and other cell structures?',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student looks at a slide of onion epidermis cells using a light microscope.\nThe eyepiece lens has a magnification of ×15 and the objective lens has a magnification of ×10.\n\nCalculate the total magnification.\n\nTotal magnification = ×______`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (×) 150 ✓` },
      { q: `Cells in the pancreas make and release large amounts of digestive enzymes.\nEnzymes are proteins.\n\nName the sub-cellular structure where these enzymes are made.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• ribosome(s) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW nucleus / mitochondria` },
      { q: `A student adds a drop of iodine solution to a thin layer of onion epidermis on a microscope slide before looking at it with a light microscope.\n\nSuggest why the student adds iodine solution.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• to stain / colour (parts of) the cells so that structures (such as the nucleus / cell wall) can be seen (more clearly) ✓\nALLOW to increase contrast\nIGNORE to make the cells bigger / to kill the cells` },
      { q: `Cells can be classified as prokaryotic or eukaryotic.\n\nWhich structure is found in both prokaryotic cells and eukaryotic cells?\nTick (✓) one box.\nChloroplast ☐\nMitochondrion ☐\nNucleus ☐\nRibosome ☐`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• Ribosome ✓\nIf more than one box is ticked award 0 marks` },
      { q: `A scientist wants to watch mitochondria moving inside living human cells.\nThe scientist has a light microscope and an electron microscope.\n\nGive one reason why the scientist cannot use the electron microscope for this.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• specimens must be dead / (living) cells are killed during preparation / the specimen is in a vacuum ✓\nALLOW specimens must be dried / fixed / coated (in metal)\nIGNORE electron microscopes are too expensive` },
      { q: `A human red blood cell has a diameter of about 7.5 μm.\n1 μm = 1 × 10⁻⁶ m\n\nWrite the diameter of the red blood cell in metres, in standard form.\n\nDiameter = ______ m`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `• 7.5 × 10⁻⁶ (m) ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 0.0000075 (not in standard form)` },
      { q: `A student draws a cell seen with a light microscope.\nThe drawing of the cell is 45 mm wide. The real cell is 0.015 mm wide.\n\nCalculate the magnification of the drawing.\nUse the equation: magnification = size of image ÷ size of real object\n\nMagnification = ×______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 45 ÷ 0.015 ✓\n• (×) 3000 ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about the sub-cellular structures in a plant cell.\n\nDraw lines to connect each structure to its function.\nStructures: Cell membrane; Chloroplast; Mitochondrion; Nucleus\nFunctions: Contains enzymes for cellular respiration; Contains the genetic material (chromosomes); Contains chlorophyll and enzymes for photosynthesis; Controls what enters and leaves the cell`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw lines', markScheme: `Cell membrane – controls what enters and leaves the cell\nChloroplast – contains chlorophyll and enzymes for photosynthesis\nMitochondrion – contains enzymes for cellular respiration\nNucleus – contains the genetic material (chromosomes)\nAll 4 correct ✓✓\n2 or 3 correct ✓\nMore than one line from a structure: that structure scores 0` },
      { q: `Scientists use electron microscopes to study the structure of mitochondria.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nUse words from the list: beam of electrons, beam of light, lower, higher, magnification, resolution.\n\nAn electron microscope uses a ______ instead of light.\nThis gives it a much ______ resolution, so it can show the folded inner membrane of a mitochondrion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• beam of electrons ✓\n• higher ✓` },
      { q: `Some bacteria carry a gene for resistance to an antibiotic. The gene is found on a plasmid.\nA human cheek cell contains genetic material too.\n\nDescribe two ways the genetic material in a bacterium is different from the genetic material in a human cheek cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• bacterial DNA is not inside a nucleus / is free in the cytoplasm, (but human DNA is in a nucleus) ✓\n• bacterium has a single (circular) chromosome / loop of DNA, (but humans have many / 46 chromosomes) ✓\n• bacterium has plasmids, (human cells do not) ✓\nALLOW human chromosomes are linear / not circular\nAnswers must be comparative or clearly about one cell type` },
      { q: `Root hair cells grow from the outer layer of a plant root, under the soil.\nRoot hair cells do not contain chloroplasts.\n\nExplain why root hair cells do not contain chloroplasts.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (root hair cells are) underground / in the dark / receive no light ✓\n• so cannot photosynthesise / chloroplasts would have no function ✓\nALLOW the cells absorb water and mineral ions rather than make glucose` },
      { q: `Scientists compared muscle cells from the legs of trained long-distance runners with muscle cells from people who do not exercise.\nThe runners' muscle cells contained more mitochondria.\n\nSuggest why the runners' muscle cells contain more mitochondria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• (more) cellular / aerobic respiration (happens in mitochondria) ✓\n• to release (more) energy (for muscle contraction during long runs) ✓\nALLOW makes more ATP\nDO NOT ALLOW produce / make energy` },
      { q: `The best resolution of a light microscope is about 200 nm.\nA ribosome is about 25 nm across.\n\nExplain why ribosomes cannot be seen with a light microscope.\nUse the data in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• ribosomes are (8 times) smaller than the resolution / 25 nm is less than 200 nm ✓\n• so (two ribosomes / a ribosome and its surroundings) cannot be distinguished / seen as separate / no detail can be seen ✓\nIGNORE ribosomes are too small (unqualified) for the first mark unless data used\nIGNORE magnification is too low` },
      { q: `The table shows the resolution of two types of microscope.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nMicroscope | Resolution (nm)\nlight microscope | 200\nelectron microscope | 0.2\n\nCalculate how many times better the resolution of the electron microscope is than the resolution of the light microscope.\n\nAnswer = ______ times`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 200 ÷ 0.2 ✓\n• 1000 ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student looks at a slide of onion epidermis cells using a light microscope.\nThe diameter of the field of view is 2.0 mm. The student counts 16 cells lying end to end across the diameter of the field of view.\n\nEstimate the mean length of one onion cell in micrometres (μm).\n1 mm = 1000 μm\n\nMean length = ______ μm`, marks: 2, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `• 2.0 ÷ 16 = 0.125 (mm) OR 2000 ÷ 16 ✓\n• 125 (μm) ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student measured the length of five cheek cells using a light microscope.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nLength of cells (μm): 42, 38, 45, 40, 35\n\nCalculate the mean length of the cheek cells.\n\nMean length = ______ μm`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (42 + 38 + 45 + 40 + 35) ÷ 5 OR 200 ÷ 5 ✓\n• 40 (μm) ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student is making a slide of cheek cells stained with methylene blue.\nThe student lowers the coverslip onto the slide slowly, at an angle, using a mounted needle.\n\nExplain why the student lowers the coverslip in this way.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• to avoid (trapping) air bubbles ✓\n• (because) air bubbles would block the view of / be confused with / hide the cells ✓\nIGNORE to stop the coverslip breaking` },
      { q: `A student has focused a light microscope on some cells using the ×10 objective lens.\nThe student then turns to the ×40 objective lens.\n\nExplain why the student should only use the fine focus knob after changing to the ×40 objective lens.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the (×40) objective lens is very close to the slide ✓\n• coarse focus moves the lens / stage a long way so could crash into / break the slide / damage the lens ✓\nALLOW fine focus gives small / precise adjustments to get a sharp image (max 1 if only this point)` },
      { q: `A scientist describes an electron micrograph of a cell taken from a sample of pond water.\nThe cell is about 3 μm long. It has a cell wall and a cell membrane. It has a loop of DNA in the cytoplasm and many very small ribosomes. There is no nucleus and there are no mitochondria.\n\nThe scientist concludes that the cell is prokaryotic.\nGive two pieces of evidence from the description that support this conclusion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• no nucleus ✓\n• (loop of) DNA (free) in the cytoplasm ✓\n• no mitochondria ✓\n• small size / 3 μm (long) ✓\nIGNORE has a cell wall / has ribosomes (also found in some eukaryotic cells)` },
      { q: `Yeast is a single-celled fungus. It is used to make bread.\nYeast cells are eukaryotic. Bacterial cells are prokaryotic.\n\nName two sub-cellular structures found in a yeast cell that are not found in a bacterial cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• nucleus ✓\n• mitochondria ✓\n• (permanent) vacuole ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW cell wall / ribosomes / cell membrane / cytoplasm\nDO NOT ALLOW chloroplasts` },
      { q: `Palisade cells are found near the upper surface of a leaf.\nEach palisade cell contains a large number of chloroplasts.\n\nExplain why palisade cells contain a large number of chloroplasts.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• chloroplasts contain chlorophyll / are where photosynthesis happens ✓\n• palisade cells receive a lot of light (near the top of the leaf) so (more chloroplasts mean) more photosynthesis / more glucose made ✓\nALLOW absorb more light` },
      { q: `A student places some red onion cells in pure water and some red blood cells in pure water.\nAfter 10 minutes the red blood cells have burst but the onion cells have not.\n\nExplain why the onion cells did not burst.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• onion / plant cells have a cell wall, (red blood / animal cells do not) ✓\n• the cell wall is strong / rigid / made of cellulose and stops the cell expanding (too far) ✓\nIGNORE cell membrane stops it bursting` },
      { q: `Some medicines must get inside body cells to work.\nThe molecules of one medicine are small enough to pass through the cell membrane.\n\nDescribe two functions of the cell membrane.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• controls what enters and leaves the cell ✓\n• holds the contents of the cell together / separates the cell from its surroundings ✓\n• is partially permeable / lets some substances through but not others ✓\nIGNORE protects the cell / gives the cell shape\nDO NOT ALLOW controls the cell` },
      { q: `An electron micrograph of a mitochondrion has a scale bar on it.\nThe scale bar is 20 mm long on the micrograph and represents 2 μm.\n1 mm = 1000 μm\n\nCalculate the magnification of the micrograph.\n\nMagnification = ×______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 20 mm = 20 000 μm OR 20 000 ÷ 2 OR 20 ÷ 0.002 ✓\n• (×) 10 000 ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `In 1665 Robert Hooke used a simple light microscope to look at thin slices of cork. He saw many small box-like spaces and called them cells.\nLater, scientists using better light microscopes saw cells in every plant and animal tissue they looked at.\n\nSuggest how these observations led scientists to the idea that all living things are made of cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• cells were observed in many / all (different) types of organism / tissue ✓\n• (so) scientists made a general explanation / theory from the repeated observations / pattern ✓\nALLOW more powerful microscopes / better resolution allowed more cells to be seen (in more tissues)` },
      { q: `A school is buying microscopes so that students can look at cells in lessons.\nThe school buys light microscopes, not electron microscopes.\n\nGive two reasons why light microscopes are more suitable for the school.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• cheaper (to buy / run) ✓\n• smaller / portable / easier to use ✓\n• can view living cells / specimens ✓\n• slides are quick / simple to prepare ✓\n• shows (natural) colour ✓\nIGNORE safer` },
      { q: `A scientist takes an electron micrograph of a mitochondrion from a liver cell.\nOn the micrograph the mitochondrion is 36 mm long. The magnification of the micrograph is ×12 000.\n\nCalculate the real length of the mitochondrion in micrometres (μm).\n1 mm = 1000 μm\n\nReal length = ______ μm`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• real size = size of image ÷ magnification ✓\n• 36 ÷ 12 000 = 0.003 (mm) ✓\n• 3 (μm) ✓\nALLOW 36 000 ÷ 12 000 for first two marks\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `A bacterium is 2 μm long.\nA scientist produces an electron micrograph of the bacterium at a magnification of ×25 000.\n1 mm = 1000 μm\n\nCalculate the length of the bacterium on the micrograph in millimetres (mm).\n\nLength on micrograph = ______ mm`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• size of image = magnification × real size ✓\n• 2 × 25 000 = 50 000 (μm) ✓\n• 50 (mm) ✓\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `A student measures a cheek cell using a scale in the eyepiece of a light microscope.\nAt the magnification used, each small division on the eyepiece scale is equal to 2.5 μm.\nThe cheek cell is 18 divisions wide.\n1 μm = 1 × 10⁻⁶ m\n\nCalculate the width of the cheek cell in metres.\nGive your answer in standard form.\n\nWidth = ______ m`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 18 × 2.5 ✓\n• = 45 (μm) ✓\n• 4.5 × 10⁻⁵ (m) ✓\nALLOW ECF from incorrect width in μm for third mark\n0.000045 m scores 2 marks (not in standard form)` },
      { q: `The table shows the approximate sizes of two biological structures.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nStructure | Approximate size (m)\nplant cell | 1 × 10⁻⁴\nvirus | 1 × 10⁻⁷\n\nCalculate how many times larger the plant cell is than the virus.\nState how many orders of magnitude larger the plant cell is.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (1 × 10⁻⁴) ÷ (1 × 10⁻⁷) ✓\n• 1000 (times) / 10³ ✓\n• 3 orders of magnitude ✓\nALLOW ECF for orders of magnitude from an incorrect ratio` },
      { q: `Using light microscopes, scientists in the 1890s saw mitochondria only as tiny granules or threads in the cytoplasm.\nIn the 1950s, scientists using electron microscopes saw that each mitochondrion has an outer membrane and a highly folded inner membrane.\n\nExplain how electron microscopes increased scientists' understanding of how mitochondria carry out their function.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• electron microscopes have (much) higher resolution (so internal structure / membranes could be seen) ✓\n• folded inner membrane gives a large surface area ✓\n• (so) space for many enzymes for (aerobic / cellular) respiration ✓\nALLOW scientists could link structure to function / the site of respiration (in the mitochondrion) could be identified\nIGNORE higher magnification (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Scientists counted the mean number of mitochondria in two types of human cell.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCell type | Mean number of mitochondria per cell\nheart muscle cell | 5000\nskin cell | 200\n\nExplain the difference in the number of mitochondria in the two types of cell.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• heart muscle cells have (25 times) more mitochondria ✓\n• (heart muscle) contracts continuously / needs a lot of energy ✓\n• mitochondria are the site of (aerobic / cellular) respiration which releases energy ✓\nALLOW makes more ATP\nDO NOT ALLOW makes / produces energy` },
      { q: `A student is comparing a bacterial cell with a plant leaf cell.\n\nComplete the table.\nPut a tick (✓) if the structure is present and a cross (✗) if it is absent.\n\nStructure | Bacterial cell | Plant leaf cell\nnucleus | ______ | ______\nplasmids | ______ | ______\nmitochondria | ______ | ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `nucleus: bacterial ✗, plant ✓ ✓\nplasmids: bacterial ✓, plant ✗ ✓\nmitochondria: bacterial ✗, plant ✓ ✓\nEach row must be fully correct for the mark` },
      { q: `A textbook describes a typical bacterium. It is a rod-shaped cell with a cell wall, a cell membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes, a single loop of DNA and a small ring of DNA.\n\nDraw a labelled diagram of the bacterium.\nLabel the cell wall, the loop of DNA and a plasmid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• rod-shaped outline with a double line / two layers shown as cell wall and membrane, with cell wall labelled on the outer layer ✓\n• (large) loop / tangle of DNA drawn free in cytoplasm (not in a nucleus) and labelled ✓\n• (at least one) small ring drawn separately and labelled plasmid ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW a nucleus drawn (max 2)` },
      { q: `In 1955 a scientist using an electron microscope reported tiny granules in the cytoplasm of many types of cell. These granules were later named ribosomes.\nAt first, some scientists thought the granules might be artefacts made when the cells were prepared for the microscope.\n\nExplain how scientists came to accept that ribosomes are real structures in cells.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the findings were published / peer reviewed (so other scientists could check them) ✓\n• other scientists repeated the work / saw the same granules (in different cells / using different preparation methods) ✓\n• (later) evidence linked the granules to a function / protein synthesis, so the explanation was supported ✓\nALLOW the results were reproducible` },
      { q: `A student makes this claim:\n'If I keep increasing the magnification of my light microscope, I will eventually see the ribosomes in a cell.'\n\nEvaluate the student's claim.\nUse ideas about magnification and resolution in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• the claim is wrong / not correct ✓\n• resolution (not magnification) limits the detail that can be seen / resolution is the smallest distance between two points that can be seen as separate ✓\n• ribosomes are smaller than the resolution of a light microscope, so increasing magnification only gives a bigger blurred image / an electron microscope is needed ✓\nALLOW light microscope resolution is about 200 nm` },
      { q: `A student prepares a slide of onion epidermis cells to look at with a light microscope.\n\nComplete the table to explain the purpose of each step.\n\nStep | Purpose\npeel a very thin layer of epidermis from the onion | ______\nplace the layer flat in a drop of water on the slide | ______\nadd a drop of iodine solution | ______\nlower a coverslip onto the layer | ______`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `thin layer: so light can pass through / cells are one layer thick (so they can be seen clearly) ✓\nflat in water: stops the cells drying out / stops the layer folding or overlapping ✓\niodine: stains (structures such as) the nucleus / cell wall so they can be seen ✓\ncoverslip: keeps the specimen flat / in place / stops it drying out / protects the objective lens ✓\nIGNORE to make it easier to see (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A student has a prepared slide of cheek cells and a light microscope with ×4, ×10 and ×40 objective lenses.\n\nDescribe how the student should use the microscope to get a clear, focused image of the cells at the highest magnification.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• place slide on stage / secure with clips, with light / lamp on ✓\n• start with the lowest power (×4) objective lens ✓\n• use the coarse focus (looking from the side, move the lens close to the slide, then focus) until the image is visible ✓\n• change to the ×10 then ×40 objective lens and use (only) the fine focus to sharpen the image ✓\nALLOW move the slide to centre the cells before changing lens` },
      { q: `An electron micrograph of a chloroplast from a leaf cell has a scale bar on it.\nThe scale bar is 25 mm long on the micrograph and represents 5 μm.\nThe chloroplast is 30 mm long on the micrograph.\n1 mm = 1000 μm\n\nCalculate the magnification of the micrograph and the real length of the chloroplast in μm.\n\nMagnification = ×______\nReal length = ______ μm`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Magnification:\n• 25 mm = 25 000 μm OR 25 000 ÷ 5 ✓\n• (×) 5000 ✓\nReal length:\n• 30 ÷ 5000 = 0.006 (mm) OR 30 000 ÷ 5000 OR 30 ÷ 25 × 5 ✓\n• 6 (μm) ✓\nALLOW ECF for real length from an incorrect magnification` },
      { q: `Scientists' understanding of cells has changed as microscopes have developed.\nIn the 1600s, light microscopes showed that living things are made of cells. By the 1890s, better light microscopes showed nuclei and tiny granules later called mitochondria. Electron microscopes were developed in the 1930s and were used to study cells from the 1950s.\n\nExplain how developments in microscopy have changed scientists' understanding of the structure and function of cells.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• what can and cannot be seen with each type of microscope\n• how new observations changed scientists' explanations\n• why scientists must be careful when interpreting electron micrographs.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Please refer to the marking instructions for guidance on marking this question.\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Explains the difference in what light and electron microscopes can show, linked to resolution, AND explains how new observations led to new or changed explanations of sub-cellular structures and their functions AND describes a limitation of interpreting electron micrographs.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes what light and electron microscopes can show AND EITHER how observations changed explanations OR a limitation of electron micrographs.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Makes a simple statement about what can be seen with a microscope, e.g. electron microscopes show more detail, OR states that new microscopes led to new discoveries.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nWhat can be seen\n• light microscope resolution about 200 nm; shows cells, nucleus, cell wall, chloroplasts, mitochondria only as dots / granules\n• electron microscope uses a beam of electrons; resolution about 0.2 nm / about 1000 times better\n• electron microscope shows ribosomes, internal membranes of mitochondria and chloroplasts, plasmids, detail of cell membrane\n• magnification alone does not show more detail; resolution limits detail\nChanging explanations\n• observations of cells in all tissues led to the idea / theory that all living things are made of cells\n• folded inner membrane of mitochondria seen; large surface area linked to enzymes for respiration\n• ribosomes discovered; later linked to protein synthesis\n• internal structure of chloroplasts linked to photosynthesis\n• prokaryotic cells seen to lack a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles, so cells classified as prokaryotic and eukaryotic\n• new technology gives new data, so explanations are modified or replaced; findings are peer reviewed and repeated by other scientists before being accepted\nInterpreting electron micrographs\n• specimens are dead / fixed / in a vacuum, so living processes cannot be watched\n• preparation (staining, cutting, drying) may create artefacts / change the structures\n• images are black and white / false colour; a micrograph shows a thin slice, so 3D shape must be worked out from many images` }
    ]
  },
  'B4.3': {
    name: 'How do organisms grow and develop?',
    questions: [
      { q: `A student wants to look at cells dividing by mitosis in an onion root.\nThe student cuts off the last 5 mm of the root, where the root is growing.\n\nWhy does the student use the tip of the root rather than a piece from further up the root?`, marks: 1, command: 'Why', markScheme: `• the tip contains a meristem / it is where cells are dividing (by mitosis) ✓\nALLOW more cells are dividing at the tip\nIGNORE the tip is growing (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A student makes a microscope slide of cells from a garlic root tip.\nBefore looking at the slide, the student adds a few drops of a stain such as toluidine blue.\n\nWhy does the student add the stain?`, marks: 1, command: 'Why', markScheme: `• to make the chromosomes / nuclei / DNA visible ✓\nALLOW to make the chromosomes show up / easier to see\nIGNORE to make the cells easier to see` },
      { q: `This question is about cell division in the human body.\nSkin cells in the human body contain 46 chromosomes. Skin cells are replaced by mitosis.\n\nHow many chromosomes are in each new skin cell produced by mitosis?`, marks: 1, command: 'How many', markScheme: `• 46 ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW 23` },
      { q: `Plant growers use tissue culture to produce many new plants from one parent plant.\nSmall pieces of the parent plant are grown on a jelly containing nutrients and plant hormones.\nThe jelly and all the equipment must be sterile.\n\nSuggest why the jelly and equipment must be sterile.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• so that microorganisms / bacteria / fungi do not grow (on the nutrients) and kill / infect / compete with the plant tissue ✓\nALLOW to prevent contamination by microorganisms\nIGNORE germs / to keep it clean` },
      { q: `Plants keep growing throughout their lives. New cells are made in regions where stem cells are found.\n\nWhere in a plant are cells found that can divide and become any type of plant cell?\nTick (✓) one box.\nPalisade layer of the leaf ☐\nPhloem in the stem ☐\nRoot tip meristem ☐\nXylem in the root ☐`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• Root tip meristem ✓\nIf more than one box is ticked award 0 marks` },
      { q: `Horses reproduce sexually. The body cells of a horse contain 64 chromosomes.\nEgg cells are made in the ovaries of a female horse.\n\nHow many chromosomes are in a horse egg cell?`, marks: 1, command: 'How many', markScheme: `• 32 ✓` },
      { q: `A student looks at a slide of an onion root tip under a light microscope.\nIn one field of view the student counts 175 cells. Of these, 14 cells are dividing by mitosis.\n\nCalculate the percentage of cells that are dividing by mitosis.\n\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (14 ÷ 175) × 100 ✓\n• = 8 (%) ✓\nALLOW 8.0\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Before a cell divides by mitosis it goes through a stage called interphase.\n\nComplete the sentences to describe what happens during interphase.\nPut a ring around each correct answer.\nThe cell grows larger and the number of chromosomes / mitochondria / nuclei increases.\nThe DNA in the cell is copied / halved / removed so that each new cell gets a full set of chromosomes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• mitochondria ✓\n• copied ✓\nIf more than one option is ringed in a sentence, that sentence scores 0` },
      { q: `Cells in the human body divide by mitosis. Mitosis is needed for growth.\n\nDescribe two other reasons why mitosis is needed in the human body.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• to repair damaged tissue / heal wounds ✓\n• to replace old / worn-out / dead cells (e.g. skin cells, cells lining the gut) ✓\n• to make new blood cells (in bone marrow) ✓\nIGNORE growth\nDO NOT ALLOW to make gametes / for sexual reproduction` },
      { q: `A health visitor measures the mass of a baby.\n(data written for this practice question)\nMass at birth = 3.5 kg\nMass at 6 months = 7.7 kg\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the baby's mass from birth to 6 months.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• increase = 7.7 − 3.5 = 4.2 (kg) ✓\n• (4.2 ÷ 3.5) × 100 = 120 (%) ✓\nALLOW 220 (7.7 ÷ 3.5 × 100) for 1 mark only\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `A gardener has a geranium plant with bright red flowers. She takes twelve cuttings from the plant and grows them into new plants.\nAll twelve new plants have bright red flowers.\n\nExplain why all of the new plants have the same flower colour as the parent plant.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the new plants are made by mitosis / asexual reproduction ✓\n• so they are genetically identical to the parent / are clones / have the same genes / alleles (for flower colour) ✓\nIGNORE they are the same species\nDO NOT ALLOW meiosis` },
      { q: `Every cell in a human embryo contains the same genes.\nAs the embryo develops, some of its stem cells become muscle cells and others become nerve cells.\n\nExplain how a stem cell becomes a muscle cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• some genes are switched on / expressed and others are switched off ✓\n• so the cell makes particular proteins (that give a muscle cell its structure and function) ✓\nALLOW the cell differentiates / becomes specialised for 1 mark if no other mark scored\nDO NOT ALLOW genes are removed / lost` },
      { q: `Scientists can obtain stem cells from early human embryos that are left over from IVF treatment. Stem cells are also found in some adult tissues such as bone marrow.\n\nGive two reasons why embryonic stem cells may be more useful to scientists than adult stem cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• embryonic stem cells can become any type of cell / all cell types ✓\n• adult stem cells can only become a limited range of cell types (e.g. blood cells) ✓\n• embryonic stem cells divide more (rapidly) / can be grown in large numbers ✓\nALLOW embryonic stem cells are more versatile` },
      { q: `Some patients with leukaemia are given a bone marrow transplant. Bone marrow contains adult stem cells.\n\nName two types of cell that the stem cells in bone marrow can become.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• red blood cells ✓\n• white blood cells / lymphocytes / phagocytes ✓\n• platelets ✓\nALLOW lymphocytes and phagocytes as two separate answers` },
      { q: `A plant nursery sells orchids. Orchid seeds are very slow to germinate and grow.\nThe nursery decides to produce its orchids by tissue culture instead of from seeds.\n\nSuggest two advantages to the nursery of using tissue culture.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• many plants can be produced quickly / from a small amount of tissue ✓\n• the plants are genetically identical / clones so all have the desired characteristics (e.g. flower colour) ✓\n• plants can be produced all year round / not dependent on season ✓\n• needs little space ✓\n• plants are disease-free (if grown from sterile meristem tissue) ✓\nIGNORE cheaper (unqualified)` },
      { q: `Almost all of the bananas sold in UK supermarkets come from one variety, the Cavendish banana. Cavendish banana plants are all clones grown from cuttings of the same parent plants.\nA fungal disease called Panama disease is killing Cavendish banana plants in many countries.\n\nExplain why cloning makes the Cavendish banana crop more at risk from Panama disease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the plants are genetically identical / have no (genetic) variation ✓\n• so if one plant is not resistant / is susceptible, none of them are / the disease can kill all of them ✓\nALLOW no plants have alleles for resistance` },
      { q: `Scientists studied cells in an onion root tip.\n(data written for this practice question)\nThe cell cycle in these cells lasts 24 hours.\nAt any moment, 6% of the cells are dividing by mitosis.\nThe percentage of cells dividing by mitosis is equal to the percentage of the cell cycle that is spent in mitosis.\n\nCalculate how long these cells spend in mitosis in each cell cycle.\nGive your answer in minutes, to the nearest minute.\n\nTime = ______ minutes`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.06 × 24 = 1.44 (hours) ✓\n• 1.44 × 60 = 86 (minutes) ✓\nALLOW 86.4 for 2 marks\nALLOW 0.06 × 1440 = 86 for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A scientist grows human skin cells in the laboratory.\n(data written for this practice question)\nThe scientist starts with one cell. The cells divide by mitosis every 20 hours. All cells keep dividing.\n\nCalculate the number of cells after 5 days.\n\nNumber of cells = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• number of divisions = (5 × 24) ÷ 20 = 6 ✓\n• 2⁶ = 64 (cells) ✓\nALLOW 1 mark for 32 or 128 (one division too few / too many)` },
      { q: `This is part of a student's method for making a slide of a root tip to observe mitosis:\n'I put the root tip on a slide, added a drop of stain and placed a coverslip on top. I then pressed down firmly on the coverslip with my thumb through a folded paper towel.'\n\nExplain why the student pressed down firmly on the coverslip.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• to spread / squash the cells into a thin layer / one cell thick ✓\n• so that light can pass through / the cells (and chromosomes) can be seen separately / do not overlap ✓\nIGNORE to remove air bubbles` },
      { q: `In a method for observing mitosis, the root tips are first placed in hydrochloric acid at 60 °C for five minutes. This softens the tissue.\nThe hydrochloric acid is an irritant.\n\nDescribe one hazard of this step and one way the student can reduce the risk.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• hazard: acid could get in eyes / on skin and cause irritation / harm ✓\nALLOW hot water bath / hot acid could scald / burn\n• precaution: wear eye protection / goggles / gloves / handle the root tips with forceps / wash off any spills with water ✓\nThe precaution must match the hazard` },
      { q: `Cells in the anther of a flower divide to make pollen grains (male gametes). Cells in the root tip of the same plant divide to make new root cells.\n\nGive two differences between the cells produced by division in the anther and the cells produced by division in the root tip.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• anther cells (meiosis) have half the number of chromosomes / root tip cells (mitosis) have the full number ✓\n• anther cells are genetically different (from each other and the parent) / root tip cells are genetically identical ✓\n• meiosis produces four cells / mitosis produces two cells ✓\nThe difference must be comparative or both sides implied\nALLOW anther cells made by meiosis, root tip by mitosis for 1 mark if no other mark scored` },
      { q: `Human sperm cells and egg cells are made by meiosis. They join at fertilisation to form a zygote.\n\nExplain why sperm cells and egg cells must contain half the number of chromosomes of a body cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• when the gametes fuse / at fertilisation, the chromosome number is restored / the zygote has the full number (46) / pairs of chromosomes ✓\n• so the number of chromosomes stays the same from one generation to the next / does not double each generation ✓\nALLOW the zygote gets one set of chromosomes from each parent` },
      { q: `A person's spinal cord has been damaged in an accident. The nerve cells in the damaged part of the spinal cord have died.\nScientists are trialling a treatment in which stem cells are injected into the damaged part of the spinal cord.\n\nExplain why stem cells might be able to treat this damage.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• stem cells are unspecialised / can differentiate / become specialised into nerve cells / neurones ✓\n• these could replace the damaged nerve cells, because differentiated nerve cells cannot divide / are not replaced naturally ✓\nALLOW repair the damaged spinal cord for second mark` },
      { q: `Doctors hope to use stem cells to treat type 1 diabetes. Stem cells would be made to develop into insulin-producing cells and placed into the patient's pancreas.\n\nGive two risks of this treatment for the patient.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• the stem cells could keep dividing / divide uncontrollably and form a tumour / cause cancer ✓\n• the cells may be rejected (by the immune system) if they come from a donor ✓\n• the cells may carry a virus / infection to the patient ✓\n• the patient may need drugs to stop rejection which increase risk of infection ✓\nIGNORE it may not work / is expensive` },
      { q: `In the UK, scientists may use human embryos up to 14 days old for stem cell research, but only with a licence from a government body. The embryos are destroyed when the stem cells are removed.\n\nGive one reason why some people are against this research and one reason why other people support it.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• against: the embryo is destroyed / an embryo is a potential human life / has a right to life / religious objection ✓\n• support: could lead to treatments / cures for diseases (e.g. diabetes, paralysis) / reduce suffering / embryos (from IVF) would be destroyed anyway ✓\nIGNORE it is wrong / it is unnatural (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A student grows a sunflower plant and measures its height every week.\n(data written for this practice question)\nHeight at week 2 = 12 cm\nHeight at week 8 = 30 cm\n\nCalculate the mean growth rate of the sunflower between week 2 and week 8.\nGive the unit.\n\nMean growth rate = ______ unit ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (30 − 12) ÷ (8 − 2) = 3 ✓\n• cm per week / cm/week ✓\nALLOW unit mark with an incorrect numerical answer\nALLOW 0.43 cm per day if converted correctly for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Plant roots grow longer because cells at the tip go through the cell cycle.\n\nDescribe what happens during the cell cycle to produce two new cells from one cell.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• (interphase) the cell grows / increases the number of sub-cellular structures / organelles (e.g. mitochondria, ribosomes) ✓\n• (interphase) the DNA / chromosomes are copied / replicated ✓\n• (mitosis) one set of chromosomes is pulled to each end of the cell ✓\n• the nucleus divides (into two) ✓\n• the cytoplasm / cell divides to form two (genetically identical) cells ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW meiosis / chromosome number halved` },
      { q: `A student counts cells in three fields of view on a slide of an onion root tip.\n(data written for this practice question)\nField 1: 40 cells in total, 6 dividing by mitosis\nField 2: 50 cells in total, 8 dividing by mitosis\nField 3: 30 cells in total, 4 dividing by mitosis\n\nCalculate the percentage of cells dividing by mitosis using the results from all three fields of view.\n\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• total dividing = 6 + 8 + 4 = 18 ✓\n• total cells = 40 + 50 + 30 = 120 ✓\n• (18 ÷ 120) × 100 = 15 (%) ✓\nALLOW mean of the three field percentages (15 + 16 + 13.3) ÷ 3 = 14.8 for 3 marks\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks` },
      { q: `Scientists cut thin sections from a bean root at different distances from the tip. They found the percentage of cells dividing by mitosis in each section.\n(data written for this practice question)\n0–2 mm from tip: 14%\n4–6 mm from tip: 5%\n8–10 mm from tip: 1%\n12–14 mm from tip: 0%\n\nExplain the pattern shown by the data.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the percentage of cells dividing decreases with distance from the tip ✓\n• the tip contains a meristem / unspecialised / stem cells that divide by mitosis ✓\n• further from the tip cells are elongating / differentiating / have become specialised (e.g. into xylem) and no longer divide ✓\nALLOW data quoted to support the pattern for the first mark` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of rooting powder on cuttings from a plant.\n(data written for this practice question)\nThe student dips the cut ends of 24 cuttings in rooting powder. Another 24 cuttings are not dipped in rooting powder.\nAfter three weeks, 21 of the cuttings dipped in rooting powder have grown roots and 9 of the cuttings without rooting powder have grown roots.\n\nCalculate the percentage of cuttings that grew roots in each group, and use your answers to write a conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• with rooting powder: (21 ÷ 24) × 100 = 87.5 (%) ✓\n• without rooting powder: (9 ÷ 24) × 100 = 37.5 (%) ✓\n• conclusion: rooting powder increases the percentage of cuttings that grow roots (by 50 percentage points) ✓\nALLOW 88% and 38%` },
      { q: `A gardener can take a small piece of stem from a plant and grow it into a whole new plant. It is not possible to grow a whole new animal from a small piece of an adult animal.\n\nExplain why plants can be grown from cuttings but animals cannot.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• plants have meristems / unspecialised cells / stem cells throughout their life ✓\n• (meristem cells) can differentiate into any type of plant cell / form all the tissues needed for a new plant (e.g. roots) ✓\n• most cells in adult animals are specialised / differentiated (and cannot become other types) ✓\n• adult animal stem cells can only become a limited range of cells ✓\nmax 3` },
      { q: `A patient needs new blood cells. Doctors can treat her using either stem cells taken from her own bone marrow or stem cells from a donor.\n\nExplain the advantage of using stem cells from the patient's own bone marrow.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the cells are genetically identical to the patient's / have the same antigens ✓\n• so they will not be recognised as foreign / attacked by the immune system / white blood cells ✓\n• so they will not be rejected / the patient does not need immunosuppressant drugs ✓\nALLOW no need to find a matching donor / no risk of infection from a donor` },
      { q: `A student measures the dry mass of a sample of bean seedlings as they grow.\n(data written for this practice question)\nDay 4: dry mass = 0.38 g\nDay 8: dry mass = 0.71 g\nDay 12: dry mass = 1.15 g\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in dry mass between day 4 and day 12.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.\n\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• increase = 1.15 − 0.38 = 0.77 (g) ✓\n• (0.77 ÷ 0.38) × 100 = 202.63 ✓\n• = 203 (%) to 3 significant figures ✓\nALLOW ECF for 3 sf mark from an incorrect calculation\nALLOW 303 (1.15 ÷ 0.38 × 100) for 1 mark only` },
      { q: `Fruit flies are used in genetics research. Fruit fly body cells contain 8 chromosomes in four pairs.\nFruit flies make gametes by meiosis.\n\nExplain how meiosis helps keep the chromosome number constant and produces variation in fruit fly offspring.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• meiosis halves the chromosome number / gametes have 4 chromosomes / one chromosome from each pair ✓\n• fertilisation restores the full number / zygote has 8 chromosomes / four pairs ✓\n• each gamete gets a different combination of chromosomes / alleles so gametes are genetically different (and fertilisation is random) ✓\nALLOW gametes are not genetically identical / are genetically different from each other` },
      { q: `Some parents choose to store blood from the umbilical cord of their newborn baby. Cord blood contains stem cells that can become blood cells. The blood is collected after the baby is born, without harming the baby.\n\nExplain why some people think cord blood is a better source of stem cells than embryos.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• no embryo is destroyed / no potential life is lost ✓\n• so there are fewer ethical / religious objections ✓\n• collected without harming the baby or mother ✓\n• cells are genetically identical to the baby so could be used to treat the child later without rejection ✓\nmax 3` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the mass of DNA in a human cell over one cell cycle.\n(data written for this practice question)\n0–10 hours: 6.6 picograms\n10–16 hours: the mass increases steadily to 13.2 picograms\n16–23 hours: 13.2 picograms\nAt 24 hours: 6.6 picograms\n\nExplain the changes in the mass of DNA during the cell cycle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (10–16 hours) the DNA is being copied / replicated (during interphase) ✓\n• the mass doubles so there are two copies of each chromosome ✓\n• (at 24 hours) the cell has divided / mitosis has happened and each new cell gets one (full) set of chromosomes / the mass halves ✓\nALLOW the copies of the chromosomes are pulled to each end / separated` },
      { q: `A garden centre wants to produce thousands of identical plants from one parent plant that has attractive flowers. It uses tissue culture.\n\nDescribe how the garden centre would produce the new plants by tissue culture.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• take small pieces of tissue (explants) from the parent plant, e.g. from a shoot tip / meristem ✓\n• sterilise the tissue / work aseptically ✓\n• place on (sterile) agar / jelly containing nutrients / glucose / mineral ions ✓\n• with plant hormones to make the cells divide / form shoots and roots ✓\n• the cells divide by mitosis (to form a mass of cells) and differentiate into tiny plants / plantlets ✓\n• transfer the plantlets to compost / soil (in a greenhouse) to grow ✓\nmax 4` },
      { q: `A student investigates the effect of the concentration of rooting hormone on the number of roots grown by cuttings. The student counts the roots on each cutting after two weeks.\n(data written for this practice question)\n0% hormone: 4, 5, 3 roots (mean 4)\n0.1% hormone: 9, 8, 10 roots (mean 9)\n0.2% hormone: 14, 6, 13 roots (mean not yet calculated)\n\nIdentify the anomalous result. Calculate the mean number of roots for 0.2% hormone, without the anomalous result. Describe what the results show.\n\nAnomalous result = ______\nMean = ______ roots`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• anomalous result: 6 (at 0.2%) ✓\n• (14 + 13) ÷ 2 ✓\n• = 13.5 (roots) ✓\n• as the concentration of rooting hormone increases, the (mean) number of roots increases ✓\nALLOW ECF for mean if wrong anomaly identified\nDO NOT ALLOW 11 (mean including 6) for the mean marks` },
      { q: `A human embryo develops from a single cell called a zygote. By the time the baby is born it has around 200 different types of specialised cell.\n\nExplain how a single zygote develops into an embryo with many different types of specialised cell.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the zygote divides by mitosis (many times) to increase the number of cells ✓\n• (the early) embryo cells are (embryonic) stem cells / unspecialised / all identical ✓\n• (the cells differentiate) different genes are switched on / off / expressed in different cells ✓\n• so different cells make different proteins and become specialised for different functions ✓\nALLOW specialised cells group together to form tissues / organs as an alternative to one of the last two marks\nDO NOT ALLOW genes are lost / removed` },
      { q: `Parkinson's disease is a condition in which some nerve cells in the brain die. These nerve cells cannot be replaced by the body. There is no cure.\nIn clinical trials, scientists have made nerve cells from embryonic stem cells and transplanted them into the brains of patients with Parkinson's disease.\nThe embryos used are spare embryos donated after IVF treatment. They are destroyed when the stem cells are removed.\n\nDiscuss the benefits, risks and ethical issues of using embryonic stem cells to treat Parkinson's disease.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Please refer to the marking instructions for guidance on marking this question.\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Explains at least one benefit AND at least one risk AND discusses ethical issues, giving views both for and against.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes benefits AND EITHER risks OR ethical issues.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Gives a simple benefit, risk or ethical issue, e.g. it could cure the disease but the embryo is killed.\nThere is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nBenefits\n• embryonic stem cells are unspecialised and can differentiate into any cell type, including nerve cells\n• they can replace the nerve cells that have died, which the body cannot replace\n• could treat the cause rather than just the symptoms / could be a cure\n• could improve quality of life / reduce the need for lifelong drugs\nRisks\n• stem cells may keep dividing uncontrollably and form a tumour / cancer\n• donor cells are not genetically identical so may be rejected by the immune system\n• patient may need immunosuppressant drugs, increasing risk of infection\n• cells could transfer a virus / infection\n• the treatment is still in trials so long-term effects are unknown / it may not work\nEthical issues\n• the embryo is destroyed; some people believe an embryo is a potential human life with a right to life / religious objections\n• others argue that spare IVF embryos would be destroyed anyway, so using them reduces suffering\n• embryos are only a few days old and have no nervous system\n• research is regulated / licensed by the government (e.g. embryos used only up to 14 days)\n• the cost / who decides which patients are treated\n• alternatives such as adult stem cells avoid destroying embryos but can make fewer cell types` }
    ]
  },
  'B5.1': {
    name: 'How do substances get into, out of, and around our bodies?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about blood.\nBlood is made of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets.\n\nWhich part of the blood carries most of the oxygen to the body cells?\nTick (✓) one box.\n☐ Plasma\n☐ Platelets\n☐ Red blood cells\n☐ White blood cells`, marks: 1, command: 'Tick', markScheme: `• Red blood cells ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one box ticked` },
      { q: `A student put a drop of blood into distilled water and looked at it with a light microscope. After a few minutes the red blood cells had swollen and burst.\n\nName the process that caused water to move into the red blood cells.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• osmosis ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW diffusion of water alone / active transport` },
      { q: `The small intestine is lined with villi. The cells on the surface of each villus absorb glucose from the gut, even when the concentration of glucose in the gut is lower than in the cells.\nThese cells contain many mitochondria.\n\nExplain why these cells contain many mitochondria.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (respiration in mitochondria) releases energy / makes ATP needed for active transport (of glucose) ✓\nALLOW energy needed to move glucose against its concentration gradient\nDO NOT ALLOW mitochondria make / produce energy` },
      { q: `The human circulatory system is a double circulatory system. Blood leaves the right ventricle of the heart and travels to the lungs, where it picks up oxygen.\n\nName the blood vessel that carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• pulmonary artery ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW pulmonary vein / aorta` },
      { q: `This question is about digestion.\nFats in food, such as the fat in cheese, are large molecules. They must be broken down into fatty acids and glycerol in the small intestine before they can be absorbed into the blood.\n\nName the type of enzyme that breaks down fats.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• lipase ✓\nIGNORE bile\nDO NOT ALLOW protease / carbohydrase / amylase` },
      { q: `A student dissected a lamb's heart. She cut across the two ventricles and measured the thickness of each wall with a ruler.\n(data written for this practice question)\nThickness of left ventricle wall = 18 mm\nThickness of right ventricle wall = 6 mm\n\nCalculate how many times thicker the left ventricle wall is than the right ventricle wall.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 3 (times) ✓\n(18 ÷ 6 = 3)\nALLOW 3 : 1` },
      { q: `This question is about gas exchange in the lungs.\nThe lungs contain millions of tiny air sacs called alveoli. Each alveolus is surrounded by blood capillaries.\n\nComplete the sentences to describe gas exchange in an alveolus.\nPut a ring around each correct answer.\nOxygen moves from the air in the alveolus into the blood by active transport / diffusion / osmosis.\nCarbon dioxide moves from the blood into / out of the alveolus because its concentration in the blood is higher / lower than in the air in the alveolus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• diffusion ✓\n• into AND higher ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one option ringed in a sentence` },
      { q: `Single-celled organisms take in all the substances they need through their surface. Scientists use cubes to model how surface area to volume ratio changes as organisms get bigger.\nA model cell is a cube with sides of 3 cm.\n\nCalculate the surface area to volume ratio of the cube.\nGive your answer in its simplest form.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• surface area = 6 × 3 × 3 = 54 (cm²) AND volume = 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 (cm³) ✓\n• 2 : 1 ✓\nALLOW 54 : 27 for 1 mark only\nALLOW 2 for 2 marks\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks` },
      { q: `This question is about the parts of the blood.\nBlood is made of four main parts. Each part has a different job.\n\nDraw lines to connect each part of the blood to its job.\nParts of blood: Plasma; Platelets; Red blood cells; White blood cells\nJobs: Carry oxygen; Defend the body against pathogens; Help the blood to clot at a wound; Transport dissolved substances such as glucose, urea and carbon dioxide`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', markScheme: `Plasma – Transport dissolved substances such as glucose, urea and carbon dioxide\nPlatelets – Help the blood to clot at a wound\nRed blood cells – Carry oxygen\nWhite blood cells – Defend the body against pathogens\n• all four correct ✓✓\n• two or three correct ✓\nMore than one line from a part of blood cancels that mark` },
      { q: `A student made cubes of pink agar jelly containing an alkali and an indicator. He put each cube into dilute hydrochloric acid. The cube turns colourless as the acid diffuses in. He timed how long each cube took to become completely colourless.\n(data written for this practice question)\nSide of cube (cm) | Surface area to volume ratio | Time to become colourless (minutes)\n1 | 6 : 1 | 4.2\n2 | 3 : 1 | 15.8\n3 | 2 : 1 | 35.5\n\nDescribe the relationship between the surface area to volume ratio and the time taken for the cube to become colourless.\nUse data from the table in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• the larger the surface area to volume ratio, the shorter the time (to become colourless) ✓\nALLOW the smaller the ratio, the longer the time\n• use of data e.g. ratio 6 : 1 took 4.2 minutes but ratio 2 : 1 took 35.5 minutes ✓\nALLOW the time does not decrease in proportion / halving the ratio from 6 : 1 to 3 : 1 almost quadruples the time (for the second mark)` },
      { q: `A pink agar cube with sides of 1 cm was put into dilute hydrochloric acid. The acid diffused in from every face. The centre of the cube is 5 mm from each face.\nThe colour change reached the centre of the cube after 4.0 minutes.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mean rate at which the acid diffused into the cube.\nGive your answer in mm per minute.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 5 ÷ 4.0 ✓\n• 1.25 (mm per minute) ✓\nALLOW 1.3 / 1.2\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks\nDO NOT ALLOW 0.8 (4 ÷ 5) for the second mark` },
      { q: `Blood leaves the left ventricle through the aorta. The blood pressure in the aorta rises to about 120 mmHg each time the ventricle contracts and falls to about 80 mmHg between contractions.\n\nExplain why the wall of the aorta contains thick layers of muscle and elastic tissue.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• blood (in the aorta) is at high pressure / the pressure rises and falls with each heartbeat ✓\n• thick wall stops the artery bursting / withstands the high pressure ✓\nOR elastic tissue stretches and recoils to smooth out the flow / keep blood moving between beats ✓\nIGNORE to pump blood` },
      { q: `A teacher puts a band around a student's upper arm so the veins on the lower arm stand out. She presses a finger on a vein near the wrist and slides a second finger towards the hand. The section of vein between her fingers stays empty.\n\nExplain why the blood does not flow back along the vein into the empty section.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• veins contain valves ✓\n• the valves close to prevent backflow / make blood flow only towards the heart ✓\nALLOW the blood in veins is at low pressure so would flow backwards without valves (for the second mark)` },
      { q: `Muscle tissue contains a large network of capillaries. Oxygen and glucose move out of the blood in the capillaries into the muscle cells.\n\nExplain how the structure of a capillary helps these substances to move into the muscle cells quickly.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• capillary wall is (only) one cell thick / very thin ✓\n• so there is a short distance for diffusion ✓\nALLOW capillaries are narrow / pass close to every cell, so a short diffusion distance (for the second mark)\nALLOW the wall is permeable / has gaps so substances can pass out` },
      { q: `Priya measured her heart rate by counting her pulse at her wrist for 15 seconds. She did this three times while sitting still.\n(data written for this practice question)\nPulse count in 15 seconds: 18, 19, 20\n\nCalculate Priya's mean heart rate in beats per minute.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• mean count = (18 + 19 + 20) ÷ 3 = 19 ✓\n• 19 × 4 = 76 (beats per minute) ✓\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect mean multiplied by 4` },
      { q: `A scientist put some red blood cells into a 3% salt solution. Normal blood plasma has a salt concentration of about 0.9%. After ten minutes the red blood cells had shrunk.\n\nExplain why the red blood cells shrank.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• water moved out of the cells by osmosis ✓\n• from a dilute solution / high water concentration (inside the cell) to a more concentrated solution / low water concentration (outside) through the partially permeable membrane ✓\nALLOW the solution outside was more concentrated than inside the cell\nDO NOT ALLOW salt moves into the cell` },
      { q: `Emphysema is a lung disease that is often caused by smoking. In a person with emphysema, the walls between neighbouring alveoli break down. The lungs then contain fewer, much larger air spaces.\n\nExplain why a person with emphysema becomes breathless when they walk upstairs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• lungs have a smaller surface area (for gas exchange) ✓\n• so less oxygen diffuses into the blood / reaches the muscles for respiration ✓\nALLOW less carbon dioxide is removed\nIGNORE they cannot breathe` },
      { q: `Cigarette smoke contains carbon monoxide. Carbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin in red blood cells more strongly than oxygen does, and it does not easily separate from the haemoglobin.\n\nExplain why smoking reduces the amount of oxygen that the blood can carry.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• carbon monoxide takes the place of oxygen / binds to haemoglobin instead of oxygen ✓\n• so less haemoglobin is available to carry oxygen ✓\nALLOW fewer oxygen binding sites free\nIGNORE carbon monoxide is poisonous` },
      { q: `Fish have a single circulatory system: blood passes through the heart once for each complete circuit of the body. Humans have a double circulatory system.\n\nDescribe the two circuits of the human double circulatory system.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• one circuit from the heart to the lungs and back (to the heart) ✓\n• the other circuit from the heart to the rest of the body and back ✓\nALLOW blood passes through the heart twice for each complete circuit of the body for 1 mark if neither circuit is described\nALLOW right side pumps to the lungs; left side pumps to the body` },
      { q: `Bread contains starch. Starch is a large, insoluble carbohydrate molecule.\nThe digestive system breaks starch down before it can be absorbed into the blood.\n\nExplain why starch must be broken down before it can be absorbed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• starch molecules are too large to pass through the wall of the small intestine / cell membranes ✓\n• it is broken down into small soluble molecules (sugars / glucose) that can be absorbed / pass into the blood ✓\nALLOW starch is insoluble so cannot dissolve in the blood plasma\nALLOW carbohydrase / amylase breaks starch into sugars` },
      { q: `This question is about digestive enzymes.\nDifferent enzymes in the digestive system break down different food molecules.\n\nComplete the table.\nEnzyme | Food molecule broken down | Product(s)\nCarbohydrase | Starch | ______\nProtease | ______ | Amino acids\nLipase | Fat | ______ and ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• sugars AND protein ✓\nALLOW glucose / maltose for sugars\n• fatty acids AND glycerol ✓\nIGNORE order of fatty acids and glycerol` },
      { q: `Coeliac disease damages the lining of the small intestine. The villi become shorter and flatter. People with untreated coeliac disease often lose weight even though they eat a normal diet.\n\nExplain why people with untreated coeliac disease lose weight.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (flattened villi give) a smaller surface area (in the small intestine) ✓\n• so less digested food / glucose / amino acids is absorbed into the blood ✓\nALLOW so less energy available from food / body uses its fat stores (for the second mark)` },
      { q: `Amoeba is a single-celled organism that lives in ponds. It does not have a transport system. Oxygen diffuses straight through its cell membrane to all parts of the cell.\n\nExplain why Amoeba does not need a transport system.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• it has a large surface area to volume ratio ✓\n• so diffusion (through the surface) is fast enough to supply the whole cell / the diffusion distance (to the centre) is short ✓\nALLOW its surface is big enough to take in enough oxygen for its volume` },
      { q: `During exercise, more blood flows to the skeletal muscles.\n(data written for this practice question)\nBlood flow to skeletal muscles at rest = 1.0 dm³ per minute\nBlood flow to skeletal muscles during hard exercise = 12.0 dm³ per minute\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in blood flow to the skeletal muscles during hard exercise.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (12.0 − 1.0) ÷ 1.0 × 100 ✓\n• 1100 (%) ✓\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 1200 (%) / 12 times` },
      { q: `Doctors measured the concentration of oxygen in blood in the pulmonary artery and in the pulmonary vein of a healthy person at rest.\n(data written for this practice question)\nPulmonary artery: 150 cm³ of oxygen per dm³ of blood\nPulmonary vein: 200 cm³ of oxygen per dm³ of blood\n\nExplain why blood in the pulmonary vein contains more oxygen than blood in the pulmonary artery.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• blood in the pulmonary vein has (just) passed through the lungs / alveoli ✓\n• oxygen diffuses from the air in the alveoli into the blood (in the capillaries) ✓\nALLOW oxygen binds to haemoglobin in the lungs\nALLOW blood in the pulmonary artery has returned from the body where cells used oxygen in respiration` },
      { q: `Substances move into and out of cells by diffusion, osmosis or active transport.\n\nComplete the table to show how each substance moves.\nPut one tick (✓) in each row.\nMovement | Diffusion | Osmosis | Active transport\nCarbon dioxide moving from muscle cells into the blood | ☐ | ☐ | ☐\nWater moving into a red blood cell placed in distilled water | ☐ | ☐ | ☐\nGlucose moving from a low concentration in the gut into villus cells | ☐ | ☐ | ☐\nUrea moving from liver cells into the blood | ☐ | ☐ | ☐`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `Carbon dioxide – Diffusion\nWater – Osmosis\nGlucose – Active transport\nUrea – Diffusion\n• all four correct ✓✓\n• two or three correct ✓\nMore than one tick in a row cancels that row` },
      { q: `Flatworms are small animals that live in streams. They have no lungs, gills or blood system. Their bodies are very thin and flat.\nA student modelled a flatworm as a block of agar 2 cm long, 2 cm wide and 0.5 cm thick.\nA cube with sides of 2 cm has a surface area to volume ratio of 3 : 1.\n\nCalculate the surface area to volume ratio of the flatworm model and use your answer to explain why a flat shape helps the flatworm survive without a transport system.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• surface area = 2 × [(2 × 2) + (2 × 0.5) + (2 × 0.5)] = 12 (cm²) AND volume = 2 × 2 × 0.5 = 2 (cm³) ✓\n• ratio = 6 : 1 ✓\n• larger surface area to volume ratio than the cube / no cell is far from the surface, so oxygen can diffuse to all cells fast enough ✓\nALLOW ECF from an incorrect ratio for the third mark if compared correctly with 3 : 1` },
      { q: `The total surface area of the alveoli in an adult's lungs is about 70 m², roughly the area of half a tennis court. The barrier between the air in an alveolus and the blood is very thin, about 0.5 µm.\nBreathing moves air in and out of the lungs, and blood flows constantly through the capillaries around the alveoli.\n\nExplain how these features make gas exchange in the lungs efficient.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• large surface area so more oxygen / gas can diffuse at the same time ✓\n• thin barrier / one-cell-thick walls give a short diffusion distance (so diffusion is fast) ✓\n• breathing / ventilation and blood flow maintain a steep concentration gradient (of oxygen and carbon dioxide) ✓\nALLOW blood carries oxygen away so its concentration in the blood stays low` },
      { q: `The lining of the small intestine is covered in millions of villi. Each villus is a finger-like projection with a wall one cell thick. Each villus contains a network of blood capillaries.\n\nExplain how villi are adapted for absorbing digested food molecules.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (many villi) give a large surface area (for absorption) ✓\n• thin wall / one cell thick gives a short diffusion distance ✓\n• capillary network / good blood supply carries absorbed food away, maintaining a concentration gradient ✓\nALLOW microvilli further increase the surface area\nALLOW many mitochondria for active transport` },
      { q: `A scientist put samples of red blood cells into salt solutions of different concentrations. After 30 minutes she counted the percentage of cells that had burst.\n(data written for this practice question)\nSalt concentration (%) | Cells burst (%)\n0.3 | 100\n0.5 | 60\n0.7 | 10\n0.9 | 0\n1.5 | 0 (cells had shrunk)\n\nExplain why all the red blood cells burst in the 0.3% salt solution.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the solution was more dilute than the cell contents / had a higher water concentration than inside the cells ✓\n• water moved into the cells by osmosis through the partially permeable (cell) membrane ✓\n• animal cells have no cell wall, so the cells swelled until the membrane broke ✓\nALLOW water moves down its concentration gradient for the second mark if osmosis named` },
      { q: `After a meal, the concentration of glucose in the small intestine is high. Several hours later it is lower than in the blood, but glucose is still absorbed.\n(data written for this practice question)\nGlucose concentration in the small intestine = 2 mmol per dm³\nGlucose concentration in the blood = 5 mmol per dm³\n\nExplain how glucose is absorbed into the blood in this situation.\nUse the data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• active transport ✓\n• glucose moves against the concentration gradient / from 2 mmol per dm³ in the gut to 5 mmol per dm³ in the blood / from low to high concentration ✓\n• needs energy from respiration / ATP AND uses carrier proteins in the membrane ✓\nALLOW diffusion cannot take place because the concentration in the gut is lower` },
      { q: `Blood passes through the heart twice on each complete circuit of the body.\nThe statements describe the path of deoxygenated blood returning from the body.\nA Blood is pumped into the pulmonary artery.\nB Blood enters the right atrium from the vena cava.\nC Blood enters the left atrium from the pulmonary vein.\nD Blood passes into the right ventricle.\nE Blood is pumped out of the left ventricle into the aorta.\nF Blood picks up oxygen in the lungs.\n\nPut statements A to F in the correct order.\nWrite one letter in each box.`, marks: 3, command: 'Put', markScheme: `B → D → A → F → C → E\n• all six in the correct order ✓✓✓\n• four or five letters in the correct position ✓✓\n• two or three letters in the correct position ✓` },
      { q: `A student wanted to find out how the length of time spent exercising affects heart rate. She stepped on and off a step for 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 minutes, resting between each test, and measured her pulse straight after each test.\n\nFor this investigation, describe:\n• the independent variable\n• the dependent variable\n• a control variable that should be kept the same.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• independent: length of time spent exercising / stepping ✓\n• dependent: heart rate / pulse rate (beats per minute) ✓\n• control: any one from same height of step / same stepping speed / same person / same rest time before each test / pulse counted for the same length of time ✓\nIGNORE amount of exercise\nDO NOT ALLOW time for the dependent variable` },
      { q: `Cardiac output is the volume of blood pumped by the heart each minute.\ncardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate\nStroke volume is the volume of blood pumped out of the left ventricle in one beat.\n(data written for this practice question)\nA man has a stroke volume of 70 cm³ and a heart rate of 72 beats per minute.\n\nCalculate the man's cardiac output.\nGive your answer in dm³ per minute.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 70 × 72 ✓\n• = 5040 (cm³ per minute) ✓\n• = 5.04 (dm³ per minute) ✓\nALLOW 5.0\nCorrect answer with no working = 3 marks\nALLOW ECF for the conversion ÷ 1000` },
      { q: `A student looked at three different blood vessels, X, Y and Z, under a microscope and recorded their features.\n(data written for this practice question)\nVessel | Wall thickness | Width of lumen | Other features\nX | 1.0 mm | 4 mm | Thick layers of muscle and elastic tissue\nY | 0.5 mm | 5 mm | Valves present\nZ | 0.001 mm | 0.008 mm | Wall one cell thick\n\nIdentify each vessel X, Y and Z.\nGive a reason for each answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• X – artery AND thick muscular / elastic wall (to withstand high pressure) ✓\n• Y – vein AND has valves / thin wall with wide lumen ✓\n• Z – capillary AND wall one cell thick / very narrow lumen ✓\nName without a reason = 0 for that vessel` },
      { q: `Amylase is a carbohydrase enzyme that breaks down starch. A student mixed amylase with starch solution at different pH values at 37 °C. Every 30 seconds she tested a drop of the mixture with iodine solution to see when the starch had gone.\n(data written for this practice question)\npH | Time for starch to disappear (s)\n3 | starch still present after 600 s\n5 | 180\n7 | 60\n9 | 240\n\nExplain the results at pH 7 and at pH 3.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• pH 7 is (close to) the optimum pH for amylase / the enzyme works fastest so starch disappears in the shortest time ✓\n• at pH 3 the (shape of the) active site changes / the enzyme is denatured ✓\n• so starch no longer fits the active site / no enzyme–substrate complexes form, so starch is not broken down ✓\nIGNORE enzyme is killed\nALLOW lock and key ideas` },
      { q: `A student made cubes of agar jelly of different sizes to model how cells of different sizes take in substances.\n\nComplete the table.\nSide of cube (cm) | Surface area (cm²) | Volume (cm³) | Surface area to volume ratio\n1 | 6 | 1 | 6 : 1\n2 | ______ | 8 | ______\n4 | 96 | ______ | ______\nUse the completed table to describe what happens to the surface area to volume ratio as the cube gets bigger.`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• 24 (cm²) ✓\n• 3 : 1 ✓\n• 64 (cm³) AND 1.5 : 1 ✓\nALLOW 3 : 2 for 1.5 : 1\n• as the cube gets bigger the surface area to volume ratio decreases / gets smaller ✓\nALLOW ECF for ratios from incorrect surface area or volume` },
      { q: `This question is about red blood cells.\nA red blood cell is a disc about 7 µm across that is thinner in the middle than at the edge (a biconcave shape). It has no nucleus and is packed with haemoglobin. It is flexible.\n\nExplain how the structure of a red blood cell is adapted to its function.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• haemoglobin binds to / carries oxygen ✓\n• no nucleus so more room for haemoglobin / more oxygen can be carried ✓\n• biconcave shape gives a large surface area (to volume ratio) ✓\n• so oxygen diffuses in and out quickly / short diffusion distance to the centre of the cell ✓\n• flexible / small so it can squeeze through narrow capillaries ✓\nDO NOT ALLOW haemoglobin absorbs / is oxygen` },
      { q: `The table shows the composition of the air a person breathes in and out.\nGas | Air breathed in (%) | Air breathed out (%)\nOxygen | 21 | 16\nCarbon dioxide | 0.04 | 4\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in oxygen between air breathed in and air breathed out, and explain the changes in oxygen and carbon dioxide.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (21 − 16) ÷ 21 × 100 ✓\n• 23.8 (%) ✓\n• oxygen diffuses from the alveoli into the blood (to be used in respiration) ✓\n• carbon dioxide (made in respiration) diffuses from the blood into the alveoli ✓\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks for the calculation\nALLOW 24 (%) for 1 mark only\nALLOW down a concentration gradient for either explanation mark` },
      { q: `A runner's leg muscles need a constant supply of oxygen for aerobic respiration. Carbon dioxide made in the muscles must be removed from the body.\n\nDescribe and explain how oxygen gets from the air into a muscle cell in the runner's leg, and how carbon dioxide gets from the muscle cell back out of the body.\nIn your answer you should refer to the lungs, the blood, the heart and the blood vessels.*`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks)\nDescribes the full route of oxygen from the air to the muscle cell AND of carbon dioxide back to the air, AND explains how at least two exchange surfaces or structures are adapted.\nThere is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\n\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks)\nDescribes most of the route for oxygen OR carbon dioxide, with some explanation of diffusion or of one adaptation.\nThere is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks)\nGives simple statements about some parts of the route, e.g. oxygen goes into the blood in the lungs.\nThe information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n\n0 marks\nNo response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nOxygen\n• air breathed in through trachea / bronchi to the alveoli\n• oxygen diffuses from alveoli into blood in capillaries down a concentration gradient\n• alveoli: large surface area, thin walls, good blood supply, ventilation maintains gradient\n• oxygen binds to haemoglobin in red blood cells\n• blood goes in the pulmonary vein to the left atrium / left ventricle\n• left ventricle (thick muscular wall) pumps blood at high pressure into the aorta / arteries\n• arteries have thick elastic walls to withstand high pressure\n• capillaries in the muscle have walls one cell thick, short diffusion distance\n• oxygen diffuses from blood into muscle cells where its concentration is low\nCarbon dioxide\n• made in aerobic respiration in the muscle cells\n• diffuses into the blood / plasma in capillaries\n• carried in veins to the vena cava / right atrium / right ventricle\n• pumped through the pulmonary artery to the lungs\n• diffuses from the blood into the alveoli and is breathed out\n• double circulation: blood passes through the heart twice` }
    ]
  },
  'B5.2': {
    name: 'How does the nervous system help us respond to changes?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about the nervous system.\nThe nervous system is made up of the central nervous system (CNS) and the peripheral nervous system. One part of the CNS is protected inside the bones of the backbone.\n\nName this part of the CNS.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• spinal cord (1)\nIGNORE nerve / spine\nDO NOT ALLOW brain` },
      { q: `When you touch a hot object, impulses travel along three types of neurone before your arm muscles contract.\n\nWhich type of neurone carries impulses from the CNS to the muscles?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\nmotor neurone      relay neurone      sensory neurone`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• motor neurone (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `Light enters the eye through the pupil and is focused by the cornea and lens.\n\nName the part of the eye that contains the light receptor cells.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• retina (1)\nALLOW rods / cones\nIGNORE optic nerve / fovea` },
      { q: `Neurones are not joined directly to each other. There is a tiny gap, called a synapse, between one neurone and the next.\n\nWhat is the name of the chemical released by a neurone into the synapse?`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• (neuro)transmitter (substance) (1)\nALLOW a named transmitter, e.g. acetylcholine / dopamine / serotonin\nIGNORE hormone / enzyme` },
      { q: `Two students measure reaction time using a ruler. One student holds a 30 cm ruler just above the open hand of the other student and lets go. The second student catches the ruler as quickly as possible.\nThe student holding the ruler is told to drop it at a random time, without counting down.\n\nSuggest why the ruler is dropped without warning.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• so the catcher cannot predict / anticipate when it will drop (so they react only to seeing it fall) (1)\nALLOW so the catcher cannot cheat / guess\nIGNORE 'to make it a fair test'` },
      { q: `This question is about the brain.\nA woman has a stroke that damages her cerebellum. After the stroke she can still talk and remember things, but she finds it hard to walk in a straight line and often loses her balance.\n\nSuggest what the cerebellum controls.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• balance / coordination of (muscle) movement (1)\nALLOW posture / fine control of movement\nIGNORE 'walking' alone` },
      { q: `This question is about how we respond to changes.\nA cyclist sees a traffic light turn red and presses the brakes.\n\nComplete the sentences.\nUse words from the list.\neffector      gland      muscle      receptor      stimulus\nThe change in the colour of the light is the ______ .\nThe ______ cells in the eye detect the change in light.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• stimulus (1)\n• receptor (1)\nBoth in the correct order for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student uses the ruler-drop test to measure his reaction time. He records the distance the ruler falls before he catches it (data written for this practice question).\nDistance fallen in cm: 15.2, 12.8, 14.0, 13.6, 14.4\n\nCalculate the mean distance fallen.\nMean = ______ cm`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (15.2 + 12.8 + 14.0 + 13.6 + 14.4) ÷ 5 / 70.0 ÷ 5 (1)\n• 14.0 (cm) (1)\nALLOW 14\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `The time taken for a ruler to fall can be calculated from the distance it falls using the equation:\nreaction time = √(2 × distance ÷ 9.8)\nwhere the distance is in metres and the time is in seconds.\nA student's ruler falls 0.14 m before she catches it (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate her reaction time.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nReaction time = ______ s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• √(2 × 0.14 ÷ 9.8) / √0.0286 (1)\n• 0.17 (s) (1)\nALLOW 1 mark for 0.169 / 0.1690… not rounded to 2 s.f.\nDO NOT ALLOW 0.2 for 2 marks` },
      { q: `In a sensory neurone that runs from the big toe to the spinal cord, an impulse travels 0.9 m in 0.015 s (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the speed of the impulse.\nUse the equation: speed = distance ÷ time\nSpeed = ______ m/s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 0.9 ÷ 0.015 (1)\n• 60 (m/s) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `When a person accidentally steps on a drawing pin, they lift their foot before they feel any pain.\n\nExplain why this reflex response is faster than a response that involves thinking about what to do.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• impulse does not pass to / through the conscious part of the brain / goes through the spinal cord only (1)\n• (so) fewer neurones / fewer synapses / shorter pathway (1)\nALLOW no time is spent deciding / processing a response\nIGNORE 'it is automatic' alone` },
      { q: `Many neurones in the human body are surrounded by a fatty layer called the myelin sheath. In multiple sclerosis (MS) the myelin sheath is damaged.\n\nSuggest how damage to the myelin sheath affects the way impulses travel along neurones.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• impulses travel more slowly / are delayed / are blocked (1)\n• because the myelin sheath insulates the neurone / the impulse can no longer jump along the axon / impulse may leak to other neurones (1)\nALLOW messages to muscles are weaker / do not arrive` },
      { q: `At a synapse, transmitter molecules are released from the end of one neurone. Receptor molecules for the transmitter are found only on the membrane of the next neurone.\n\nExplain why impulses can only travel in one direction across a synapse.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• transmitter is only released from the first / pre-synaptic neurone (1)\n• receptors are only on the second / post-synaptic neurone (so only it can be stimulated) (1)` },
      { q: `Curare is a poison made by some South American plants. Curare molecules have a similar shape to the transmitter released by motor neurones at muscles. Curare binds to the receptors on the muscle cells but does not cause an impulse.\n\nExplain why a person poisoned with curare cannot move their muscles.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• curare blocks the receptors / transmitter cannot bind to the receptors (1)\n• (so) no impulse is started in the muscle / muscle is not stimulated to contract (1)\nALLOW muscles are paralysed for second mark only if linked to no impulse / no contraction` },
      { q: `Nicotine is a drug found in tobacco. Nicotine molecules have a similar shape to a transmitter substance found at some synapses in the brain.\n\nSuggest how nicotine increases the number of impulses in the neurones after these synapses.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• nicotine binds to the (transmitter) receptors (on the next neurone) (1)\n• (and) triggers / starts an impulse (even without the transmitter being released) (1)\nALLOW nicotine mimics / acts like the transmitter` },
      { q: `A person walks out of a dark room into bright sunlight. The size of their pupils changes.\n\nDescribe how the muscles of the iris bring about this change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• circular muscles (of the iris) contract (1)\n• radial muscles relax (and the pupil gets smaller / constricts) (1)\nALLOW 1 mark for 'pupil gets smaller' if no muscles are described correctly\nDO NOT ALLOW 'iris contracts'` },
      { q: `Sam is short-sighted. He can see the words in a book clearly, but the board at the front of the classroom looks blurred.\nSam's eyeball is slightly longer than normal.\n\nExplain why distant objects look blurred to Sam.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• light (rays) from distant objects are focused / meet in front of the retina (1)\n• (so) the image on the retina is not focused / is blurred (1)\nALLOW the lens / cornea refracts the light too much` },
      { q: `Priya is long-sighted. She wears glasses to read.\n\nWhat type of lens is used in Priya's glasses and how does it correct her vision?`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• converging / convex lens (1)\n• (refracts / bends light inwards before it enters the eye so) light is focused on the retina (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW diverging / concave` },
      { q: `A student investigates whether drinking a cola drink containing caffeine affects reaction time. He measures his reaction time using a ruler-drop test before and 20 minutes after drinking the cola.\n\nIdentify the independent variable and the dependent variable in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• independent: whether or not cola / caffeine has been drunk / caffeine intake (1)\n• dependent: reaction time / distance ruler falls (before it is caught) (1)\nIGNORE time alone` },
      { q: `In the caffeine investigation, the student's mean reaction time was 0.24 s before drinking cola and 0.21 s after drinking cola (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in reaction time.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (0.24 − 0.21) ÷ 0.24 × 100 / 0.03 ÷ 0.24 × 100 (1)\n• 12.5 (%) (1)\nALLOW 13 / 12\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `Scientists use functional MRI (fMRI) scans to find out which parts of the brain are working while a person does a task. An fMRI scanner detects where blood flow to the brain increases.\n\nExplain why the areas with increased blood flow are the active areas of the brain.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• active (brain) cells / neurones respire more / faster (1)\n• (so) need more oxygen / glucose (delivered by the blood) (1)\nALLOW need more energy for 1 mark only if not linked to respiration` },
      { q: `A man has damaged part of his spinal cord in a fall. Doctors explain that the damage to his nervous system may be permanent.\n\nSuggest two reasons why damage to the central nervous system is difficult to repair.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any 2 from:\n• neurones do not (usually) divide / regenerate / grow back (1)\n• CNS is hard to reach / surgery risks causing more damage (1)\n• CNS is very complex / neurones have many connections that are hard to re-make (1)\nALLOW stem cell treatments are still being developed / not yet available` },
      { q: `A reflex arc involves five parts. They are listed below in the wrong order.\nA motor neurone\nB receptor\nC relay neurone\nD effector\nE sensory neurone\n\nPut the letters in the correct order, starting with the stimulus.\nWrite one letter in each box.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• B E C A D (2)\nALLOW 1 mark for B E first two correct OR A D last two correct` },
      { q: `When you look up from a book to look at a distant tree, your eyes change focus.\n\nComplete the sentences to describe how the eye focuses on the distant tree.\nPut a ring around the correct options.\nThe ciliary muscles contract / relax and the suspensory ligaments become slack / tight.\nThe lens becomes thinner / fatter and refracts light less / more.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• relax AND tight (1)\n• thinner AND less (1)` },
      { q: `A student repeats a ruler-drop reaction time test five times (data written for this practice question).\nReaction times in s: 0.19, 0.22, 0.21, 0.17, 0.21\n\nCalculate the mean reaction time and its uncertainty.\nUse half the range as the uncertainty.\nMean = ______ ± ______ s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• mean = 0.20 (s) (1)\n• uncertainty = (0.22 − 0.17) ÷ 2 = 0.025 (s) (1)\nALLOW ± 0.03` },
      { q: `A woman's spinal cord is damaged in her neck. She can no longer feel her legs or move them, even though the neurones in her legs are not damaged.\n\nExplain why she cannot feel or move her legs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• impulses from (receptors in) the legs / sensory neurones cannot reach the brain (so cannot feel) (1)\n• impulses from the brain cannot reach the motor neurones / muscles (effectors) in the legs (so cannot move) (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about synapses.\nAn electrical impulse arrives at the end of a neurone. The next neurone is on the other side of a synapse.\n\nDescribe how the impulse is passed across the synapse to the next neurone.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (impulse causes) transmitter / chemical to be released (from the first neurone) (1)\n• transmitter diffuses across the gap / synapse (1)\n• transmitter binds to receptors on the next neurone, starting a new (electrical) impulse (1)\nIGNORE impulse jumps across the gap` },
      { q: `A teacher gives a class this table to convert the distance a ruler falls into a reaction time.\nDistance fallen (cm) | Reaction time (s)\n5 | 0.10\n10 | 0.14\n15 | 0.17\n20 | 0.20\n25 | 0.23\nA student catches the ruler at these distances (data written for this practice question):\n15 cm, 10 cm, 20 cm, 15 cm, 10 cm\n\nUse the table to calculate the student's mean reaction time.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nMean reaction time = ______ s`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• converts each distance: 0.17, 0.14, 0.20, 0.17, 0.14 (1)\n• (0.17 + 0.14 + 0.20 + 0.17 + 0.14) ÷ 5 / 0.82 ÷ 5 = 0.164 (1)\n• 0.16 (s) (1)\nALLOW mean distance 14 cm read from table / interpolated to 0.16–0.17 s for 2 marks max` },
      { q: `A gardener grabs a stinging nettle and immediately lets go of it.\n\nExplain how a reflex arc brings about this response.\nUse ideas about the neurones involved.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• receptor (in skin) detects the stimulus / sting and impulse travels along a sensory neurone to the spinal cord / CNS (1)\n• impulse passes (across a synapse) to a relay neurone (in the spinal cord) (1)\n• (across a synapse) to a motor neurone, which carries impulse to the muscle / effector, which contracts (so hand lets go) (1)` },
      { q: `A student looks up from her phone at a bird in the sky, then looks back down at her phone. Her eyes change their focus each time.\n\nExplain how the eye focuses light from the phone on the retina.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• ciliary muscles contract (1)\n• suspensory ligaments slacken / loosen (1)\n• lens becomes fatter / more rounded so refracts / bends light more (onto the retina) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 'lens muscles' for ciliary muscles` },
      { q: `Serotonin is a transmitter substance found at some synapses in the brain. After it has been released, serotonin is normally taken back up into the first neurone.\nSome antidepressant drugs stop serotonin being taken back up into the first neurone.\n\nExplain how these drugs affect the transmission of impulses at these synapses.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• serotonin stays in the synapse / gap for longer / concentration of serotonin in the synapse increases (1)\n• (so) more serotonin binds to the receptors (on the next neurone) / receptors stimulated for longer (1)\n• (so) more impulses / impulses for longer in the next neurone (1)` },
      { q: `A group of students tested the reaction times of three people before and after drinking a can of cola containing caffeine (data written for this practice question).\nMean reaction time before cola (s): 0.25, 0.27, 0.26\nMean reaction time after cola (s): 0.22, 0.24, 0.23\nThe students conclude: "Caffeine makes everyone react faster."\n\nEvaluate the students' conclusion.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• supports: all three people had a shorter reaction time after cola / mean fell from 0.26 s to 0.23 s (1)\n• against: only three people tested / sample too small to say 'everyone' (1)\n• against: cola contains other substances, e.g. sugar, so cannot be sure it was the caffeine / people may have improved with practice / no control group (1)` },
      { q: `Doctors have learned about the functions of brain regions by studying people whose brains have been damaged by injury or disease.\n\nExplain why this method has limitations for finding out the function of a brain region.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any 3 from:\n• damage is often to more than one region / does not have clear boundaries (1)\n• different regions are interconnected / a function may involve several regions (1)\n• the brain may adapt / other regions may take over a function after damage (1)\n• few patients have damage in exactly the same place so hard to compare / small samples (1)\n• patients may not be able to give consent / ethical issues (1)` },
      { q: `When a bright light is shone into a person's eye, their pupil gets smaller. This is a reflex.\n\nExplain how the nervous system brings about this response.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• light receptors in the retina detect (bright) light (1)\n• impulses travel along sensory neurones / optic nerve to the brain / CNS (1)\n• impulses travel along motor neurones to the iris (muscles) / circular muscles contract (so pupil constricts) (1)\nIGNORE 'iris contracts'` },
      { q: `Impulses travel at about 100 m/s along neurones with a myelin sheath and at about 2 m/s along neurones without a myelin sheath (data written for this practice question).\nA neurone running from the foot to the spinal cord is 1.2 m long.\n\nCalculate how many times longer an impulse would take to travel along this neurone if it had no myelin sheath.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• time with myelin = 1.2 ÷ 100 = 0.012 s (1)\n• time without myelin = 1.2 ÷ 2 = 0.6 s (1)\n• 0.6 ÷ 0.012 = 50 (times longer) (1)\nALLOW 100 ÷ 2 = 50 for 3 marks` },
      { q: `Mr Jones is 65. He finds he has to hold his newspaper further away from his eyes to read it. His optician tells him he is long-sighted because his lens has become less flexible.\n\nExplain why Mr Jones cannot see near objects clearly and how his vision can be corrected.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• lens cannot become fat / rounded enough / cannot refract light enough (1)\n• (so) light from near objects is focused behind the retina / image on retina is blurred (1)\n• corrected with converging / convex lens (glasses / contact lenses) (1)\nALLOW laser surgery / lens replacement for third mark` },
      { q: `A motor neurone carries impulses from the spinal cord to a muscle in the foot. It has a cell body, dendrites, a long axon covered by a myelin sheath, and nerve endings at the muscle.\n\nExplain how the structure of the motor neurone is related to its function.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any 4 from:\n• long axon so impulses carried a long distance / from the CNS to the effector (1)\n• myelin sheath insulates the axon (1)\n• (so) impulses travel faster (1)\n• dendrites / branched endings connect with many other neurones / receive impulses from relay neurones (1)\n• nerve endings release transmitter to stimulate the muscle (1)\n• cell body contains nucleus / mitochondria to make transmitter / release energy (1)` },
      { q: `Two students want to use a 30 cm ruler to compare the reaction times of their right hand and their left hand.\n\nDescribe a method they could use.\nIn your answer you should include how they would make their results repeatable and how they would process the data.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any 4 from:\n• one student holds the ruler vertically with the zero mark level with the top of the other student's thumb / between finger and thumb (1)\n• ruler dropped without warning and the catcher catches it as quickly as possible (1)\n• read / record the distance at the top of the thumb (1)\n• repeat several times (e.g. 5) for each hand (1)\n• control: same catcher / same ruler / same starting gap between finger and thumb / arm resting on table edge / same conditions (1)\n• calculate a mean distance and convert to reaction time (using a table / equation) (1)` },
      { q: `Scientists measured the mean reaction time of a group of volunteers after different numbers of hours of sleep (data written for this practice question).\nHours of sleep | Mean reaction time (s)\n8 | 0.21\n6 | 0.24\n4 | 0.29\n2 | 0.36\n\nDescribe what the data shows and calculate the percentage increase in reaction time between 8 hours and 2 hours of sleep.\nSuggest one reason why lack of sleep could be dangerous for a car driver.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• the less sleep, the longer the reaction time / reaction time increases more quickly as sleep decreases (1)\n• (0.36 − 0.21) ÷ 0.21 × 100 (1)\n• 71(.4) (%) (1)\n• driver takes longer to respond / brake when a hazard appears so more likely to crash (1)` },
      { q: `This question is about responses to stimuli.*\nA chef picks up a hot pan with a bare hand. If the pan is empty, the chef drops it immediately. If the pan contains food the chef has just cooked, the chef may hold on to it for a moment and put it down carefully.\n\nExplain how the nervous system brings about both of these responses.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• the pathway of impulses in each response\n• how impulses pass between neurones\n• why one response is faster than the other.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of the reflex pathway AND how the brain overrides it, including synaptic transmission and a clear reason for the difference in speed. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes the reflex pathway with some detail AND gives some explanation of either the conscious response or synapses. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about receptors, neurones or the reflex being fast. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nReflex (dropping)\n• temperature / pain receptors in the skin detect heat\n• impulse along sensory neurone to spinal cord\n• relay neurone in spinal cord, then motor neurone\n• effector is arm / hand muscle, which contracts\n• automatic, no conscious thought\nConscious / overridden response\n• impulse also sent up the spinal cord to the brain\n• brain (cerebral cortex) makes a decision\n• brain sends impulses down spinal cord to motor neurones that stop / override the reflex\nSynapses\n• transmitter released, diffuses across gap, binds to receptors on next neurone\n• some brain neurones release transmitter that stops / inhibits an impulse\nSpeed\n• reflex has fewer neurones / synapses / shorter pathway\n• diffusion across each synapse takes time, so the longer brain pathway is slower` }
    ]
  },
  'B5.3': {
    name: 'How do hormones control responses in the human body?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about the endocrine system.\nThe endocrine system is made up of glands that secrete hormones. One gland in the neck secretes a hormone that controls the rate of metabolism.\n\nName this gland.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• thyroid (gland) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW thymus / pituitary / adrenal` },
      { q: `Hormones are secreted by glands. The hormones then reach target organs in other parts of the body.\n\nHow are hormones transported from a gland to their target organ?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• in the blood / in (blood) plasma (1)\nALLOW bloodstream / circulatory system\nDO NOT ALLOW along nerves / in neurones` },
      { q: `The menstrual cycle is controlled by four hormones made in the pituitary gland and the ovaries.\n\nWhich hormone causes a mature egg to be released from the ovary?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\nFSH      LH      oestrogen      progesterone`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• LH (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `A doctor is treating a woman who cannot become pregnant because the eggs in her ovaries do not mature.\nThe doctor prescribes a fertility drug that contains a hormone normally made by the pituitary gland.\n\nName the hormone in the fertility drug that causes eggs to mature in the ovary.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• FSH / follicle stimulating hormone (1)\nIGNORE LH (alone)\nDO NOT ALLOW oestrogen / progesterone` },
      { q: `A contraceptive implant is a small rod placed under the skin of the arm. It releases progesterone slowly and works for up to three years.\n\nSuggest one advantage of using an implant rather than taking a contraceptive pill.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• the woman does not have to remember to take it every day (1)\nALLOW lasts a long time / no daily action needed / cannot be forgotten so more reliable\nIGNORE cheaper / safer (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A graph shows the concentration of LH in the blood of a woman during one 28-day menstrual cycle (data written for this practice question).\nThe LH concentration stays low from day 1 to day 11. It rises very sharply to a peak on day 14, then falls back to a low level by day 16 and stays low until day 28.\n\nOn which day did ovulation most likely happen?`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• day 14 (1)\nALLOW day 14–15\nDO NOT ALLOW day 1 / day 28` },
      { q: `This question is about how the body uses hormones and nerves.\n\nComplete the sentences about the endocrine system.\nPut a ring around the correct options.\nHormones are chemical / electrical messengers.\nHormones are secreted by glands / neurones directly into the blood.\nHormones only affect cells that have the correct enzymes / receptors.\nCompared with nervous responses, hormonal responses usually last for a longer / shorter time.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• chemical; glands; receptors; longer\nAll 4 correct = 2 marks\n2 or 3 correct = 1 mark\n0 or 1 correct = 0 marks` },
      { q: `The human body has several endocrine glands.\n\nDraw lines to connect each gland to the hormone it secretes.\nGlands: Adrenal gland; Ovary; Pituitary gland; Thyroid gland\nHormones: Adrenaline; FSH; Oestrogen; Thyroxine`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw lines', markScheme: `• adrenal gland – adrenaline\n• ovary – oestrogen\n• pituitary gland – FSH\n• thyroid gland – thyroxine\nAll 4 correct = 2 marks\n2 or 3 correct = 1 mark\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one line from a gland` },
      { q: `A cyclist sees a car pull out in front of her. She brakes immediately. A few seconds later she notices her heart is pounding, and it keeps pounding for several minutes.\nBraking is controlled by the nervous system. The pounding heart is caused by the hormone adrenaline.\n\nGive two differences between the nervous response and the hormonal response in this example.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• nervous response is faster / hormonal response is slower (to start) (1)\n• nervous response is shorter-lasting / hormonal response lasts longer (1)\n• nervous response uses electrical impulses, hormonal response uses chemicals (1)\n• nervous impulses travel along neurones, hormones travel in the blood (1)\n• nervous response acts on a precise area / hormone can act on many organs (1)\nALLOW comparisons given either way round\nDO NOT ALLOW one-sided statements, e.g. 'nerves are fast' with no comparison` },
      { q: `Thyroxine is secreted into the blood and travels to every part of the body. Adrenaline also travels all round the body, but only some organs respond to it.\n\nExplain why a hormone affects only certain cells, even though it reaches every cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• only target cells / organs have receptors for that hormone (1)\n• the hormone binds to / fits the receptor (and this triggers a response) (1)\nALLOW receptor is complementary to the hormone\nIGNORE 'the hormone knows where to go'` },
      { q: `A student measured his resting heart rate. He then watched a frightening film clip, which caused adrenaline to be released into his blood. His heart rate was measured again (data written for this practice question).\nResting heart rate = 72 beats per minute\nHeart rate during the film clip = 126 beats per minute\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the student's heart rate.\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• increase = 126 − 72 = 54 (beats per minute) (1)\n• (54 ÷ 72) × 100 = 75 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer of 75 with no working = 2 marks\nALLOW ecf for second mark from an incorrect subtraction` },
      { q: `This question is about adrenaline.\nWhen a person is frightened, adrenaline is released from the adrenal glands. Adrenaline increases heart rate and breathing rate.\n\nExplain how an increased heart rate helps the person to run away from danger.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• more (oxygenated) blood / oxygen and glucose delivered to (muscle) cells (1)\n• so (aerobic) respiration in muscles increases, releasing more energy (for contraction) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 'produces energy' / 'makes energy'\nIGNORE 'more energy' without respiration` },
      { q: `Adrenaline acts on the liver. It causes the liver to convert stored glycogen into glucose, which is released into the blood.\n\nExplain why this effect is useful in a 'fight or flight' situation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• raises blood glucose concentration / more glucose available to (muscle) cells (1)\n• glucose is used in respiration to release energy for (muscle) contraction / movement (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW energy is made / produced` },
      { q: `Thyroxine is a hormone that controls the basal metabolic rate. The basal metabolic rate is the rate of chemical reactions in the body when it is at rest.\nA person whose thyroid gland makes too little thyroxine often feels cold, even in a warm room.\n\nExplain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (less thyroxine means) lower metabolic rate / less respiration (in cells) (1)\n• so less energy is released / transferred as heat (to keep the body warm) (1)\nALLOW fewer chemical reactions so less thermal energy released\nDO NOT ALLOW less heat is made / produced (by itself) — must link to reactions or respiration` },
      { q: `The amount of thyroxine in the blood is controlled by negative feedback. The pituitary gland secretes TSH, which stimulates the thyroid gland to secrete thyroxine.\nA scientist measures a high thyroxine concentration in a person's blood.\n\nDescribe how the body responds to bring the thyroxine concentration back to normal.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• high thyroxine inhibits / reduces (secretion of) TSH (from the pituitary) (1)\n• so the thyroid secretes less thyroxine (and the level falls) (1)\nALLOW hypothalamus releases less TRH so less TSH\nIGNORE 'the body makes less thyroxine' with no mechanism for first mark` },
      { q: `A graph shows the thickness of the lining of the uterus during one menstrual cycle (data written for this practice question).\nThe lining breaks down from day 1 to day 5. From day 5 to day 14 the lining becomes thicker. The lining stays thick from day 14 to day 26.\n\nName the hormone that causes the lining to thicken from day 5 to day 14, and state where it is made.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• oestrogen (1)\n• ovary / ovaries (1)\nALLOW developing follicle in ovary for second mark\nDO NOT ALLOW pituitary for second mark` },
      { q: `Progesterone keeps the lining of the uterus thick after ovulation. If the egg is not fertilised, the concentration of progesterone in the blood falls at about day 25 of the cycle.\n\nExplain what happens to the uterus lining when the progesterone concentration falls.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the lining is no longer maintained / it breaks down (1)\n• and is lost from the body (with blood) as a period / menstruation (1)\nALLOW shed / comes away` },
      { q: `Asha records the first day of her period in a diary (data written for this practice question).\nOne period started on 3 March. The next period started on 31 March.\nOvulation usually happens about 14 days before the next period starts.\n\nCalculate the length of Asha's menstrual cycle, and give the date on which she most likely ovulated.\nCycle length = ______ days\nDate of ovulation = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 28 (days) (1)\n• 17 March (1)\nALLOW 16–18 March\nALLOW ecf for date from an incorrect cycle length (14 days before 31 March)` },
      { q: `The combined contraceptive pill contains oestrogen and progesterone. A woman takes one pill every day.\n\nExplain how the hormones in the combined pill prevent pregnancy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (oestrogen / progesterone) inhibit FSH (and LH) secretion (from the pituitary) (1)\n• so no eggs mature / no ovulation (1)\nALLOW thickens mucus in cervix so sperm cannot reach egg as alternative second mark\nDO NOT ALLOW kills sperm / kills eggs` },
      { q: `Some couples use condoms as a method of contraception. Condoms are a barrier method. They do not contain hormones.\n\nGive one advantage of using condoms instead of a hormonal method of contraception, and explain why it is an advantage.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any one advantage with explanation:\n• protects against sexually transmitted infections (1) because it stops body fluids / pathogens passing between partners (1)\n• no hormonal side effects (1) e.g. no mood changes / weight gain / increased risk of blood clots (1)\n• only used when needed (1) so no daily pill / no long-term effect on the woman's cycle (1)\n• can be used by the man (1) so responsibility is shared (1)\nIGNORE cheaper (unqualified)` },
      { q: `A survey followed 100 couples who used male condoms as their only method of contraception for one year (data written for this practice question).\nDuring the year, 18 of the women became pregnant.\n\nCalculate the percentage of couples for whom condoms prevented pregnancy.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 100 − 18 = 82 (couples) (1)\n• (82 ÷ 100) × 100 = 82 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer of 82 with no working = 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 18 (%) (percentage who became pregnant)` },
      { q: `A fertility clinic recorded the results of in vitro fertilisation (IVF) treatment for one year (data written for this practice question).\nNumber of IVF treatment cycles = 2400\nNumber of treatment cycles that led to a live birth = 696\n\nCalculate the success rate of IVF at this clinic as a percentage.\nSuccess rate = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 696 ÷ 2400 (× 100) (1)\n• = 29 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer of 29 with no working = 2 marks\nALLOW 0.29 for 1 mark` },
      { q: `In IVF treatment, eggs are collected from a woman's ovaries and fertilised by sperm in a dish. Before the eggs are collected, the woman is given injections of FSH and LH.\n\nExplain why she is given FSH and LH.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• FSH stimulates (several) eggs / follicles to mature (1)\n• LH stimulates the release of the eggs / ovulation (so eggs can be collected) (1)\nALLOW so that many eggs are available for collection / fertilisation\nDO NOT ALLOW FSH and LH make eggs` },
      { q: `Nerve impulses in a motor neurone can travel at about 100 m/s (data written for this practice question).\nA nerve impulse travels 1.5 m from the spinal cord to a muscle in the foot.\n\nCalculate the time taken for the nerve impulse to travel this distance.\nUse the equation: time = distance ÷ speed\nTime = ______ s`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1.5 ÷ 100 (1)\n• = 0.015 (s) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks\nALLOW 1.5 × 10⁻² s / 15 ms` },
      { q: `Progesterone-only contraceptive methods include an injection given every 13 weeks. The injection does not contain oestrogen.\n\nDescribe two ways that progesterone can prevent pregnancy.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (makes) mucus in the cervix thicker so sperm cannot pass through / reach the egg (1)\n• inhibits FSH / LH so eggs do not mature / are not released / no ovulation (1)\nALLOW thins the uterus lining so an embryo cannot implant\nDO NOT ALLOW kills sperm` },
      { q: `The pituitary gland is a small gland at the base of the brain. It secretes TSH, FSH and LH into the blood.\nThe pituitary gland is sometimes called the 'master gland'.\n\nSuggest why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• its hormones (e.g. TSH / FSH / LH) act on other (endocrine) glands (1)\n• and stimulate these glands to secrete their own hormones, e.g. thyroid secretes thyroxine / ovaries secrete oestrogen (1)\nALLOW it controls / coordinates other glands for first mark\nIGNORE 'it is in the brain'` },
      { q: `Thyroxine is a hormone that contains iodine. People get iodine from foods such as fish and dairy products.\nIn some parts of the world, diets contain very little iodine. Many people there develop a goitre, which is a swelling of the thyroid gland caused by the thyroid being stimulated more than normal.\n\nExplain why a diet low in iodine leads to a goitre.\nUse ideas about negative feedback in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (without iodine) the thyroid cannot make enough thyroxine / thyroxine level is low (1)\n• low thyroxine does not inhibit (secretion of) TSH / pituitary keeps secreting TSH (1)\n• so (high) TSH keeps stimulating the thyroid (which grows / swells) (1)\nALLOW TRH from hypothalamus keeps stimulating pituitary\nIGNORE 'thyroid works harder' without reference to TSH` },
      { q: `The hypothalamus in the brain secretes a hormone called TRH. TRH stimulates the pituitary gland to secrete TSH. TSH stimulates the thyroid gland to secrete thyroxine.\nA person's thyroxine concentration falls below normal.\n\nExplain how negative feedback returns the thyroxine concentration to normal.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• low thyroxine is detected (by the hypothalamus / pituitary) (1)\n• more TRH / TSH is secreted (1)\n• which stimulates the thyroid to secrete more thyroxine (so the level rises back to normal) (1)\nALLOW less inhibition of TSH for second mark\nDO NOT ALLOW the thyroid detects the change (for first mark)` },
      { q: `A student writes: 'Hormones act more slowly than nerves, so hormonal control is less useful to the body than nervous control.'\n\nEvaluate the student's statement.\nUse examples in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• correct that hormones act more slowly / nerves give rapid responses, e.g. reflexes / pulling hand from heat (1)\n• but hormone effects last longer / are widespread, so are suited to long-term control, e.g. thyroxine / metabolic rate / menstrual cycle / growth (1)\n• conclusion: each system suits different jobs, so hormones are not less useful (1)\nALLOW adrenaline as example of a hormone that prepares many organs at once` },
      { q: `A graph shows the concentrations of FSH, LH and oestrogen in a woman's blood during one 28-day cycle (data written for this practice question).\nOestrogen rises steadily from day 5 and reaches a peak on day 12. FSH rises from day 1 to day 6, then falls until day 11. LH is low until day 11, then rises sharply to a peak on day 14.\n\nUse the data to explain how oestrogen controls the release of an egg.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• oestrogen rises / peaks just before LH peaks (day 12 then day 14) (1)\n• high oestrogen stimulates the pituitary to secrete LH / causes LH surge (1)\n• LH causes ovulation / release of the egg (on day 14) (1)\nALLOW oestrogen inhibits FSH so no more eggs mature as an additional credit-worthy point in place of first mark` },
      { q: `FSH is secreted by the pituitary gland at the start of each menstrual cycle. FSH makes a follicle containing an egg mature in the ovary. The maturing follicle secretes oestrogen.\nUsually only one egg matures in each cycle.\n\nExplain why the FSH concentration falls in the middle part of the cycle, and why this means usually only one egg matures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• oestrogen (concentration) rises (as follicle matures) (1)\n• oestrogen inhibits FSH secretion (by the pituitary) (1)\n• so no further follicles / eggs are stimulated to mature (1)\nALLOW negative feedback for second mark if explained` },
      { q: `If an egg is fertilised and an embryo implants in the uterus, the concentration of progesterone in the woman's blood stays high for the rest of the pregnancy.\n\nExplain why a pregnant woman does not have periods and does not release more eggs.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• progesterone maintains the uterus lining (1)\n• so the lining does not break down / no menstruation (1)\n• progesterone inhibits FSH / LH so no more eggs mature / are released (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW progesterone kills eggs` },
      { q: `One type of combined contraceptive pill is taken every day for 21 days. The woman then takes no pills for 7 days before starting the next pack. During these 7 days she has a bleed like a period.\n\nExplain why the bleed happens during the 7 pill-free days.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• no pills so the concentration of (progesterone / oestrogen) hormones in the blood falls (1)\n• the uterus lining is no longer maintained (1)\n• so the lining breaks down and is lost (1)\nALLOW 'it mimics the fall in progesterone at the end of a normal cycle'` },
      { q: `Statements A to F describe the stages in IVF treatment.\nA Embryos develop in the laboratory for a few days.\nB The woman is given FSH and LH.\nC Eggs are collected from the ovaries.\nD One or two embryos are placed in the woman's uterus.\nE Eggs are mixed with sperm from the father.\nF Eggs mature in the ovaries.\n\nPut statements A to F in the correct order.\nThe first one has been done for you.\nB → ___ → ___ → ___ → ___ → ___`, marks: 3, command: 'Order', markScheme: `• B, F, C, E, A, D\nAll five letters in the correct place = 3 marks\n3 or 4 letters in the correct place = 2 marks\n1 or 2 letters in the correct place = 1 mark` },
      { q: `A woman is treated with a fertility drug that contains FSH. Several of her eggs mature and are released at the same time.\nDoctors monitor women given these drugs very closely.\n\nExplain why a woman given FSH as a fertility drug has an increased chance of having twins or triplets, and suggest why this is a risk.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• FSH causes many / more than one egg to mature (1)\n• so more than one egg may be released and fertilised (1)\n• multiple pregnancies have greater risk, e.g. premature birth / low birth weight / miscarriage / health risk to mother (1)\nALLOW identical twins are not caused by this, but non-identical twins are, as a correct expansion` },
      { q: `An athlete's blood was tested just before the start of a race and again when she was resting the next day (data written for this practice question).\nJust before race: heart rate 104 beats per minute; blood glucose 6.4 mmol/dm³; breathing rate 22 breaths per minute\nResting next day: heart rate 64 beats per minute; blood glucose 4.8 mmol/dm³; breathing rate 14 breaths per minute\n\nExplain how adrenaline caused the results just before the race and how these changes prepare the athlete for the race.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• adrenaline increases heart rate and breathing rate (shown by 104 vs 64 / 22 vs 14) (1)\n• adrenaline causes liver to convert glycogen to glucose so blood glucose is higher (6.4 vs 4.8) (1)\n• more oxygen and glucose reach muscles for (faster) respiration / more energy released for contraction (1)\nAt least one data comparison needed for full marks\nDO NOT ALLOW energy produced` },
      { q: `This question is about the hormones that control the menstrual cycle.\n\nComplete the table.\nColumns: Hormone | Where it is secreted | One effect in the menstrual cycle\nFSH | pituitary gland | ______\nLH | ______ | causes ovulation\nOestrogen | ovaries | ______\nProgesterone | ______ | maintains the uterus lining`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• FSH effect: causes an egg / follicle to mature (in the ovary) (1)\nALLOW stimulates ovary to secrete oestrogen\n• LH: pituitary (gland) (1)\n• oestrogen effect: makes the uterus lining thicken / repair (1)\nALLOW stimulates LH / inhibits FSH\n• progesterone: ovaries / corpus luteum (1)\nALLOW empty follicle in ovary\nDO NOT ALLOW uterus for progesterone` },
      { q: `A woman visits her doctor because she is always tired, has gained weight and feels cold. The doctor arranges a blood test (data written for this practice question).\nThyroxine: patient 7 pmol/dm³; normal range 12–22 pmol/dm³\nTSH: patient 9.8 units; normal range 0.4–4.0 units\n\nUse the data to explain what is wrong with the woman, and suggest how she could be treated.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• thyroxine is below the normal range (7 compared with 12–22) / TSH is above the normal range (9.8 compared with 0.4–4.0) (1)\n• the thyroid gland is not producing enough thyroxine (even though TSH is high) / thyroid is underactive (1)\n• low thyroxine means no inhibition of TSH so pituitary secretes lots of TSH (1)\n• treatment: take thyroxine (tablets) (1)\nALLOW low thyroxine explains low metabolic rate / tiredness / weight gain as alternative to third mark\nIGNORE give her iodine (unless linked to iodine deficiency)` },
      { q: `The table shows information about three methods of contraception (data written for this practice question).\nColumns: Method | Contains hormones? | Pregnancies per 100 women in one year (typical use) | Protects against STIs?\nCombined pill | yes | 9 | no\nMale condom | no | 18 | yes\nImplant | yes | fewer than 1 | no\n\nA couple in a long-term relationship, neither of whom has an STI, want to avoid pregnancy for the next three years.\nEvaluate which method would be best for them.\nUse the data in the table.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• implant is the most effective / fewest pregnancies (fewer than 1 per 100) (1)\n• it lasts (up to) three years / no need to remember daily, so typical use is close to perfect use (1)\n• STI protection is not needed because neither partner has an STI, so condom advantage is less important (1)\n• but implant / pill contain hormones so may cause side effects / condom has no hormonal side effects (1)\nALLOW reasoned conclusion for a different method if justified with data\nMax 3 if no data quoted` },
      { q: `A graph shows the concentrations of four hormones in a woman's blood during one 28-day menstrual cycle in which she did not become pregnant (data written for this practice question).\n• FSH rises from day 1 to a peak on day 6, falls until day 11, rises slightly to a small peak on day 14, then stays low.\n• Oestrogen rises steadily from day 5 to a peak on day 12, falls sharply after day 13, then rises a little between days 18 and 23.\n• LH is low until day 11, rises sharply to a peak on day 14, then falls back to a low level by day 16.\n• Progesterone is low until day 14, rises to a peak on day 21, then falls to a low level by day 28.\n\n* Use the data to explain how the four hormones interact to control the menstrual cycle.\nIn your answer you should describe:\n• the role of each hormone\n• how the hormones affect each other.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Explains the role of all four hormones AND explains at least two interactions between hormones (e.g. oestrogen inhibiting FSH and stimulating LH; progesterone inhibiting FSH/LH), linked to the timings in the data. The reasoning is clear and logically structured. The information is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Describes the role of at least three hormones with some use of the data, AND explains at least one interaction between hormones. There is a line of reasoning with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Describes the role of at least one or two hormones OR simply describes changes in hormone levels from the data. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nRoles\n• FSH (from pituitary) causes an egg / follicle to mature in the ovary (peak day 6)\n• FSH stimulates the ovary to secrete oestrogen\n• oestrogen (from ovary) causes uterus lining to thicken / repair (days 5–12)\n• LH (from pituitary) causes ovulation (peak day 14)\n• progesterone (from empty follicle / corpus luteum) maintains uterus lining (days 14–28)\nInteractions\n• rising oestrogen inhibits FSH (FSH falls days 6–11) so no more eggs mature\n• high oestrogen stimulates LH surge (oestrogen peak day 12 before LH peak day 14)\n• progesterone (and oestrogen) inhibit FSH and LH (both low after day 16)\n• no pregnancy so progesterone falls (by day 28), lining breaks down / menstruation\n• fall in progesterone removes inhibition so FSH rises and a new cycle starts\n• these are examples of negative feedback` }
    ]
  },
  'B5.4': {
    name: 'Why do we need to maintain a constant internal environment?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about controlling blood glucose.\nAfter a meal, the concentration of glucose in the blood rises. A hormone is released into the blood to bring the glucose concentration back down.\n\nWhich organ releases this hormone?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\nkidney      liver      pancreas      pituitary gland`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• pancreas (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `When a person swims in cold sea water, their body loses heat quickly. Hypothermia is where body temperature falls below 35 °C.\n\nWhat is the normal core body temperature of a healthy human?`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `• 37 °C (1)\nALLOW any value in the range 36.5–37.5 °C` },
      { q: `Changes in the temperature of the blood flowing through the brain are detected by receptors in one part of the brain. This part of the brain then coordinates the body's response.\n\nName this part of the brain.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• hypothalamus (1)\nALLOW phonetic spelling\nDO NOT ALLOW pituitary gland / cerebrum / medulla` },
      { q: `The kidneys filter the blood. The liquid filtered out of the blood is called filtrate.\nIn a healthy person, one substance in the filtrate is completely reabsorbed back into the blood, so none of it is found in the urine.\n\nWhich substance is this?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\nglucose      mineral ions      urea      water`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• glucose (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `Kai is 45 years old. His doctor tells him he has a high risk of developing type 2 diabetes because his body mass is high for his height.\n\nSuggest one change Kai could make to his lifestyle to reduce his risk of developing type 2 diabetes.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• any one from (1):\n  – take more exercise / be more active\n  – eat less (sugar / fat / high-energy food) / eat a balanced diet / lose weight\nIGNORE 'eat healthily' unqualified\nIGNORE stop smoking / drink less alcohol` },
      { q: `A student models the effect of cold water on the body. She fills a test tube with water at 37 °C, stands it in a beaker of cold water and records the temperature of the water in the tube every minute.\n\nGive one reason why a test tube of water is not a good model of the human body.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• any one from (1):\n  – the body produces heat / respires / has a metabolism but the tube does not\n  – the body can respond to (control) its temperature / sweat / shiver / change blood flow, the tube cannot\n  – the body has insulation such as fat / skin / hair\n  – different size / shape / surface area to volume ratio from the body\nIGNORE 'it is not alive' unqualified` },
      { q: `This question is about homeostasis.\nThe conditions inside the body, such as temperature and the concentration of glucose in the blood, must be kept within narrow limits.\n\nComplete the sentence about homeostasis.\nUse words from the list.\nchanging      constant      external      genetic      internal\nHomeostasis is maintaining a ______ internal environment in response to ______ and external changes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• constant (1)\n• internal (1)\nmarks in the order shown` },
      { q: `When the body gets too hot or too cold, effectors respond to bring the temperature back to normal.\n\nDraw lines to connect each response to its effect on body temperature.\nResponses: Sweating; Shivering\nEffects on body temperature: Raises body temperature; Lowers body temperature; Has no effect on body temperature`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw lines', markScheme: `• sweating → lowers body temperature (1)\n• shivering → raises body temperature (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one line from a response` },
      { q: `Many reactions in body cells are controlled by enzymes. Core body temperature is kept close to 37 °C.\n\nExplain why body temperature must be kept close to 37 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• 37 °C is the optimum temperature for enzymes / enzymes work best at this temperature (1)\n• (if temperature rises too far) enzymes denature / active site changes shape OR (if temperature falls) reactions / enzymes are too slow (1)\nALLOW metabolic reactions take place at the right rate for 2 marks if linked to enzymes` },
      { q: `A student models the effect of cold water on the body. She puts hot water into a test tube and stands it in iced water (data written for this practice question).\nThe temperature of the water in the tube falls from 60 °C to 42 °C in 6 minutes.\n\nCalculate the mean rate of cooling in °C per minute.\nRate of cooling = ______ °C per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 60 − 42 = 18 (°C) (1)\n• 18 ÷ 6 = 3 (°C per minute) (1)\nALLOW 3 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student uses test tubes of hot water to model heat loss from the body. He wraps one tube in a layer of cotton wool and leaves a second tube unwrapped. The wrapped tube cools more slowly.\n\nWhich part of a mammal is the cotton wool a model of, and explain why it reduces heat loss.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (layer of) fat / hair / fur / skin (1)\n• traps air / is an insulator so less heat (energy) is transferred to the surroundings (1)\nALLOW hair stands up and traps a layer of air` },
      { q: `On a cold day, Ana's hands and face look pale. Her core body temperature stays at 37 °C.\n\nExplain how changes to her blood vessels reduce heat loss from her skin.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• vasoconstriction / blood vessels (arterioles) supplying the skin get narrower (1)\n• less blood flows near the skin surface so less heat is lost (to the surroundings) by radiation (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW capillaries / veins move (away from the skin)\nDO NOT ALLOW blood vessels shrink / contract (for first mark only if 'narrower' idea is absent)` },
      { q: `Sam runs a half marathon on a hot day. He loses 1800 cm³ of water as sweat during a run lasting 90 minutes (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the mean volume of sweat Sam loses per minute.\nVolume = ______ cm³ per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1800 ÷ 90 (1)\n• = 20 (cm³ per minute) (1)\nALLOW 20 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Athletes are advised not to lose more than 2% of their body mass as sweat during a race, because this affects their performance.\nAn athlete has a body mass of 70 kg.\n\nCalculate the maximum mass of sweat the athlete should lose during a race.\nMass = ______ kg`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 70 × 2 ÷ 100 / 70 × 0.02 (1)\n• = 1.4 (kg) (1)\nALLOW 1400 g if unit changed for 2 marks\nALLOW 1.4 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `The concentration of glucose in the blood is controlled by a control system with receptors, a processing centre and effectors.\nThe pancreas detects a rise in blood glucose concentration and releases insulin. Insulin causes liver cells to take in glucose and store it.\n\nName the receptor and the effector in this control system.\nReceptor ______\nEffector ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• receptor: (cells in the) pancreas (1)\n• effector: liver (cells) (1)\nALLOW muscle (cells) / body cells as effector` },
      { q: `Mia eats a large bowl of pasta. An hour later, the concentration of glucose in her blood is higher than normal.\nHer pancreas releases insulin.\n\nDescribe how insulin causes Mia's blood glucose concentration to decrease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (insulin causes) cells / liver / muscles to take up / absorb glucose from the blood (1)\n• (liver / muscles) convert glucose into glycogen (for storage) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW insulin converts glucose to glycogen (insulin is not an enzyme) for second mark\nDO NOT ALLOW glucagon for glycogen` },
      { q: `Tom is cycling in a long race. He has not eaten for several hours and the concentration of glucose in his blood is starting to fall below normal.\nHis pancreas releases the hormone glucagon.\n\nExplain how glucagon helps to return Tom's blood glucose concentration to normal.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• glucagon causes the liver to convert / break down glycogen into glucose (1)\n• glucose is released into the blood (so the blood glucose concentration rises) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW glucagon is converted into glucose\nDO NOT ALLOW glucagon / glycogen confusion` },
      { q: `A doctor measures the concentration of glucose in a patient's blood after a meal (data written for this practice question).\nThe concentration falls from 9.6 mmol/dm³ to 5.4 mmol/dm³ in 120 minutes.\n\nCalculate the mean rate at which the blood glucose concentration falls.\nGive your answer in mmol/dm³ per minute.\nRate = ______ mmol/dm³ per minute`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 9.6 − 5.4 = 4.2 (mmol/dm³) (1)\n• 4.2 ÷ 120 = 0.035 (mmol/dm³ per minute) (1)\nALLOW 0.035 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Ellie is 9 years old. She has just been diagnosed with type 1 diabetes. She will need to inject insulin every day for the rest of her life.\n\nExplain why Ellie needs to inject insulin.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• her pancreas does not produce (enough) insulin / the cells that make insulin have been destroyed (1)\n• (without insulin) her blood glucose concentration would rise too high / would not be controlled (1)\nIGNORE 'her body cannot control sugar' without reference to insulin for first mark\nDO NOT ALLOW her cells do not respond to insulin (type 2)` },
      { q: `Scientists studied the risk of developing type 2 diabetes in adults with different body mass index (BMI) values (data written for this practice question).\nBMI 20: relative risk 1.0\nBMI 25: relative risk 2.4\nBMI 30: relative risk 6.1\nBMI 35: relative risk 15.2\n\nDescribe what the data shows about the risk of developing type 2 diabetes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• as BMI increases, the (relative) risk of type 2 diabetes increases (1)\n• the risk increases by more for each increase in BMI / increases more steeply at higher BMI / a quantitative comparison, e.g. a BMI of 35 has 15.2 times the risk of a BMI of 20 (1)\nIGNORE 'BMI causes diabetes'` },
      { q: `In one country, 4.3 million people have been diagnosed with diabetes. The population of the country is 67 million (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage of the population who have been diagnosed with diabetes.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 4.3 ÷ 67 × 100 (1)\n• = 6.4 (%) (1)\nALLOW 6.417… or 6.42 for 1 mark only (not given to 2 s.f.)` },
      { q: `It is important that the human body maintains blood plasma at the correct concentration.\nIf a person drinks a very large volume of water in a short time, the blood plasma becomes too dilute.\n\nComplete the sentence to explain what happens to body cells.\nPut a ring around the correct options.\nWater moves into / out of the cells by osmosis / active transport and the cells swell / shrink.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• into AND osmosis (1)\n• swell (1)\nALLOW 'burst' if written in` },
      { q: `The kidneys of a healthy adult filter about 180 dm³ of liquid from the blood each day. They produce about 1.5 dm³ of urine each day.\n\nCalculate the percentage of the filtered liquid that is reabsorbed back into the blood.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (180 − 1.5) ÷ 180 × 100 / 178.5 ÷ 180 × 100 (1)\n• = 99.2 (%) (1)\nALLOW 99.17 / 99.166… for 1 mark only (not given to 3 s.f.)\nALLOW 0.833% (percentage not reabsorbed) for 1 mark only` },
      { q: `Blood is filtered as it passes through the kidneys. A sample of blood plasma and a sample of the filtrate from the same person are tested. The plasma contains protein but the filtrate contains no protein.\n\nExplain why the filtrate contains no protein.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• protein molecules are (too) large (1)\n• so they cannot pass through the filter / out of the blood capillaries (into the kidney tubule) (1)\nIGNORE 'protein is reabsorbed'` },
      { q: `The table shows the water gained and lost by a person in one day (data written for this practice question).\nWater gained: drinks 1500 cm³; food 750 cm³; respiration 350 cm³\nWater lost: urine ______ cm³; sweat 500 cm³; breathing out 400 cm³; faeces 150 cm³\nThe person's water gain is equal to their water loss.\n\nCalculate the volume of water lost in urine.\nVolume = ______ cm³`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• total gained = 1500 + 750 + 350 = 2600 (cm³) OR other losses = 500 + 400 + 150 = 1050 (cm³) (1)\n• 2600 − 1050 = 1550 (cm³) (1)\nALLOW 1550 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `On a hot day, Leo has not had anything to drink for several hours. The concentration of his blood plasma has increased. His pituitary gland releases more of the hormone ADH.\n\nDescribe how ADH changes the volume and concentration of Leo's urine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• ADH makes the (kidney) tubules / collecting ducts more permeable to water so more water is reabsorbed (into the blood) (1)\n• (so) a smaller volume of more concentrated urine is produced (1)\nIGNORE 'ADH stops urine being made'` },
      { q: `Priya works in a hot kitchen all day and does not drink much water.\nIn the evening she notices that she has produced only a small volume of dark yellow urine.\n\nExplain why Priya's urine is a small volume and dark in colour.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• she loses a lot of water by sweating (and drinks little) (1)\n• so her blood (plasma) becomes more concentrated / water content of blood falls (1)\n• kidneys reabsorb more water (into the blood) / more ADH released (1)\n• so urine contains less water / urea and salts are more concentrated (which makes it darker) (1)\nmax 3` },
      { q: `A man sits in a sauna at 80 °C. His core body temperature rises slightly above 37 °C. Receptors in his hypothalamus detect this and he starts to sweat. After he leaves the sauna, his core temperature returns to 37 °C and he stops sweating.\n\nExplain why this is an example of negative feedback.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a change (rise in temperature) from the normal level / set point is detected (by receptors) (1)\n• effectors (sweat glands) bring about a response that reverses the change / lowers temperature back to 37 °C (1)\n• when the temperature returns to normal the response stops / is reduced (1)\nALLOW 'the response opposes the original change' for second mark` },
      { q: `A student drinks a sugary drink. She measures her blood glucose concentration with a glucose meter before the drink and 30 minutes after it (data written for this practice question).\nBefore drink: 4.6 mmol/dm³\n30 minutes after drink: 7.8 mmol/dm³\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in her blood glucose concentration.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 7.8 − 4.6 = 3.2 (mmol/dm³) (1)\n• 3.2 ÷ 4.6 × 100 (1)\n• = 70 (%) (1)\nALLOW 69.6 / 69.57 for 2 marks (not given to 2 s.f.)\nALLOW 170 (%) for 1 mark (7.8 ÷ 4.6 × 100)` },
      { q: `Rob is 58 years old and has type 2 diabetes. His doctor first advises him to change his diet and take more exercise. If this does not work, he will be given medicine. Rob asks why he is not being given insulin injections.\n\nExplain why insulin injections are not usually the first treatment for type 2 diabetes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• in type 2 diabetes the pancreas still makes insulin (1)\n• but body cells do not respond (properly) to insulin / cells are resistant to insulin (1)\n• so (extra) insulin may not lower the blood glucose concentration much OR changing diet / exercise lowers blood glucose / reduces body mass / improves the response to insulin (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 'the pancreas makes no insulin'` },
      { q: `The concentration of glucose in the blood plasma of a healthy person is about 1.0 g/dm³. The kidneys filter 180 dm³ of plasma each day.\nThe filtrate has the same glucose concentration as the plasma.\n\nCalculate the mass of glucose filtered by the kidneys each day, and explain why a healthy person has no glucose in their urine.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1.0 × 180 (1)\n• = 180 (g) (1)\n• all the glucose is (selectively) reabsorbed (back into the blood from the tubule) (1)\nALLOW 180 g with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW glucose is needed for respiration so it is reabsorbed for third mark` },
      { q: `This question is about the kidney.\nBlood enters the kidney at high pressure. Urine leaves the kidney and travels to the bladder.\n\nDescribe how the kidney produces urine from the blood.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (small molecules such as) water, glucose, urea and (mineral) ions are filtered out of the blood (into the kidney tubule) (1)\n• (large molecules / proteins / blood cells) stay in the blood (1)\n• useful substances / all glucose / some water / some ions are (selectively) reabsorbed (into the blood) (1)\n• urea / excess water / excess ions are left and form urine (1)\nmax 3` },
      { q: `A woman drinks about 7 dm³ of water in two hours during a drinking competition. Her blood plasma becomes very dilute. She develops a headache and becomes confused because her brain cells are affected.\n\nExplain why drinking such a large volume of water affects her brain cells.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• plasma has a higher water concentration (is more dilute) than the cytoplasm of the cells (1)\n• water moves into the (brain) cells by osmosis (1)\n• (brain) cells swell / increase in size (and could burst) / cannot work properly (1)\nALLOW 'down a concentration gradient' through a partially permeable membrane for second mark` },
      { q: `An open-water swimmer swims in the sea at 12 °C. After 30 minutes her skin is pale and covered in goose bumps, and she is shivering.\n\nExplain how shivering and the pale skin help to stop her core body temperature from falling.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• shivering is (rapid, involuntary) muscle contraction (1)\n• which increases respiration so releases (more) heat (energy) (1)\n• pale skin shows vasoconstriction / less blood flows near the skin surface so less heat is lost (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW 'respiration produces energy' (must be released / transferred)\nDO NOT ALLOW blood vessels move away from the skin` },
      { q: `A student investigates how drinking affects urine production. On two different days he empties his bladder and then drinks 1 dm³ of liquid. He collects all his urine for the next 3 hours (data written for this practice question).\nDay 1: drinks 1 dm³ of water — urine produced 900 cm³\nDay 2: drinks 1 dm³ of salty water — urine produced 400 cm³\n\nUse ideas about blood concentration to explain the difference in the volume of urine produced.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• water makes the blood (plasma) more dilute (so excess water is removed) (1)\n• salty water makes the blood more concentrated / less dilute (than drinking water) (1)\n• so (on day 2) more water is reabsorbed by the kidneys / more ADH released and less urine is produced (1)\nALLOW reverse arguments` },
      { q: `After playing football on a hot day, Jay's face looks red and his skin feels warm and wet.\n\nExplain how changes in Jay's skin help to cool him down.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• vasodilation / blood vessels (arterioles) supplying the skin widen / more blood flows near the skin surface (making it red) (1)\n• (more) heat is lost (to the surroundings) by radiation (1)\n• sweat evaporates from the skin, which transfers heat (energy) away from the body (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW blood vessels move nearer the skin\nDO NOT ALLOW 'sweat is cold / cools the skin' without evaporation` },
      { q: `Dialysis is a treatment for people whose kidneys have stopped working. Their blood passes through a dialysis machine which removes waste.\nA patient's urea concentration in the blood is measured before and after dialysis (data written for this practice question).\nBefore dialysis: 25.0 mmol/dm³\nAfter dialysis: 10.0 mmol/dm³\nThe normal range is 2.5–7.8 mmol/dm³.\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in urea concentration and explain why the patient needs regular dialysis.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Calculation\n• 25.0 − 10.0 = 15.0 (mmol/dm³) (1)\n• 15.0 ÷ 25.0 × 100 = 60 (%) (1)\nExplanation – any two from:\n• the urea concentration is still above the normal range (after dialysis) (1)\n• (without working kidneys) urea is not removed so it builds up again / is made continuously (by the liver) (1)\n• urea is toxic / poisonous (at high concentration) (1)\nmax 2 for explanation\nALLOW 60% with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `A long-distance walker is lost in the desert for a day without water. He loses water by sweating and breathing, and his blood plasma becomes too concentrated.\n\nDescribe how his body responds to reduce water loss in urine.\nIn your answer, refer to the processing centre and the hormone involved.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• (receptors in the) hypothalamus detect the increase in blood (plasma) concentration (1)\n• pituitary gland releases more ADH (into the blood) (1)\n• ADH makes the (kidney) tubules / collecting ducts more permeable to water (1)\n• more water is reabsorbed into the blood (1)\n• a small volume of concentrated urine is produced (1)\nmax 4\nDO NOT ALLOW the kidney produces ADH` },
      { q: `A glucose tolerance test was carried out on a healthy person. After fasting overnight, she drank a solution containing 75 g of glucose. Her blood glucose concentration was measured every 30 minutes (data written for this practice question).\n0 minutes: 4.8 mmol/dm³\n30 minutes: 7.9 mmol/dm³\n60 minutes: 6.6 mmol/dm³\n90 minutes: 5.4 mmol/dm³\n120 minutes: 4.9 mmol/dm³\n\nDescribe and explain the changes in her blood glucose concentration.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Describe\n• rises to a peak of 7.9 mmol/dm³ at 30 minutes (1)\n• then falls back to close to the starting value / to 4.9 mmol/dm³ by 120 minutes (1)\nExplain\n• the rise is due to glucose being absorbed into the blood (from the gut / small intestine) (1)\n• the pancreas releases insulin (when it detects the rise) (1)\n• insulin causes cells / liver / muscles to take up glucose / liver converts glucose to glycogen (1)\nmax 4\nmust have at least one describe and one explain mark for 4 marks` },
      { q: `Two adults, Person A and Person B, each fasted overnight and then drank a solution containing 75 g of glucose. Their blood glucose concentrations were measured (data written for this practice question).\nPerson A: 0 min 4.9; 30 min 7.6; 60 min 6.2; 120 min 5.0 (all in mmol/dm³)\nPerson B: 0 min 8.4; 30 min 13.1; 60 min 15.6; 120 min 14.2 (all in mmol/dm³)\nThe blood of Person B contained a normal concentration of insulin.\n\n* Use the data to compare the control of blood glucose in Person A and Person B.\nExplain which type of diabetes Person B is most likely to have, and how it could be treated.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed comparison of the data for both people using values, AND a clear explanation of the control of blood glucose by insulin in Person A, AND correctly identifies type 2 diabetes in Person B with a reason linked to the insulin data, AND describes a suitable treatment. The reasoning is clear and logically structured. The information is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Compares the data for both people with some use of values AND either explains how insulin controls Person A's glucose OR identifies type 2 diabetes in Person B with a reason or treatment. There is a line of reasoning with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple description of the data, e.g. Person B's glucose is higher, OR identifies that Person B has diabetes. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nData\n• Person A starts at a normal fasting level (4.9 mmol/dm³); Person B starts higher (8.4 mmol/dm³)\n• Person A peaks at 30 minutes (7.6) then returns to near the starting value (5.0) by 120 minutes\n• Person B keeps rising to a peak at 60 minutes (15.6) and is still high (14.2) at 120 minutes\n• Person B's rise is much larger (7.2 mmol/dm³ compared with 2.7 mmol/dm³)\nPerson A\n• pancreas detects rise and releases insulin\n• insulin causes cells / liver / muscle to take up glucose; liver stores it as glycogen\n• negative feedback returns glucose to normal\nPerson B\n• type 2 diabetes\n• insulin is still produced (normal concentration) but cells do not respond to it / are insulin-resistant\n• so glucose is not taken up and stays in the blood\n• (type 1 would show little or no insulin)\nTreatment\n• change diet: less sugar / fewer carbohydrates / less high-energy food\n• more exercise / lose body mass\n• medication (that helps cells respond to insulin / lowers glucose); insulin injections in some later cases\n• monitor blood glucose regularly` }
    ]
  },
  'B6.1': {
    name: 'How was the theory of evolution developed?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about the development of the theory of evolution.\nIn 1858 two scientists presented a joint paper to the Linnean Society in London. The paper explained how species change by natural selection. One of the scientists was Charles Darwin.\n\nName the other scientist who developed the same explanation independently.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• (Alfred Russel) Wallace (1)\nALLOW phonetic spelling, e.g. Walace\nDO NOT ALLOW Lamarck / Mendel` },
      { q: `A geologist studies a cliff made of layers of sedimentary rock. The rock layers have not been disturbed since they formed.\nFossils are found in layers A, B, C and D. Layer A is at the top of the cliff and layer D is at the bottom.\n\nIn which layer are the oldest fossils most likely to be found?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\nA      B      C      D`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• D (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `In 1859 Charles Darwin published his book 'On the Origin of Species'. In the book he explained how species have changed over a very long time.\n\nWhat name did Darwin give to the process that he said causes species to change?`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• natural selection (1)\nIGNORE evolution / survival of the fittest on its own\nDO NOT ALLOW selective breeding` },
      { q: `Jean-Baptiste Lamarck was a French scientist who suggested an explanation for how species change, about 50 years before Darwin.\n\nWhich statement describes Lamarck's idea?\nTick (✓) one box.\n☐ Characteristics an organism develops during its lifetime are passed on to its offspring.\n☐ Individuals with the best-suited variations survive and reproduce more.\n☐ Species were each created separately and never change.\n☐ New species form when populations are isolated.`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• Characteristics an organism develops during its lifetime are passed on to its offspring. (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one box ticked` },
      { q: `Doctors have seen populations of bacteria become resistant to antibiotics within a few years. This is often used as evidence that evolution is still happening.\n\nSuggest why evolution can be seen happening in bacteria in only a few years.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• bacteria reproduce very quickly / have a short generation time (so many generations pass in a few years) (1)\nALLOW bacteria divide every 20 minutes (in good conditions)\nIGNORE bacteria are small` },
      { q: `Most living things that die do not become fossils. Fossils are much more common for animals with shells or bones than for animals such as jellyfish and worms.\n\nGive one reason why jellyfish and worms rarely form fossils.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• they have no hard parts / are soft-bodied, so they decay (before they can be preserved) (1)\nALLOW they have no bones / shells / skeleton\nIGNORE they are small` },
      { q: `This question is about how Darwin explained his observations.\nComplete the sentences about natural selection.\nUse words from the list. Each word may be used once, more than once or not at all.\ncompete      die out      genes      inherit      reproduce      variation\n\nIndividuals in a population show ______ .\nIndividuals ______ for limited resources such as food.\nThose best suited to the environment are more likely to survive and ______ .\nTheir offspring ______ the characteristics that helped their parents survive.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• variation; compete; reproduce; inherit\n4 correct = 2 marks\n2 or 3 correct = 1 mark\n0 or 1 correct = 0 marks` },
      { q: `Darwin and Wallace's ideas were first presented to other scientists at a meeting of the Linnean Society in 1858. Darwin then published a book giving his evidence in detail in 1859.\n\nExplain why it was important for Darwin and Wallace to share their explanation with other scientists.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• other scientists can check / evaluate the evidence and reasoning (1)\n• (they can) test the explanation / look for new evidence / make predictions to test it (1)\nALLOW peer review (as one of the marking points if linked to checking)\nALLOW explanation more likely to be accepted if others agree / it is confirmed\nIGNORE so they get the credit on its own` },
      { q: `Darwin bred pigeons at his home. He knew that pigeon breeders had produced many different breeds, such as fantails and pouters, from one wild species, the rock dove.\n\nSuggest how pigeon breeding helped Darwin to develop his idea of natural selection.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• selective breeding showed that choosing which individuals breed can cause large changes in a species over generations (1)\n• Darwin suggested that in nature the environment / competition does the 'choosing' (instead of humans) (1)\nALLOW pigeons showed there is (inherited) variation within a species for selection to act on` },
      { q: `Darwin spent about 20 years collecting evidence before he published 'On the Origin of Species' in 1859. Most people in Britain at that time believed that every species had been created by God and had never changed.\n\nSuggest two reasons why Darwin waited so long before publishing his ideas.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• the idea conflicted with religious beliefs / he expected strong opposition / criticism (1)\n• he wanted to collect enough evidence to convince other scientists (1)\n• he was worried about damage to his reputation / upsetting his family (1)\nALLOW he was not sure himself / he was ill (1)` },
      { q: `When 'On the Origin of Species' was published, many people refused to accept Darwin's explanation. Some scientists also criticised it.\n\nGive two reasons why many people did not accept Darwin's explanation at the time.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• it conflicted with religious belief that God created all species / humans (1)\n• there was not enough evidence at the time / fossil record had gaps (1)\n• Darwin could not explain how characteristics were inherited / genes were unknown (1)\n• people did not like the idea that humans are related to / evolved from apes (1)` },
      { q: `A scientist studies layers of mud at the bottom of a lake (data written for this practice question).\nThe mud builds up at a rate of 2 cm every 1000 years.\nA fossil of a fish is found 3.6 m below the top of the mud.\n\nCalculate the age of the fish fossil.\nAge = ______ years`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 3.6 m = 360 cm and 360 ÷ 2 = 180 (thousands) (1)\n• 180 000 (years) (1)\nALLOW 1.8 × 10⁵ years\nALLOW 1 mark for 1800 years (no conversion of m to cm)` },
      { q: `Lamarck explained the long necks of giraffes in a different way from Darwin.\n\nDescribe how Lamarck would have explained why giraffes have long necks.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• giraffes stretched their necks (to reach leaves high in trees) during their lifetime, so their necks became longer (1)\n• the longer necks were passed on to their offspring (1)\nALLOW acquired characteristics are inherited (for second point)\nDO NOT ALLOW answers describing variation and survival of giraffes with longer necks (Darwin's idea)` },
      { q: `In the 1880s the scientist August Weismann cut off the tails of mice and then bred the mice together. He did this for several generations. All of the baby mice were born with normal-length tails.\n\nExplain why this evidence does not support Lamarck's explanation of how species change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Lamarck's idea predicts that characteristics gained / changes made during life are passed on (so tails should get shorter) (1)\n• the mice were born with normal tails, so the change was not inherited (1)\nALLOW cutting the tail does not change the genes / DNA (passed to offspring)` },
      { q: `Wallace collected thousands of animal specimens in the islands of South-East Asia. He worked thousands of kilometres away from Darwin and reached the same explanation for how species change.\n\nExplain why Wallace reaching the same conclusion made scientists more confident in the explanation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• two scientists working independently / separately, using different observations / organisms (1)\n• came up with the same explanation, so it is less likely to be wrong / biased / a coincidence (1)\nALLOW idea of reproducible conclusions` },
      { q: `A newspaper article says: "Evolution is only a theory, so it is just a guess."\n\nExplain why scientists disagree with the newspaper article.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a scientific theory is an explanation supported by a large amount of evidence (1)\n• it has been tested many times / by many scientists and its predictions have been confirmed (1)\nALLOW it explains a wide range of observations (for 1 mark)\nIGNORE it has been proved` },
      { q: `In 1861, two years after Darwin published his book, a fossil called Archaeopteryx was found in Germany. It had feathers and wings like a bird, but also teeth, claws on its wings and a long bony tail like a reptile.\n\nExplain why the discovery of Archaeopteryx supported Darwin's explanation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• it has features of both reptiles and birds / is an intermediate / transitional form (1)\n• suggests birds evolved from reptiles / reptiles and birds share a common ancestor (1)\nALLOW it filled a gap in the fossil record (for 1 mark)` },
      { q: `Scientists predicted that animals that were partly fish and partly land animals lived about 375 million years ago. They searched rocks of this age in northern Canada and in 2004 found a fossil, Tiktaalik, with fins, gills, lungs and a neck.\n\nExplain how this discovery shows the theory of evolution being tested.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the theory was used to make a prediction (about what fossils would be found and where / in rocks of what age) (1)\n• the evidence found matched / agreed with the prediction, so it supports the theory (1)\nALLOW if the fossil had not been found, the theory might have needed to change (for second mark)` },
      { q: `A team of scientists announces that it has found a fossil that does not fit the accepted ideas about the order in which groups of animals evolved.\n\nSuggest two things other scientists would do before accepting the team's claim.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• check the claim through peer review / read the published paper (1)\n• examine the fossil themselves / check it is genuine (1)\n• check the dating / age of the rock (1)\n• look for other fossils / further evidence that agrees (1)\nALLOW repeat the measurements / dating (1)` },
      { q: `Linnaeus classified organisms in the 1700s using features that can be seen, such as body shape. Today scientists also compare the DNA base sequences of different species.\n\nExplain why comparing DNA can show more accurately how closely two species are related.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• species with more similar DNA (base sequences) share a more recent common ancestor / are more closely related (1)\n• visible features can be similar in unrelated species (because they live in similar environments) / can be misleading (1)\nALLOW DNA is what is inherited, so it shows evolutionary relationships directly` },
      { q: `Whales were once thought to be most closely related to fish. Comparisons of DNA now show that whales are mammals and that their closest living relatives are hippopotamuses.\n\nExplain why the classification of an organism may change.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• new evidence is discovered (e.g. DNA sequences / new fossils) (1)\n• the evidence shows the organism is more closely related to a different group, so scientists change the explanation / group (1)\nALLOW new technology allows new evidence to be collected (for first mark)` },
      { q: `Wild cabbage is a plant that grows on cliffs in Europe. Over hundreds of years, farmers have produced broccoli, cauliflower, kale and Brussels sprouts from wild cabbage.\n\nDescribe how farmers produced broccoli from wild cabbage.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• select / choose plants with the desired characteristic (e.g. large flower heads) and breed them together (1)\n• select the best offspring and repeat over many generations (1)\nALLOW selective breeding / artificial selection for 1 mark if no description` },
      { q: `Some breeds of pedigree dog have been bred from closely related parents for many generations. Many dogs of these breeds suffer from inherited health problems.\n\nExplain why breeding closely related dogs leads to more inherited health problems.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• reduces genetic variation / the gene pool (1)\n• increases the chance of offspring inheriting two copies of a harmful recessive allele (1)\nALLOW inbreeding (for first marking point)` },
      { q: `Scientists tested the idea that birds eat pale peppered moths more often than dark moths in woods where the tree bark is darkened by soot (data written for this practice question).\nThey released 120 dark moths and 120 pale moths into a sooty wood. The next day they caught 30 of the dark moths and 12 of the pale moths again.\n\nCalculate the percentage of the released dark moths that were caught again.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (30 ÷ 120) × 100 (1)\n• 25 (%) (1)\nALLOW 25% with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Hospital scientists recorded the percentage of samples of one type of bacterium that were resistant to an antibiotic (data written for this practice question).\nIn 2010, 4% of samples were resistant. In 2020, 22% of samples were resistant.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the proportion of resistant samples from 2010 to 2020.\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (22 − 4) ÷ 4 × 100 (1)\n• 450 (%) (1)\nALLOW 1 mark for 18 (percentage points) as final answer` },
      { q: `Today scientists know that genes are made of DNA and can be changed by mutation. Darwin did not know about genes when he published his theory.\n\nExplain how the discovery of genes has supported Darwin's theory.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• genes explain how characteristics are passed on / inherited from parents to offspring (1)\n• mutations in genes explain where new variation comes from (1)\nALLOW Darwin could not explain inheritance / variation, but genes do (for 1 mark)` },
      { q: `On the Galapagos Islands Darwin saw finches with different beak shapes on different islands. On one island, the main food is hard seeds and most finches have short, thick beaks.\n\nUse Darwin's ideas to explain why most finches on this island have short, thick beaks.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• there was variation in beak shape in the (original) finch population (1)\n• finches with short, thick beaks could crack hard seeds / get more food, so were more likely to survive (and reproduce) (1)\n• they passed on the (genes for) short, thick beak to their offspring, so over many generations most finches had this beak (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW finches changed their beaks to crack seeds / Lamarckian ideas` },
      { q: `The fossil record shows how horses have changed over the last 55 million years (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime (million years ago) | Height at shoulder (m) | Number of toes on each foot\n55 | 0.4 | 4\n35 | 0.6 | 3\n20 | 1.0 | 3\n5 | 1.3 | 1\nNow | 1.6 | 1\n\nDescribe how horses have changed over time and explain how this is evidence for evolution.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• height increased (from 0.4 m to 1.6 m) (1)\n• number of toes decreased (from 4 to 1) (1)\n• shows species change gradually over time / modern horses evolved from (smaller) ancestors (1)\nALLOW change happens over millions of years / many generations` },
      { q: `The fossil record shows that the shoulder height of horses has increased from 0.4 m 55 million years ago to 1.6 m today (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the mean increase in shoulder height in mm per million years.\nGive your answer to 2 significant figures.\nMean increase = ______ mm per million years`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• increase = 1.6 − 0.4 = 1.2 m = 1200 mm (1)\n• 1200 ÷ 55 = 21.8 (1)\n• 22 (mm per million years) (1)\nALLOW 2 marks for 0.022 (m per million years) / 21.8 not rounded\nALLOW ECF for rounding from an incorrect value` },
      { q: `A fossil of an ancient fish was found in rock that formed from mud at the bottom of a sea.\n\nExplain how the fish became a fossil instead of decaying completely.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the fish was covered / buried quickly by mud / sediment (1)\n• conditions lacked oxygen (or were too cold / acidic) so decomposers / microorganisms could not decay it (1)\n• hard parts (bones) were gradually replaced by minerals / turned to rock over a long time (1)\nALLOW the shape of the fish left an impression / mould in the rock (for third point)` },
      { q: `A student writes: "Giraffes have long necks because their ancestors needed to reach high leaves, so their necks grew longer and they passed this on."\n\nThe student's explanation uses Lamarck's ideas, not Darwin's.\nExplain how Darwin's explanation is different from the student's explanation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Darwin said there was (natural) variation in neck length already in the population (1)\n• giraffes with longer necks got more food so were more likely to survive and reproduce (1)\n• only inherited characteristics are passed on / changes during an organism's lifetime are not inherited (1)\nALLOW the population changes over many generations, not an individual (for third point)` },
      { q: `Most scientists did not fully accept natural selection until the early 20th century, more than 40 years after Darwin's book was published.\n\nExplain why the theory of evolution by natural selection is now accepted by almost all scientists.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• lots of evidence / new evidence supports it, e.g. more fossils / DNA comparisons (1)\n• the discovery of genes explained how variation arises and is inherited (1)\n• it has been tested and its predictions have been confirmed (1)\n• it explains a wide range of observations better than other explanations (1)\n• it has been checked by peer review / by many scientists (1)` },
      { q: `A course of antibiotics was used to treat many patients in a hospital. A few years later most of the bacteria of that type in the hospital were resistant to the antibiotic.\n\nExplain how this provides evidence for evolution by natural selection.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a mutation made some bacteria resistant (variation) (1)\n• the antibiotic killed non-resistant bacteria, so resistant bacteria survived and reproduced (1)\n• resistant bacteria passed on the resistance gene, so the population changed over generations – the change can be observed (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW bacteria became resistant in order to survive / antibiotic caused the mutation` },
      { q: `The average yield of milk from dairy cows on a farm increased from 3500 litres per cow per year in 1960 to 8400 litres per cow per year in 2020 (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in milk yield and suggest one reason for the increase.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (8400 − 3500) ÷ 3500 × 100 (1)\n• 140 (%) (1)\n• selective breeding of cows with high milk yield (over many generations) (1)\nALLOW better feed / health care for the third mark` },
      { q: `Darwin made observations of living things and fossils, and wrote an explanation. Other scientists then tested his explanation.\n\nDescribe how the development of the theory of evolution shows how scientific explanations are developed.\nUse ideas about data, explanations and testing.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• scientists collect data / make observations (e.g. Darwin on HMS Beagle / finches / fossils) (1)\n• they use creative thinking to suggest an explanation that accounts for the data (1)\n• the explanation is used to make predictions which are tested; if they agree, confidence increases / if not, the explanation changes (1)\nALLOW peer review / shared with other scientists for checking (for third point)` },
      { q: `Fungi were once classified as plants. Scientists later found that fungi do not photosynthesise, have cell walls made of chitin rather than cellulose, and have DNA more similar to animals than to plants.\n\nExplain how this new evidence has changed the way fungi are classified and what this shows about scientific explanations.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• fungi are now classified in their own (separate) kingdom / not as plants (1)\n• because their features / DNA show they are not closely related to plants (1)\n• scientific explanations / classifications can change when new evidence is found (1)\nALLOW fungi are more closely related to animals than plants (for second point)` },
      { q: `Darwin sailed around the world on HMS Beagle from 1831 to 1836. On the Galapagos Islands he observed that the finches and tortoises on each island were slightly different from each other and from those on the South American mainland.\n\nDescribe how Darwin used these observations and other ideas to develop his theory of evolution by natural selection.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• Darwin noticed each island's finches had features (e.g. beaks) suited to the food / environment on that island (1)\n• he suggested they had all descended from a common ancestor from the mainland (1)\n• he used ideas from selective breeding / pigeon breeding that species can change (1)\n• he used the idea that organisms produce more offspring than can survive, so there is competition (1)\n• he explained that individuals with variations best suited to the environment survive and reproduce, passing these on (1)\n• he spent many years collecting evidence before publishing his explanation (1)` },
      { q: `When Darwin published his theory, critics said that if species had changed gradually there should be fossils of many intermediate forms. Darwin replied that the fossil record was incomplete.\n\nExplain why the fossil record is incomplete and how later discoveries have affected the critics' argument.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Incomplete (max 3):\n• most organisms decay completely / are eaten after death (1)\n• soft-bodied organisms rarely fossilise (1)\n• fossilisation needs rare conditions, e.g. rapid burial / no oxygen (1)\n• many fossils have been destroyed by geological activity / have not yet been found (1)\nLater discoveries (max 2):\n• many intermediate / transitional fossils have since been found, e.g. Archaeopteryx / Tiktaalik / horse ancestors (1)\n• so the critics' argument is now much weaker / the evidence supports Darwin (1)` },
      { q: `Scientists compared part of the DNA sequence of a gene in humans, chimpanzees, gorillas and mice. The table shows the percentage of the bases that are the same as in humans (data written for this practice question).\n\nSpecies | Bases the same as humans (%)\nChimpanzee | 98.8\nGorilla | 98.4\nMouse | 85.0\n\nExplain how DNA evidence like this supports the theory of evolution and how it has affected the classification of organisms.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• chimpanzees have the most similar DNA, so are most closely related to humans / share the most recent common ancestor (1)\n• mice have least similar DNA, so share a common ancestor further back in time (1)\n• supports the idea that species are related / evolved from common ancestors (1)\n• classification is now based on DNA / evolutionary relationships, and some organisms have been reclassified (1)\nALLOW DNA evidence agrees with fossil evidence (for third point)\nALLOW DNA evidence was not available to Darwin – new evidence supports the theory` },
      { q: `Darwin's explanation of evolution by natural selection was published in 1859. At first many people, including some scientists, rejected it. Today it is accepted by almost all scientists.\n\n* Explain how Darwin and Wallace developed their explanation and why it has become accepted by scientists.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• the observations and ideas the explanation was based on\n• how the explanation was shared, tested and supported by evidence.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of how the theory was developed from observations and ideas AND a clear explanation of how it was shared, tested and supported by evidence, including evidence found after Darwin. The reasoning is clear and logically structured. The information is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some explanation of how the theory was developed AND some explanation of why it was accepted, OR a detailed account of one of these. There is a line of reasoning with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple statement about Darwin's observations OR about evidence for evolution. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nDevelopment\n• Darwin's observations on the voyage of HMS Beagle, e.g. Galapagos finches / tortoises suited to each island\n• Wallace's independent observations in South-East Asia\n• fossils showing organisms had changed over time\n• selective breeding showed species can change\n• overproduction of offspring leads to competition\n• variation – best-suited individuals survive, reproduce and pass on characteristics\n• creative thinking to produce an explanation for the data\nSharing, testing and acceptance\n• joint paper at the Linnean Society 1858; 'On the Origin of Species' 1859\n• other scientists evaluated it / peer review\n• opposition: conflict with religious beliefs, gaps in fossil record, no mechanism of inheritance\n• predictions tested, e.g. transitional fossils found (Archaeopteryx, Tiktaalik)\n• discovery of genes / DNA / mutation explained inheritance and variation\n• observed evolution, e.g. antibiotic resistance in bacteria\n• DNA comparisons show relationships consistent with fossils\n• rival explanation (Lamarck) rejected because acquired characteristics are not inherited\n• explains a wide range of observations, so accepted as the best explanation` }
    ]
  },
  'B6.2': {
    name: 'How does evolution take place?',
    questions: [
      { q: `Brown rats eat crops and stored grain on farms. A poison called warfarin was used to kill rats in the UK from the 1950s. A few years later, some rats were found that were not killed by warfarin.\nThe rats that survived had a different allele of one gene.\n\nWhat type of change in DNA first produced this new allele?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• (random) mutation (1)\nALLOW change in the base sequence of DNA / gene\nIGNORE variation / adaptation on its own\nDO NOT ALLOW the rats became immune / got used to the poison` },
      { q: `Two populations of birds live on opposite sides of a large lake. They look very similar.\n\nWhich statement shows that the two populations are now different species?\nTick (✓) one box.\n☐ They eat different types of food.\n☐ They cannot breed together to produce fertile offspring.\n☐ They have different coloured feathers.\n☐ They live in different habitats.`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• They cannot breed together to produce fertile offspring. (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one box ticked` },
      { q: `This question is about natural selection and selective breeding.\nComplete the sentence.\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\n\nIn natural selection, the individuals that survive and reproduce are decided by  farmers / scientists / the environment.`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• the environment (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `A student measures the hand span of 30 students in her class. The hand spans range from 16.5 cm to 22.0 cm. Every value in this range is possible.\n\nWhat type of variation is shown by hand span?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• continuous (variation) (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW discontinuous` },
      { q: `Ultraviolet light from the Sun can cause mutations in the DNA of skin cells.\nA woman has a mutation in one of the skin cells on her arm.\n\nExplain why this mutation cannot be passed on to her children.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the mutation is not in a gamete / sex cell / egg (cell) (1)\nALLOW it is in a body cell, not a cell that makes eggs\nIGNORE it is not in her genes` },
      { q: `Peppered moths can be pale or dark. The colour is controlled by one gene. Dark moths have a different version of the gene from pale moths.\n\nWhat name is given to different versions of the same gene?`, marks: 1, command: 'What', markScheme: `• allele(s) (1)\nALLOW (genetic) variant(s)\nIGNORE genotype / phenotype\nDO NOT ALLOW chromosomes` },
      { q: `This question is about how a population of hares in a snowy region evolved white fur in winter.\nComplete the sentences.\nUse words from the list. Each word may be used once, more than once or not at all.\n\nalleles      compete      learn      mutation      offspring      predators\n\nLong ago a ______ produced an allele for white winter fur. Hares with white fur were less likely to be seen by ______ , so they survived to breed and passed on the allele to their ______ . Over many generations the allele for white fur became more common.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• mutation (1)\n• predators AND offspring (1)\nAll three correct = 2 marks; one or two correct = 1 mark` },
      { q: `MRSA is a type of bacterium that is resistant to several antibiotics. Infections caused by MRSA are more common in hospitals than in the rest of the community.\n\nSuggest two reasons why antibiotic-resistant bacteria such as MRSA are more common in hospitals.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any 2 of:\n• antibiotics are used a lot in hospitals, so there is a strong selection pressure / non-resistant bacteria are killed (1)\n• resistant bacteria survive and reproduce with less competition (1)\n• many patients are ill / have weakened immune systems so bacteria are not destroyed (1)\n• many people are close together so bacteria are passed between patients (on hands / equipment) (1)\nIGNORE hospitals are dirty` },
      { q: `On the Galapagos Islands there are many species of finch. On one island most of the food is large, hard seeds. The finches on this island have deep, strong beaks.\n\nExplain why the finches on this island have evolved deep, strong beaks.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• finches with deeper / stronger beaks could crack the hard seeds / get more food (so were more likely to survive) (1)\n• (they) reproduced and passed on the allele(s) for deep beaks to their offspring (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW the finches developed strong beaks by cracking seeds / beaks grew stronger through use` },
      { q: `A scientist caught peppered moths in a wood near a city (data written for this practice question).\nShe caught 240 moths. 168 of the moths were the dark form.\n\nCalculate the percentage of moths that were the dark form.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (168 ÷ 240) × 100 (1)\n• = 70 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks` },
      { q: `Rats with the allele for warfarin resistance need much more vitamin K in their diet than other rats. If they do not get enough vitamin K they bleed easily.\nIn areas where warfarin has never been used, the allele for warfarin resistance is very rare.\n\nExplain why the allele is very rare in these areas.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (without warfarin) resistance gives no advantage / resistant rats are at a disadvantage because they need more vitamin K / bleed easily (1)\n• so resistant rats are less likely to survive and reproduce / pass on the allele (than non-resistant rats) (1)\nALLOW non-resistant rats are selected for` },
      { q: `A horse and a donkey can mate and produce offspring called a mule. Mules are healthy, strong animals but they cannot have offspring of their own.\n\nExplain why horses and donkeys are classed as two different species.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a species is a group of organisms that can breed to produce fertile offspring (1)\n• mules are infertile / cannot reproduce, so horses and donkeys do not produce fertile offspring (1)` },
      { q: `Mutations happen all the time in the DNA of living cells. Most mutations have no effect on the phenotype of the organism.\n\nGive two reasons why most mutations have no effect on the phenotype.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any 2 of:\n• the mutation is in a part of the DNA that does not code for a protein / is not in a gene (1)\n• the protein made is still the same / has the same amino acid sequence (1)\n• the protein made still works / has the same shape (1)\n• the mutation is in a recessive allele / is masked by a dominant allele (1)\n• the gene is not expressed / switched on in that cell (1)` },
      { q: `Blackgrass is a weed that grows in wheat fields. Farmers spray chemicals called herbicides to kill it. In many fields in the UK, blackgrass is now resistant to the herbicides that are used most often.\nFarmers are advised to use a mixture of different herbicides and to plough some fields instead of spraying.\n\nExplain why this advice could slow down the spread of herbicide resistance.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a plant resistant to one herbicide is likely to be killed by a different herbicide / by ploughing (1)\n• so fewer resistant plants survive to reproduce / pass on resistance alleles (1)\nALLOW reduces the selection pressure for resistance to one herbicide` },
      { q: `Identical twins have exactly the same alleles. Two identical twins were measured at age 40. One twin had a mass of 68 kg. The other twin had a mass of 79 kg.\n\nExplain why the twins have different masses.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the difference must be caused by the environment / is environmental variation (1)\n• because they have the same genes / genotype (1)\nALLOW example of environmental factor, e.g. different diet / amount of exercise, for the first mark` },
      { q: `Scientists compared the DNA base sequence of one gene in four species of mammal. The base sequence in species A and species B is 98% the same. The base sequence in species A and species C is 85% the same.\n\nExplain what this evidence suggests about how species A, B and C are related.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• species A is more closely related to species B than to species C (1)\n• A and B shared a common ancestor more recently / have had less time for mutations to build up (1)` },
      { q: `Bacteria reproduce by dividing into two. In good conditions one type of bacterium divides every 20 minutes.\nOne bacterium with a mutation for antibiotic resistance is in a wound.\n\nCalculate how many resistant bacteria there will be after 2 hours, if every bacterium divides every 20 minutes.\nNumber of bacteria = ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 120 ÷ 20 = 6 divisions / generations (1)\n• 2⁶ = 64 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 1 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 with an arithmetic error` },
      { q: `Modern breeds of dog, such as dachshunds and greyhounds, were produced from wolves by selective breeding. Wolves evolved by natural selection.\n\nDescribe two differences between selective breeding and natural selection.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any 2 of:\n• in selective breeding humans choose which individuals breed; in natural selection the environment / survival decides (1)\n• selective breeding selects characteristics useful / wanted by humans; natural selection selects characteristics that help survival in the environment (1)\n• selective breeding is usually faster / takes fewer generations (1)\n• selective breeding often reduces genetic variation / natural selection usually keeps more variation (1)\nA difference must be comparative for each mark` },
      { q: `Apple maggot flies lay their eggs in fruit. In North America they once laid eggs only in hawthorn fruit. About 200 years ago some flies began to lay their eggs in apples. Flies mate on the type of fruit they hatched from. Apples ripen several weeks earlier than hawthorn fruit.\nScientists think the two groups of flies may be forming two separate species, even though they live in the same area.\n\nExplain why the two groups of flies rarely interbreed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• flies mate on different fruit / in different places (1)\n• flies are ready to mate at different times / apple flies breed weeks earlier (1)\nALLOW they are (reproductively) isolated for one mark if neither reason is given` },
      { q: `The table shows the percentage of peppered moths that were the dark form in a wood near a city in the north of England (data written for this practice question).\nClean air laws reduced the amount of soot from factories from the 1960s.\n\nYear | Dark moths (%)\n1960 | 90\n1970 | 86\n1980 | 72\n1990 | 41\n2000 | 12\n\nDescribe the trend shown by the data.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• the percentage of dark moths decreases (from 1960 to 2000) (1)\n• slowly at first then faster (after 1980) / quantified, e.g. falls by only 4% from 1960 to 1970 but by 31% from 1980 to 1990 (1)` },
      { q: `A female cod can lay over a million eggs in one year. The number of cod in an area of sea stays about the same from year to year.\n\nExplain why the population of cod does not increase.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• cod compete for limited resources, e.g. food / space (1)\n• most eggs / young are eaten / die before they can reproduce (1)\nALLOW predation / disease kills most young` },
      { q: `Sickle-cell anaemia is an inherited disorder caused by a recessive allele. People with one copy of the allele are carriers and are healthy. Carriers are less likely to die from malaria than people with no copies of the allele.\n\nSuggest why the sickle-cell allele is more common in parts of Africa where malaria is common than in places with no malaria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• carriers / people with one copy are more likely to survive malaria (and reproduce) (1)\n• so they pass on the allele to their children (so it stays common in the population) (1)\nALLOW in places without malaria the allele gives no advantage` },
      { q: `There is very little genetic variation in the cheetah population. Scientists think that thousands of years ago almost all cheetahs died out and the population grew again from only a few individuals.\n\nExplain why low genetic variation increases the risk that cheetahs will become extinct.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• if the environment changes / a new disease appears, there may be no individuals with variants that help them survive (1)\n• so all / most of the population could die (before they can reproduce) (1)` },
      { q: `Mice usually have dark brown fur. A mutation in one gene can give mice pale fur. The gene codes for a protein involved in making the brown pigment.\n\nExplain how a mutation in this gene could stop the protein from working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the mutation changes the base sequence (of the gene) so the order / sequence of amino acids in the protein changes (1)\n• so the protein has a different shape / folds differently (and cannot do its job) (1)\nALLOW active site of enzyme changed shape` },
      { q: `Marine iguanas are lizards that swim in the sea and feed on seaweed. They are found only on the Galapagos Islands, about 1000 km from the coast of South America.\n\nSuggest why marine iguanas are not found anywhere else.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• the islands are isolated / far from the mainland, so iguanas could not move / breed with other populations (1)\n• they evolved by natural selection to suit conditions on the islands (into a new species) (1)` },
      { q: `Doctors noticed that a type of bacterium that had always been killed by an antibiotic became resistant to it after the antibiotic had been in use for a few years.\n\nExplain why antibiotic resistance is evidence that evolution by natural selection is happening today.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the inherited characteristics of the population have changed over time (1)\n• because resistant bacteria survived the antibiotic and reproduced / passed on resistance (1)\nALLOW we can observe it happening because bacteria reproduce quickly` },
      { q: `Warfarin was used to kill rats on farms. After some years most rats on the farms were resistant to warfarin.\nThe statements A to E describe how the rat population became resistant. They are in the wrong order.\n\nA Resistant rats survive and reproduce.\nB A mutation produces an allele for warfarin resistance.\nC Rats without the allele are killed by the warfarin.\nD Over many generations most rats in the population have the allele.\nE Offspring inherit the allele from their resistant parents.\n\nPut statements A to E in the correct order.\nWrite one letter in each box.`, marks: 3, command: 'Order', markScheme: `• B C A E D\n3 marks for all five letters in the correct order\n2 marks for B first and D last with one pair swapped\n1 mark for B first OR D last\nALLOW B A C E D (A and C in either order) for 3 marks` },
      { q: `Cane toads were introduced to northern Australia in 1935. Since then they have spread thousands of kilometres across the country. Scientists found that toads at the front of the invasion have longer legs than toads in areas where they first arrived.\n\nExplain how natural selection has caused toads at the front of the invasion to have longer legs.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• there is (genetic) variation in leg length in the toad population (1)\n• toads with longer legs travel faster / further, so they reach new areas first (with more food / less competition) (1)\n• the long-legged toads at the front breed with each other and pass on alleles for long legs to their offspring (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW legs grew longer from use / toads developed longer legs to travel` },
      { q: `A scientist measured the beak depth of 50 finches on one island (data written for this practice question).\n\nBeak depth (mm) | Number of finches\n8 | 5\n9 | 12\n10 | 18\n11 | 10\n12 | 5\n\nCalculate the mean beak depth of the finches.\nGive your answer to 3 significant figures.\nMean beak depth = ______ mm`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (8 × 5) + (9 × 12) + (10 × 18) + (11 × 10) + (12 × 5) = 498 (1)\n• 498 ÷ 50 = 9.96 (1)\n• 9.96 (mm) given to 3 significant figures (1)\nALLOW 2 marks for 9.96 with no working shown\nDO NOT ALLOW 10 (answer must be to 3 s.f.)` },
      { q: `On the Hawaiian island of Kauai, male field crickets sing to attract females. A parasitic fly finds male crickets by listening for their song and lays its eggs on them. The fly larvae kill the crickets.\nIn 2003 scientists found that most males on Kauai had a mutation that gives them flat wings. Flat-winged males cannot sing.\n\nExplain why the number of flat-winged males increased in only a few years.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• flat-winged males are silent, so the flies cannot find them / they are less likely to be killed by the fly (1)\n• so flat-winged males survive (longer) and mate / reproduce more (1)\n• the allele for flat wings is passed on and crickets have short generation times / several generations a year (1)\nALLOW they can still mate with females attracted by singing males` },
      { q: `A sheep farmer wants to produce sheep that grow more wool.\n\nDescribe how the farmer could use selective breeding to do this.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• choose / select the sheep (ram and ewe) that produce the most wool (1)\n• breed them together (1)\n• select the offspring with the most wool and breed them, repeating for many generations (1)\nIGNORE genetic engineering steps` },
      { q: `Hospital scientists tested samples of one type of bacterium from patients (data written for this practice question).\nIn 2010, 14% of samples were resistant to an antibiotic. In 2020, 35% of samples were resistant.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in resistant samples from 2010 to 2020.\nPercentage increase = ______ %`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 35 − 14 = 21 (1)\n• 21 ÷ 14 (× 100) (1)\n• = 150 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 3 marks\nALLOW 1 mark for 21 as the final answer` },
      { q: `In 1971 scientists moved five pairs of Italian wall lizards from one small island in the Adriatic Sea to a nearby island where there were no lizards of that species. On the new island there were few insects to eat but many plants.\nWhen scientists returned over 30 years later, the lizards on the new island had larger heads and stronger bites than lizards on the original island.\n\nSuggest how natural selection could have caused this change.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• (genetic) variation in head size / bite strength among the lizards (1)\n• lizards with larger heads / stronger bites could eat more (tough) plants so were more likely to survive (1)\n• they reproduced and passed on their alleles, so over many generations the alleles became more common (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW heads got bigger by chewing plants` },
      { q: `Before the 1960s, tree bark in a wood near a city was blackened by soot. Most peppered moths in the wood were the dark form. After clean air laws were introduced, less soot was produced and the bark became pale again. The percentage of dark moths fell from 90% in 1960 to 12% in 2000 (data written for this practice question).\nBirds eat peppered moths that rest on tree bark.\n\nExplain why the percentage of dark moths fell.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• dark moths were now easy to see on pale bark / pale moths were camouflaged (1)\n• so birds ate more dark moths / pale moths were more likely to survive (1)\n• pale moths reproduced and passed on the allele for pale colour, so it became more common (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW moths changed colour to match the bark` },
      { q: `A student writes:\n"Bacteria become resistant to antibiotics because they get used to the antibiotic each time a person takes it. The bacteria then change so the antibiotic cannot harm them."\n\nExplain why the student's explanation is wrong. Use ideas about natural selection in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• resistance is caused by a (random) mutation that was already present / not caused by the antibiotic (1)\n• the antibiotic kills the non-resistant bacteria and the resistant ones survive (1)\n• resistant bacteria reproduce and pass on the resistance allele / the population changes, not individual bacteria (1)\nIGNORE bacteria become immune` },
      { q: `In 1950 the myxoma virus was released in Australia to control rabbits, which were a pest. At first the virus killed over 99% of infected rabbits. Some years later, rabbit numbers had grown again. Many rabbits now survived infection.\n\nExplain why rabbit numbers increased again.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• some rabbits had alleles / variants that made them resistant to the virus (1)\n• these rabbits survived and reproduced / had less competition for food (1)\n• the offspring inherited resistance, so the proportion of resistant rabbits in the population increased (1)\nALLOW the virus became less deadly / milder strains survived for one mark if no other mark for the virus is given` },
      { q: `The Grand Canyon in the USA is a very deep valley, more than 1.5 km deep. Long ago one population of tree squirrels lived in the area. As the canyon formed, the squirrels were separated into a population on the north side and a population on the south side. The two sides have different climates and different trees.\nToday the squirrels on the two sides look different.\n\nExplain how the two populations could eventually become two separate species.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the canyon is a barrier / isolates the populations so they cannot interbreed / exchange alleles (1)\n• different mutations / genetic variation arise in each population (1)\n• different conditions on each side so natural selection favours different characteristics / alleles (1)\n• over a long time / many generations the populations become so different they cannot interbreed to produce fertile offspring (1)` },
      { q: `Medium ground finches live on Daphne Major, a small island in the Galapagos. Scientists measured the finches before and after a drought (data written for this practice question).\nDuring the drought, small soft seeds ran out. The only food left was large hard seeds.\n\nYear | Number of finches | Mean beak depth (mm)\n1976 (before drought) | 1300 | 9.4\n1978 (after drought) | 180 | 9.9\n\nDescribe and explain the changes shown in the table.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Describe:\n• number of finches fell (by 1120 / by about 86%) (1)\n• mean beak depth increased (by 0.5 mm) (1)\nExplain:\n• finches with deeper beaks could crack the large hard seeds / small-beaked finches could not get food and died (1)\n• survivors bred and passed on alleles for deep beaks to their offspring (1)` },
      { q: `Scientists recorded the percentage of rats that were resistant to warfarin on a group of farms (data written for this practice question).\nThe farmers used warfarin from 1960 to 1980. They then stopped using it and used a different poison.\n\nYear | Resistant rats (%)\n1960 | 2\n1970 | 40\n1980 | 78\n1995 | 45\n\nResistant rats need more vitamin K than other rats. If they do not get enough they bleed easily.\n\nExplain the changes in the percentage of resistant rats between 1960 and 1995.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1960–1980 increase:\n• warfarin killed non-resistant rats; resistant rats survived (1)\n• resistant rats reproduced and passed on the resistance allele (so percentage rose from 2% to 78%) (1)\n1980–1995 decrease:\n• without warfarin, resistance gave no advantage / resistant rats were at a disadvantage because they need more vitamin K / bleed easily (1)\n• so non-resistant rats were more likely to survive and reproduce / resistance allele became less common (1)` },
      { q: `About 2 million years ago a small group of finches flew from South America to the Galapagos Islands. Today there are more than 15 species of finch on the islands. Each species has a beak suited to a different food, such as seeds, insects or cactus flowers. Many of the species live on different islands.\n\n* Explain how one species of finch could have evolved into many different species on the Galapagos Islands.\nIn your answer you should include:\n• how variation arises and how natural selection acts\n• the role of isolation in forming new species.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of how variation arises and how natural selection acts on finches AND a clear explanation of how isolation leads to the formation of new species. The reasoning is clear and logically structured. The information is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some explanation of natural selection AND some explanation of isolation/speciation, OR a detailed explanation of one of these. There is a line of reasoning with some structure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple statement about variation, natural selection or isolation. The information is basic and communicated in an unstructured way.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nVariation and natural selection\n• genetic variation in beak shape / size within the finch population\n• new alleles arise by (random) mutation\n• different islands have different food / conditions / selection pressures\n• finches compete for limited food\n• finches with beaks best suited to the available food survive and reproduce\n• they pass on the alleles for that beak shape to offspring\n• over many generations the allele becomes more common in that population\nIsolation and speciation\n• populations on different islands are separated by sea (geographical isolation)\n• so they cannot interbreed / alleles are not exchanged between populations\n• different mutations occur in different populations\n• populations become more and more different over a long time\n• eventually they cannot interbreed to produce fertile offspring, so are new species\n• some may later return to the same island but no longer interbreed\nDO NOT ALLOW beaks changed during a bird's life and were passed on` }
    ]
  },
  'B6.3': {
    name: 'How is biodiversity affected by human activity?',
    questions: [
      { q: `This question is about biodiversity.\nBiodiversity can be described at three levels: genetic, species and ecosystem.\n\nA population of cheetahs has very few different alleles of each gene.\nWhich level of biodiversity is low in this population?\nPut a ring around the correct answer.\necosystem      genetic      species`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• genetic (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one answer ringed` },
      { q: `Grey squirrels were brought to Britain from North America in the 1870s. Since then the number of native red squirrels in Britain has fallen greatly.\n\nWhat term is used to describe a species, such as the grey squirrel, that has been introduced by humans to an area where it is not naturally found?`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• non-indigenous / non-native / invasive / alien (species) (1)\nALLOW introduced species\nIGNORE foreign / pest` },
      { q: `Ecosystems provide humans with many benefits. These benefits are called ecosystem services.\n\nWhich of these is an example of an ecosystem service?\nTick (✓) one box.\n☐ Bees pollinating apple trees in an orchard.\n☐ A farmer spraying a crop with pesticide.\n☐ A factory treating its waste water.\n☐ A gardener mowing a lawn.`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• Bees pollinating apple trees in an orchard. (1)\nDO NOT ALLOW more than one box ticked` },
      { q: `The Millennium Seed Bank in the UK stores seeds from thousands of wild plant species from around the world.\nBefore they are stored, the seeds are dried and then kept at about −20 °C.\n\nSuggest why the seeds are stored dry and at a low temperature.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• so the seeds do not germinate / remain dormant / stay viable for a long time (1)\nALLOW to slow down (enzyme activity / respiration in) the seeds\nALLOW to stop the seeds decaying / stop growth of fungi or bacteria\nIGNORE to keep them fresh / safe` },
      { q: `Coral reefs are being damaged in many parts of the world. One cause is a rise in the temperature of the sea.\n\nGive one human activity that is causing the temperature of the sea to rise.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• burning fossil fuels / named fossil fuel (1)\nALLOW deforestation / burning forests\nALLOW releasing (more) carbon dioxide / greenhouse gases (into the atmosphere)\nIGNORE pollution on its own\nDO NOT ALLOW damage to the ozone layer` },
      { q: `In the North Sea, governments set a maximum mass of cod that each fishing fleet is allowed to catch each year. This is called a fishing quota.\n\nGive one reason why fishing quotas are set.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• to stop overfishing / to allow enough fish to survive to breed and keep the population stable (1)\nALLOW so that fish stocks are sustainable / do not run out\nALLOW to prevent cod becoming extinct (in the area)\nIGNORE to save fish on its own` },
      { q: `This question is about biodiversity and human activity.\nComplete the sentences.\nUse words from the list. Each word may be used once, more than once or not at all.\necosystems      extinct      genes      habitats      population      species\n\nBiodiversity is the variety of living things, including the variety of ______ within a species, the variety of ______ and the variety of ______ .\nWhen humans clear land for building they destroy the ______ of many organisms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• genes / alleles; species; ecosystems; habitats\nALLOW species and ecosystems in either order\n4 correct = 2 marks\n2 or 3 correct = 1 mark\n0 or 1 correct = 0 marks` },
      { q: `Scientists estimate the total mass of adult cod in an area of sea each year. This is called the spawning stock.\nIn 2010 the spawning stock was 120 000 tonnes. In 2020 it was 84 000 tonnes. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the spawning stock between 2010 and 2020.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 120 000 − 84 000 = 36 000 (tonnes) (1)\n• (36 000 ÷ 120 000) × 100 = 30 (%) (1)\nALLOW 30 with no working for 2 marks\nALLOW 70 (%) for 1 mark (84 000 ÷ 120 000 × 100)` },
      { q: `Large areas of tropical rainforest in South America are cut down every year to make space for cattle farming and growing soya.\n\nDescribe two ways that cutting down the rainforest reduces biodiversity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• destroys the habitat of many species (1)\n• removes food sources / breaks food chains or webs (1)\n• (some) species may become extinct / populations decrease (1)\n• replaced by a monoculture / only one or a few species are farmed (1)\n• the remaining forest is split into small fragments / small isolated populations (1)\nIGNORE less oxygen / climate change on their own` },
      { q: `Many hedgerows on UK farms were removed between 1950 and 1990 to make fields larger. Hedgerows contain many different plant species and provide nesting sites.\n\nExplain why removing hedgerows reduces the number of bird species found on a farm.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• loss of nesting sites / shelter / habitat (for birds) (1)\n• loss of food sources, e.g. berries / seeds / insects that live in hedges (1)\nALLOW birds move away / cannot breed / die out locally (as a consequence of either point)\nIGNORE hedgerows give birds oxygen` },
      { q: `Fishing nets have holes called mesh. The law in some areas requires fishing boats to use nets with a larger mesh size than before.\nLarger mesh lets smaller, younger fish escape through the net.\n\nExplain how using nets with a larger mesh size helps to keep fish populations stable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• young / small fish survive (and are not caught) (1)\n• so they can grow to adults / reach breeding age and reproduce (replacing fish that are caught) (1)\nALLOW fewer fish are caught / reduces bycatch (for first point)\nIGNORE saves fish on its own` },
      { q: `The Javan rhino is critically endangered. There are fewer than 100 Javan rhinos left in the wild, and they live in one national park in Indonesia. Some people have suggested a captive breeding programme in zoos.\n\nGive two reasons why captive breeding alone may not be enough to save the Javan rhino.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• small number of animals means low genetic diversity / inbreeding (1)\n• animals bred in captivity may not survive when released / lack survival skills (1)\n• their natural habitat may be destroyed / there may be nowhere suitable to release them (1)\n• the cause of decline, e.g. poaching, may not have been removed (1)\n• rhinos breed slowly / may not breed well in captivity (1)\n• it is expensive (1)` },
      { q: `A student used a 1 m² quadrat to count the number of different plant species at ten random positions in a wildflower meadow.\nThe numbers of species in the ten quadrats were:\n4      7      5      6      3      5      8      4      6      2\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mean number of plant species per quadrat.\nMean = ______ species per quadrat`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 4 + 7 + 5 + 6 + 3 + 5 + 8 + 4 + 6 + 2 = 50 (1)\n• 50 ÷ 10 = 5 (species per quadrat) (1)\nALLOW 5 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Woodlands in the UK provide many ecosystem services.\n\nGive two ways that humans benefit from the biodiversity in a woodland.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• timber / wood / fuel (1)\n• food, e.g. fruits / nuts / game (1)\n• source of new medicines (1)\n• trees absorb carbon dioxide / store carbon (1)\n• reduce flooding / soil erosion (1)\n• pollinators / natural predators of crop pests live there (1)\n• recreation / tourism / improves wellbeing (1)\nALLOW provides oxygen (1)\nIGNORE habitat for animals unless linked to a human benefit` },
      { q: `The rosy periwinkle is a plant that grows in Madagascar. Drugs made from chemicals found in the rosy periwinkle are used to treat some types of cancer, such as leukaemia.\nOnly about 10% of Madagascar's original forest remains.\n\nExplain why loss of forest worries scientists who are developing new medicines.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• species / plants could become extinct before they are studied / discovered (1)\n• these species may contain chemicals that could be used to make new medicines / treat diseases (1)\nALLOW the loss of species that might have been useful as medicines` },
      { q: `Satellite images show that a country lost 18 000 km² of forest over a 6-year period. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the mean area of forest lost each year.\nGive the unit for your answer.\nMean area lost = ______ unit ______`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 18 000 ÷ 6 = 3000 (1)\n• km² per year / km²/year / km² yr⁻¹ (1)\nALLOW unit mark independently` },
      { q: `Polar bears hunt seals from floating sea ice in the Arctic. Climate change is causing the area of sea ice in summer to decrease.\n\nExplain how a decrease in sea ice could cause the polar bear population to decrease.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• polar bears have less ice to hunt from / cannot reach seals / have less food (1)\n• so fewer bears survive / fewer bears breed or raise cubs (1)\nALLOW bears have to swim further and use more energy (for first point)\nALLOW starvation (for second point)` },
      { q: `Large amounts of plastic waste end up in the oceans. Some plastics break into very small pieces called microplastics.\n\nDescribe two ways that plastic waste can harm marine organisms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• animals become trapped / tangled in plastic, e.g. nets / rings (1)\n• animals eat plastic and it blocks their digestive system / makes them starve (1)\n• microplastics are eaten by small organisms and pass along food chains (1)\n• plastic releases toxic chemicals (1)\n• plastic blocks light / smothers organisms on the sea bed or coral (1)\nIGNORE pollution / kills them on its own` },
      { q: `The signal crayfish was brought to Britain from North America in the 1970s. Since then the population of the native white-clawed crayfish has decreased.\nSignal crayfish are larger than white-clawed crayfish and carry a disease called crayfish plague that kills white-clawed crayfish.\n\nExplain why the population of white-clawed crayfish has decreased.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• signal crayfish outcompete white-clawed crayfish for food / space / shelter (1)\n• signal crayfish spread crayfish plague / a pathogen to white-clawed crayfish, which die (1)\nALLOW signal crayfish eat / prey on white-clawed crayfish (for first point)\nIGNORE fight on its own` },
      { q: `In South-East Asia, large areas of rainforest have been replaced by oil palm plantations. A plantation is a large area with only oil palm trees of the same age.\n\nExplain why a plantation has a lower biodiversity than the rainforest it replaced.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a plantation is a monoculture / has only one plant species (1)\n• so there are fewer habitats / food sources / niches for animals (1)\nALLOW pesticides / herbicides are used which kill other species (for second point)\nALLOW fewer layers / less variety in structure, so fewer places to live` },
      { q: `A government wants to create a protected area in a forest to conserve tigers. People living near the forest have used it for many years to collect firewood and graze their cattle.\n\nSuggest two reasons why some local people may be against the protected area.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• they can no longer collect firewood / food / resources from the forest (1)\n• they lose land for grazing / farming / lose income (1)\n• tigers may attack their livestock / people (1)\n• they may have to move from their homes / land (1)\n• they were not consulted / lose traditional way of life (1)` },
      { q: `Cheetahs have very low genetic diversity because their population became very small thousands of years ago.\n\nExplain why a species with low genetic diversity is more at risk of extinction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• there is little variation (in alleles / characteristics) in the population (1)\n• so if the environment changes / a new disease appears, few or no individuals may be able to survive it (1)\nALLOW inbreeding leads to more harmful recessive conditions (for second point)\nIGNORE they cannot adapt on its own` },
      { q: `Scientists assessed 16 000 species of animals in a region. They found that 2400 of these species were threatened with extinction. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage of the species assessed that were threatened with extinction.\nPercentage = ______ %`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (2400 ÷ 16 000) × 100 (1)\n• = 15 (%) (1)\nALLOW 15 with no working for 2 marks` },
      { q: `Building roads and towns has split a large woodland into several small patches. Conservationists plan to plant strips of trees, called wildlife corridors, to join the patches together.\n\nExplain how wildlife corridors can help to conserve species in the woodland.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• animals can move between patches to find food / mates / new territory (1)\n• so populations are larger / can interbreed, which increases genetic diversity / reduces inbreeding (1)\nALLOW reduces the risk of a small population dying out (for second point)` },
      { q: `Many shark species are caught by fishing boats, often by accident as bycatch. Sharks take many years to reach breeding age and produce only a few young at a time.\n\nExplain why sharks are more at risk from overfishing than fish such as sardines, which breed at one year old and produce thousands of eggs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• sharks reproduce slowly / produce few offspring (1)\n• so the population cannot replace the sharks that are caught (quickly enough) / many are caught before they have bred (1)\nALLOW sardine populations can recover quickly (for second point if linked to fast reproduction)` },
      { q: `A student counted the bees seen in 10 minutes on two farms on one day in June.\nFarm A uses pesticides: 14 bees seen.\nFarm B does not use pesticides: 26 bees seen.\n(data written for this practice question)\nThe student concluded that pesticides reduce the number of bees on farms.\n\nGive two reasons why this conclusion may not be valid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• only one count / not repeated / only one day (1)\n• only two farms were compared / sample size too small (1)\n• other variables were not controlled, e.g. weather / time of day / number of flowers / crop grown (1)\n• bees move between farms / a count of bees seen may not measure population (1)\nIGNORE it is not a fair test without further explanation` },
      { q: `A farmer spread too much nitrate fertiliser on a field next to a river. Heavy rain washed some of the fertiliser into the river. A few weeks later many fish in the river died.\n\nExplain why the fish died.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• nitrates cause rapid growth of algae / algal bloom (1)\n• algae block light so plants (under the water) die (1)\n• (dead plants / algae are broken down by) decomposers / bacteria which increase in number (1)\n• decomposers / bacteria use up oxygen in the water (for respiration) (1)\n• fish cannot respire (aerobically) so die (1)\nALLOW eutrophication for 1 mark if no other marks awarded\nDO NOT ALLOW fertiliser poisons the fish` },
      { q: `Coral reefs have a very high biodiversity. Corals contain algae that provide them with sugars made by photosynthesis.\nWhen the sea becomes too warm the corals lose their algae and turn white. This is called coral bleaching. If the high temperatures continue, the corals die.\n\nExplain how the burning of fossil fuels can lead to a fall in the biodiversity of coral reefs.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide (into the atmosphere) (1)\n• (increasing greenhouse effect so) global temperatures / sea temperatures rise (1)\n• corals bleach / lose algae and die, so many species that depend on them for food / habitat decrease or die out (1)\nALLOW carbon dioxide dissolves in the sea making it more acidic, which damages coral (for third point)` },
      { q: `Hedgehogs are found in many British gardens and fields.\nA survey estimated that there were 1240 hedgehogs in a county in 2000 and 310 in 2020. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the number of hedgehogs between 2000 and 2020.\nSuggest one human activity that could have caused this decrease.\nPercentage decrease = ______ %\nHuman activity ______`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Calculation:\n• 1240 − 310 = 930 (1)\n• (930 ÷ 1240) × 100 = 75 (%) (1)\nALLOW 75 with no working for 2 marks\nSuggestion (1):\n• removal of hedgerows / building houses or roads / loss of habitat (1)\nALLOW use of pesticides / slug pellets reducing food / poisoning\nALLOW road traffic / fences between gardens stopping movement` },
      { q: `Some plant species are conserved by storing their seeds in a seed bank. Other plant species are conserved by protecting the places where they grow.\n\nExplain two advantages and one disadvantage of conserving plants in a seed bank rather than in their natural habitat.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Advantages (max 2):\n• seeds can be stored for a long time / many years (1)\n• a large number of species / seeds can be stored in a small space / cheaply (1)\n• seeds are protected from threats such as habitat loss / disease / climate change / natural disasters (1)\n• seeds can be used to grow new plants to replant / reintroduce if the species dies out in the wild (1)\nDisadvantage (max 1):\n• the plants do not evolve / adapt to changes in the environment while stored (1)\n• the rest of the ecosystem / animals that depend on the plant are not protected (1)\n• some seeds cannot survive drying / freezing, or may lose viability (1)\n• may store only a small part of the genetic diversity of the species (1)` },
      { q: `In 1935 about 100 cane toads were brought from South America to Australia to eat beetles that were damaging sugar cane crops. There are now more than 200 million cane toads in Australia.\nCane toads produce a poison. Native Australian predators, such as quolls and snakes, die if they eat a cane toad.\n\nExplain why the introduction of cane toads has reduced biodiversity in Australia.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• cane toads had no natural predators / diseases in Australia, so the population increased rapidly (1)\n• native predators die when they eat cane toads (so their populations fall) (1)\n• cane toads compete with native animals for food / space (1)\n• cane toads eat many native species / insects (1)\n• some native species may become (locally) extinct (1)\nIGNORE cane toads are an invasive species without explanation` },
      { q: `Red kites became extinct in England in the 1870s, mainly because they were shot and poisoned. In 1989 conservationists began releasing red kites from Spain into the Chilterns. There are now thousands of red kites in England.\n\nSuggest three things conservationists should have checked before releasing the red kites.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• the cause of the original extinction had been removed / shooting and poisoning had stopped / were illegal (1)\n• there was enough suitable habitat / nesting sites (1)\n• there was enough food / prey (1)\n• the birds were healthy / free from disease (1)\n• enough birds were released to give genetic diversity / a breeding population (1)\n• the release would not harm other species in the area (1)\n• local people / landowners supported the release (1)` },
      { q: `Governments use different methods to make fishing sustainable. These methods include fishing quotas and closed seasons, when fishing is not allowed during the time of year when the fish breed.\n\nExplain how these methods help to conserve fish stocks.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• quotas limit the number / mass of fish caught (1)\n• so enough adult fish are left to breed / the population can be replaced (1)\n• closed seasons allow fish to breed / spawn without being disturbed or caught (1)\nALLOW the rate of fishing is no faster than the rate the population can reproduce (for second point)` },
      { q: `Mangrove forests grow along tropical coasts. Young fish of many species live among mangrove roots before moving out to sea. Mangroves also reduce damage from storm waves.\nIn some countries mangrove forests have been cleared to build shrimp farms.\n\nExplain why clearing mangroves could cause economic problems for people living on the coast.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• fewer young fish survive / loss of nursery habitat (1)\n• so fish populations at sea decrease / smaller catches for fishermen (1)\n• (less income from fishing / less food) (1)\n• coast is less protected, so more damage to homes / land from storms / flooding / erosion (1)\n• costs of building sea defences / repairs (1)` },
      { q: `Scientists compared the numbers of lobsters caught in traps inside and outside a marine protected area where fishing is not allowed. The table shows their results.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nYear | Mean lobsters per trap inside the protected area | Mean lobsters per trap outside the protected area\n2010 | 1.2 | 1.1\n2014 | 2.6 | 1.0\n2018 | 4.1 | 1.3\n\nUse the data to describe the effect of the protected area and explain the results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• inside: the mean number per trap increased from 1.2 to 4.1 / more than tripled (1)\n• outside: the number stayed about the same (1.0–1.3) (1)\n• (inside) lobsters are not caught so more survive to breed / grow, so the population increases (1)\nALLOW numbers were similar in 2010 and inside became much higher than outside` },
      { q: `Wolves were removed from Yellowstone National Park in the USA by 1926. Without wolves, the number of elk (a type of deer) increased. The elk ate young willow and aspen trees by the rivers.\nIn 1995 wolves were reintroduced to the park.\n\nExplain how reintroducing wolves could increase biodiversity in the park.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• wolves eat / prey on elk so the elk population decreases (1)\n• elk avoid areas near rivers / graze less (1)\n• more young trees / willow / aspen survive and grow (1)\n• provides habitat / food for other species, e.g. beavers / birds / insects (1)\n• wolves leave carcasses that feed scavengers (1)` },
      { q: `The table shows the area of rainforest in a country and the number of known bird species living there.\nThe government made it illegal to clear rainforest in 2012.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nYear | Area of rainforest (million km²) | Number of bird species recorded\n2000 | 3.4 | 42\n2006 | 3.1 | 31\n2012 | 2.9 | 20\n2018 | 2.9 | 18\n\nA newspaper says: "Making clearing illegal has saved the country's birds."\nEvaluate this claim. Use the data in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `For the claim (max 2):\n• the area of rainforest decreased from 2000 to 2012 but did not decrease after 2012 / stayed at 2.9 million km² (1)\n• the decrease in bird species slowed after 2012 / only fell by 2 from 2012 to 2018 compared with 11 in each earlier period (1)\nAgainst the claim (max 2):\n• the number of bird species still fell after 2012 (from 20 to 18) (1)\n• (overall) 24 species / 57% of species have been lost since 2000 (1)\n• the remaining forest may be fragmented / other causes of decline, e.g. hunting / climate change (1)\n• only correlation / data do not prove the law caused the change / species may be recorded differently (1)\nALLOW a conclusion that the law has helped but not saved all the birds` },
      { q: `A company wants to build 500 new houses on an area of heathland. The heathland is home to rare species, including the sand lizard and the Dartford warbler. The local council must decide whether to allow the development.\n\nDiscuss the arguments for and against allowing the development.\nIn your answer, include biological, economic and social points.`, marks: 4, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `For (max 2):\n• homes are needed for people / reduces housing shortage (1)\n• creates jobs / brings money to the local economy (1)\n• the company could create or protect another area of heathland / move the animals (1)\nAgainst (max 3):\n• destroys habitat of rare species / may cause local extinction (1)\n• reduces biodiversity / affects food webs / other species that depend on the heathland (1)\n• loss of ecosystem services, e.g. recreation / tourism / carbon storage (1)\n• rare species may be protected by law / moving animals often fails (1)\nTo gain 4 marks at least one point for and one point against must be given` },
      { q: `Zoos around the world work together to run captive breeding programmes for endangered species such as the Amur leopard. Each zoo keeps records of every animal's parents. Animals are moved between zoos for breeding.\n\nExplain why zoos move animals between zoos for breeding and describe the challenges of releasing captive-bred animals back into the wild.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Moving animals (max 2):\n• to avoid breeding closely related animals / inbreeding (1)\n• to keep / increase genetic diversity (1)\n• so offspring are less likely to inherit harmful (recessive) conditions / population is better able to survive changes or disease (1)\nChallenges (max 2):\n• animals may lack survival skills, e.g. hunting / avoiding predators (1)\n• their habitat may have been lost / the original threat, e.g. poaching, may remain (1)\n• animals may carry diseases / have no immunity to diseases in the wild (1)\n• local people may be against the release / conflict with farmers (1)\n• it is expensive / animals need monitoring after release (1)` },
      { q: `Orangutans live only in the rainforests of Borneo and Sumatra. The table shows estimates of the area of forest on Borneo and the number of Bornean orangutans.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nYear | Forest area on Borneo (million hectares) | Estimated number of Bornean orangutans\n1973 | 55 | 230 000\n2000 | 40 | 150 000\n2020 | 31 | 100 000\n\nMuch of the forest has been cleared for oil palm plantations and logging. Many local people earn their living from palm oil.\n\n* Explain how human activities have affected biodiversity on Borneo and evaluate ways in which the orangutans and their forest could be conserved.\nUse the data in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Uses the data to give a detailed explanation of how human activities have reduced biodiversity AND evaluates at least two conservation methods, giving benefits and drawbacks including social or economic points. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured. The information presented is relevant and substantiated.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explains how human activities have affected biodiversity, with some use of the data, AND describes at least one conservation method with a benefit or drawback. There is a line of reasoning presented with some structure. The information presented is relevant and supported by some evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Describes a change in the data OR states a conservation method. There is an attempt at a logical structure with a line of reasoning. The information is in the most part relevant.\n0 marks: No response or no response worthy of credit.\n\nIndicative scientific points may include:\nEffects of human activities\n• forest area fell from 55 to 31 million hectares (fall of 24 million hectares / about 44%)\n• orangutan numbers fell from 230 000 to 100 000 (about 57%)\n• numbers fell faster than forest area, suggesting other factors, e.g. hunting / fragmentation / fires\n• loss of habitat and food sources (fruit trees)\n• oil palm plantations are monocultures with low biodiversity\n• fragmentation leaves small isolated populations with low genetic diversity\n• loss of other species and ecosystem services, e.g. carbon storage, medicines\n• clearing / burning forest releases carbon dioxide, adding to climate change\nConservation methods and evaluation\n• protected areas / national parks – protect whole ecosystem, but need enforcing and may take land / income from local people\n• wildlife corridors joining fragments – allow interbreeding, increase genetic diversity\n• captive breeding / rescue and rehabilitation centres – increase numbers, but expensive, low genetic diversity, animals may not survive release\n• sustainable / certified palm oil, using land already cleared – keeps jobs, but hard to check and consumers may pay more\n• replanting / restoring forest – slow to recover\n• ecotourism – gives local people income from conserving forest\n• laws against hunting / logging – need enforcement\n• the needs of local people and economic benefits must be balanced against conservation` }
    ]
  }
};
